{
19: {skill_tested: "Identify a detail that explains the narrator's reaction.", question: "According to Passage A, why does the narrator initially feel more like an exhibit label than a useful visitor?", option1: "Mara records the narrator's explanation instead of using the old tool immediately.", option2: "The apprentices refuse to follow the narrator's instructions about the doorway.", option3: "The renovated theater no longer contains a backstage work area.", option4: "Mara asks the narrator to leave before the theater reopens.", correctAnswer: "Mara records the narrator's explanation instead of using the old tool immediately.", explanation: "Step 1: The narrator expects identifying the wooden wheel to produce a sense of usefulness.<br><br>Step 2: Mara photographs the wheel and stores the explanation in an archive, making the narrator feel recorded as part of the past rather than involved in present work.<br><br>Answer: Mara records the narrator's explanation instead of using the old tool immediately.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators returning to institutions where they once worked.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>The side entrance of the Bellwether Theater still resisted being opened. I leaned my shoulder against it, just as I had during six seasons of arriving before dawn to paint scenery. The door finally yielded, but the familiar smell of sawdust and hot cables had been replaced by the mineral scent of fresh plaster. In the lobby, volunteers were fastening silver letters to a wall that now read BELLWETHER ARTS CENTER.<br><br>I had come because Mara, the new technical director, had asked whether I could identify several unlabeled tools before the theater reopened. She met me carrying a tablet instead of the ring of brass keys that Mr. Vale, my old supervisor, used to wear. As we crossed the stage, floor panels lit beneath our feet to mark safe paths through the darkness. I tried not to stare. Mr. Vale had taught us to navigate by counting paces and feeling for seams with our shoes.<br><br>Backstage, most of the tools were ordinary. I named a canvas stretcher, two hand drills, and a curved blade used to trim painted cloth. Then Mara uncovered a wooden wheel with a deep groove along its rim. I explained how two such wheels had once guided ropes above the stage. She listened, photographed the wheel, and entered my explanation into the building's digital archive. I expected to feel useful. Instead, I felt like a label being attached to an exhibit.<br><br>A group of teenage apprentices entered carrying sections of a half-built doorway. One student set a corner down too quickly, twisting the frame. Before Mara could speak, I showed them how to brace the joint with a temporary diagonal strip. The students crowded close as I measured, tapped the frame square, and removed the strip. When they lifted the doorway again, it held. A girl with blue chalk across her sleeve asked why the brace worked, and my hands answered before my voice did.<br><br>On my way out, I passed the wall of silver letters. Through the open auditorium doors, I could hear the apprentices arguing cheerfully about paint. The old brass keys were gone, and no one would cross the stage by counting Mr. Vale's careful paces. Yet the doorway stood straight because a method had traveled from his hands to mine and, for at least one afternoon, from mine to theirs. The building had not kept my past intact. It had made room for it to continue without looking the same.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When I volunteered at the municipal greenhouse in college, visitors rarely saw me. I watered seedlings before opening, mixed soil behind the propagation shed, and left as school groups began rattling the gate. Ten years later, I returned as an architect assigned to design a rainwater system for the greenhouse's expansion. I arrived with rolled plans, polished shoes, and the confidence of someone who had rehearsed every answer.<br><br>Mrs. Okafor, who had supervised the volunteers, was waiting beside the old cistern. She greeted me warmly and then ignored my plans. Instead, she asked me to help move trays before the afternoon heat. Soon soil marked my sleeves, and my polished shoes had acquired a border of mud. I found myself checking the undersides of leaves for pests, an old habit so automatic that Mrs. Okafor laughed when she noticed.<br><br>At the planning meeting, I presented calculations showing that water captured from the new roofs could supply nearly half the greenhouse's summer needs. The board members studied the diagrams politely. Then Mrs. Okafor placed one of my seedling trays on the table. She explained that I had once spent an entire season experimenting with ways to keep young plants alive during watering restrictions. The proposed system, she said, was not merely an architect's solution; it grew from knowledge acquired in this greenhouse.<br><br>Her story unsettled me at first. I had wanted the board to judge the design as professional work, not as an affectionate project by a former volunteer. But when questions began, the members asked about both the calculations and the plants. Because I understood where runoff collected and where seedlings dried fastest, I could connect each pipe on the plan to a specific need. The two kinds of knowledge strengthened rather than diminished each other.<br><br>After the meeting, Mrs. Okafor handed me the tray. Its seedlings were descendants of a variety I had helped cultivate. I had imagined returning in a new role would require leaving the earlier one outside the gate. Carrying the tray and my plans together, I understood that the greenhouse had not summoned me backward. It had shown me that the work I did now had roots I no longer needed to conceal."},
20: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of a figurative word.", question: "As used in the final paragraph of Passage B, the word 'roots' most nearly refers to the narrator's:", option1: "continuing dependence on Mrs. Okafor's approval.", option2: "earlier experience that helped shape current expertise.", option3: "plan to replace the greenhouse's existing plants.", option4: "reluctance to accept professional responsibility.", correctAnswer: "earlier experience that helped shape current expertise.", explanation: "Step 1: The narrator carries both the seedling tray associated with earlier volunteer work and the plans associated with current professional work.<br><br>Step 2: The narrator recognizes that the earlier experience contributed to the present design, so 'roots' refers to that formative experience.<br><br>Answer: earlier experience that helped shape current expertise.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators returning to institutions where they once worked.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>The side entrance of the Bellwether Theater still resisted being opened. I leaned my shoulder against it, just as I had during six seasons of arriving before dawn to paint scenery. The door finally yielded, but the familiar smell of sawdust and hot cables had been replaced by the mineral scent of fresh plaster. In the lobby, volunteers were fastening silver letters to a wall that now read BELLWETHER ARTS CENTER.<br><br>I had come because Mara, the new technical director, had asked whether I could identify several unlabeled tools before the theater reopened. She met me carrying a tablet instead of the ring of brass keys that Mr. Vale, my old supervisor, used to wear. As we crossed the stage, floor panels lit beneath our feet to mark safe paths through the darkness. I tried not to stare. Mr. Vale had taught us to navigate by counting paces and feeling for seams with our shoes.<br><br>Backstage, most of the tools were ordinary. I named a canvas stretcher, two hand drills, and a curved blade used to trim painted cloth. Then Mara uncovered a wooden wheel with a deep groove along its rim. I explained how two such wheels had once guided ropes above the stage. She listened, photographed the wheel, and entered my explanation into the building's digital archive. I expected to feel useful. Instead, I felt like a label being attached to an exhibit.<br><br>A group of teenage apprentices entered carrying sections of a half-built doorway. One student set a corner down too quickly, twisting the frame. Before Mara could speak, I showed them how to brace the joint with a temporary diagonal strip. The students crowded close as I measured, tapped the frame square, and removed the strip. When they lifted the doorway again, it held. A girl with blue chalk across her sleeve asked why the brace worked, and my hands answered before my voice did.<br><br>On my way out, I passed the wall of silver letters. Through the open auditorium doors, I could hear the apprentices arguing cheerfully about paint. The old brass keys were gone, and no one would cross the stage by counting Mr. Vale's careful paces. Yet the doorway stood straight because a method had traveled from his hands to mine and, for at least one afternoon, from mine to theirs. The building had not kept my past intact. It had made room for it to continue without looking the same.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When I volunteered at the municipal greenhouse in college, visitors rarely saw me. I watered seedlings before opening, mixed soil behind the propagation shed, and left as school groups began rattling the gate. Ten years later, I returned as an architect assigned to design a rainwater system for the greenhouse's expansion. I arrived with rolled plans, polished shoes, and the confidence of someone who had rehearsed every answer.<br><br>Mrs. Okafor, who had supervised the volunteers, was waiting beside the old cistern. She greeted me warmly and then ignored my plans. Instead, she asked me to help move trays before the afternoon heat. Soon soil marked my sleeves, and my polished shoes had acquired a border of mud. I found myself checking the undersides of leaves for pests, an old habit so automatic that Mrs. Okafor laughed when she noticed.<br><br>At the planning meeting, I presented calculations showing that water captured from the new roofs could supply nearly half the greenhouse's summer needs. The board members studied the diagrams politely. Then Mrs. Okafor placed one of my seedling trays on the table. She explained that I had once spent an entire season experimenting with ways to keep young plants alive during watering restrictions. The proposed system, she said, was not merely an architect's solution; it grew from knowledge acquired in this greenhouse.<br><br>Her story unsettled me at first. I had wanted the board to judge the design as professional work, not as an affectionate project by a former volunteer. But when questions began, the members asked about both the calculations and the plants. Because I understood where runoff collected and where seedlings dried fastest, I could connect each pipe on the plan to a specific need. The two kinds of knowledge strengthened rather than diminished each other.<br><br>After the meeting, Mrs. Okafor handed me the tray. Its seedlings were descendants of a variety I had helped cultivate. I had imagined returning in a new role would require leaving the earlier one outside the gate. Carrying the tray and my plans together, I understood that the greenhouse had not summoned me backward. It had shown me that the work I did now had roots I no longer needed to conceal."},
21: {skill_tested: "Identify a central idea shared by paired passages.", question: "Which statement best expresses a central idea shared by both passages?", option1: "Institutions preserve their traditions most effectively by resisting technological change.", option2: "Professional expertise is weakened when it becomes connected to personal memory.", option3: "Returning to former work can reveal that past experience remains useful in altered circumstances.", option4: "People understand their former institutions better when they avoid participating in current work.", correctAnswer: "Returning to former work can reveal that past experience remains useful in altered circumstances.", explanation: "Step 1: In Passage A, the narrator's inherited method helps the apprentices repair a frame.<br><br>Step 2: In Passage B, the narrator's volunteer knowledge strengthens a professional design.<br><br>Answer: Returning to former work can reveal that past experience remains useful in altered circumstances.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators returning to institutions where they once worked.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>The side entrance of the Bellwether Theater still resisted being opened. I leaned my shoulder against it, just as I had during six seasons of arriving before dawn to paint scenery. The door finally yielded, but the familiar smell of sawdust and hot cables had been replaced by the mineral scent of fresh plaster. In the lobby, volunteers were fastening silver letters to a wall that now read BELLWETHER ARTS CENTER.<br><br>I had come because Mara, the new technical director, had asked whether I could identify several unlabeled tools before the theater reopened. She met me carrying a tablet instead of the ring of brass keys that Mr. Vale, my old supervisor, used to wear. As we crossed the stage, floor panels lit beneath our feet to mark safe paths through the darkness. I tried not to stare. Mr. Vale had taught us to navigate by counting paces and feeling for seams with our shoes.<br><br>Backstage, most of the tools were ordinary. I named a canvas stretcher, two hand drills, and a curved blade used to trim painted cloth. Then Mara uncovered a wooden wheel with a deep groove along its rim. I explained how two such wheels had once guided ropes above the stage. She listened, photographed the wheel, and entered my explanation into the building's digital archive. I expected to feel useful. Instead, I felt like a label being attached to an exhibit.<br><br>A group of teenage apprentices entered carrying sections of a half-built doorway. One student set a corner down too quickly, twisting the frame. Before Mara could speak, I showed them how to brace the joint with a temporary diagonal strip. The students crowded close as I measured, tapped the frame square, and removed the strip. When they lifted the doorway again, it held. A girl with blue chalk across her sleeve asked why the brace worked, and my hands answered before my voice did.<br><br>On my way out, I passed the wall of silver letters. Through the open auditorium doors, I could hear the apprentices arguing cheerfully about paint. The old brass keys were gone, and no one would cross the stage by counting Mr. Vale's careful paces. Yet the doorway stood straight because a method had traveled from his hands to mine and, for at least one afternoon, from mine to theirs. The building had not kept my past intact. It had made room for it to continue without looking the same.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When I volunteered at the municipal greenhouse in college, visitors rarely saw me. I watered seedlings before opening, mixed soil behind the propagation shed, and left as school groups began rattling the gate. Ten years later, I returned as an architect assigned to design a rainwater system for the greenhouse's expansion. I arrived with rolled plans, polished shoes, and the confidence of someone who had rehearsed every answer.<br><br>Mrs. Okafor, who had supervised the volunteers, was waiting beside the old cistern. She greeted me warmly and then ignored my plans. Instead, she asked me to help move trays before the afternoon heat. Soon soil marked my sleeves, and my polished shoes had acquired a border of mud. I found myself checking the undersides of leaves for pests, an old habit so automatic that Mrs. Okafor laughed when she noticed.<br><br>At the planning meeting, I presented calculations showing that water captured from the new roofs could supply nearly half the greenhouse's summer needs. The board members studied the diagrams politely. Then Mrs. Okafor placed one of my seedling trays on the table. She explained that I had once spent an entire season experimenting with ways to keep young plants alive during watering restrictions. The proposed system, she said, was not merely an architect's solution; it grew from knowledge acquired in this greenhouse.<br><br>Her story unsettled me at first. I had wanted the board to judge the design as professional work, not as an affectionate project by a former volunteer. But when questions began, the members asked about both the calculations and the plants. Because I understood where runoff collected and where seedlings dried fastest, I could connect each pipe on the plan to a specific need. The two kinds of knowledge strengthened rather than diminished each other.<br><br>After the meeting, Mrs. Okafor handed me the tray. Its seedlings were descendants of a variety I had helped cultivate. I had imagined returning in a new role would require leaving the earlier one outside the gate. Carrying the tray and my plans together, I understood that the greenhouse had not summoned me backward. It had shown me that the work I did now had roots I no longer needed to conceal."},
22: {skill_tested: "Infer how a character's actions revise the narrator's interpretation.", question: "In Passage A, the apprentices' arrival primarily changes the narrator's understanding of the visit by showing that:", option1: "Mara lacks the technical knowledge required to supervise them.", option2: "the theater's digital archive contains inaccurate descriptions.", option3: "the apprentices prefer traditional tools to modern equipment.", option4: "practical knowledge can continue through use even when older customs disappear.", correctAnswer: "practical knowledge can continue through use even when older customs disappear.", explanation: "Step 1: Before the apprentices arrive, the narrator experiences the old tools and archived explanation as evidence of being confined to the past.<br><br>Step 2: Repairing the doorway lets the narrator transmit a learned method to new workers.<br><br>Step 3: That transfer supports the later realization that continuity does not require the institution to remain visually unchanged.<br><br>Answer: practical knowledge can continue through use even when older customs disappear.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators returning to institutions where they once worked.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>The side entrance of the Bellwether Theater still resisted being opened. I leaned my shoulder against it, just as I had during six seasons of arriving before dawn to paint scenery. The door finally yielded, but the familiar smell of sawdust and hot cables had been replaced by the mineral scent of fresh plaster. In the lobby, volunteers were fastening silver letters to a wall that now read BELLWETHER ARTS CENTER.<br><br>I had come because Mara, the new technical director, had asked whether I could identify several unlabeled tools before the theater reopened. She met me carrying a tablet instead of the ring of brass keys that Mr. Vale, my old supervisor, used to wear. As we crossed the stage, floor panels lit beneath our feet to mark safe paths through the darkness. I tried not to stare. Mr. Vale had taught us to navigate by counting paces and feeling for seams with our shoes.<br><br>Backstage, most of the tools were ordinary. I named a canvas stretcher, two hand drills, and a curved blade used to trim painted cloth. Then Mara uncovered a wooden wheel with a deep groove along its rim. I explained how two such wheels had once guided ropes above the stage. She listened, photographed the wheel, and entered my explanation into the building's digital archive. I expected to feel useful. Instead, I felt like a label being attached to an exhibit.<br><br>A group of teenage apprentices entered carrying sections of a half-built doorway. One student set a corner down too quickly, twisting the frame. Before Mara could speak, I showed them how to brace the joint with a temporary diagonal strip. The students crowded close as I measured, tapped the frame square, and removed the strip. When they lifted the doorway again, it held. A girl with blue chalk across her sleeve asked why the brace worked, and my hands answered before my voice did.<br><br>On my way out, I passed the wall of silver letters. Through the open auditorium doors, I could hear the apprentices arguing cheerfully about paint. The old brass keys were gone, and no one would cross the stage by counting Mr. Vale's careful paces. Yet the doorway stood straight because a method had traveled from his hands to mine and, for at least one afternoon, from mine to theirs. The building had not kept my past intact. It had made room for it to continue without looking the same.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When I volunteered at the municipal greenhouse in college, visitors rarely saw me. I watered seedlings before opening, mixed soil behind the propagation shed, and left as school groups began rattling the gate. Ten years later, I returned as an architect assigned to design a rainwater system for the greenhouse's expansion. I arrived with rolled plans, polished shoes, and the confidence of someone who had rehearsed every answer.<br><br>Mrs. Okafor, who had supervised the volunteers, was waiting beside the old cistern. She greeted me warmly and then ignored my plans. Instead, she asked me to help move trays before the afternoon heat. Soon soil marked my sleeves, and my polished shoes had acquired a border of mud. I found myself checking the undersides of leaves for pests, an old habit so automatic that Mrs. Okafor laughed when she noticed.<br><br>At the planning meeting, I presented calculations showing that water captured from the new roofs could supply nearly half the greenhouse's summer needs. The board members studied the diagrams politely. Then Mrs. Okafor placed one of my seedling trays on the table. She explained that I had once spent an entire season experimenting with ways to keep young plants alive during watering restrictions. The proposed system, she said, was not merely an architect's solution; it grew from knowledge acquired in this greenhouse.<br><br>Her story unsettled me at first. I had wanted the board to judge the design as professional work, not as an affectionate project by a former volunteer. But when questions began, the members asked about both the calculations and the plants. Because I understood where runoff collected and where seedlings dried fastest, I could connect each pipe on the plan to a specific need. The two kinds of knowledge strengthened rather than diminished each other.<br><br>After the meeting, Mrs. Okafor handed me the tray. Its seedlings were descendants of a variety I had helped cultivate. I had imagined returning in a new role would require leaving the earlier one outside the gate. Carrying the tray and my plans together, I understood that the greenhouse had not summoned me backward. It had shown me that the work I did now had roots I no longer needed to conceal."},
23: {skill_tested: "Determine the function of concrete contrasting details.", question: "The references in Passage A to Mara's tablet, the illuminated floor panels, and Mr. Vale's brass keys primarily serve to:", option1: "contrast institutional methods while preparing the question of what can persist through change.", option2: "establish the narrator's belief that the renovated theater is unsafe.", option3: "demonstrate that Mara intends to remove every object associated with Mr. Vale.", option4: "explain why the theater hired apprentices rather than experienced workers.", correctAnswer: "contrast institutional methods while preparing the question of what can persist through change.", explanation: "Step 1: The tablet and floor lights represent current systems, while the brass keys and counted paces represent earlier practices.<br><br>Step 2: The passage later distinguishes vanished routines from knowledge that can still be transmitted.<br><br>Step 3: The early contrast therefore establishes the change against which the narrator eventually identifies a less visible continuity.<br><br>Answer: contrast institutional methods while preparing the question of what can persist through change.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators returning to institutions where they once worked.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>The side entrance of the Bellwether Theater still resisted being opened. I leaned my shoulder against it, just as I had during six seasons of arriving before dawn to paint scenery. The door finally yielded, but the familiar smell of sawdust and hot cables had been replaced by the mineral scent of fresh plaster. In the lobby, volunteers were fastening silver letters to a wall that now read BELLWETHER ARTS CENTER.<br><br>I had come because Mara, the new technical director, had asked whether I could identify several unlabeled tools before the theater reopened. She met me carrying a tablet instead of the ring of brass keys that Mr. Vale, my old supervisor, used to wear. As we crossed the stage, floor panels lit beneath our feet to mark safe paths through the darkness. I tried not to stare. Mr. Vale had taught us to navigate by counting paces and feeling for seams with our shoes.<br><br>Backstage, most of the tools were ordinary. I named a canvas stretcher, two hand drills, and a curved blade used to trim painted cloth. Then Mara uncovered a wooden wheel with a deep groove along its rim. I explained how two such wheels had once guided ropes above the stage. She listened, photographed the wheel, and entered my explanation into the building's digital archive. I expected to feel useful. Instead, I felt like a label being attached to an exhibit.<br><br>A group of teenage apprentices entered carrying sections of a half-built doorway. One student set a corner down too quickly, twisting the frame. Before Mara could speak, I showed them how to brace the joint with a temporary diagonal strip. The students crowded close as I measured, tapped the frame square, and removed the strip. When they lifted the doorway again, it held. A girl with blue chalk across her sleeve asked why the brace worked, and my hands answered before my voice did.<br><br>On my way out, I passed the wall of silver letters. Through the open auditorium doors, I could hear the apprentices arguing cheerfully about paint. The old brass keys were gone, and no one would cross the stage by counting Mr. Vale's careful paces. Yet the doorway stood straight because a method had traveled from his hands to mine and, for at least one afternoon, from mine to theirs. The building had not kept my past intact. It had made room for it to continue without looking the same.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When I volunteered at the municipal greenhouse in college, visitors rarely saw me. I watered seedlings before opening, mixed soil behind the propagation shed, and left as school groups began rattling the gate. Ten years later, I returned as an architect assigned to design a rainwater system for the greenhouse's expansion. I arrived with rolled plans, polished shoes, and the confidence of someone who had rehearsed every answer.<br><br>Mrs. Okafor, who had supervised the volunteers, was waiting beside the old cistern. She greeted me warmly and then ignored my plans. Instead, she asked me to help move trays before the afternoon heat. Soon soil marked my sleeves, and my polished shoes had acquired a border of mud. I found myself checking the undersides of leaves for pests, an old habit so automatic that Mrs. Okafor laughed when she noticed.<br><br>At the planning meeting, I presented calculations showing that water captured from the new roofs could supply nearly half the greenhouse's summer needs. The board members studied the diagrams politely. Then Mrs. Okafor placed one of my seedling trays on the table. She explained that I had once spent an entire season experimenting with ways to keep young plants alive during watering restrictions. The proposed system, she said, was not merely an architect's solution; it grew from knowledge acquired in this greenhouse.<br><br>Her story unsettled me at first. I had wanted the board to judge the design as professional work, not as an affectionate project by a former volunteer. But when questions began, the members asked about both the calculations and the plants. Because I understood where runoff collected and where seedlings dried fastest, I could connect each pipe on the plan to a specific need. The two kinds of knowledge strengthened rather than diminished each other.<br><br>After the meeting, Mrs. Okafor handed me the tray. Its seedlings were descendants of a variety I had helped cultivate. I had imagined returning in a new role would require leaving the earlier one outside the gate. Carrying the tray and my plans together, I understood that the greenhouse had not summoned me backward. It had shown me that the work I did now had roots I no longer needed to conceal."},
24: {skill_tested: "Trace the relationship between an initial concern and later evidence.", question: "How does the planning meeting in Passage B affect the narrator's earlier desire to appear exclusively professional?", option1: "It confirms that the board distrusts knowledge gained through volunteer work.", option2: "It shows that practical experience and technical calculations can jointly support professional authority.", option3: "It causes the narrator to abandon the rainwater design in favor of Mrs. Okafor's proposal.", option4: "It persuades the narrator to conceal all previous connections to the greenhouse.", correctAnswer: "It shows that practical experience and technical calculations can jointly support professional authority.", explanation: "Step 1: The narrator initially fears that Mrs. Okafor's story will make the design seem sentimental rather than professional.<br><br>Step 2: During questions, knowledge of both plant needs and calculations allows the narrator to explain the design more convincingly.<br><br>Step 3: The meeting therefore revises the assumed conflict between personal experience and professional expertise.<br><br>Answer: It shows that practical experience and technical calculations can jointly support professional authority.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators returning to institutions where they once worked.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>The side entrance of the Bellwether Theater still resisted being opened. I leaned my shoulder against it, just as I had during six seasons of arriving before dawn to paint scenery. The door finally yielded, but the familiar smell of sawdust and hot cables had been replaced by the mineral scent of fresh plaster. In the lobby, volunteers were fastening silver letters to a wall that now read BELLWETHER ARTS CENTER.<br><br>I had come because Mara, the new technical director, had asked whether I could identify several unlabeled tools before the theater reopened. She met me carrying a tablet instead of the ring of brass keys that Mr. Vale, my old supervisor, used to wear. As we crossed the stage, floor panels lit beneath our feet to mark safe paths through the darkness. I tried not to stare. Mr. Vale had taught us to navigate by counting paces and feeling for seams with our shoes.<br><br>Backstage, most of the tools were ordinary. I named a canvas stretcher, two hand drills, and a curved blade used to trim painted cloth. Then Mara uncovered a wooden wheel with a deep groove along its rim. I explained how two such wheels had once guided ropes above the stage. She listened, photographed the wheel, and entered my explanation into the building's digital archive. I expected to feel useful. Instead, I felt like a label being attached to an exhibit.<br><br>A group of teenage apprentices entered carrying sections of a half-built doorway. One student set a corner down too quickly, twisting the frame. Before Mara could speak, I showed them how to brace the joint with a temporary diagonal strip. The students crowded close as I measured, tapped the frame square, and removed the strip. When they lifted the doorway again, it held. A girl with blue chalk across her sleeve asked why the brace worked, and my hands answered before my voice did.<br><br>On my way out, I passed the wall of silver letters. Through the open auditorium doors, I could hear the apprentices arguing cheerfully about paint. The old brass keys were gone, and no one would cross the stage by counting Mr. Vale's careful paces. Yet the doorway stood straight because a method had traveled from his hands to mine and, for at least one afternoon, from mine to theirs. The building had not kept my past intact. It had made room for it to continue without looking the same.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When I volunteered at the municipal greenhouse in college, visitors rarely saw me. I watered seedlings before opening, mixed soil behind the propagation shed, and left as school groups began rattling the gate. Ten years later, I returned as an architect assigned to design a rainwater system for the greenhouse's expansion. I arrived with rolled plans, polished shoes, and the confidence of someone who had rehearsed every answer.<br><br>Mrs. Okafor, who had supervised the volunteers, was waiting beside the old cistern. She greeted me warmly and then ignored my plans. Instead, she asked me to help move trays before the afternoon heat. Soon soil marked my sleeves, and my polished shoes had acquired a border of mud. I found myself checking the undersides of leaves for pests, an old habit so automatic that Mrs. Okafor laughed when she noticed.<br><br>At the planning meeting, I presented calculations showing that water captured from the new roofs could supply nearly half the greenhouse's summer needs. The board members studied the diagrams politely. Then Mrs. Okafor placed one of my seedling trays on the table. She explained that I had once spent an entire season experimenting with ways to keep young plants alive during watering restrictions. The proposed system, she said, was not merely an architect's solution; it grew from knowledge acquired in this greenhouse.<br><br>Her story unsettled me at first. I had wanted the board to judge the design as professional work, not as an affectionate project by a former volunteer. But when questions began, the members asked about both the calculations and the plants. Because I understood where runoff collected and where seedlings dried fastest, I could connect each pipe on the plan to a specific need. The two kinds of knowledge strengthened rather than diminished each other.<br><br>After the meeting, Mrs. Okafor handed me the tray. Its seedlings were descendants of a variety I had helped cultivate. I had imagined returning in a new role would require leaving the earlier one outside the gate. Carrying the tray and my plans together, I understood that the greenhouse had not summoned me backward. It had shown me that the work I did now had roots I no longer needed to conceal."},
25: {skill_tested: "Compare the narrators' initial responses to links with their former work.", question: "At first, both narrators respond to evidence connecting their past and present roles with:", option1: "pride that their former supervisors have preserved every working method.", option2: "certainty that the institutions value memory more than practical results.", option3: "concern that the connection will limit how their present contribution is understood.", option4: "relief that someone else will assume responsibility for their work.", correctAnswer: "concern that the connection will limit how their present contribution is understood.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A's narrator feels reduced to an archival label when treated as a source about an obsolete tool.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B's narrator worries that the board will see the design as an affectionate former volunteer's project rather than professional work.<br><br>Step 3: Both concerns arise because a past identity initially seems capable of narrowing recognition of present usefulness.<br><br>Answer: concern that the connection will limit how their present contribution is understood.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators returning to institutions where they once worked.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>The side entrance of the Bellwether Theater still resisted being opened. I leaned my shoulder against it, just as I had during six seasons of arriving before dawn to paint scenery. The door finally yielded, but the familiar smell of sawdust and hot cables had been replaced by the mineral scent of fresh plaster. In the lobby, volunteers were fastening silver letters to a wall that now read BELLWETHER ARTS CENTER.<br><br>I had come because Mara, the new technical director, had asked whether I could identify several unlabeled tools before the theater reopened. She met me carrying a tablet instead of the ring of brass keys that Mr. Vale, my old supervisor, used to wear. As we crossed the stage, floor panels lit beneath our feet to mark safe paths through the darkness. I tried not to stare. Mr. Vale had taught us to navigate by counting paces and feeling for seams with our shoes.<br><br>Backstage, most of the tools were ordinary. I named a canvas stretcher, two hand drills, and a curved blade used to trim painted cloth. Then Mara uncovered a wooden wheel with a deep groove along its rim. I explained how two such wheels had once guided ropes above the stage. She listened, photographed the wheel, and entered my explanation into the building's digital archive. I expected to feel useful. Instead, I felt like a label being attached to an exhibit.<br><br>A group of teenage apprentices entered carrying sections of a half-built doorway. One student set a corner down too quickly, twisting the frame. Before Mara could speak, I showed them how to brace the joint with a temporary diagonal strip. The students crowded close as I measured, tapped the frame square, and removed the strip. When they lifted the doorway again, it held. A girl with blue chalk across her sleeve asked why the brace worked, and my hands answered before my voice did.<br><br>On my way out, I passed the wall of silver letters. Through the open auditorium doors, I could hear the apprentices arguing cheerfully about paint. The old brass keys were gone, and no one would cross the stage by counting Mr. Vale's careful paces. Yet the doorway stood straight because a method had traveled from his hands to mine and, for at least one afternoon, from mine to theirs. The building had not kept my past intact. It had made room for it to continue without looking the same.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When I volunteered at the municipal greenhouse in college, visitors rarely saw me. I watered seedlings before opening, mixed soil behind the propagation shed, and left as school groups began rattling the gate. Ten years later, I returned as an architect assigned to design a rainwater system for the greenhouse's expansion. I arrived with rolled plans, polished shoes, and the confidence of someone who had rehearsed every answer.<br><br>Mrs. Okafor, who had supervised the volunteers, was waiting beside the old cistern. She greeted me warmly and then ignored my plans. Instead, she asked me to help move trays before the afternoon heat. Soon soil marked my sleeves, and my polished shoes had acquired a border of mud. I found myself checking the undersides of leaves for pests, an old habit so automatic that Mrs. Okafor laughed when she noticed.<br><br>At the planning meeting, I presented calculations showing that water captured from the new roofs could supply nearly half the greenhouse's summer needs. The board members studied the diagrams politely. Then Mrs. Okafor placed one of my seedling trays on the table. She explained that I had once spent an entire season experimenting with ways to keep young plants alive during watering restrictions. The proposed system, she said, was not merely an architect's solution; it grew from knowledge acquired in this greenhouse.<br><br>Her story unsettled me at first. I had wanted the board to judge the design as professional work, not as an affectionate project by a former volunteer. But when questions began, the members asked about both the calculations and the plants. Because I understood where runoff collected and where seedlings dried fastest, I could connect each pipe on the plan to a specific need. The two kinds of knowledge strengthened rather than diminished each other.<br><br>After the meeting, Mrs. Okafor handed me the tray. Its seedlings were descendants of a variety I had helped cultivate. I had imagined returning in a new role would require leaving the earlier one outside the gate. Carrying the tray and my plans together, I understood that the greenhouse had not summoned me backward. It had shown me that the work I did now had roots I no longer needed to conceal."},
26: {skill_tested: "Synthesize how parallel objects support different but related realizations.", question: "The straight doorway in Passage A and the seedling tray in Passage B are most similar in that each:", option1: "proves that the institution's newest procedures are less reliable than its former ones.", option2: "causes the narrator to seek formal recognition from the institution's leaders.", option3: "reminds the narrator of a task that was never completed successfully.", option4: "provides tangible evidence that earlier experience can contribute to present work.", correctAnswer: "provides tangible evidence that earlier experience can contribute to present work.", explanation: "Step 1: The doorway remains square because the narrator applies and passes on a method learned at the theater.<br><br>Step 2: The tray embodies the narrator's earlier cultivation work and accompanies the current architectural plans.<br><br>Step 3: Each object therefore turns an abstract connection between past and present into visible evidence of continuing usefulness.<br><br>Answer: provides tangible evidence that earlier experience can contribute to present work.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators returning to institutions where they once worked.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>The side entrance of the Bellwether Theater still resisted being opened. I leaned my shoulder against it, just as I had during six seasons of arriving before dawn to paint scenery. The door finally yielded, but the familiar smell of sawdust and hot cables had been replaced by the mineral scent of fresh plaster. In the lobby, volunteers were fastening silver letters to a wall that now read BELLWETHER ARTS CENTER.<br><br>I had come because Mara, the new technical director, had asked whether I could identify several unlabeled tools before the theater reopened. She met me carrying a tablet instead of the ring of brass keys that Mr. Vale, my old supervisor, used to wear. As we crossed the stage, floor panels lit beneath our feet to mark safe paths through the darkness. I tried not to stare. Mr. Vale had taught us to navigate by counting paces and feeling for seams with our shoes.<br><br>Backstage, most of the tools were ordinary. I named a canvas stretcher, two hand drills, and a curved blade used to trim painted cloth. Then Mara uncovered a wooden wheel with a deep groove along its rim. I explained how two such wheels had once guided ropes above the stage. She listened, photographed the wheel, and entered my explanation into the building's digital archive. I expected to feel useful. Instead, I felt like a label being attached to an exhibit.<br><br>A group of teenage apprentices entered carrying sections of a half-built doorway. One student set a corner down too quickly, twisting the frame. Before Mara could speak, I showed them how to brace the joint with a temporary diagonal strip. The students crowded close as I measured, tapped the frame square, and removed the strip. When they lifted the doorway again, it held. A girl with blue chalk across her sleeve asked why the brace worked, and my hands answered before my voice did.<br><br>On my way out, I passed the wall of silver letters. Through the open auditorium doors, I could hear the apprentices arguing cheerfully about paint. The old brass keys were gone, and no one would cross the stage by counting Mr. Vale's careful paces. Yet the doorway stood straight because a method had traveled from his hands to mine and, for at least one afternoon, from mine to theirs. The building had not kept my past intact. It had made room for it to continue without looking the same.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When I volunteered at the municipal greenhouse in college, visitors rarely saw me. I watered seedlings before opening, mixed soil behind the propagation shed, and left as school groups began rattling the gate. Ten years later, I returned as an architect assigned to design a rainwater system for the greenhouse's expansion. I arrived with rolled plans, polished shoes, and the confidence of someone who had rehearsed every answer.<br><br>Mrs. Okafor, who had supervised the volunteers, was waiting beside the old cistern. She greeted me warmly and then ignored my plans. Instead, she asked me to help move trays before the afternoon heat. Soon soil marked my sleeves, and my polished shoes had acquired a border of mud. I found myself checking the undersides of leaves for pests, an old habit so automatic that Mrs. Okafor laughed when she noticed.<br><br>At the planning meeting, I presented calculations showing that water captured from the new roofs could supply nearly half the greenhouse's summer needs. The board members studied the diagrams politely. Then Mrs. Okafor placed one of my seedling trays on the table. She explained that I had once spent an entire season experimenting with ways to keep young plants alive during watering restrictions. The proposed system, she said, was not merely an architect's solution; it grew from knowledge acquired in this greenhouse.<br><br>Her story unsettled me at first. I had wanted the board to judge the design as professional work, not as an affectionate project by a former volunteer. But when questions began, the members asked about both the calculations and the plants. Because I understood where runoff collected and where seedlings dried fastest, I could connect each pipe on the plan to a specific need. The two kinds of knowledge strengthened rather than diminished each other.<br><br>After the meeting, Mrs. Okafor handed me the tray. Its seedlings were descendants of a variety I had helped cultivate. I had imagined returning in a new role would require leaving the earlier one outside the gate. Carrying the tray and my plans together, I understood that the greenhouse had not summoned me backward. It had shown me that the work I did now had roots I no longer needed to conceal."},
27: {skill_tested: "Compare how each passage qualifies the idea of institutional continuity.", question: "Taken together, the endings of the passages most strongly support which conclusion about continuity?", option1: "Continuity can survive visible change when experience is adapted, transmitted, or incorporated into new work.", option2: "Continuity depends on preserving the physical setting in which knowledge was first acquired.", option3: "Continuity is meaningful only when an institution formally records every former worker's contribution.", option4: "Continuity prevents people from developing identities beyond their earliest responsibilities.", correctAnswer: "Continuity can survive visible change when experience is adapted, transmitted, or incorporated into new work.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A rejects the idea that continuity requires old keys, old routes, or an unchanged building; the learned method continues through new apprentices.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B rejects the idea that professional growth requires concealing volunteer experience; that experience becomes part of a new design.<br><br>Step 3: Both endings redefine continuity as transformation and use rather than exact preservation.<br><br>Answer: Continuity can survive visible change when experience is adapted, transmitted, or incorporated into new work.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators returning to institutions where they once worked.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>The side entrance of the Bellwether Theater still resisted being opened. I leaned my shoulder against it, just as I had during six seasons of arriving before dawn to paint scenery. The door finally yielded, but the familiar smell of sawdust and hot cables had been replaced by the mineral scent of fresh plaster. In the lobby, volunteers were fastening silver letters to a wall that now read BELLWETHER ARTS CENTER.<br><br>I had come because Mara, the new technical director, had asked whether I could identify several unlabeled tools before the theater reopened. She met me carrying a tablet instead of the ring of brass keys that Mr. Vale, my old supervisor, used to wear. As we crossed the stage, floor panels lit beneath our feet to mark safe paths through the darkness. I tried not to stare. Mr. Vale had taught us to navigate by counting paces and feeling for seams with our shoes.<br><br>Backstage, most of the tools were ordinary. I named a canvas stretcher, two hand drills, and a curved blade used to trim painted cloth. Then Mara uncovered a wooden wheel with a deep groove along its rim. I explained how two such wheels had once guided ropes above the stage. She listened, photographed the wheel, and entered my explanation into the building's digital archive. I expected to feel useful. Instead, I felt like a label being attached to an exhibit.<br><br>A group of teenage apprentices entered carrying sections of a half-built doorway. One student set a corner down too quickly, twisting the frame. Before Mara could speak, I showed them how to brace the joint with a temporary diagonal strip. The students crowded close as I measured, tapped the frame square, and removed the strip. When they lifted the doorway again, it held. A girl with blue chalk across her sleeve asked why the brace worked, and my hands answered before my voice did.<br><br>On my way out, I passed the wall of silver letters. Through the open auditorium doors, I could hear the apprentices arguing cheerfully about paint. The old brass keys were gone, and no one would cross the stage by counting Mr. Vale's careful paces. Yet the doorway stood straight because a method had traveled from his hands to mine and, for at least one afternoon, from mine to theirs. The building had not kept my past intact. It had made room for it to continue without looking the same.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When I volunteered at the municipal greenhouse in college, visitors rarely saw me. I watered seedlings before opening, mixed soil behind the propagation shed, and left as school groups began rattling the gate. Ten years later, I returned as an architect assigned to design a rainwater system for the greenhouse's expansion. I arrived with rolled plans, polished shoes, and the confidence of someone who had rehearsed every answer.<br><br>Mrs. Okafor, who had supervised the volunteers, was waiting beside the old cistern. She greeted me warmly and then ignored my plans. Instead, she asked me to help move trays before the afternoon heat. Soon soil marked my sleeves, and my polished shoes had acquired a border of mud. I found myself checking the undersides of leaves for pests, an old habit so automatic that Mrs. Okafor laughed when she noticed.<br><br>At the planning meeting, I presented calculations showing that water captured from the new roofs could supply nearly half the greenhouse's summer needs. The board members studied the diagrams politely. Then Mrs. Okafor placed one of my seedling trays on the table. She explained that I had once spent an entire season experimenting with ways to keep young plants alive during watering restrictions. The proposed system, she said, was not merely an architect's solution; it grew from knowledge acquired in this greenhouse.<br><br>Her story unsettled me at first. I had wanted the board to judge the design as professional work, not as an affectionate project by a former volunteer. But when questions began, the members asked about both the calculations and the plants. Because I understood where runoff collected and where seedlings dried fastest, I could connect each pipe on the plan to a specific need. The two kinds of knowledge strengthened rather than diminished each other.<br><br>After the meeting, Mrs. Okafor handed me the tray. Its seedlings were descendants of a variety I had helped cultivate. I had imagined returning in a new role would require leaving the earlier one outside the gate. Carrying the tray and my plans together, I understood that the greenhouse had not summoned me backward. It had shown me that the work I did now had roots I no longer needed to conceal."},
28: {skill_tested: "Identify an explicitly stated reason for a character's preference.", question: "According to Passage B, why did the narrator originally prefer working after visitors had left?", option1: "The telescope produced clearer images only after public hours.", option2: "The narrator was more comfortable with measurements than with crowds.", option3: "Arun prohibited volunteers from speaking with visitors.", option4: "The observatory recorded temperatures only when the dome was empty.", correctAnswer: "The narrator was more comfortable with measurements than with crowds.", explanation: "Step 1: The opening of Passage B contrasts orderly columns of measurements with crowds that interrupt and ask questions.<br><br>Step 2: This contrast directly explains why the narrator favors the quiet work performed after visitors leave.<br><br>Answer: The narrator was more comfortable with measurements than with crowds.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators whose usually private work unexpectedly becomes public.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>For four months I had cleaned a storm-darkened mural in the city museum one square inch at a time. Most mornings, before visitors arrived, I climbed the scaffold with cotton swabs, solvent, and a lamp angled close to the wall. Beneath a gray film, a harbor slowly returned: copper roofs, green water, and workers unloading baskets of silver fish. The work rewarded patience so quietly that I could leave each evening without anyone noticing what had changed.<br><br>On the day the scaffold came down, the museum director, Elise, asked me to speak at the mural's unveiling. I told her the painting should be the visible thing. Conservators, I said, were successful when no one could detect where their hands had been. Elise accepted my refusal, though the pause before she answered suggested that she considered invisibility an incomplete ambition.<br><br>During the unveiling, I stood behind the last row of folding chairs. A reporter praised the mural's newly brilliant colors and asked Elise whether modern cleaning chemicals had made the restoration simple. Elise could have offered the pleasing answer. Instead, she described the tests performed along the frame, the weeks spent distinguishing soot from original shadow, and the afternoon when I stopped the cleaning because a blue pigment had begun reacting unpredictably. She did not call me to the front, but she said my name.<br><br>Several people turned. Heat rose beneath my collar. Yet the attention did not feel like applause. It felt like the moment when I switched on my close lamp and made a previously hidden decision available for inspection. A conservator's restraint, I realized, could disappear as completely as careless work if no one explained it. I remained behind the chairs, but when the reporter approached afterward, I showed her the test photographs and told her why one strip of harbor sky remained slightly dim.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>My favorite hour at the Hillcrest Observatory began after the public left. I would cap the visitor telescope, enter its temperature readings, and help Arun compare the night's observations with older logs. I had joined the volunteer team because numbers behaved better than crowds. A column of measurements never interrupted, leaned too close, or asked whether a faint smudge was really a galaxy.<br><br>Then a comet appeared earlier in the season than predicted. The observatory announced an additional viewing night, and two hundred people climbed the hill. Arun placed me beside the main telescope with instructions to explain what visitors were seeing. I reminded him that I maintained equipment; I did not perform. He handed me a red flashlight and replied that knowing how the instrument behaved was precisely why I should speak.<br><br>My first explanations were mechanical. I named the lens, stated the comet's estimated distance, and warned everyone not to touch the focus knob. A boy at the eyepiece looked for several seconds and then announced that he saw only a fuzzy seed. The waiting adults laughed, but the comparison was accurate. I asked him what might happen if the seed had a tail made of dust. He described wind carrying it through darkness. Using his image, I explained how sunlight pushed material away from the comet.<br><br>After that, the line became less like an audience and more like a problem we were solving together. One visitor noticed that the comet seemed still while thin clouds moved. Another asked why the tail did not trail behind it like a bicycle ribbon. Their questions forced me to translate measurements I had understood only in the observatory's shorthand. Arun stayed near the steps, directing newcomers toward me but never answering in my place.<br><br>When the final visitor left, I expected relief. Instead, the silent dome seemed briefly unfinished. Recording the temperature still mattered, as did protecting the focus mechanism from impatient fingers. But the observations in our logs had acquired voices: a seed, a ribbon, moving clouds. I had not surrendered the private precision I valued. I had discovered that explaining it could reveal portions of the work that even I had not fully seen."},
29: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of a descriptive term.", question: "As used in the third paragraph of Passage B, the word 'mechanical' most nearly indicates that the narrator's first explanations were:", option1: "delivered through a recording rather than in person.", option2: "spoken so quietly that visitors could not hear them.", option3: "limited to technical facts and instructions.", option4: "designed to persuade visitors to operate the telescope themselves.", correctAnswer: "limited to technical facts and instructions.", explanation: "Step 1: The narrator initially names a lens, states a distance, and gives a warning about the focus knob.<br><br>Step 2: These statements communicate technical information without engaging the visitors' understanding, so 'mechanical' means limited to facts and instructions.<br><br>Answer: limited to technical facts and instructions.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators whose usually private work unexpectedly becomes public.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>For four months I had cleaned a storm-darkened mural in the city museum one square inch at a time. Most mornings, before visitors arrived, I climbed the scaffold with cotton swabs, solvent, and a lamp angled close to the wall. Beneath a gray film, a harbor slowly returned: copper roofs, green water, and workers unloading baskets of silver fish. The work rewarded patience so quietly that I could leave each evening without anyone noticing what had changed.<br><br>On the day the scaffold came down, the museum director, Elise, asked me to speak at the mural's unveiling. I told her the painting should be the visible thing. Conservators, I said, were successful when no one could detect where their hands had been. Elise accepted my refusal, though the pause before she answered suggested that she considered invisibility an incomplete ambition.<br><br>During the unveiling, I stood behind the last row of folding chairs. A reporter praised the mural's newly brilliant colors and asked Elise whether modern cleaning chemicals had made the restoration simple. Elise could have offered the pleasing answer. Instead, she described the tests performed along the frame, the weeks spent distinguishing soot from original shadow, and the afternoon when I stopped the cleaning because a blue pigment had begun reacting unpredictably. She did not call me to the front, but she said my name.<br><br>Several people turned. Heat rose beneath my collar. Yet the attention did not feel like applause. It felt like the moment when I switched on my close lamp and made a previously hidden decision available for inspection. A conservator's restraint, I realized, could disappear as completely as careless work if no one explained it. I remained behind the chairs, but when the reporter approached afterward, I showed her the test photographs and told her why one strip of harbor sky remained slightly dim.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>My favorite hour at the Hillcrest Observatory began after the public left. I would cap the visitor telescope, enter its temperature readings, and help Arun compare the night's observations with older logs. I had joined the volunteer team because numbers behaved better than crowds. A column of measurements never interrupted, leaned too close, or asked whether a faint smudge was really a galaxy.<br><br>Then a comet appeared earlier in the season than predicted. The observatory announced an additional viewing night, and two hundred people climbed the hill. Arun placed me beside the main telescope with instructions to explain what visitors were seeing. I reminded him that I maintained equipment; I did not perform. He handed me a red flashlight and replied that knowing how the instrument behaved was precisely why I should speak.<br><br>My first explanations were mechanical. I named the lens, stated the comet's estimated distance, and warned everyone not to touch the focus knob. A boy at the eyepiece looked for several seconds and then announced that he saw only a fuzzy seed. The waiting adults laughed, but the comparison was accurate. I asked him what might happen if the seed had a tail made of dust. He described wind carrying it through darkness. Using his image, I explained how sunlight pushed material away from the comet.<br><br>After that, the line became less like an audience and more like a problem we were solving together. One visitor noticed that the comet seemed still while thin clouds moved. Another asked why the tail did not trail behind it like a bicycle ribbon. Their questions forced me to translate measurements I had understood only in the observatory's shorthand. Arun stayed near the steps, directing newcomers toward me but never answering in my place.<br><br>When the final visitor left, I expected relief. Instead, the silent dome seemed briefly unfinished. Recording the temperature still mattered, as did protecting the focus mechanism from impatient fingers. But the observations in our logs had acquired voices: a seed, a ribbon, moving clouds. I had not surrendered the private precision I valued. I had discovered that explaining it could reveal portions of the work that even I had not fully seen."},
30: {skill_tested: "Identify a central idea developed across paired passages.", question: "Which statement best expresses a central idea shared by both passages?", option1: "Technical work loses precision when it is explained to people without specialized training.", option2: "People who avoid public attention are usually uncertain about the quality of their work.", option3: "Public institutions should require every technical worker to deliver formal presentations.", option4: "Making hidden work understandable can reveal its value without replacing the work itself.", correctAnswer: "Making hidden work understandable can reveal its value without replacing the work itself.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A shows that explaining restoration decisions makes otherwise invisible restraint understandable.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B shows that explaining observations gives technical records new meaning while preserving their precision.<br><br>Answer: Making hidden work understandable can reveal its value without replacing the work itself.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators whose usually private work unexpectedly becomes public.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>For four months I had cleaned a storm-darkened mural in the city museum one square inch at a time. Most mornings, before visitors arrived, I climbed the scaffold with cotton swabs, solvent, and a lamp angled close to the wall. Beneath a gray film, a harbor slowly returned: copper roofs, green water, and workers unloading baskets of silver fish. The work rewarded patience so quietly that I could leave each evening without anyone noticing what had changed.<br><br>On the day the scaffold came down, the museum director, Elise, asked me to speak at the mural's unveiling. I told her the painting should be the visible thing. Conservators, I said, were successful when no one could detect where the