vehicle may record engine heat. Residents who volunteer equipment may be concentrated in neighborhoods already active in environmental programs, leaving other areas sparsely measured. Even a correctly placed sensor describes its immediate surroundings, not every apartment on the block. Researchers compare instruments, document placement, repeat routes, and report gaps so that a detailed map does not imply certainty it has not earned.<br><br>The maps become most useful when combined with information about people and buildings. A hot block with air-conditioned offices may require a different response from a slightly cooler block where many residents lack reliable cooling. Tree planting may help one street, while shaded transit stops, reflective roofs, adjusted work hours, or accessible cooling centers may address another form of exposure sooner. Temperature data identify physical patterns; they do not select a policy by themselves.<br><br>This is why neighborhood measurement supplements rather than replaces the official station. The station provides a stable regional record used for forecasts and long-term comparison. Distributed sensors reveal variation inside that regional condition and help explain why one citywide warning can have unequal consequences. The apparent conflict between one official number and many local numbers disappears once their scales are understood.<br><br>The volunteer's rising and falling readings were therefore neither a correction of the official temperature nor a collection of isolated curiosities. Repeated carefully, such walks can show where heat is stored, where it is released, and where people receive little relief. The broader lesson is methodological: before treating a measurement as incomplete, researchers must ask which scale it was designed to represent—and before treating a detailed map as complete, they must ask what it still fails to observe."},
68: {skill_tested: "Infer why similar daytime peaks can produce different risks.", question: "Two neighborhoods with the same afternoon maximum may have different heat risks primarily because:", option1: "only one neighborhood is included in the official forecast.", option2: "stored heat and limited vegetation may prevent one neighborhood from cooling overnight.", option3: "daytime sensors cannot record temperatures near masonry.", option4: "the cooler neighborhood necessarily has more air-conditioned offices.", correctAnswer: "stored heat and limited vegetation may prevent one neighborhood from cooling overnight.", explanation: "Step 1: Masonry can release stored heat slowly after sunset, especially where vegetation is limited.<br><br>Step 2: Health risk depends on duration and opportunity for recovery, not only the highest temperature.<br><br>Step 3: Equal peaks can therefore conceal unequal overnight exposure.<br><br>Answer: stored heat and limited vegetation may prevent one neighborhood from cooling overnight.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how researchers measure heat across urban neighborhoods.<br><br>During an evening survey, a volunteer carrying a temperature sensor turned from a tree-lined street into a broad commercial parking lot. The reading climbed before she had crossed half the pavement. Several blocks later, beside an irrigated playing field, it fell again. The official weather station for the city reported one temperature throughout this walk. That station was not malfunctioning. It was answering a different question.<br><br>Weather stations are deliberately standardized. Their instruments are shielded from direct sunlight, positioned above suitable ground, and kept away from nearby surfaces that could distort regional measurements. This consistency makes observations from different days and cities comparable. But a station designed 