s to observe."},
71: {skill_tested: "Synthesize safeguards needed to support a local heat conclusion.", question: "Which study design would best support a claim that one neighborhood consistently retains more nighttime heat than another?", option1: "Repeat matched nighttime routes, compare calibrated sensors and placements, and document differences in surfaces, vegetation, and airflow.", option2: "Compare one sensor from each neighborhood on a single afternoon.", option3: "Use the citywide station and assign its reading to every block in both neighborhoods.", option4: "Survey only homes whose residents already participate in environmental programs.", correctAnswer: "Repeat matched nighttime routes, compare calibrated sensors and placements, and document differences in surfaces, vegetation, and airflow.", explanation: "Step 1: Repetition at matched times reduces the influence of temporary events and changing daily temperature gaps.<br><br>Step 2: Calibration and documented placement address instrument distortion and false precision.<br><br>Step 3: Recording interacting physical features helps explain a persistent difference rather than merely mapping it.<br><br>Answer: Repeat matched nighttime routes, compare calibrated sensors and placements, and document differences in surfaces, vegetation, and airflow.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how researchers measure heat across urban neighborhoods.<br><br>During an evening survey, a volunteer carrying a temperature sensor turned from a tree-lined street into a broad commercial parking lot. The reading climbed before she had crossed half the pavement. Several blocks later, beside an irrigated playing field, it fell again. The official weather station for the city reported one temperature throughout this walk. That station was not malfunctioning. It was answering a different question.<br><br>Weather stations are deliberately standardized. Their instruments are shielded from direct sunlight, positioned above suitable ground, and kept away from nearby surfaces that could distort regional measurements. This consistency makes observations from different days and cities comparable. But a station designed to represent general conditions cannot describe every place where residents encounter heat. A person waiting beside a dark wall, walking beneath mature trees, or sleeping in an upper apartment experiences conditions shaped by a much smaller area.<br><br>Researchers seeking that local variation use mobile sensors on cars, bicycles, or backpacks, as well as fixed sensors distributed among neighborhoods. Routes are repeated at similar times because the temperature gap between locations changes during the day. Sunlit pavement may be hottest in late afternoon, while brick and concrete can continue releasing stored heat after sunset. A route measured only once might capture a passing cloud, traffic delay, or sprinkler rather than a durable pattern.<br><br>Surface materials explain part of the variation. Dark roofs and pavement absorb solar energy; plants shade surfaces and release water vapor; closely spaced buildings can restrict air movement. Yet the effect of any feature depends on its surroundings. A small tree may shade one bench without changing the temperature of a wide intersection. A park can cool adjacent blocks under some wind conditions but have little influence farther away. Researchers therefore examine combinations of cover, building form, moisture, and airflow rather than assigning one fixed cooling value to every tree or lawn.<br><br>Distributed measurements have often revealed that nighttime differences deserve as much attention as daytime peaks. Neighborhoods with abundant masonry and little vegetation may release stored heat slowly, giving buildings and bodies less opportunity to cool. Two areas that reach similar afternoon temperatures can therefore present different overnight risks. This distinction matters because health depends not only on the highest reading but also on the duration of exposure and the possibility of recovery.<br><br>Local data, however, can create an illusion of precision. A sensor attached too close to a vehicle may record engine heat. Residents who volunteer equipment may be concentrated in neighborhoods already active in environmental programs, leaving other areas sparsely measured. Even a correctly placed sensor describes its immediate surroundings, not every apartment on the block. Researchers compare instruments, document placement, repeat routes, and report gaps so that a detailed map does not imply certainty it has not earned.<br><br>The maps become most useful when combined with information about people and buildings. A hot block with air-conditioned offices may require a different response from a slightly cooler block where many residents lack reliable cooling. Tree planting may help one street, while shaded transit stops, reflective roofs, adjusted work hours, or accessible cooling centers may address another form of exposure sooner. Temperature data identify physical patterns; they do not select a policy by themselves.<br><br>This is why neighborhood measurement supplements rather than replaces the official station. The station provides a stable regional record used for forecasts and long-term comparison. Distributed sensors reveal variation inside that regional condition and help explain why one citywide warning can have unequal consequences. The apparent conflict between one official number and many local numbers disappears once their scales are understood.<br><br>The volunteer's rising and falling readings were therefore neither a correction of the official temperature nor a collection of isolated curiosities. Repeated carefully, such walks can show where heat is stored, where it is released, and where people receive little relief. The broader lesson is methodological: before treating a measurement as incomplete, researchers must ask which scale it was designed to represent—and before treating a detailed map as complete, they must ask what it still fails to observe."},
72: {skill_tested: "Reconcile seemingly conflicting measurements by identifying their scales.", question: "A neighborhood sensor reads several degrees above the official station for three evenings. Based on the passage, the most justified interpretation is that:", option1: "the official station should be discarded because regional records are useless.", option2: "the readings may both be valid, with one representing standardized regional conditions and the other a repeatable local environment.", option3: "the neighborhood sensor proves that every home nearby has the same indoor temperature.", option4: "the neighborhood difference must be caused entirely by dark pavement.", correctAnswer: "the readings may both be valid, with one representing standardized regional conditions and the other a repeatable local environment.", explanation: "Step 1: Official stations are standardized for regional comparison and intentionally avoid nearby distortions.<br><br>Step 2: Repeated local readings can reveal conditions created by surfaces, buildings, vegetation, and airflow.<br><br>Step 3: Neither measurement establishes every indoor condition or a single cause, but their difference is compatible once each spatial scale is identified.<br><br>Answer: the readings may both be valid, with one representing standardized regional conditions and the other a repeatable local environment.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how researchers measure heat across urban neighborhoods.<br><br>During an evening survey, a volunteer carrying a temperature sensor turned from a tree-lined street into a broad commercial parking lot. The reading climbed before she had crossed half the pavement. Several blocks later, beside an irrigated playing field, it fell again. The official weather station for the city reported one temperature throughout this walk. That station was not malfunctioning. It was answering a different question.<br><br>Weather stations are deliberately standardized. Their instruments are shielded from direct sunlight, positioned above suitable ground, and kept away from nearby surfaces that could distort regional measurements. This consistency makes observations from different days and cities comparable. But a station designed to represent general conditions cannot describe every place where residents encounter heat. A person waiting beside a dark wall, walking beneath mature trees, or sleeping in an upper apartment experiences conditions shaped by a much smaller area.<br><br>Researchers seeking that local variation use mobile sensors on cars, bicycles, or backpacks, as well as fixed sensors distributed among neighborhoods. Routes are repeated at similar times because the temperature gap between locations changes during the day. Sunlit pavement may be hottest in late afternoon, while brick and concrete can continue releasing stored heat after sunset. A route measured only once might capture a passing cloud, traffic delay, or sprinkler rather than a durable pattern.<br><br>Surface materials explain part of the variation. Dark roofs and pavement absorb solar energy; plants shade surfaces and release water vapor; closely spaced buildings can restrict air movement. Yet the effect of any feature depends on its surroundings. A small tree may shade one bench without changing the temperature of a wide intersection. A park can cool adjacent blocks under some wind conditions but have little influence farther away. Researchers therefore examine combinations of cover, building form, moisture, and airflow rather than assigning one fixed cooling value to every tree or lawn.<br><br>Distributed measurements have often revealed that nighttime differences deserve as much attention as daytime peaks. Neighborhoods with abundant masonry and little vegetation may release stored heat slowly, giving buildings and bodies less opportunity to cool. Two areas that reach similar afternoon temperatures can therefore present different overnight risks. This distinction matters because health depends not only on the highest reading but also on the duration of exposure and the possibility of recovery.<br><br>Local data, however, can create an illusion of precision. A sensor attached too close to a vehicle may record engine heat. Residents who volunteer equipment may be concentrated in neighborhoods already active in environmental programs, leaving other areas sparsely measured. Even a correctly placed sensor describes its immediate surroundings, not every apartment on the block. Researchers compare instruments, document placement, repeat routes, and report gaps so that a detailed map does not imply certainty it has not earned.<br><br>The maps become most useful when combined with information about people and buildings. A hot block with air-conditioned offices may require a different response from a slightly cooler block where many residents lack reliable cooling. Tree planting may help one street, while shaded transit stops, reflective roofs, adjusted work hours, or accessible cooling centers may address another form of exposure sooner. Temperature data identify physical patterns; they do not select a policy by themselves.<br><br>This is why neighborhood measurement supplements rather than replaces the official station. The station provides a stable regional record used for forecasts and long-term comparison. Distributed sensors reveal variation inside that regional condition and help explain why one citywide warning can have unequal consequences. The apparent conflict between one official number and many local numbers disappears once their scales are understood.<br><br>The volunteer's rising and falling readings were therefore neither a correction of the official temperature nor a collection of isolated curiosities. Repeated carefully, such walks can show where heat is stored, where it is released, and where people receive little relief. The broader lesson is methodological: before treating a measurement as incomplete, researchers must ask which scale it was designed to represent—and before treating a detailed map as complete, they must ask what it still fails to observe."},
73: {skill_tested: "Identify a stated sorting decision.", question: "Why does Mrs. Alvarez place the mitten beside the notebook?", option1: "Both are beyond repair.", option2: "The notebook describes how to wash the mitten.", option3: "They may have come from the same household.", option4: "Their colors match.", correctAnswer: "They may have come from the same household.", explanation: "Step 1: The matching surname suggests a shared household, so proximity may help an owner recognize both.<br><br>Answer: They may have come from the same household.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how ordinary objects acquire meanings beyond their practical uses.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>After the river withdrew, the community center became a temporary home for objects without owners. Volunteers carried in plastic bins filled with whatever the water had scattered: a dented saucepan, single shoes, framed photographs clouded beneath their glass. I was assigned to arrange the tables. Believing efficiency required certainty, I made signs for kitchenware, clothing, documents, and items beyond repair.<br><br>Mrs. Alvarez, who coordinated the room, moved slowly behind me. She placed a child's red mitten beside a water-stained recipe notebook rather than with the other clothes. When I pointed out the mistake, she showed me a name written inside the mitten and the same surname on the notebook. The objects, she explained, might have left one house together. Keeping them close could help someone recognize both.<br><br>My categories began to seem too confident. A brass key could be hardware, but it could also identify a house whose door was gone. A chipped blue cup belonged among dishes until a man recognized it as the pencil holder from his wife's desk. He did not take it immediately. He stood beside the table, telling us how she sharpened pencils over the sink because she disliked crumbs of graphite on paper.<br><br>By evening, the room looked less orderly but more legible. We grouped objects by uncertain connections, leaving room between clusters for later discoveries. People supplied stories that no label could have predicted. I had thought our task was to return property. Mrs. Alvarez understood that before an object could be returned, it sometimes had to return a memory.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The paper map in my coat pocket was already obsolete when I bought it. The transit authority had released an app, and station screens announced delays more accurately than printed lines could. Still, I liked unfolding the map across my knees. Its colored routes made the city appear graspable, even when a train stopped between stations and the lights flickered.<br><br>Each crease became associated with a different trip. One corner tore during a rainstorm when I used the map as a roof for a bag of library books. A coffee ring marked the afternoon my cousin and I planned three destinations but reached only one. I wrote no notes on the paper, yet wear supplied a record more personal than any saved itinerary.<br><br>On a crowded train, a boy beside me asked whether the blue line reached the aquarium. His father was trying to enlarge a route diagram on a phone with a cracked screen. I handed them the map and traced a transfer with my finger. The boy held the sheet carefully, as if it were an official document rather than something softened by months in my pocket.<br><br>They left at the next stop, carrying the map by accident. I noticed only when the doors closed. For a moment I was irritated, then I pictured the boy opening it beneath the aquarium sign. The map had ceased recording my journeys and entered someone else's. Its usefulness, which I had treated as a private kind of memory, had become portable again.<br><br>The next morning I bought another copy from the same kiosk. It was crisp, accurate, and entirely blank in the way that mattered. I folded it along the printed lines and put it in my pocket, knowing that use would gradually replace the city's perfect geometry with a smaller history of weather, waiting, and chance exchanges.<br><br>A week later, I saw a family studying a station diagram and almost offered the new map. My hand reached toward my pocket before I remembered that generosity did not guarantee a return. I offered it anyway, but waited while they copied the route. The paper came back with one new crease, folded by a stranger along a line I would not have chosen. I left the crease in place. It was evidence that the map could gather a shared history without remaining with every person who used it."},
74: {skill_tested: "Determine contextual meaning.", question: "In Passage A, the word legible most nearly means:", option1: "written in large letters.", option2: "safe from further water damage.", option3: "arranged by monetary value.", option4: "capable of being understood.", correctAnswer: "capable of being understood.", explanation: "Step 1: The room becomes less neat but its uncertain relationships become more understandable.<br><br>Answer: capable of being understood.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how ordinary objects acquire meanings beyond their practical uses.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>After the river withdrew, the community center became a temporary home for objects without owners. Volunteers carried in plastic bins filled with whatever the water had scattered: a dented saucepan, single shoes, framed photographs clouded beneath their glass. I was assigned to arrange the tables. Believing efficiency required certainty, I made signs for kitchenware, clothing, documents, and items beyond repair.<br><br>Mrs. Alvarez, who coordinated the room, moved slowly behind me. She placed a child's red mitten beside a water-stained recipe notebook rather than with the other clothes. When I pointed out the mistake, she showed me a name written inside the mitten and the same surname on the notebook. The objects, she explained, might have left one house together. Keeping them close could help someone recognize both.<br><br>My categories began to seem too confident. A brass key could be hardware, but it could also identify a house whose door was gone. A chipped blue cup belonged among dishes until a man recognized it as the pencil holder from his wife's desk. He did not take it immediately. He stood beside the table, telling us how she sharpened pencils over the sink because she disliked crumbs of graphite on paper.<br><br>By evening, the room looked less orderly but more legible. We grouped objects by uncertain connections, leaving room between clusters for later discoveries. People supplied stories that no label could have predicted. I had thought our task was to return property. Mrs. Alvarez understood that before an object could be returned, it sometimes had to return a memory.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The paper map in my coat pocket was already obsolete when I bought it. The transit authority had released an app, and station screens announced delays more accurately than printed lines could. Still, I liked unfolding the map across my knees. Its colored routes made the city appear graspable, even when a train stopped between stations and the lights flickered.<br><br>Each crease became associated with a different trip. One corner tore during a rainstorm when I used the map as a roof for a bag of library books. A coffee ring marked the afternoon my cousin and I planned three destinations but reached only one. I wrote no notes on the paper, yet wear supplied a record more personal than any saved itinerary.<br><br>On a crowded train, a boy beside me asked whether the blue line reached the aquarium. His father was trying to enlarge a route diagram on a phone with a cracked screen. I handed them the map and traced a transfer with my finger. The boy held the sheet carefully, as if it were an official document rather than something softened by months in my pocket.<br><br>They left at the next stop, carrying the map by accident. I noticed only when the doors closed. For a moment I was irritated, then I pictured the boy opening it beneath the aquarium sign. The map had ceased recording my journeys and entered someone else's. Its usefulness, which I had treated as a private kind of memory, had become portable again.<br><br>The next morning I bought another copy from the same kiosk. It was crisp, accurate, and entirely blank in the way that mattered. I folded it along the printed lines and put it in my pocket, knowing that use would gradually replace the city's perfect geometry with a smaller history of weather, waiting, and chance exchanges.<br><br>A week later, I saw a family studying a station diagram and almost offered the new map. My hand reached toward my pocket before I remembered that generosity did not guarantee a return. I offered it anyway, but waited while they copied the route. The paper came back with one new crease, folded by a stranger along a line I would not have chosen. I left the crease in place. It was evidence that the map could gather a shared history without remaining with every person who used it."},
75: {skill_tested: "Identify a shared idea.", question: "Which idea is shared by both passages?", option1: "Use can give ordinary objects meanings that fixed categories do not capture.", option2: "Damaged objects should always be replaced.", option3: "Digital records preserve memory better than physical objects.", option4: "Ownership can be established only through written names.", correctAnswer: "Use can give ordinary objects meanings that fixed categories do not capture.", explanation: "Step 1: The recovered belongings and worn map gain meaning through connections and experience.<br><br>Answer: Use can give ordinary objects meanings that fixed categories do not capture.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how ordinary objects acquire meanings beyond their practical uses.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>After the river withdrew, the community center became a temporary home for objects without owners. Volunteers carried in plastic bins filled with whatever the water had scattered: a dented saucepan, single shoes, framed photographs clouded beneath their glass. I was assigned to arrange the tables. Believing efficiency required certainty, I made signs for kitchenware, clothing, documents, and items beyond repair.<br><br>Mrs. Alvarez, who coordinated the room, moved slowly behind me. She placed a child's red mitten beside a water-stained recipe notebook rather than with the other clothes. When I pointed out the mistake, she showed me a name written inside the mitten and the same surname on the notebook. The objects, she explained, might have left one house together. Keeping them close could help someone recognize both.<br><br>My categories began to seem too confident. A brass key could be hardware, but it could also identify a house whose door was gone. A chipped blue cup belonged among dishes until a man recognized it as the pencil holder from his wife's desk. He did not take it immediately. He stood beside the table, telling us how she sharpened pencils over the sink because she disliked crumbs of graphite on paper.<br><br>By evening, the room looked less orderly but more legible. We grouped objects by uncertain connections, leaving room between clusters for later discoveries. People supplied stories that no label could have predicted. I had thought our task was to return property. Mrs. Alvarez understood that before an object could be returned, it sometimes had to return a memory.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The paper map in my coat pocket was already obsolete when I bought it. The transit authority had released an app, and station screens announced delays more accurately than printed lines could. Still, I liked unfolding the map across my knees. Its colored routes made the city appear graspable, even when a train stopped between stations and the lights flickered.<br><br>Each crease became associated with a different trip. One corner tore during a rainstorm when I used the map as a roof for a bag of library books. A coffee ring marked the afternoon my cousin and I planned three destinations but reached only one. I wrote no notes on the paper, yet wear supplied a record more personal than any saved itinerary.<br><br>On a crowded train, a boy beside me asked whether the blue line reached the aquarium. His father was trying to enlarge a route diagram on a phone with a cracked screen. I handed them the map and traced a transfer with my finger. The boy held the sheet carefully, as if it were an official document rather than something softened by months in my pocket.<br><br>They left at the next stop, carrying the map by accident. I noticed only when the doors closed. For a moment I was irritated, then I pictured the boy opening it beneath the aquarium sign. The map had ceased recording my journeys and entered someone else's. Its usefulness, which I had treated as a private kind of memory, had become portable again.<br><br>The next morning I bought another copy from the same kiosk. It was crisp, accurate, and entirely blank in the way that mattered. I folded it along the printed lines and put it in my pocket, knowing that use would gradually replace the city's perfect geometry with a smaller history of weather, waiting, and chance exchanges.<br><br>A week later, I saw a family studying a station diagram and almost offered the new map. My hand reached toward my pocket before I remembered that generosity did not guarantee a return. I offered it anyway, but waited while they copied the route. The paper came back with one new crease, folded by a stranger along a line I would not have chosen. I left the crease in place. It was evidence that the map could gather a shared history without remaining with every person who used it."},
76: {skill_tested: "Analyze a character shift.", question: "The blue cup causes the narrator of Passage A to recognize that:", option1: "all damaged dishes should be discarded.", option2: "an object's significance may depend on a story supplied by its owner.", option3: "Mrs. Alvarez knows every displaced resident.", option4: "volunteers should avoid speaking with visitors.", correctAnswer: "an object's significance may depend on a story supplied by its owner.", explanation: "Step 1: The cup's category changes when the man explains its connection to his wife.<br><br>Answer: an object's significance may depend on a story supplied by its owner.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how ordinary objects acquire meanings beyond their practical uses.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>After the river withdrew, the community center became a temporary home for objects without owners. Volunteers carried in plastic bins filled with whatever the water had scattered: a dented saucepan, single shoes, framed photographs clouded beneath their glass. I was assigned to arrange the tables. Believing efficiency required certainty, I made signs for kitchenware, clothing, documents, and items beyond repair.<br><br>Mrs. Alvarez, who coordinated the room, moved slowly behind me. She placed a child's red mitten beside a water-stained recipe notebook rather than with the other clothes. When I pointed out the mistake, she showed me a name written inside the mitten and the same surname on the notebook. The objects, she explained, might have left one house together. Keeping them close could help someone recognize both.<br><br>My categories began to seem too confident. A brass key could be hardware, but it could also identify a house whose door was gone. A chipped blue cup belonged among dishes until a man recognized it as the pencil holder from his wife's desk. He did not take it immediately. He stood beside the table, telling us how she sharpened pencils over the sink because she disliked crumbs of graphite on paper.<br><br>By evening, the room looked less orderly but more legible. We grouped objects by uncertain connections, leaving room between clusters for later discoveries. People supplied stories that no label could have predicted. I had thought our task was to return property. Mrs. Alvarez understood that before an object could be returned, it sometimes had to return a memory.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The paper map in my coat pocket was already obsolete when I bought it. The transit authority had released an app, and station screens announced delays more accurately than printed lines could. Still, I liked unfolding the map across my knees. Its colored routes made the city appear graspable, even when a train stopped between stations and the lights flickered.<br><br>Each crease became associated with a different trip. One corner tore during a rainstorm when I used the map as a roof for a bag of library books. A coffee ring marked the afternoon my cousin and I planned three destinations but reached only one. I wrote no notes on the paper, yet wear supplied a record more personal than any saved itinerary.<br><br>On a crowded train, a boy beside me asked whether the blue line reached the aquarium. His father was trying to enlarge a route diagram on a phone with a cracked screen. I handed them the map and traced a transfer with my finger. The boy held the sheet carefully, as if it were an official document rather than something softened by months in my pocket.<br><br>They left at the next stop, carrying the map by accident. I noticed only when the doors closed. For a moment I was irritated, then I pictured the boy opening it beneath the aquarium sign. The map had ceased recording my journeys and entered someone else's. Its usefulness, which I had treated as a private kind of memory, had become portable again.<br><br>The next morning I bought another copy from the same kiosk. It was crisp, accurate, and entirely blank in the way that mattered. I folded it along the printed lines and put it in my pocket, knowing that use would gradually replace the city's perfect geometry with a smaller history of weather, waiting, and chance exchanges.<br><br>A week later, I saw a family studying a station diagram and almost offered the new map. My hand reached toward my pocket before I remembered that generosity did not guarantee a return. I offered it anyway, but waited while they copied the route. The paper came back with one new crease, folded by a stranger along a line I would not have chosen. I left the crease in place. It was evidence that the map could gather a shared history without remaining with every person who used it."},
77: {skill_tested: "Analyze structural function.", question: "The map's coffee ring and torn corner primarily serve to:", option1: "prove the map is no longer accurate.", option2: "explain why the transit authority created an app.", option3: "show how physical wear records particular experiences.", option4: "show that the narrator is careless with every possession.", correctAnswer: "show how physical wear records particular experiences.", explanation: "Step 1: Each mark is tied to a remembered trip, turning wear into a personal record.<br><br>Answer: show how physical wear records particular experiences.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how ordinary objects acquire meanings beyond their practical uses.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>After the river withdrew, the community center became a temporary home for objects without owners. Volunteers carried in plastic bins filled with whatever the water had scattered: a dented saucepan, single shoes, framed photographs clouded beneath their glass. I was assigned to arrange the tables. Believing efficiency required certainty, I made signs for kitchenware, clothing, documents, and items beyond repair.<br><br>Mrs. Alvarez, who coordinated the room, moved slowly behind me. She placed a child's red mitten beside a water-stained recipe notebook rather than with the other clothes. When I pointed out the mistake, she showed me a name written inside the mitten and the same surname on the notebook. The objects, she explained, might have left one house together. Keeping them close could help someone recognize both.<br><br>My categories began to seem too confident. A brass key could be hardware, but it could also identify a house whose door was gone. A chipped blue cup belonged among dishes until a man recognized it as the pencil holder from his wife's desk. He did not take it immediately. He stood beside the table, telling us how she sharpened pencils over the sink because she disliked crumbs of graphite on paper.<br><br>By evening, the room looked less orderly but more legible. We grouped objects by uncertain connections, leaving room between clusters for later discoveries. People supplied stories that no label could have predicted. I had thought our task was to return property. Mrs. Alvarez understood that before an object could be returned, it sometimes had to return a memory.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The paper map in my coat pocket was already obsolete when I bought it. The transit authority had released an app, and station screens announced delays more accurately than printed lines could. Still, I liked unfolding the map across my knees. Its colored routes made the city appear graspable, even when a train stopped between stations and the lights flickered.<br><br>Each crease became associated with a different trip. One corner tore during a rainstorm when I used the map as a roof for a bag of library books. A coffee ring marked the afternoon my cousin and I planned three destinations but reached only one. I wrote no notes on the paper, yet wear supplied a record more personal than any saved itinerary.<br><br>On a crowded train, a boy beside me asked whether the blue line reached the aquarium. His father was trying to enlarge a route diagram on a phone with a cracked screen. I handed them the map and traced a transfer with my finger. The boy held the sheet carefully, as if it were an official document rather than something softened by months in my pocket.<br><br>They left at the next stop, carrying the map by accident. I noticed only when the doors closed. For a moment I was irritated, then I pictured the boy opening it beneath the aquarium sign. The map had ceased recording my journeys and entered someone else's. Its usefulness, which I had treated as a private kind of memory, had become portable again.<br><br>The next morning I bought another copy from the same kiosk. It was crisp, accurate, and entirely blank in the way that mattered. I folded it along the printed lines and put it in my pocket, knowing that use would gradually replace the city's perfect geometry with a smaller history of weather, waiting, and chance exchanges.<br><br>A week later, I saw a family studying a station diagram and almost offered the new map. My hand reached toward my pocket before I remembered that generosity did not guarantee a return. I offered it anyway, but waited while they copied the route. The paper came back with one new crease, folded by a stranger along a line I would not have chosen. I left the crease in place. It was evidence that the map could gather a shared history without remaining with every person who used it."},
78: {skill_tested: "Trace a sequence of understanding.", question: "Which event most directly changes the narrator's reaction to losing the map?", option1: "Buying the original map from a kiosk.", option2: "Waiting while a train stops between stations.", option3: "Folding a new map along printed lines.", option4: "Imagining the boy using it at the aquarium.", correctAnswer: "Imagining the boy using it at the aquarium.", explanation: "Step 1: The imagined continued use transforms irritation into acceptance.<br><br>Answer: Imagining the boy using it at the aquarium.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how ordinary objects acquire meanings beyond their practical uses.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>After the river withdrew, the community center became a temporary home for objects without owners. Volunteers carried in plastic bins filled with whatever the water had scattered: a dented saucepan, single shoes, framed photographs clouded beneath their glass. I was assigned to arrange the tables. Believing efficiency required certainty, I made signs for kitchenware, clothing, documents, and items beyond repair.<br><br>Mrs. Alvarez, who coordinated the room, moved slowly behind me. She placed a child's red mitten beside a water-stained recipe notebook rather than with the other clothes. When I pointed out the mistake, she showed me a name written inside the mitten and the same surname on the notebook. The objects, she explained, might have left one house together. Keeping them close could help someone recognize both.<br><br>My categories began to seem too confident. A brass key could be hardware, but it could also identify a house whose door was gone. A chipped blue cup belonged among dishes until a man recognized it as the pencil holder from his wife's desk. He did not take it immediately. He stood beside the table, telling us how she sharpened pencils over the sink because she disliked crumbs of graphite on paper.<br><br>By evening, the room looked less orderly but more legible. We grouped objects by uncertain connections, leaving room between clusters for later discoveries. People supplied stories that no label could have predicted. I had thought our task was to return property. Mrs. Alvarez understood that before an object could be returned, it sometimes had to return a memory.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The paper map in my coat pocket was already obsolete when I bought it. The transit authority had released an app, and station screens announced delays more accurately than printed lines could. Still, I liked unfolding the map across my knees. Its colored routes made the city appear graspable, even when a train stopped between stations and the lights flickered.<br><br>Each crease became associated with a different trip. One corner tore during a rainstorm when I used the map as a roof for a bag of library books. A coffee ring marked the afternoon my cousin and I planned three destinations but reached only one. I wrote no notes on the paper, yet wear supplied a record more personal than any saved itinerary.<br><br>On a crowded train, a boy beside me asked whether the blue line reached the aquarium. His father was trying to enlarge a route diagram on a phone with a cracked screen. I handed them the map and traced a transfer with my finger. The boy held the sheet carefully, as if it were an official document rather than something softened by months in my pocket.<br><br>They left at the next stop, carrying the map by accident. I noticed only when the doors closed. For a moment I was irritated, then I pictured the boy opening it beneath the aquarium sign. The map had ceased recording my journeys and entered someone else's. Its usefulness, which I had treated as a private kind of memory, had become portable again.<br><br>The next morning I bought another copy from the same kiosk. It was crisp, accurate, and entirely blank in the way that mattered. I folded it along the printed lines and put it in my pocket, knowing that use would gradually replace the city's perfect geometry with a smaller history of weather, waiting, and chance exchanges.<br><br>A week later, I saw a family studying a station diagram and almost offered the new map. My hand reached toward my pocket before I remembered that generosity did not guarantee a return. I offered it anyway, but waited while they copied the route. The paper came back with one new crease, folded by a stranger along a line I would not have chosen. I left the crease in place. It was evidence that the map could gather a shared history without remaining with every person who used it."},
