cognized it as the pencil holder from his wife's desk. He did not take it immediately. He stood beside the table, telling us how she sharpened pencils over the sink because she disliked crumbs of graphite on paper.<br><br>By evening, the room looked less orderly but more legible. We grouped objects by uncertain connections, leaving room between clusters for later discoveries. People supplied stories that no label could have predicted. I had thought our task was to return property. Mrs. Alvarez understood that before an object could be returned, it sometimes had to return a memory.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The paper map in my coat pocket was already obsolete when I bought it. The transit authority had released an app, and station screens announced delays more accurately than printed lines could. Still, I liked unfolding the map across my knees. Its colored routes made the city appear graspable, even when a train stopped between stations and the lights flickered.<br><br>Each crease became associated with a different trip. One corner tore during a rainstorm when I used the map as a roof for a bag of library books. A coffee ring marked the afternoon my cousin and I planned three destinations but reached only one. I wrote no notes on the paper, yet wear supplied a record more personal than any saved itinerary.<br><br>On a crowded train, a boy beside me asked whether the blue line reached the aquarium. His father was trying to enlarge a route diagram on a phone with a cracked screen. I handed them the map and traced a transfer with my finger. The boy held the sheet carefully, as if it were an official document rather than something softened by months in my pocket.<br><br>They left at the next stop, carrying the map by accident. I noticed only when the doors closed. For a moment I was irritated, then I pictured the boy opening it beneath the aquarium sign. The map had ceased recording my journeys and entered someone else's. Its usefulness, which I had treated as a private kind of memory, had become portable again.<br><br>The next morning I bought another copy from the same kiosk. It was crisp, accurate, and entirely blank in the way that mattered. I folded it along the printed lines and put it in my pocket, knowing that use would gradually replace the city's perfect geometry with a smaller history of weather, waiting, and chance exchanges.<br><br>A week later, I saw a family studying a station diagram and almost offered the new map. My hand reached toward my pocket before I remembered that generosity did not guarantee a return. I offered it anyway, but waited while they copied the route. The paper came back with one new crease, folded by a stranger along a line I would not have chosen. I left the crease in place. It was evidence that the map could gather a shared history without remaining with every person who used it."},
80: {skill_tested: "Synthesize symbolic roles.", question: "The unlabeled spaces in Passage A and the blank new map in Passage B both represent:", option1: "evidence that the narrators have failed.", option2: "possibilities that future experience may fill.", option3: "places from which all memories have been erased permanently.", option4: "official systems that cannot be revised.", correctAnswer: "possibilities that future experience may fill.", explanation: "Step 1: Both forms of blankness leave room for later connections rather than signaling emptiness alone.<br><br>Answer: possibilities that future experience may fill.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how ordinary objects acquire meanings beyond their practical uses.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>After the river withdrew, the community center became a temporary home for objects without owners. Volunteers carried in plastic bins filled with whatever the water had scattered: a dented saucepan, single shoes, framed photographs clouded beneath their glass. I was assigned to arrange the tables. Believing efficiency required certainty, I made signs for kitchenware, clothing, documents, and items beyond repair.<br><br>Mrs. Alvarez, who coordinated the room, moved slowly behind me. She placed a child's red mitten beside a water-stained recipe notebook rather than with the other clothes. When I pointed out the mistake, she showed me a name written inside the mitten and the same surname on the notebook. The objects, she explained, might have left one house together. Keeping them close could help someone recognize both.<br><br>My categories began to seem too confident. A brass key could be hardware, but it could also identify a house whose door was gone. A chipped blue cup belonged among dishes until a man recognized it as the pencil holder from his wife's desk. He did not take it immediately. He stood beside the table, telling us how she sharpened pencils over the sink because she disliked crumbs of graphite on paper.<br><br>By evening, the room looked less orderly but more legible. We grouped objects by uncertain connections, leaving room between clusters for later discoveries. People supplied stories that no label could have predicted. I had thought our task was to return property. Mrs. Alvarez understood that before an object could be returned, it sometimes had to return a memory.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The paper map in my coat pocket was already obsolete when I bought it. The transit authority had released an app, and station screens announced delays more accurately than printed lines could. Still, I liked unfolding the map across my knees. Its colored routes made the city appear graspable, even when a train stopped between stations and the lights flickered.<br><br>Each crease became associated with a different trip. One corner tore during a rainstorm when I used the map as a roof for a bag of library books. A coffee ring marked the afternoon my cousin and I planned three destinations but reached only one. I wrote no notes on the paper, yet wear supplied a record more personal than any saved itinerary.<br><br>On a crowded train, a boy beside me asked whether the blue line reached the aquarium. His father was trying to enlarge a route diagram on a phone with a cracked screen. I handed them the map and traced a transfer with my finger. The boy held the sheet carefully, as if it were an official document rather than something softened by months in my pocket.<br><br>They left at the next stop, carrying the map by accident. I noticed only when the doors closed. For a moment I was irritated, then I pictured the boy opening it beneath the aquarium sign. The map had ceased recording my journeys and entered someone else's. Its usefulness, which I had treated as a private kind of memory, had become portable again.<br><br>The next morning I bought another copy from the same kiosk. It was crisp, accurate, and entirely blank in the way that mattered. I folded it along the printed lines and put it in my pocket, knowing that use would gradually replace the city's perfect geometry with a smaller history of weather, waiting, and chance exchanges.<br><br>A week later, I saw a family studying a station diagram and almost offered the new map. My hand reached toward my pocket before I remembered that generosity did not guarantee a return. I offered it anyway, but waited while they copied the route. The paper came back with one new crease, folded by a stranger along a line I would not have chosen. I left the crease in place. It was evidence that the map could gather a shared history without remaining with every person who used it."},
81: {skill_tested: "Compare development of meaning.", question: "Which statement best compares how meaning develops in the passages?", option1: "Both passages locate meaning only in an object's original condition.", option2: "Passage A rejects stories while Passage B rejects physical evidence.", option3: "Passage A builds meaning by reuniting scattered clues, whereas Passage B follows meaning as it travels from one user to another.", option4: "Both narrators know from the beginning what each object will signify.", correctAnswer: "Passage A builds meaning by reuniting scattered clues, whereas Passage B follows meaning as it travels from one user to another.", explanation: "Step 1: One passage reconstructs connections; the other tracks a meaningful object's circulation.<br><br>Answer: Passage A builds meaning by reuniting scattered clues, whereas Passage B follows meaning as it travels from one user to another.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how ordinary objects acquire meanings beyond their practical uses.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>After the river withdrew, the community center became a temporary home for objects without owners. Volunteers carried in plastic bins filled with whatever the water had scattered: a dented saucepan, single shoes, framed photographs clouded beneath their glass. I was assigned to arrange the tables. Believing efficiency required certainty, I made signs for kitchenware, clothing, documents, and items beyond repair.<br><br>Mrs. Alvarez, who coordinated the room, moved slowly behind me. She placed a child's red mitten beside a water-stained recipe notebook rather than with the other clothes. When I pointed out the mistake, she showed me a name written inside the mitten and the same surname on the notebook. The objects, she explained, might have left one house together. Keeping them close could help someone recognize both.<br><br>My categories began to seem too confident. A brass key could be hardware, but it could also identify a house whose door was gone. A chipped blue cup belonged among dishes until a man recognized it as the pencil holder from his wife's desk. He did not take it immediately. He stood beside the table, telling us how she sharpened pencils over the sink because she disliked crumbs of graphite on paper.<br><br>By evening, the room looked less orderly but more legible. We grouped objects by uncertain connections, leaving room between clusters for later discoveries. People supplied stories that no label could have predicted. I had thought our task was to return property. Mrs. Alvarez understood that before an object could be returned, it sometimes had to return a memory.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The paper map in my coat pocket was already obsolete when I bought it. The transit authority had released an app, and station screens announced delays more accurately than printed lines could. Still, I liked unfolding the map across my knees. Its colored routes made the city appear graspable, even when a train stopped between stations and the lights flickered.<br><br>Each crease became associated with a different trip. One corner tore during a rainstorm when I used the map as a roof for a bag of library books. A coffee ring marked the afternoon my cousin and I planned three destinations but reached only one. I wrote no notes on the paper, yet wear supplied a record more personal than any saved itinerary.<br><br>On a crowded train, a boy beside me asked whether the blue line reached the aquarium. His father was trying to enlarge a route diagram on a phone with a cracked screen. I handed them the map and traced a transfer with my finger. The boy held the sheet carefully, as if it were an official document rather than something softened by months in my pocket.<br><br>They left at the next stop, carrying the map by accident. I noticed only when the doors closed. For a moment I was irritated, then I pictured the boy opening it beneath the aquarium sign. The map had ceased recording my journeys and entered someone else's. Its usefulness, which I had treated as a private kind of memory, had become portable again.<br><br>The next morning I bought another copy from the same kiosk. It was crisp, accurate, and entirely blank in the way that mattered. I folded it along the printed lines and put it in my pocket, knowing that use would gradually replace the city's perfect geometry with a smaller history of weather, waiting, and chance exchanges.<br><br>A week later, I saw a family studying a station diagram and almost offered the new map. My hand reached toward my pocket before I remembered that generosity did not guarantee a return. I offered it anyway, but waited while they copied the route. The paper came back with one new crease, folded by a stranger along a line I would not have chosen. I left the crease in place. It was evidence that the map could gather a shared history without remaining with every person who used it."},
82: {skill_tested: "Identify an explicitly stated purpose of a work practice.", question: "According to Passage B, why does Rosa leave narrow gaps among boxes?", option1: "To separate every pantry's frozen food from its dry goods", option2: "To make each cart appear less heavily loaded", option3: "To reserve room for labels that will be added later", option4: "To provide places where volunteers can grip and shift boxes", correctAnswer: "To provide places where volunteers can grip and shift boxes", explanation: "Step 1: Rosa describes the gaps as handles made of air.<br><br>Step 2: The final paragraph confirms that they allow boxes to be shifted without unloading a whole cart.<br><br>Answer: To provide places where volunteers can grip and shift boxes", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages describe narrators learning how public services are coordinated.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>From the passenger deck, a ferry terminal appears to operate by bells. One sounds, a gate opens, and people move toward a boat. During my first week in the operations room, I learned that the bell came near the end of a much longer sentence. Before it rang, arrival times, vehicle counts, tides, crew hours, and connecting buses had already been arranged into agreement.<br><br>Leah, the dispatcher training me, kept a pencil tucked behind her ear despite having three computer screens. She used it to mark a paper grid listing each crossing. I assumed the grid was a backup. Instead, she called it her horizon. The screens showed where vessels were now; the grid showed which later departures would be disturbed by any decision made now.<br><br>Late one afternoon, fog slowed an incoming ferry while a bus carrying forty passengers approached the terminal. Holding the next boat would preserve the connection but delay commuters already waiting. Sending it on time would strand the bus passengers for an hour. Leah asked the dock supervisor how quickly vehicles could unload, checked when one crew member's shift ended, and moved a smaller vessel to a later route. She then held the boat for six minutes.<br><br>Passengers in the waiting room heard only the revised departure announcement. Some sighed. They could not see that six minutes had protected a bus connection without pushing a crew beyond its permitted hours or leaving the later route uncovered. Leah made two pencil marks and watched the fog erase the far end of the pier.<br><br>I had thought dispatching meant responding quickly to whatever happened. Leah showed me that speed was less important than seeing how far a response traveled. By the end of the month, I had begun drawing my own paper horizon. The bell still sounded like a beginning downstairs. Upstairs, I heard it as confirmation that many possible beginnings had already been considered.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The distribution center looked calm at seven in the morning. Pallets formed square aisles, labels faced outward, and empty carts stood nested by the door. At nine, vans from six neighborhood pantries would arrive for weekly food orders. I was helping Rosa check the loads and wondered why she moved so slowly when there was so much to finish.<br><br>Rosa read each order from bottom to top. Heavy boxes were loaded first, but that was not her only rule. She separated glass jars from metal cans, kept items needed at the first delivery closest to the van door, and left narrow gaps where volunteers could grip the boxes. She called the gaps handles made of air. To me, they looked like wasted space.<br><br>At eight fifteen, a freezer alarm sounded. The maintenance worker estimated that repairs would take several hours. Rosa did not begin moving every frozen item. She checked the temperature log, identified the foods most likely to soften first, and called two pantries that had unused freezer capacity. Meanwhile, she reassigned volunteers so the ordinary dry-goods orders continued. The room grew louder, but the aisles remained open.<br><br>When the vans arrived, drivers took the threatened frozen foods to temporary storage along with their regular orders. No pantry received everything at its usual time, yet none lost a full delivery. Only after the last van left did Rosa explain that moving every frozen box at once would have blocked the aisles, delayed all six orders, and placed foods with different temperature limits into one undifferentiated emergency.<br><br>The empty gaps in the morning loads no longer looked careless. They allowed boxes to be shifted without unloading an entire cart. Rosa's slow checking had not postponed action; it had preserved options. I began to understand calm not as the absence of urgency but as a way of preventing one urgent problem from occupying all the available space."},
83: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of a figurative term.", question: "In Passage A, Leah calls the paper grid her 'horizon' primarily because it helps her:", option1: "see future effects beyond the vessels' current positions.", option2: "estimate how quickly fog will disappear from the pier.", option3: "record only crossings already completed.", option4: "replace all information shown on the computer screens.", correctAnswer: "see future effects beyond the vessels' current positions.", explanation: "Step 1: The screens show where vessels are at present.<br><br>Step 2: The grid shows which later departures may be affected by a current decision, so 'horizon' refers to extending her view into future consequences.<br><br>Answer: see future effects beyond the vessels' current positions.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages describe narrators learning how public services are coordinated.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>From the passenger deck, a ferry terminal appears to operate by bells. One sounds, a gate opens, and people move toward a boat. During my first week in the operations room, I learned that the bell came near the end of a much longer sentence. Before it rang, arrival times, vehicle counts, tides, crew hours, and connecting buses had already been arranged into agreement.<br><br>Leah, the dispatcher training me, kept a pencil tucked behind her ear despite having three computer screens. She used it to mark a paper grid listing each crossing. I assumed the grid was a backup. Instead, she called it her horizon. The screens showed where vessels were now; the grid showed which later departures would be disturbed by any decision made now.<br><br>Late one afternoon, fog slowed an incoming ferry while a bus carrying forty passengers approached the terminal. Holding the next boat would preserve the connection but delay commuters already waiting. Sending it on time would strand the bus passengers for an hour. Leah asked the dock supervisor how quickly vehicles could unload, checked when one crew member's shift ended, and moved a smaller vessel to a later route. She then held the boat for six minutes.<br><br>Passengers in the waiting room heard only the revised departure announcement. Some sighed. They could not see that six minutes had protected a bus connection without pushing a crew beyond its permitted hours or leaving the later route uncovered. Leah made two pencil marks and watched the fog erase the far end of the pier.<br><br>I had thought dispatching meant responding quickly to whatever happened. Leah showed me that speed was less important than seeing how far a response traveled. By the end of the month, I had begun drawing my own paper horizon. The bell still sounded like a beginning downstairs. Upstairs, I heard it as confirmation that many possible beginnings had already been considered.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The distribution center looked calm at seven in the morning. Pallets formed square aisles, labels faced outward, and empty carts stood nested by the door. At nine, vans from six neighborhood pantries would arrive for weekly food orders. I was helping Rosa check the loads and wondered why she moved so slowly when there was so much to finish.<br><br>Rosa read each order from bottom to top. Heavy boxes were loaded first, but that was not her only rule. She separated glass jars from metal cans, kept items needed at the first delivery closest to the van door, and left narrow gaps where volunteers could grip the boxes. She called the gaps handles made of air. To me, they looked like wasted space.<br><br>At eight fifteen, a freezer alarm sounded. The maintenance worker estimated that repairs would take several hours. Rosa did not begin moving every frozen item. She checked the temperature log, identified the foods most likely to soften first, and called two pantries that had unused freezer capacity. Meanwhile, she reassigned volunteers so the ordinary dry-goods orders continued. The room grew louder, but the aisles remained open.<br><br>When the vans arrived, drivers took the threatened frozen foods to temporary storage along with their regular orders. No pantry received everything at its usual time, yet none lost a full delivery. Only after the last van left did Rosa explain that moving every frozen box at once would have blocked the aisles, delayed all six orders, and placed foods with different temperature limits into one undifferentiated emergency.<br><br>The empty gaps in the morning loads no longer looked careless. They allowed boxes to be shifted without unloading an entire cart. Rosa's slow checking had not postponed action; it had preserved options. I began to understand calm not as the absence of urgency but as a way of preventing one urgent problem from occupying all the available space."},
84: {skill_tested: "Identify a theme shared by paired passages.", question: "Which statement best expresses a central idea shared by both passages?", option1: "Public services function best when workers conceal every delay from the public.", option2: "Effective responses preserve the larger system instead of allowing one immediate problem to dominate it.", option3: "Paper records are always more reliable than computer systems.", option4: "The fastest possible response is usually the fairest one.", correctAnswer: "Effective responses preserve the larger system instead of allowing one immediate problem to dominate it.", explanation: "Step 1: Leah protects several routes, a connection, and crew limits while addressing one delayed ferry.<br><br>Step 2: Rosa protects six ordinary orders while moving only the frozen goods in greatest danger.<br><br>Answer: Effective responses preserve the larger system instead of allowing one immediate problem to dominate it.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages describe narrators learning how public services are coordinated.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>From the passenger deck, a ferry terminal appears to operate by bells. One sounds, a gate opens, and people move toward a boat. During my first week in the operations room, I learned that the bell came near the end of a much longer sentence. Before it rang, arrival times, vehicle counts, tides, crew hours, and connecting buses had already been arranged into agreement.<br><br>Leah, the dispatcher training me, kept a pencil tucked behind her ear despite having three computer screens. She used it to mark a paper grid listing each crossing. I assumed the grid was a backup. Instead, she called it her horizon. The screens showed where vessels were now; the grid showed which later departures would be disturbed by any decision made now.<br><br>Late one afternoon, fog slowed an incoming ferry while a bus carrying forty passengers approached the terminal. Holding the next boat would preserve the connection but delay commuters already waiting. Sending it on time would strand the bus passengers for an hour. Leah asked the dock supervisor how quickly vehicles could unload, checked when one crew member's shift ended, and moved a smaller vessel to a later route. She then held the boat for six minutes.<br><br>Passengers in the waiting room heard only the revised departure announcement. Some sighed. They could not see that six minutes had protected a bus connection without pushing a crew beyond its permitted hours or leaving the later route uncovered. Leah made two pencil marks and watched the fog erase the far end of the pier.<br><br>I had thought dispatching meant responding quickly to whatever happened. Leah showed me that speed was less important than seeing how far a response traveled. By the end of the month, I had begun drawing my own paper horizon. The bell still sounded like a beginning downstairs. Upstairs, I heard it as confirmation that many possible beginnings had already been considered.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The distribution center looked calm at seven in the morning. Pallets formed square aisles, labels faced outward, and empty carts stood nested by the door. At nine, vans from six neighborhood pantries would arrive for weekly food orders. I was helping Rosa check the loads and wondered why she moved so slowly when there was so much to finish.<br><br>Rosa read each order from bottom to top. Heavy boxes were loaded first, but that was not her only rule. She separated glass jars from metal cans, kept items needed at the first delivery closest to the van door, and left narrow gaps where volunteers could grip the boxes. She called the gaps handles made of air. To me, they looked like wasted space.<br><br>At eight fifteen, a freezer alarm sounded. The maintenance worker estimated that repairs would take several hours. Rosa did not begin moving every frozen item. She checked the temperature log, identified the foods most likely to soften first, and called two pantries that had unused freezer capacity. Meanwhile, she reassigned volunteers so the ordinary dry-goods orders continued. The room grew louder, but the aisles remained open.<br><br>When the vans arrived, drivers took the threatened frozen foods to temporary storage along with their regular orders. No pantry received everything at its usual time, yet none lost a full delivery. Only after the last van left did Rosa explain that moving every frozen box at once would have blocked the aisles, delayed all six orders, and placed foods with different temperature limits into one undifferentiated emergency.<br><br>The empty gaps in the morning loads no longer looked careless. They allowed boxes to be shifted without unloading an entire cart. Rosa's slow checking had not postponed action; it had preserved options. I began to understand calm not as the absence of urgency but as a way of preventing one urgent problem from occupying all the available space."},
85: {skill_tested: "Analyze how a visible event relates to hidden preparation.", question: "The bell in Passage A primarily represents:", option1: "the moment when Leah first begins planning a crossing.", option2: "an outdated device that the narrator plans to replace.", option3: "a simple public signal produced after numerous constraints have been reconciled.", option4: "a warning that fog has made every route unsafe.", correctAnswer: "a simple public signal produced after numerous constraints have been reconciled.", explanation: "Step 1: Passengers experience the bell as the beginning of boarding.<br><br>Step 2: The operations room has already coordinated arrivals, loads, tides, staffing, and connections.<br><br>Step 3: The bell condenses that hidden process into one visible and audible outcome.<br><br>Answer: a simple public signal produced after numerous constraints have been reconciled.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages describe narrators learning how public services are coordinated.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>From the passenger deck, a ferry terminal appears to operate by bells. One sounds, a gate opens, and people move toward a boat. During my first week in the operations room, I learned that the bell came near the end of a much longer sentence. Before it rang, arrival times, vehicle counts, tides, crew hours, and connecting buses had already been arranged into agreement.<br><br>Leah, the dispatcher training me, kept a pencil tucked behind her ear despite having three computer screens. She used it to mark a paper grid listing each crossing. I assumed the grid was a backup. Instead, she called it her horizon. The screens showed where vessels were now; the grid showed which later departures would be disturbed by any decision made now.<br><br>Late one afternoon, fog slowed an incoming ferry while a bus carrying forty passengers approached the terminal. Holding the next boat would preserve the connection but delay commuters already waiting. Sending it on time would strand the bus passengers for an hour. Leah asked the dock supervisor how quickly vehicles could unload, checked when one crew member's shift ended, and moved a smaller vessel to a later route. She then held the boat for six minutes.<br><br>Passengers in the waiting room heard only the revised departure announcement. Some sighed. They could not see that six minutes had protected a bus connection without pushing a crew beyond its permitted hours or leaving the later route uncovered. Leah made two pencil marks and watched the fog erase the far end of the pier.<br><br>I had thought dispatching meant responding quickly to whatever happened. Leah showed me that speed was less important than seeing how far a response traveled. By the end of the month, I had begun drawing my own paper horizon. The bell still sounded like a beginning downstairs. Upstairs, I heard it as confirmation that many possible beginnings had already been considered.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The distribution center looked calm at seven in the morning. Pallets formed square aisles, labels faced outward, and empty carts stood nested by the door. At nine, vans from six neighborhood pantries would arrive for weekly food orders. I was helping Rosa check the loads and wondered why she moved so slowly when there was so much to finish.<br><br>Rosa read each order from bottom to top. Heavy boxes were loaded first, but that was not her only rule. She separated glass jars from metal cans, kept items needed at the first delivery closest to the van door, and left narrow gaps where volunteers could grip the boxes. She called the gaps handles made of air. To me, they looked like wasted space.<br><br>At eight fifteen, a freezer alarm sounded. The maintenance worker estimated that repairs would take several hours. Rosa did not begin moving every frozen item. She checked the temperature log, identified the foods most likely to soften first, and called two pantries that had unused freezer capacity. Meanwhile, she reassigned volunteers so the ordinary dry-goods orders continued. The room grew louder, but the aisles remained open.<br><br>When the vans arrived, drivers took the threatened frozen foods to temporary storage along with their regular orders. No pantry received everything at its usual time, yet none lost a full delivery. Only after the last van left did Rosa explain that moving every frozen box at once would have blocked the aisles, delayed all six orders, and placed foods with different temperature limits into one undifferentiated emergency.<br><br>The empty gaps in the morning loads no longer looked careless. They allowed boxes to be shifted without unloading an entire cart. Rosa's slow checking had not postponed action; it had preserved options. I began to understand calm not as the absence of urgency but as a way of preventing one urgent problem from occupying all the available space."},
86: {skill_tested: "Infer why a response is deliberately limited.", question: "Why does Rosa avoid moving every frozen item immediately after the alarm?", option1: "She doubts that the freezer is actually warming.", option2: "The neighborhood pantries refuse to store any frozen products.", option3: "She expects the drivers to repair the freezer before loading.", option4: "A total evacuation could obstruct ordinary work and treat foods with different risks as identical.", correctAnswer: "A total evacuation could obstruct ordinary work and treat foods with different risks as identical.", explanation: "Step 1: Rosa checks the temperature history and selects the foods likely to soften first.<br><br>Step 2: She later explains that moving everything would block aisles, delay all orders, and combine items with different limits.<br><br>Step 3: Her selective action contains both the physical disruption and the risk rather than maximizing visible activity.<br><br>Answer: A total evacuation could obstruct ordinary work and treat foods with different risks as identical.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages describe narrators learning how public services are coordinated.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>From the passenger deck, a ferry terminal appears to operate by bells. One sounds, a gate opens, and people move toward a boat. During my first week in the operations room, I learned that the bell came near the end of a much longer sentence. Before it rang, arrival times, vehicle counts, tides, crew hours, and connecting buses had already been arranged into agreement.<br><br>Leah, the dispatcher training me, kept a pencil tucked behind her ear despite having three computer screens. She used it to mark a paper grid listing each crossing. I assumed the grid was a backup. Instead, she called it her horizon. The screens showed where vessels were now; the grid showed which later departures would be disturbed by any decision made now.<br><br>Late one afternoon, fog slowed an incoming ferry while a bus carrying forty passengers approached the terminal. Holding the next boat would preserve the connection but delay commuters already waiting. Sending it on time would strand the bus passengers for an hour. Leah asked the dock supervisor how quickly vehicles could unload, checked when one crew member's shift ended, and moved a smaller vessel to a later route. She then held the boat for six minutes.<br><br>Passengers in the waiting room heard only the revised departure announcement. Some sighed. They could not see that six minutes had protected a bus connection without pushing a crew beyond its permitted hours or leaving the later route uncovered. Leah made two pencil marks and watched the fog erase the far end of the pier.<br><br>I had thought dispatching meant responding quickly to whatever happened. Leah showed me that speed was less important than seeing how far a response traveled. By the end of the month, I had begun drawing my own paper horizon. The bell still sounded like a beginning downstairs. Upstairs, I heard it as confirmation that many possible beginnings had already been considered.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The distribution center looked calm at seven in the morning. Pallets formed square aisles, labels faced outward, and empty carts stood nested by the door. At nine, vans from six neighborhood pantries would arrive for weekly food orders. I was helping Rosa check the loads and wondered why she moved so slowly when there was so much to finish.<br><br>Rosa read each order from bottom to top. Heavy boxes were loaded first, but that was not her only rule. She separated glass jars from metal cans, kept items needed at the first delivery closest to the van door, and left narrow gaps where volunteers could grip the boxes. She called the gaps handles made of air. To me, they looked like wasted space.<br><br>At eight fifteen, a freezer alarm sounded. The maintenance worker estimated that repairs would take several hours. Rosa did not begin moving every frozen item. She checked the temperature log, identified the foods most likely to soften first, and called two pantries that had unused freezer capacity. Meanwhile, she reassigned volunteers so the ordinary dry-goods orders continued. The room grew louder, but the aisles remained open.<br><br>When the vans arrived, drivers took the threatened frozen foods to temporary storage along with their regular orders. No pantry received everything at its usual time, yet none lost a full delivery. Only after the last van left did Rosa explain that moving every frozen box at once would have blocked the aisles, delayed all six orders, and placed foods with different temperature limits into one undifferentiated emergency.<br><br>The empty gaps in the morning loads no longer looked careless. They allowed boxes to be shifted without unloading an entire cart. Rosa's slow checking had not postponed action; it had preserved options. I began to understand calm not as the absence of urgency but as a way of preventing one urgent problem from occupying all the available space."},
87: {skill_tested: "Trace how an initial interpretation is revised by later events.", question: "In Passage B, the narrator's view of Rosa's slow checking changes from seeing it as:", option1: "unnecessary delay to seeing it as preparation that preserved choices.", option2: "careful training to seeing it as avoidance of responsibility.", option3: "efficient loading to seeing it as the cause of blocked aisles.", option4: "an emergency response to seeing it as an ordinary delivery rule.", correctAnswer: "unnecessary delay to seeing it as preparation that preserved choices.", explanation: "Step 1: The narrator initially wonders why Rosa moves slowly with much still to finish.<br><br>Step 2: Her earlier checking and spacing allow the freezer problem to be managed without halting all deliveries.<br><br>Step 3: The narrator finally understands that the apparent delay created flexibility for later action.<br><br>Answer: unnecessary delay to seeing it as preparation that preserved choices.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages describe narrators learning how public services are coordinated.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>From the passenger deck, a ferry terminal appears to operate by bells. One sounds, a gate opens, and people move toward a boat. During my first week in the operations room, I learned that the bell came near the end of a much longer sentence. Before it rang, arrival times, vehicle counts, tides, crew hours, and connecting buses had already been arranged into agreement.<br><br>Leah, the dispatcher training me, kept a pencil tucked behind her ear despite having three computer screens. She used it to mark a paper grid listing each crossing. I assumed the grid was a backup. Instead, she called it her horizon. The screens showed where vessels were now; the grid showed which later departures would be disturbed by any decision made now.<br><br>Late one afternoon, fog slowed an incoming ferry while a bus carrying forty passengers approached the terminal. Holding the next boat would preserve the connection but delay commuters already waiting. Sending it on time would strand the bus passengers for an hour. Leah asked the dock supervisor how quickly vehicles could unload, checked when one crew member's shift ended, and moved a smaller vessel to a later route. She then held the boat for six minutes.<br><br>Passengers in the waiting room heard only the revised departure announcement. Some sighed. They could not see that six minutes had protected a bus connection without pushing a crew beyond its permitted hours or leaving the later route uncovered. Leah made two pencil marks and watched the fog erase the far end of the pier.<br><br>I had thought dispatching meant responding quickly to whatever happened. Leah showed me that speed was less important than seeing how far a response traveled. By the end of the month, I had begun drawing my own paper horizon. The bell still sounded like a beginning downstairs. Upstairs, I heard it as confirmation that many possible beginnings had already been considered.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The distribution center looked calm at seven in the morning. Pallets formed square aisles, labels faced outward, and empty carts stood nested by the door. At nine, vans from six neighborhood pantries would arrive for weekly food orders. I was helping Rosa check the loads and wondered why she moved so slowly when there was so much to finish.<br><br>Rosa read each order from bottom to top. Heavy boxes were loaded first, but that was not her only rule. She separated glass jars from metal cans, kept items needed at the first delivery closest to the van door, and left narrow gaps where volunteers could grip the boxes. She called the gaps handles made of air. To me, they looked like wasted space.<br><br>At eight fifteen, a freezer alarm sounded. The maintenance worker estimated that repairs would take several hours. Rosa did not begin moving every frozen item. She checked the temperature log, identified the foods most likely to soften first, and called two pantries that had unused freezer capacity. Meanwhile, she reassigned volunteers so the ordinary dry-goods orders continued. The room grew louder, but the aisles remained open.<br><br>When the vans arrived, drivers took the threatened frozen foods to temporary storage along with their regular orders. No pantry received everything at its usual time, yet none lost a full delivery. Only after the last van left did Rosa explain that moving every frozen box at once would have blocked the aisles, delayed all six orders, and placed foods with different temperature limits into one undifferentiated emergency.<br><br>The empty gaps in the morning loads no longer looked careless. They allowed boxes to be shifted without unloading an entire cart. Rosa's slow checking had not postponed action; it had preserved options. I began to understand calm not as the absence of urgency but as a way of preventing one urgent problem from occupying all the available space."},
88: {skill_tested: "Compare how mentors teach systems thinking.", question: "Leah and Rosa are most similar in that each teaches the narrator primarily by:", option1: "explaining every decision before any problem occurs.", option2: "using a disruption to reveal consequences that are not immediately visible.", option3: "allowing an emergency to stop all routine operations.", option4: "asking the narrator to make the final decision independently.", correctAnswer: "using a disruption to reveal consequences that are not immediately visible.", explanation: "Step 1: Leah's response to fog reveals effects on connections, crew hours, and later routes.<br><br>Step 2: Rosa's response to the freezer reveals effects on aisles, ordinary orders, and foods with different limits.<br><br>Step 3: In both passages, the narrator learns by seeing one problem managed within a larger network of consequences.<br><br>Answer: using a disruption to reveal consequences that are not immediately visible.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages describe narrators learning how public services are coordinated.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>From the passenger deck, a ferry terminal appears to operate by bells. One sounds, a gate opens, and people move toward a boat. During my first week in the operations room, I learned that the bell came near the end of a much longer sentence. Before it rang, arrival times, vehicle counts, tides, crew hours, and connecting buses had already been arranged into agreement.<br><br>Leah, the dispatcher training me, kept a pencil tucked behind her ear despite having three computer screens. She used it to mark a paper grid listing each crossing. I assumed the grid was a backup. Instead, she called it her horizon. The screens showed where vessels were now; the grid showed which later departures would be disturbed by any decision made now.<br><br>Late one afternoon, fog slowed an incoming ferry while a bus carrying forty passengers approached the terminal. Holding the next boat would preserve the connection but delay commuters already waiting. Sending it on time would strand the bus passengers for an hour. Leah asked the dock supervisor how quickly vehicles could unload, checked when one crew member's shift ended, and moved a smaller vessel to a later route. She then held the boat for six minutes.<br><br>Passengers in the waiting room heard only the revised departure announcement. Some sighed. They could not see that six minutes had protected a bus connection without pushing a crew beyond its permitted hours or leaving the later route uncovered. Leah made two pencil marks and watched the fog erase the far end of the pier.<br><br>I had thought dispatching meant responding quickly to whatever happened. Leah showed me that speed was less important than seeing how far a response traveled. By the end of the month, I had begun drawing my own paper horizon. The bell still sounded like a beginning downstairs. Upstairs, I heard it as confirmation that many possible beginnings had already been considered.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The distribution center looked calm at seven in the morning. Pallets formed square aisles, labels faced outward, and empty carts stood nested by the door. At nine, vans from six neighborhood pantries would arrive for weekly food orders. I was helping Rosa check the loads and wondered why she moved so slowly when there was so much to finish.<br><br>Rosa read each order from bottom to top. Heavy boxes were loaded first, but that was not her only rule. She separated glass jars from metal cans, kept items needed at the first delivery closest to the van door, and left narrow gaps where volunteers could grip the boxes. She called the gaps handles made of air. To me, they looked like wasted space.<br><br>At eight fifteen, a freezer alarm sounded. The maintenance worker estimated that repairs would take several hours. Rosa did not begin moving every frozen item. She checked the temperature log, identified the foods most likely to soften first, and called two pantries that had unused freezer capacity. Meanwhile, she reassigned volunteers so the ordinary dry-goods orders continued. The room grew louder, but the aisles remained open.<br><br>When the vans arrived, drivers took the threatened frozen foods to temporary storage along with their regular orders. No pantry received everything at its usual time, yet none lost a full delivery. Only after the last van left did Rosa explain that moving every frozen box at once would have blocked the aisles, delayed all six orders, and placed foods with different temperature limits into one undifferentiated emergency.<br><br>The empty gaps in the morning loads no longer looked careless. They allowed boxes to be shifted without unloading an entire cart. Rosa's slow checking had not postponed action; it had preserved options. I began to understand calm not as the absence of urgency but as a way of preventing one urgent problem from occupying all the available space."},
89: {skill_tested: "Synthesize parallel spatial metaphors for preserving future action.", question: "Leah's paper horizon and Rosa's handles made of air are most alike because each:", option1: "serves as a replacement for all digital equipment.", option2: "allows the public to observe every operational decision.", option3: "occupies space that initially appears unnecessary but protects later options.", option4: "eliminates the need to revise a schedule during disruption.", correctAnswer: "occupies space that initially appears unnecessary but protects later options.", explanation: "Step 1: Leah's paper grid extends attention beyond current vessel locations into later departures.<br><br>Step 2: Rosa's empty gaps appear wasteful but allow boxes to be moved without dismantling a load.<br><br>Step 3: Both devices reserve practical room—temporal in one passage and physical in the other—for adjustments not yet known to be necessary.<br><br>Answer: occupies space that initially appears unnecessary but protects later options.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages describe narrators learning how public services are coordinated.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>From the passenger deck, a ferry terminal appears to operate by bells. One sounds, a gate opens, and people move toward a boat. During my first week in the operations room, I learned that the bell came near the end of a much longer sentence. Before it rang, arrival times, vehicle counts, tides, crew hours, and connecting buses had already been arranged into agreement.<br><br>Leah, the dispatcher training me, kept a pencil tucked behind her ear despite having three computer screens. She used it to mark a paper grid listing each crossing. I assumed the grid was a backup. Instead, she called it her horizon. The screens showed where vessels were now; the grid showed which later departures would be disturbed by any decision made now.<br><br>Late one afternoon, fog slowed an incoming ferry while a bus carrying forty passengers approached the terminal. Holding the next boat would preserve the connection but delay commuters already waiting. Sending it on time would strand the bus passengers for an hour. Leah asked the dock supervisor how quickly vehicles could unload, checked when one crew member's shift ended, and moved a smaller vessel to a later route. She then held the boat for six minutes.<br><br>Passengers in the waiting room heard only the revised departure announcement. Some sighed. They could not see that six minutes had protected a bus connection without pushing a crew beyond its permitted hours or leaving the later route uncovered. Leah made two pencil marks and watched the fog erase the far end of the pier.<br><br>I had thought dispatching meant responding quickly to whatever happened. Leah showed me that speed was less important than seeing how far a response traveled. By the end of the month, I had begun drawing my own paper horizon. The bell still sounded like a beginning downstairs. Upstairs, I heard it as confirmation that many possible beginnings had already been considered.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The distribution center looked calm at seven in the morning. Pallets formed square aisles, labels faced outward, and empty carts stood nested by the door. At nine, vans from six neighborhood pantries would arrive for weekly food orders. I was helping Rosa check the loads and wondered why she moved so slowly when there was so much to finish.<br><br>Rosa read each order from bottom to top. Heavy boxes were loaded first, but that was not her only rule. She separated glass jars from metal cans, kept items needed at the first delivery closest to the van door, and left narrow gaps where volunteers could grip the boxes. She called the gaps handles made of air. To me, they looked like wasted space.<br><br>At eight fifteen, a freezer alarm sounded. The maintenance worker estimated that repairs would take several hours. Rosa did not begin moving every frozen item. She checked the temperature log, identified the foods most likely to soften first, and called two pantries that had unused freezer capacity. Meanwhile, she reassigned volunteers so the ordinary dry-goods orders continued. The room grew louder, but the aisles remained open.<br><br>When the vans arrived, drivers took the threatened frozen foods to temporary storage along with their regular orders. No pantry received everything at its usual time, yet none lost a full delivery. Only after the last van left did Rosa explain that moving every frozen box at once would have blocked the aisles, delayed all six orders, and placed foods with different temperature limits into one undifferentiated emergency.<br><br>The empty gaps in the morning loads no longer looked careless. They allowed boxes to be shifted without unloading an entire cart. Rosa's slow checking had not postponed action; it had preserved options. I began to understand calm not as the absence of urgency but as a way of preventing one urgent problem from occupying all the available space."},
90: {skill_tested: "Compare revised definitions of effective action.", question: "Taken together, the passages redefine effective action as action that is:", option1: "immediate enough to prevent the public from noticing inconvenience.", option2: "limited to the most visible part of an operational problem.", option3: "delayed until workers can guarantee a perfect outcome for everyone.", option4: "proportionate to the disruption and informed by its effects across the system.", correctAnswer: "proportionate to the disruption and informed by its effects across the system.", explanation: "Step 1: Leah chooses a six-minute hold after tracing effects on passengers, staffing, and later service.<br><br>Step 2: Rosa moves the most vulnerable food while continuing other orders and preserving aisle access.<br><br>Step 3: Neither response is maximal or perfect; each is calibrated to contain the immediate problem without exporting greater harm elsewhere.<br><br>Answer: proportionate to the disruption and informed by its effects across the system.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages describe narrators learning how public services are coordinated.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>From the passenger deck, a ferry terminal appears to operate by bells. One sounds, a gate opens, and people move toward a boat. During my first week in the operations room, I learned that the bell came near the end of a much longer sentence. Before it rang, arrival times, vehicle counts, tides, crew hours, and connecting buses had already been arranged into agreement.<br><br>Leah, the dispatcher training me, kept a pencil tucked behind her ear despite having three computer screens. She used it to mark a paper grid listing each crossing. I assumed the grid was a backup. Instead, she called it her horizon. The screens showed where vessels were now; the grid showed which later departures would be disturbed by any decision made now.<br><br>Late one afternoon, fog slowed an incoming ferry while a bus carrying forty passengers approached the terminal. Holding the next boat would preserve the connection but delay commuters already waiting. Sending it on time would strand the bus passengers for an hour. Leah asked the dock supervisor how quickly vehicles could unload, checked when one crew member's shift ended, and moved a smaller vessel to a later route. She then held the boat for six minutes.<br><br>Passengers in the waiting room heard only the revised departure announcement. Some sighed. They could not see that six minutes had protected a bus connection without pushing a crew beyond its permitted hours or leaving the later route uncovered. Leah made two pencil marks and watched the fog erase the far end of the pier.<br><br>I had thought dispatching meant responding quickly to whatever happened. Leah showed me that speed was less important than seeing how far a response traveled. By the end of the month, I had begun drawing my own paper horizon. The bell still sounded like a beginning downstairs. Upstairs, I heard it as confirmation that many possible beginnings had already been considered.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The distribution center looked calm at seven in the morning. Pallets formed square aisles, labels faced outward, and empty carts stood nested by the door. At nine, vans from six neighborhood pantries would arrive for weekly food orders. I was helping Rosa check the loads and wondered why she moved so slowly when there was so much to finish.<br><br>Rosa read each order from bottom to top. Heavy boxes were loaded first, but that was not her only rule. She separated glass jars from metal cans, kept items needed at the first delivery closest to the van door, and left narrow gaps where volunteers could grip the boxes. She called the gaps handles made of air. To me, they looked like wasted space.<br><br>At eight fifteen, a freezer alarm sounded. The maintenance worker estimated that repairs would take several hours. Rosa did not begin moving every frozen item. She checked the temperature log, identified the foods most likely to soften first, and called two pantries that had unused freezer capacity. Meanwhile, she reassigned volunteers so the ordinary dry-goods orders continued. The room grew louder, but the aisles remained open.<br><br>When the vans arrived, drivers took the threatened frozen foods to temporary storage along with their regular orders. No pantry received everything at its usual time, yet none lost a full delivery. Only after the last van left did Rosa explain that moving every frozen box at once would have blocked the aisles, delayed all six orders, and placed foods with different temperature limits into one undifferentiated emergency.<br><br>The empty gaps in the morning loads no longer looked careless. They allowed boxes to be shifted without unloading an entire cart. Rosa's slow checking had not postponed action; it had preserved options. I began to understand calm not as the absence of urgency but as a way of preventing one urgent problem from occupying all the available space."},
91: {skill_tested: "Identify an explicitly stated limitation of early transport boxes.", question: "According to the second paragraph, why were many early transport boxes of limited usefulness?", option1: "They often worked only within the system of one company.", option2: "They could be opened only inside ship holds.", option3: "They required goods to be removed at every local warehouse.", option4: "They were too small to reduce repeated handling.", correctAnswer: "They often worked only within the system of one company.", explanation: "Step 1: Early boxes reduced handling on routes controlled by one railway or shipping company.<br><br>Step 2: Their usefulness often ended at that company's boundary.<br><br>Answer: They often worked only within the system of one company.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>HUMANITIES:</b> This passage examines how standardization transformed the reusable freight container.<br><br>The history of container shipping is often told as the story of replacing loose cargo with a large metal box. That summary gives the box too much credit. Merchants had packed goods into reusable chests, barrels, detachable railway bodies, and truck-sized boxes long before container ships crossed oceans. The decisive innovation was not enclosure alone. It was an agreement about how boxes, cranes, ships, trains, ports, and paperwork would fit together.<br><br>Before widespread containerization, a shipment might arrive at a dock in sacks, crates, and cartons. Workers sorted the pieces, carried them into a warehouse, and loaded them again according to a ship's plan. At the destination, the process ran in reverse. The labor was skilled, but repeated handling consumed time and exposed goods to damage and loss. Early transport boxes reduced some handling, especially on routes controlled by one railway or shipping company. Their usefulness often ended at the boundary of that company's system.<br><br>A box that could travel broadly needed standardized interfaces. Its corner fittings had to meet lifting equipment at predictable points. Its dimensions had to suit ship cells, railway clearances, road rules, and stacking loads. A door latch that projected a few centimeters too far or a corner too weak for repeated lifting could disrupt the sequence. Standardization therefore concerned not only overall length and width but also the small places where one machine transferred responsibility to another.<br><br>Agreement was difficult because participants had already invested in different equipment. A port with narrow cranes favored one size; a railway favored another; a trucking company wanted maximum road capacity. Converting too early to a losing design could make expensive machinery useless. Converting too late could isolate a company from growing routes. Governments, manufacturers, carriers, insurers, and port authorities negotiated technical rules while also protecting economic interests.<br><br>Once enough participants accepted compatible designs, the value of each container increased. A box could leave a factory by truck, move by train, cross an ocean, and return to road transport without its contents being unpacked at every transfer. Ports redesigned yards around cranes and stacks rather than warehouses and loose piles. Ships changed shape, schedules tightened, and inland distribution centers grew near rail and highway connections. The standard did not merely improve an old box; it reorganized the landscape through which the box moved.<br><br>This reorganization also shifted labor rather than simply eliminating it. Less work occurred inside a ship's hold, while more work appeared in crane operation, equipment maintenance, scheduling, tracking, and inland logistics. Some port districts lost jobs and activity because older piers lacked space for container yards. Other locations expanded rapidly. Describing the change only as faster loading therefore overlooks who absorbed its costs and where new kinds of work emerged.<br><br>Historians still debate how to assign credit. One entrepreneur may demonstrate a profitable route, an engineer may refine a corner fitting, and a standards committee may make the design transferable. Earlier experiments can look like complete inventions when viewed backward, even if they never crossed organizational boundaries. Conversely, a later promoter may receive too much credit because commercial success makes one version visible.<br><br>Information had to become transferable as well. A container identified differently by a port, railway, and carrier could be physically compatible yet operationally lost. Shared identification formats and records allowed participants to track the same unit across transfers. In that sense, standardized paperwork performed a task parallel to standardized corner fittings: each gave separate organizations a common point of contact without requiring them to become one organization.<br><br>The better question is not who first imagined a strong reusable box. It is how a collection of partial ideas became a dependable system. Containerization took hold when physical design, operating practice, and institutional agreement reinforced one another. The familiar metal rectangle is the system's most visible object, but its real achievement is less visible: a transfer can occur because many independent participants have agreed in advance about what will meet at the corners."},
92: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of an economic term.", question: "As used in the fourth paragraph, the word 'isolate' most nearly means:", option1: "protect from physical damage.", option2: "separate from expanding transport connections.", option3: "move into a private warehouse.", option4: "identify with a tracking number.", correctAnswer: "separate from expanding transport connections.", explanation: "Step 1: Companies risked adopting a losing design early, but they also risked waiting while compatible routes grew.<br><br>Step 2: In that context, being 'isolated' means being separated from those expanding connections.<br><br>Answer: separate from expanding transport connections.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>HUMANITIES:</b> This passage examines how standardization transformed the reusable freight container.<br><br>The history of container shipping is often told as the story of replacing loose cargo with a large metal box. That summary gives the box too much credit. Merchants had packed goods into reusable chests, barrels, detachable railway bodies, and truck-sized boxes long before container ships crossed oceans. The decisive innovation was not enclosure alone. It was an agreement about how boxes, cranes, ships, trains, ports, and paperwork would fit together.<br><br>Before widespread containerization, a shipment might arrive at a dock in sacks, crates, and cartons. Workers sorted the pieces, carried them into a warehouse, and loaded them again according to a ship's plan. At the destination, the process ran in reverse. The labor was skilled, but repeated handling consumed time and exposed goods to damage and loss. Early transport boxes reduced some handling, especially on routes controlled by one railway or shipping company. Their usefulness often ended at the boundary of that company's system.<br><br>A box that could travel broadly needed standardized interfaces. Its corner fittings had to meet lifting equipment at predictable points. Its dimensions had to suit ship cells, railway clearances, road rules, and stacking loads. A door latch that projected a few centimeters too far or a corner too weak for repeated lifting could disrupt the sequence. Standardization therefore concerned not only overall length and width but also the small places where one machine transferred responsibility to another.<br><br>Agreement was difficult because participants had already invested in different equipment. A port with narrow cranes favored one size; a railway favored another; a trucking company wanted maximum road capacity. Converting too early to a losing design could make expensive machinery useless. Converting too late could isolate a company from growing routes. Governments, manufacturers, carriers, insurers, and port authorities negotiated technical rules while also protecting economic interests.<br><br>Once enough participants accepted compatible designs, the value of each container increased. A box could leave a factory by truck, move by train, cross an ocean, and return to road transport without its contents being unpacked at every transfer. Ports redesigned yards around cranes and stacks rather than warehouses and loose piles. Ships changed shape, schedules tightened, and inland distribution centers grew near rail and highway connections. The standard did not merely improve an old box; it reorganized the landscape through which the box moved.<br><br>This reorganization also shifted labor rather than simply eliminating it. Less work occurred inside a ship's hold, while more work appeared in crane operation, equipment maintenance, scheduling, tracking, and inland logistics. Some port districts lost jobs and activity because older piers lacked space for container yards. Other locations expanded rapidly. Describing the change only as faster loading therefore overlooks who absorbed its costs and where new kinds of work emerged.<br><br>Historians still debate how to assign credit. One entrepreneur may demonstrate a profitable route, an engineer may refine a corner fitting, and a standards committee may make the design transferable. Earlier experiments can look like complete inventions when viewed backward, even if they never crossed organizational boundaries. Conversely, a later promoter may receive too much credit because commercial success makes one version visible.<br><br>Information had to become transferable as well. A container identified differently by a port, railway, and carrier could be physically compatible yet operationally lost. Shared identification formats and records allowed participants to track the same unit across transfers. In that sense, standardized paperwork performed a task parallel to standardized corner fittings: each gave separate organizations a common point of contact without requiring them to become one organization.<br><br>The better question is not who first imagined a strong reusable box. It is how a collection of partial ideas became a dependable system. Containerization took hold when physical design, operating practice, and institutional agreement reinforced one another. The familiar metal rectangle is the system's most visible object, but its real achievement is less visible: a transfer can occur because many independent participants have agreed in advance about what will meet at the corners."},
93: {skill_tested: "Identify the central claim of a historical explanation.", question: "Which statement best expresses the passage's central claim?", option1: "The first reusable freight box immediately created modern container shipping.", option2: "Government committees contributed more to containerization than engineers or carriers did.", option3: "Containerization became transformative through compatible design, operations, information, and institutional agreement.", option4: "Container shipping eliminated port labor without creating other forms of work.", correctAnswer: "Containerization became transformative through compatible design, operations, information, and institutional agreement.", explanation: "Step 1: The passage distinguishes earlier boxes from a system capable of crossing organizational boundaries.<br><br>Step 2: It develops physical interfaces, investment decisions, tracking, and shared standards as mutually necessary parts.<br><br>Answer: Containerization became transformative through compatible design, operations, information, and institutional agreement.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>HUMANITIES:</b> This passage examines how standardization transformed the reusable freight container.<br><br>The history of container shipping is often told as the story of replacing loose cargo with a large metal box. That summary gives the box too much credit. Merchants had packed goods into reusable chests, barrels, detachable railway bodies, and truck-sized boxes long before container ships crossed oceans. The decisive innovation was not enclosure alone. It was an agreement about how boxes, cranes, ships, trains, ports, and paperwork would fit together.<br><br>Before widespread containerization, a shipment might arrive at a dock in sacks, crates, and cartons. Workers sorted the pieces, carried them into a warehouse, and loaded them again according to a ship's plan. At the destination, the process ran in reverse. The labor was skilled, but repeated handling consumed time and exposed goods to damage and loss. Early transport boxes reduced some handling, especially on routes controlled by one railway or shipping company. Their usefulness often ended at the boundary of that company's system.<br><br>A box that could travel broadly needed standardized interfaces. Its corner fittings had to meet lifting equipment at predictable points. Its dimensions had to suit ship cells, railway clearances, road rules, and stacking loads. A door latch that projected a few centimeters too far or a corner too weak for repeated lifting could disrupt the sequence. Standardization therefore concerned not only overall length and width but also the small places where one machine transferred responsibility to another.<br><br>Agreement was difficult because participants had already invested in different equipment. A port with narrow cranes favored one size; a railway favored another; a trucking company wanted maximum road capacity. Converting too early to a losing design could make expensive machinery useless. Converting too late could isolate a company from growing routes. Governments, manufacturers, carriers, insurers, and port authorities negotiated technical rules while also protecting economic interests.<br><br>Once enough participants accepted compatible designs, the value of each container increased. A box could leave a factory by truck, move by train, cross an ocean, and return to road transport without its contents being unpacked at every transfer. Ports redesigned yards around cranes and stacks rather than warehouses and loose piles. Ships changed shape, schedules tightened, and inland distribution centers grew near rail and highway connections. The standard did not merely improve an old box; it reorganized the landscape through which the box moved.<br><br>This reorganization also shifted labor rather than simply eliminating it. Less work occurred inside a ship's hold, while more work appeared in crane operation, equipment maintenance, scheduling, tracking, and inland logistics. Some port districts lost jobs and activity because older piers lacked space for container yards. Other locations expanded rapidly. Describing the change only as faster loading therefore overlooks who absorbed its costs and where new kinds of work emerged.<br><br>Historians still debate how to assign credit. One entrepreneur may demonstrate a profitable route, an engineer may refine a corner fitting, and a standards committee may make the design transferable. Earlier experiments can look like complete inventions when viewed backward, even if they never crossed organizational boundaries. Conversely, a later promoter may receive too much credit because commercial success makes one version visible.<br><br>Information had to become transferable as well. A container identified differently by a port, railway, and carrier could be physically compatible yet operationally lost. Shared identification formats and records allowed participants to track the same unit across transfers. In that sense, standardized paperwork performed a task parallel to standardized corner fittings: each gave separate organizations a common point of contact without requiring them to become one organization.<br><br>The better question is not who first imagined a strong reusable box. It is how a collection of partial ideas became a dependable system. Containerization took hold when physical design, operating practice, and institutional agreement reinforced one another. The familiar metal rectangle is the system's most visible object, but its real achievement is less visible: a transfer can occur because many independent participants have agreed in advance about what will meet at the corners."},
94: {skill_tested: "Infer why small interface details have system-wide importance.", question: "Why does the third paragraph emphasize a projecting latch and a weak corner?", option1: "To show that cosmetic differences were the main obstacle to adoption", option2: "To prove that early containers could never be repaired", option3: "To explain why all containers had to carry identical goods", option4: "To illustrate how a small incompatibility can interrupt transfers across an otherwise connected system", correctAnswer: "To illustrate how a small incompatibility can interrupt transfers across an otherwise connected system", explanation: "Step 1: Standard dimensions alone are insufficient if lifting and stacking interfaces do not fit or endure.<br><br>Step 2: Each transfer depends on predictable contact between a box and different machines.<br><br>Step 3: A small defect at that contact point can prevent the larger sequence from functioning.<br><br>Answer: To illustrate how a small incompatibility can interrupt transfers across an otherwise connected system", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>HUMANITIES:</b> This passage examines how standardization transformed the reusable freight container.<br><br>The history of container shipping is often told as the story of replacing loose cargo with a large metal box. That summary gives the box too much credit. Merchants had packed goods into reusable chests, barrels, detachable railway bodies, and truck-sized boxes long before container ships crossed oceans. The decisive innovation was not enclosure alone. It was an agreement about how boxes, cranes, ships, trains, ports, and paperwork would fit together.<br><br>Before widespread containerization, a shipment might arrive at a dock in sacks, crates, and cartons. Workers sorted the pieces, carried them into a warehouse, and loaded them again according to a ship's plan. At the destination, the process ran in reverse. The labor was skilled, but repeated handling consumed time and exposed goods to damage and loss. Early transport boxes reduced some handling, especially on routes controlled by one railway or shipping company. Their usefulness often ended at the boundary of that company's system.<br><br>A box that could travel broadly needed standardized interfaces. Its corner fittings had to meet lifting equipment at predictable points. Its dimensions had to suit ship cells, railway clearances, road rules, and stacking loads. A door latch that projected a few centimeters too far or a corner too weak for repeated lifting could disrupt the sequence. Standardization therefore concerned not only overall length and width but also the small places where one machine transferred responsibility to another.<br><br>Agreement was difficult because participants had already invested in different equipment. A port with narrow cranes favored one size; a railway favored another; a trucking company wanted maximum road capacity. Converting too early to a losing design could make expensive machinery useless. Converting too late could isolate a company from growing routes. Governments, manufacturers, carriers, insurers, and port authorities negotiated technical rules while also protecting economic interests.<br><br>Once enough participants accepted compatible designs, the value of each container increased. A box could leave a factory by truck, move by train, cross an ocean, and return to road transport without its contents being unpacked at every transfer. Ports redesigned yards around cranes and stacks rather than warehouses and loose piles. Ships changed shape, schedules tightened, and inland distribution centers grew near rail and highway connections. The standard did not merely improve an old box; it reorganized the landscape through which the box moved.<br><br>This reorganization also shifted labor rather than simply eliminating it. Less work occurred inside a ship's hold, while more work appeared in crane operation, equipment maintenance, scheduling, tracking, and inland logistics. Some port districts lost jobs and activity because older piers lacked space for container yards. Other locations expanded rapidly. Describing the change only as faster loading therefore overlooks who absorbed its costs and where new kinds of work emerged.<br><br>Historians still debate how to assign credit. One entrepreneur may demonstrate a profitable route, an engineer may refine a corner fitting, and a standards committee may make the design transferable. Earlier experiments can look like complete inventions when viewed backward, even if they never crossed organizational boundaries. Conversely, a later promoter may receive too much credit because commercial success makes one version visible.<br><br>Information had to become transferable as well. A container identified differently by a port, railway, and carrier could be physically compatible yet operationally lost. Shared identification formats and records allowed participants to track the same unit across transfers. In that sense, standardized paperwork performed a task parallel to standardized corner fittings: each gave separate organizations a common point of contact without requiring them to become one organization.<br><br>The better question is not who first imagined a strong reusable box. It is how a collection of partial ideas became a dependable system. Containerization took hold when physical design, operating practice, and institutional agreement reinforced one another. The familiar metal rectangle is the system's most visible object, but its real achievement is less visible: a transfer can occur because many independent participants have agreed in advance about what will meet at the corners."},
95: {skill_tested: "Explain how adoption incentives change as participation increases.", question: "The fifth paragraph most strongly suggests that compatible containers became more valuable as adoption spread because:", option1: "each box could move through more transport modes and locations without being unpacked.", option2: "standards required every participant to use the same business organization.", option3: "ports no longer needed cranes, schedules, or inland connections.", option4: "goods could be shipped without records or identification.", correctAnswer: "each box could move through more transport modes and locations without being unpacked.", explanation: "Step 1: Compatibility allows one box to transfer among truck, train, ship, and road transport.<br><br>Step 2: Each additional compatible participant or route expands where the same box can travel.<br><br>Step 3: Adoption therefore creates a network effect rather than merely improving one isolated box.<br><br>Answer: each box could move through more transport modes and locations without being unpacked.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>HUMANITIES:</b> This passage examines how standardization transformed the reusable freight container.<br><br>The history of container shipping is often told as the story of replacing loose cargo with a large metal box. That summary gives the box too much credit. Merchants had packed goods into reusable chests, barrels, detachable railway bodies, and truck-sized boxes long before container ships crossed oceans. The decisive innovation was not enclosure alone. It was an agreement about how boxes, cranes, ships, trains, ports, and paperwork would fit together.<br><br>Before widespread containerization, a shipment might arrive at a dock in sacks, crates, and cartons. Workers sorted the pieces, carried them into a warehouse, and loaded them again according to a ship's plan. At the destination, the process r