116: {skill_tested: "Synthesize evidence and qualification.", question: "Which finding would best support the passage's conclusion without exceeding its limits?", option1: "One visitor says every person prefers the same bench.", option2: "Different seating arrangements repeatedly attract different uses, though interviews reveal varied personal reasons.", option3: "A planner claims a new bench guarantees friendship.", option4: "No one ever alters a movable chair.", correctAnswer: "Different seating arrangements repeatedly attract different uses, though interviews reveal varied personal reasons.", explanation: "Step 1: The answer combines stable behavioral patterns with diverse motives.<br><br>Answer: Different seating arrangements repeatedly attract different uses, though interviews reveal varied personal reasons.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how the design and placement of public benches influence the ways people use shared space.<br><br>A bench appears to be a simple invitation: sit here. Yet researchers who observe plazas and parks find that the invitation contains several quieter instructions. A seat facing a path offers a view of passing activity. One turned toward another bench creates the possibility of conversation. A bench divided by armrests marks individual territories. None of these features forces a particular behavior, but each changes which actions feel convenient, exposed, or polite.<br><br>To study those effects, a research team observed one downtown plaza for six weeks. The plaza contained movable chairs, fixed benches along a fountain, and a low stone wall not officially intended as seating. Observers recorded where people sat, how long they remained, whether they arrived alone, and whether they spoke with strangers. They did not record names or conversations. Their goal was to identify patterns in spatial choices, not to interpret private motives.<br><br>The fixed benches were used steadily at lunchtime, but people seated at opposite ends rarely spoke. Their shared orientation toward the fountain allowed them to occupy the same bench without repeatedly meeting each other's eyes. The movable chairs produced a different pattern. Friends turned them into small circles, while solitary visitors often angled a chair partly toward the walkway and partly toward an open area. That diagonal position provided both a view and a boundary.<br><br>The stone wall complicated the team's initial categories. Although it lacked backs and was cold in the morning, it became the plaza's most common waiting place. Bus riders could see approaching vehicles from it, and its long surface allowed them to adjust their distance from others. A feature designed as an edge therefore supported a use that the official seating did not serve as well.<br><br>Midway through the study, maintenance workers temporarily moved several chairs beside the fountain. Stranger conversations increased there during the next three days. It would be tempting to credit proximity alone, but the weather was also unusually warm, a street musician performed nearby, and the fountain had just reopened after repairs. The researchers treated the change as a clue rather than proof.<br><br>They later tested one factor more carefully by placing pairs of chairs at different angles before the plaza opened. Chairs facing each other were often moved by people who arrived alone. Chairs set at roughly a right angle were moved less often and supported both conversation and independent watching. The finding suggested that flexible arrangements succeed partly because users can negotiate how much attention they offer one another.<br><br>Critics of observational design studies note that visible behavior cannot reveal every reason for a choice. A person may avoid a bench because of sunlight, a painful knee, a remembered encounter, or an appointment elsewhere. Researchers can compare repeated patterns and alter physical conditions, but they should not confuse a strong pattern with a complete explanation.<br><br>For planners, that limitation is useful rather than defeating. It discourages the claim that one ideal bench will manufacture community. A better aim is to provide varied opportunities: some seats for watching, some for talking, some near routes, and some slightly apart. Public furniture works not when it dictates social life, but when it gives different forms of social life somewhere to occur.<br><br>The team also compared weekday and weekend use. Office workers favored seats with quick access to sidewalks, while weekend visitors stayed longer near shaded edges. The same bench could therefore support rapid meals on Tuesday and extended conversation on Saturday. Use reflected the interaction of design with schedules rather than a permanent identity attached to the object.<br><br>The researchers recommended testing changes incrementally. A chair moved for a week can reveal more than a costly redesign defended by assumption. Observation, adjustment, and further observation make the plaza an ongoing inquiry rather than a finished diagram. This process remains deliberately provisional."},
117: {skill_tested: "Evaluate final reasoning.", question: "Why does the author call the study's limitation useful?", option1: "It proves public furniture has no effects.", option2: "It allows researchers to stop collecting evidence.", option3: "It redirects planners from controlling behavior toward supporting multiple choices.", option4: "It ensures every plaza will look identical.", correctAnswer: "It redirects planners from controlling behavior toward supporting multiple choices.", explanation: "Step 1: Acknowledged uncertainty leads to a plural, opportunity-based design goal.<br><br>Answer: It redirects planners from controlling behavior toward supporting multiple choices.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how the design and placement of public benches influence the ways people use shared space.<br><br>A bench appears to be a simple invitation: sit here. Yet researchers who observe plazas and parks find that the invitation contains several quieter instructions. A seat facing a path offers a view of passing activity. One turned toward another bench creates the possibility of conversation. A bench divided by armrests marks individual territories. None of these features forces a particular behavior, but each changes which actions feel convenient, exposed, or polite.<br><br>To study those effects, a research team observed one downtown plaza for six weeks. The plaza contained movable chairs, fixed benches along a fountain, and a low stone wall not officially intended as seating. Observers recorded where people sat, how long they remained, whether they arrived alone, and whether they spoke with strangers. They did not record names or conversations. Their goal was to identify patterns in spatial choices, not to interpret private motives.<br><br>The fixed benches were used steadily at lunchtime, but people seated at opposite ends rarely spoke. Their shared orientation toward the fountain allowed them to occupy the same bench without repeatedly meeting each other's eyes. The movable chairs produced a different pattern. Friends turned them into small circles, while solitary visitors often angled a chair partly toward the walkway and partly toward an open area. That diagonal position provided both a view and a boundary.<br><br>The stone wall complicated the team's initial categories. Although it lacked backs and was cold in the morning, it became the plaza's most common waiting place. Bus riders could see approaching vehicles from it, and its long surface allowed them to adjust their distance from others. A feature designed as an edge therefore supported a use that the official seating did not serve as well.<br><br>Midway through the study, maintenance workers temporarily moved several chairs beside the fountain. Stranger conversations increased there during the next three days. It would be tempting to credit proximity alone, but the weather was also unusually warm, a street musician performed nearby, and the fountain had just reopened after repairs. The researchers treated the change as a clue rather than proof.<br><br>They later tested one factor more carefully by placing pairs of chairs at different angles before the plaza opened. Chairs facing each other were often moved by people who arrived alone. Chairs set at roughly a right angle were moved less often and supported both conversation and independent watching. The finding suggested that flexible arrangements succeed partly because users can negotiate how much attention they offer one another.<br><br>Critics of observational design studies note that visible behavior cannot reveal every reason for a choice. A person may avoid a bench because of sunlight, a painful knee, a remembered encounter, or an appointment elsewhere. Researchers can compare repeated patterns and alter physical conditions, but they should not confuse a strong pattern with a complete explanation.<br><br>For planners, that limitation is useful rather than defeating. It discourages the claim that one ideal bench will manufacture community. A better aim is to provide varied opportunities: some seats for watching, some for talking, some near routes, and some slightly apart. Public furniture works not when it dictates social life, but when it gives different forms of social life somewhere to occur.<br><br>The team also compared weekday and weekend use. Office workers favored seats with quick access to sidewalks, while weekend visitors stayed longer near shaded edges. The same bench could therefore support rapid meals on Tuesday and extended conversation on Saturday. Use reflected the interaction of design with schedules rather than a permanent identity attached to the object.<br><br>The researchers recommended testing changes incrementally. A chair moved for a week can reveal more than a costly redesign defended by assumption. Observation, adjustment, and further observation make the plaza an ongoing inquiry rather than a finished diagram. This process remains deliberately provisional."},
118: {skill_tested: "Identify a physiological tradeoff.", question: "What is one disadvantage of a cool roost?", option1: "It prevents metabolism from slowing.", option2: "It always causes dangerous water loss.", option3: "It attracts more insects into the box.", option4: "A torpid bat may need more time to warm its muscles before flight.", correctAnswer: "A torpid bat may need more time to warm its muscles before flight.", explanation: "Step 1: Cool conditions save resting energy but lengthen reheating.<br><br>Answer: A torpid bat may need more time to warm its muscles before flight.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage describes research on how small bats choose among roosts with different temperatures.<br><br>For a small bat, rest is not biologically inactive. Even while hanging still, the animal must balance energy conservation against the need to become alert quickly. A cool shelter can reduce the energy required to maintain body temperature if the bat enters torpor, a controlled state in which metabolism slows. Yet leaving torpor requires reheating muscles before flight. A warmer shelter costs more energy during rest but may shorten that delay.<br><br>Researchers studying canyon bats suspected that roost choice would change with immediate conditions rather than follow a fixed preference. They installed wooden roost boxes on the same cliff face. Dark outer panels made some boxes warm rapidly after sunrise; reflective panels kept others cooler. Sensors recorded air and wall temperatures, and harmless tags detected when individual bats entered or departed.<br><br>During mild nights, many bats selected cooler boxes before dawn. These animals remained inside well into the morning and showed body temperatures consistent with torpor. The pattern fit an energy-saving strategy: when no early activity was necessary, a cool roost allowed metabolism to remain low.<br><br>The pattern shifted after nights when researchers played brief recordings of hunting owls near the cliff. The recordings ended hours before sunrise and no predator was present at the boxes. Nevertheless, tagged bats more often entered warmer roosts. They also departed sooner when a second owl recording was played after dawn. Researchers proposed that a warmer body reduced the time required to begin controlled flight.<br><br>That interpretation required caution. The owl recordings might have changed where insects gathered, or they might have caused bats to avoid a part of the cliff rather than select temperature itself. To separate location from warmth, the team rotated dark and reflective panels among boxes every few days. Preference followed the thermal treatment more closely than box position, weakening the simple location explanation.<br><br>A second experiment added heating pads to several cool-looking boxes while leaving their exterior panels unchanged. Bats used the secretly warmed boxes at rates similar to naturally warm ones after owl recordings. This result indicated that internal temperature, not visible panel color, influenced the choice. It did not prove that rapid escape was the only benefit, but it connected the behavior more directly to thermal conditions.<br><br>Adult females carrying young did not follow the general pattern as strongly. They favored warmer boxes even without predator cues. Developing young cannot regulate temperature as effectively as adults, and repeated cooling could slow growth. The needs of offspring therefore appeared to narrow the range of acceptable choices.<br><br>Weather added another constraint. On very hot afternoons, the darkest boxes reached temperatures that could cause dangerous water loss. Bats abandoned them regardless of earlier owl recordings. A feature beneficial under one condition became costly when pushed beyond a safe range.<br><br>The study does not reduce roost choice to a single calculation. Temperature interacts with predators, reproduction, weather, and the time at which flight may be needed. What appears to be inconsistency may instead be flexible decision-making: bats shift among shelters because the biological value of warmth changes from one night to the next.<br><br>Individual history mattered as well. Bats that had occupied one box repeatedly were somewhat more likely to return to it, even when another box offered a slightly more favorable temperature. Familiarity may reduce the time spent inspecting crevices or the risk of entering an unsafe shelter. When temperature differences became large, however, thermal conditions outweighed that tendency.<br><br>The researchers did not treat box choice as identical to natural roost choice. Rock cracks vary in depth, humidity, airflow, and access routes in ways that wooden boxes cannot reproduce. The boxes allowed controlled comparisons, but observations in natural cracks were still needed to test whether the same tradeoffs operated there. Repeated seasons would also show whether individual bats preserve these preferences as their reproductive condition and experience change."},
119: {skill_tested: "Identify experimental design.", question: "Why did researchers rotate panels among boxes?", option1: "To separate temperature effects from preferences for particular locations.", option2: "To make every box the same color.", option3: "To measure the bats' body mass.", option4: "To keep owls permanently near one box.", correctAnswer: "To separate temperature effects from preferences for particular locations.", explanation: "Step 1: Moving treatments tests whether behavior tracks warmth rather than site.<br><br>Answer: To separate temperature effects from preferences for particular locations.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage describes research on how small bats choose among roosts with different temperatures.<br><br>For a small bat, rest is not biologically inactive. Even while hanging still, the animal must balance energy conservation against the need to become alert quickly. A cool shelter can reduce the energy required to maintain body temperature if the bat enters torpor, a controlled state in which metabolism slows. Yet leaving torpor requires reheating muscles before flight. A warmer shelter costs more energy during rest but may shorten that delay.<br><br>Researchers studying canyon bats suspected that roost choice would change with immediate conditions rather than follow a fixed preference. They installed wooden roost boxes on the same cliff face. Dark outer panels made some boxes warm rapidly after sunrise; reflective panels kept others cooler. Sensors recorded air and wall temperatures, and harmless tags detected when individual bats entered or departed.<br><br>During mild nights, many bats selected cooler boxes before dawn. These animals remained inside well into the morning and showed body temperatures consistent with torpor. The pattern fit an energy-saving strategy: when no early activity was necessary, a cool roost allowed metabolism to remain low.<br><br>The pattern shifted after nights when researchers played brief recordings of hunting owls near the cliff. The recordings ended hours before sunrise and no predator was present at the boxes. Nevertheless, tagged bats more often entered warmer roosts. They also departed sooner when a second owl recording was played after dawn. Researchers proposed that a warmer body reduced the time required to begin controlled flight.<br><br>That interpretation required caution. The owl recordings might have changed where insects gathered, or they might have caused bats to avoid a part of the cliff rather than select temperature itself. To separate location from warmth, the team rotated dark and reflective panels among boxes every few days. Preference followed the thermal treatment more closely than box position, weakening the simple location explanation.<br><br>A second experiment added heating pads to several cool-looking boxes while leaving their exterior panels unchanged. Bats used the secretly warmed boxes at rates similar to naturally warm ones after owl recordings. This result indicated that internal temperature, not visible panel color, influenced the choice. It did not prove that rapid escape was the only benefit, but it connected the behavior more directly to thermal conditions.<br><br>Adult females carrying young did not follow the general pattern as strongly. They favored warmer boxes even without predator cues. Developing young cannot regulate temperature as effectively as adults, and repeated cooling could slow growth. The needs of offspring therefore appeared to narrow the range of acceptable choices.<br><br>Weather added another constraint. On very hot afternoons, the darkest boxes reached temperatures that could cause dangerous water loss. Bats abandoned them regardless of earlier owl recordings. A feature beneficial under one condition became costly when pushed beyond a safe range.<br><br>The study does not reduce roost choice to a single calculation. Temperature interacts with predators, reproduction, weather, and the time at which flight may be needed. What appears to be inconsistency may instead be flexible decision-making: bats shift among shelters because the biological value of warmth changes from one night to the next.<br><br>Individual history mattered as well. Bats that had occupied one box repeatedly were somewhat more likely to return to it, even when another box offered a slightly more favorable temperature. Familiarity may reduce the time spent inspecting crevices or the risk of entering an unsafe shelter. When temperature differences became large, however, thermal conditions outweighed that tendency.<br><br>The researchers did not treat box choice as identical to natural roost choice. Rock cracks vary in depth, humidity, airflow, and access routes in ways that wooden boxes cannot reproduce. The boxes allowed controlled comparisons, but observations in natural cracks were still needed to test whether the same tradeoffs operated there. Repeated seasons would also show whether individual bats preserve these preferences as their reproductive condition and experience change."},
120: {skill_tested: "State the main idea.", question: "Which statement best summarizes the passage?", option1: "All bats consistently choose the coolest shelter.", option2: "Bats adjust roost choices as the benefits and costs of warmth change with conditions.", option3: "Panel color alone determines every roost choice.", option4: "Predator sounds cause bats to stop using roosts.", correctAnswer: "Bats adjust roost choices as the benefits and costs of warmth change with conditions.", explanation: "Step 1: The findings show flexible tradeoffs across risk, offspring, and weather.<br><br>Answer: Bats adjust roost choices as the benefits and costs of warmth change with conditions.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage describes research on how small bats choose among roosts with different temperatures.<br><br>For a small bat, rest is not biologically inactive. Even while hanging still, the animal must balance energy conservation against the need to become alert quickly. A cool shelter can reduce the energy required to maintain body temperature if the bat enters torpor, a controlled state in which metabolism slows. Yet leaving torpor requires reheating muscles before flight. A warmer shelter costs more energy during rest but may shorten that delay.<br><br>Researchers studying canyon bats suspected that roost choice would change with immediate conditions rather than follow a fixed preference. They installed wooden roost boxes on the same cliff face. Dark outer panels made some boxes warm rapidly after sunrise; reflective panels kept others cooler. Sensors recorded air and wall temperatures, and harmless tags detected when individual bats entered or departed.<br><br>During mild nights, many bats selected cooler boxes before dawn. These animals remained inside well into the morning and showed body temperatures consistent with torpor. The pattern fit an energy-saving strategy: when no early activity was necessary, a cool roost allowed metabolism to remain low.<br><br>The pattern shifted after nights when researchers played brief recordings of hunting owls near the cliff. The recordings ended hours before sunrise and no predator was present at the boxes. Nevertheless, tagged bats more often entered warmer roosts. They also departed sooner when a second owl recording was played after dawn. Researchers proposed that a warmer body reduced the time required to begin controlled flight.<br><br>That interpretation required caution. The owl recordings might have changed where insects gathered, or they might have caused bats to avoid a part of the cliff rather than select temperature itself. To separate location from warmth, the team rotated dark and reflective panels among boxes every few days. Preference followed the thermal treatment more closely than box position, weakening the simple location explanation.<br><br>A second experiment added heating pads to several cool-looking boxes while leaving their exterior panels unchanged. Bats used the secretly warmed boxes at rates similar to naturally warm ones after owl recordings. This result indicated that internal temperature, not visible panel color, influenced the choice. It did not prove that rapid escape was the only benefit, but it connected the behavior more directly to thermal conditions.<br><br>Adult females carrying young did not follow the general pattern as strongly. They favored warmer boxes even without predator cues. Developing young cannot regulate temperature as effectively as adults, and repeated cooling could slow growth. The needs of offspring therefore appeared to narrow the range of acceptable choices.<br><br>Weather added another constraint. On very hot afternoons, the darkest boxes reached temperatures that could cause dangerous water loss. Bats abandoned them regardless of earlier owl recordings. A feature beneficial under one condition became costly when pushed beyond a safe range.<br><br>The study does not reduce roost choice to a single calculation. Temperature interacts with predators, reproduction, weather, and the time at which flight may be needed. What appears to be inconsistency may instead be flexible decision-making: bats shift among shelters because the biological value of warmth changes from one night to the next.<br><br>Individual history mattered as well. Bats that had occupied one box repeatedly were somewhat more likely to return to it, even when another box offered a slightly more favorable temperature. Familiarity may reduce the time spent inspecting crevices or the risk of entering an unsafe shelter. When temperature differences became large, however, thermal conditions outweighed that tendency.<br><br>The researchers did not treat box choice as identical to natural roost choice. Rock cracks vary in depth, humidity, airflow, and access routes in ways that wooden boxes cannot reproduce. The boxes allowed controlled comparisons, but observations in natural cracks were still needed to test whether the same tradeoffs operated there. Repeated seasons would also show whether individual bats preserve these preferences as their reproductive condition and experience change."},
