uce roost choice to a single calculation. Temperature interacts with predators, reproduction, weather, and the time at which flight may be needed. What appears to be inconsistency may instead be flexible decision-making: bats shift among shelters because the biological value of warmth changes from one night to the next.<br><br>Individual history mattered as well. Bats that had occupied one box repeatedly were somewhat more likely to return to it, even when another box offered a slightly more favorable temperature. Familiarity may reduce the time spent inspecting crevices or the risk of entering an unsafe shelter. When temperature differences became large, however, thermal conditions outweighed that tendency.<br><br>The researchers did not treat box choice as identical to natural roost choice. Rock cracks vary in depth, humidity, airflow, and access routes in ways that wooden boxes cannot reproduce. The boxes allowed controlled comparisons, but observations in natural cracks were still needed to test whether the same tradeoffs operated there. Repeated seasons would also show whether individual bats preserve these preferences as their reproductive condition and experience change."},
