cts with predators, reproduction, weather, and the time at which flight may be needed. What appears to be inconsistency may instead be flexible decision-making: bats shift among shelters because the biological value of warmth changes from one night to the next.<br><br>Individual history mattered as well. Bats that had occupied one box repeatedly were somewhat more likely to return to it, even when another box offered a slightly more favorable temperature. Familiarity may reduce the time spent inspecting crevices or the risk of entering an unsafe shelter. When temperature differences became large, however, thermal conditions outweighed that tendency.<br><br>The researchers did not treat box choice as identical to natural roost choice. Rock cracks vary in depth, humidity, airflow, and access routes in ways that wooden boxes cannot reproduce. The boxes allowed controlled comparisons, but observations in natural cracks were still needed to test whether the same tradeoffs operated there. Repeated seasons would also show whether individual bats preserve these preferences as their reproductive condition and experience change."},
125: {skill_tested: "Evaluate alternatives.", question: "Which result most directly weakens the claim that bats merely avoided one cliff location?", option1: "Sensors recorded wall temperature.", option2: "Some bats stayed until morning.", option3: "Preferences changed when thermal panels were rotated among locations.", option4: "Females often used warm boxes.", correctAnswer: "Preferences changed when thermal panels were rotated among locations.", explanation: "Step 1: Tracking treatment across places distinguishes warmth from fixed-location avoidance.<br><br>Answer: Preferences changed when thermal panels were rotated among locations.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage describes research on how small bats choose among roosts with different temperatures.<br><br>For a small bat, rest is not biologically inactive. Even while hanging still, the animal must balance energy conservation against the need to become alert quickly. A cool shelter can reduce the energy required to maintain body temperature if the bat enters torpor, a controlled state in which metabolism slows. Yet leaving torpor requires reheating muscles before flight. A warmer shelter costs more energy during rest but may shorten that delay.<br><br>Researchers studying canyon bats suspected that roost choice would change with immediate conditions rather than follow a fixed preference. They installed wooden roost boxes on the same cliff face. Dark outer panels made some boxes warm rapidly after sunrise; reflective panels kept others cooler. Sensors recorded air and wall temperatures, and harmless tags detected when individual bats entered or departed.<br><br>During mild nights, many bats selected cooler boxes before dawn. These animals remained inside well into the morning and showed body temperatures consistent with torpor. The pattern fit an energy-saving strategy: when no early activity wa