a single calculation. Temperature interacts with predators, reproduction, weather, and the time at which flight may be needed. What appears to be inconsistency may instead be flexible decision-making: bats shift among shelters because the biological value of warmth changes from one night to the next.<br><br>Individual history mattered as well. Bats that had occupied one box repeatedly were somewhat more likely to return to it, even when another box offered a slightly more favorable temperature. Familiarity may reduce the time spent inspecting crevices or the risk of entering an unsafe shelter. When temperature differences became large, however, thermal conditions outweighed that tendency.<br><br>The researchers did not treat box choice as identical to natural roost choice. Rock cracks vary in depth, humidity, airflow, and access routes in ways that wooden boxes cannot reproduce. The boxes allowed controlled comparisons, but observations in natural cracks were still needed to test whether the same tradeoffs operated there. Repeated seasons would also show whether individual bats preserve these preferences as their reproductive condition and experience change."},
127: {skill_tested: "Identify a navigation cue.", question: "What information do polarized-light patterns provide?", option1: "Direction.", option2: "The chemical identity of food.", option3: "The exact width of the nest.", option4: "The age of a landmark.", correctAnswer: "Direction.", explanation: "Step 1: The passage distinguishes celestial direction from step-based distance.<br><br>Answer: Direction.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how desert ants maintain a homeward direction when the sky and landscape provide imperfect guidance.<br><br>A foraging desert ant may travel hundreds of body lengths across nearly featureless ground and still return to a nest entrance scarcely wider than its body. It cannot follow a scent trail that survives reliably on hot, shifting sand. Instead, the ant continually estimates the direction and distance of its outbound path, then uses that accumulated estimate to calculate a route home.<br><br>Direction is partly derived from patterns of polarized light in the sky. Human eyes do not ordinarily perceive these patterns, but specialized receptors in an ant's eyes respond to them. The pattern remains informative even when the sun itself is hidden by thin cloud. Distance is estimated through signals related to walking, including the number and length of steps.<br><br>Neither estimate is perfect. A gust can push an ant sideways, uneven ground can change its stride, and the celestial pattern shifts as time passes. Near the expected nest position, ants therefore stop following a straight line and begin a widening search. The shape of that search gives researchers a visible record of where the ant believes home should be.<br><br>In one experiment, scientists trained ants to walk through a channel from their nest to a feeder. After an ant reached the food, they moved it to a flat testing field. Most ants ran the distance and direction predicted by the training route, then began searching. This showed that the insects carried an internal estimate rather than relying only on landmarks encountered during return.<br><br>The researchers next placed a filter above part of the training channel. The filter rotated the apparent direction of polarized light. Ants leaving the nest initially walked according to the altered sky cue, but their paths gradually became less deflected on repeated trips. When later tested without the filter, experienced ants briefly erred in the opposite direction. The reversal suggested that they had adjusted, or calibrated, the sky signal against other information during training.<br><br>What supplied that other information? The channel walls limited the view of distant landmarks, but the ants could still sense the direction of gravity while walking on a slightly sloped floor. When researchers reversed the slope while keeping the light filter unchanged, adaptation slowed. The ants apparently treated the stable relationship among multiple cues as evidence about which signal required correction.<br><br>Calibration was not unlimited. A filter rotation of moderate size produced gradual adjustment, whereas an extreme rotation caused erratic paths and broad searches. The ants did not simply replace one compass reading with another. Large disagreement reduced confidence in the entire estimate.<br><br>Researchers also varied the time between training and testing. Recent calibration influenced the ants strongly, but its effect weakened after several days without exposure to the altered condition. Such fading may prevent a temporary local distortion from becoming a permanent error.<br><br>The ant's navigation is remarkable not because any single measurement is exact, but because uncertain measurements are compared and revised. The widening search near the nest is part of the same system, not a sign that the system has failed. A flexible navigator must know not only where to go, but when its estimate has become uncertain enough to justify looking around.<br><br>The width of the final search also changed predictably. Ants trained on smooth, short routes searched within a smaller area than ants that crossed irregular terrain or experienced conflicting filters. Researchers could therefore use search spread as an indirect measure of uncertainty, even though the ant offered no explicit report of confidence.<br><br>Experiments under overcast skies produced another contrast. When polarized patterns weakened, ants relied more heavily on a familiar panorama near the nest. Newly displaced ants without that view searched sooner. No cue was universally dominant; reliability depended on what the environment made available and what the ant had previously learned. This weighting lets the navigation system remain useful as visibility and terrain change."},
128: {skill_tested: "Identify method.", question: "Why were ants moved to a flat testing field?", option1: "To expose them to stronger scent trails.", option2: "To test their internal route estimate without retracing familiar landmarks.", option3: "To prevent them from beginning a search.", option4: "To change the length of their legs.", correctAnswer: "To test their internal route estimate without retracing familiar landmarks.", explanation: "Step 1: Displacement reveals stored path integration.<br><br>Answer: To test their internal route estimate without retracing familiar landmarks.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how desert ants maintain a homeward direction when the sky and landscape provide imperfect guidance.<br><br>A foraging desert ant may travel hundreds of body lengths across nearly featureless ground and still return to a nest entrance scarcely wider than its body. It cannot follow a scent trail that survives reliably on hot, shifting sand. Instead, the ant continually estimates the direction and distance of its outbound path, then uses that accumulated estimate to calculate a route home.<br><br>Direction is partly derived from patterns of polarized light in the sky. Human eyes do not ordinarily perceive these patterns, but specialized receptors in an ant's eyes respond to them. The pattern remains informative even when the sun itself is hidden by thin cloud. Distance is estimated through signals related to walking, including the number and length of steps.<br><br>Neither estimate is perfect. A gust can push an ant sideways, uneven ground can change its stride, and the celestial pattern shifts as time passes. Near the expected nest position, ants therefore stop following a straight line and begin a widening search. The shape of that search gives researchers a visible record of where the ant believes home should be.<br><br>In one experiment, scientists trained ants to walk through a channel from their nest to a feeder. After an ant reached the food, they moved it to a flat testing field. Most ants ran the distance and direction predicted by the training route, then began searching. This showed that the insects carried an internal estimate rather than relying only on landmarks encountered during return.<br><br>The researchers next placed a filter above part of the training channel. The filter rotated the apparent direction of polarized light. Ants leaving the nest initially walked according to the altered sky cue, but their paths gradually became less deflected on repeated trips. When later tested without the filter, experienced ants briefly erred in the opposite direction. The reversal suggested that they had adjusted, or calibrated, the sky signal against other information during training.<br><br>What supplied that other information? The channel walls limited the view of distant landmarks, but the ants could still sense the direction of gravity while walking on a slightly sloped floor. When researchers reversed the slope while keeping the light filter unchanged, adaptation slowed. The ants apparently treated the stable relationship among multiple cues as evidence about which signal required correction.<br><br>Calibration was not unlimited. A filter rotation of moderate size produced gradual adjustment, whereas an extreme rotation caused erratic paths and broad searches. The ants did not simply replace one compass reading with another. Large disagreement reduced confidence in the entire estimate.<br><br>Researchers also varied the time between training and testing. Recent calibration influenced the ants strongly, but its effect weakened after several days without exposure to the altered condition. Such fading may prevent a temporary local distortion from becoming a permanent error.<br><br>The ant's navigation is remarkable not because any single measurement is exact, but because uncertain measurements are compared and revised. The widening search near the nest is part of the same system, not a sign that the system has failed. A flexible navigator must know not only where to go, but when its estimate has become uncertain enough to justify looking around.<br><br>The width of the final search also changed predictably. Ants trained on smooth, short routes searched within a smaller area than ants that crossed irregular terrain or experienced conflicting filters. Researchers could therefore use search spread as an indirect measure of uncertainty, even though the ant offered no explicit report of confidence.<br><br>Experiments under overcast skies produced another contrast. When polarized patterns weakened, ants relied more heavily on a familiar panorama near the nest. Newly displaced ants without that view searched sooner. No cue was universally dominant; reliability depended on what the environment made available and what the ant had previously learned. This weighting lets the navigation system remain useful as visibility and terrain change."},
129: {skill_tested: "State the central idea.", question: "Which statement best summarizes the passage?", option1: "Ants follow a single fixed sky signal without correction.", option2: "Landmarks are the only reliable desert cue.", option3: "Ants navigate by comparing imperfect cues, recalibrating them, and searching when uncertainty remains.", option4: "Navigation ends whenever cues disagree.", correctAnswer: "Ants navigate by comparing imperfect cues, recalibrating them, and searching when uncertainty remains.", explanation: "Step 1: The passage emphasizes integration, adjustment, and uncertainty.<br><br>Answer: Ants navigate by comparing imperfect cues, recalibrating them, and searching when uncertainty remains.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how desert ants maintain a homeward direction when the sky and landscape provide imperfect guidance.<br><br>A foraging desert ant may travel hundreds of body lengths across nearly featureless ground and still return to a nest entrance scarcely wider than its body. It cannot follow a scent trail that survives reliably on hot, shifting sand. Instead, the ant continually estimates the direction and distance of its outbound path, then uses that accumulated estimate to calculate a route home.<br><br>Direction is partly derived from patterns of polarized light in the sky. Human eyes do not ordinarily perceive these patterns, but specialized receptors in an ant's eyes respond to them. The pattern remains informative even when the sun itself is hidden by thin cloud. Distance is estimated through signals related to walking, including the number and length of steps.<br><br>Neither estimate is perfect. A gust can push an ant sideways, uneven ground can change its stride, and the celestial pattern shifts as time passes. Near the expected nest position, ants therefore stop following a straight line and begin a widening search. The shape of that search gives researchers a visible record of where the ant believes home should be.<br><br>In one experiment, scientists trained ants to walk through a channel from their nest to a feeder. After an ant reached the food, they moved it to a flat testing field. Most ants ran the distance and direction predicted by the training route, then began searching. This showed that the insects carried an internal estimate rather than relying only on landmarks encountered during return.<br><br>The researchers next placed a filter above part of the training channel. The filter rotated the apparent direction of polarized light. Ants leaving the nest initially walked according to the altered sky cue, but their paths gradually became less deflected on repeated trips. When later tested without the filter, experienced ants briefly erred in the opposite direction. The reversal suggested that they had adjusted, or calibrated, the sky signal against other information during training.<br><br>What supplied that other information? The channel walls limited the view of distant landmarks, but the ants could still sense the direction of gravity while walking on a slightly sloped floor. When researchers reversed the slope while keeping the light filter unchanged, adaptation slowed. The ants apparently treated the stable relationship among multiple cues as evidence about which signal required correction.<br><br>Calibration was not unlimited. A filter rotation of moderate size produced gradual adjustment, whereas an extreme rotation caused erratic paths and broad searches. The ants did not simply replace one compass reading with another. Large disagreement reduced confidence in the entire estimate.<br><br>Researchers also varied the time between training and testing. Recent calibration influenced the ants strongly, but its effect weakened after several days without exposure to the altered condition. Such fading may prevent a temporary local distortion from becoming a permanent error.<br><br>The ant's navigation is remarkable not because any single measurement is exact, but because uncertain measurements are compared and revised. The widening search near the nest is part of the same system, not a sign that the system has failed. A flexible navigator must know not only where to go, but when its estimate has become uncertain enough to justify looking around.<br><br>The width of the final search also changed predictably. Ants trained on smooth, short routes searched within a smaller area than ants that crossed irregular terrain or experienced conflicting filters. Researchers could therefore use search spread as an indirect measure of uncertainty, even though the ant offered no explicit report of confidence.<br><br>Experiments under overcast skies produced another contrast. When polarized patterns weakened, ants relied more heavily on a familiar panorama near the nest. Newly displaced ants without that view searched sooner. No cue was universally dominant; reliability depended on what the environment made available and what the ant had previously learned. This weighting lets the navigation system remain useful as visibility and terrain change."},
130: {skill_tested: "Infer from reversed error.", question: "Why did experienced ants err in the opposite direction after the filter was removed?", option1: "They forgot the feeder's distance entirely.", option2: "The testing field contained the filter.", option3: "Their step length permanently doubled.", option4: "Their prior calibration continued briefly under normal sky conditions.", correctAnswer: "Their prior calibration continued briefly under normal sky conditions.", explanation: "Step 1: An adjustment useful under distortion creates a reversed error when distortion disappears.<br><br>Answer: Their prior calibration continued briefly under normal sky conditions.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how desert ants maintain a homeward direction when the sky and landscape provide imperfect guidance.<br><br>A foraging desert ant may travel hundreds of body lengths across nearly featureless ground and still return to a nest entrance scarcely wider than its body. It cannot follow a scent trail that survives reliably on hot, shifting sand. Instead, the ant continually estimates the direction and distance of its outbound path, then uses that accumulated estimate to calculate a route home.<br><br>Direction is partly derived from patterns of polarized light in the sky. Human eyes do not ordinarily perceive these patterns, but specialized receptors in an ant's eyes respond to them. The pattern remains informative even when the sun itself is hidden by thin cloud. Distance is estimated through signals related to walking, including the number and length of steps.<br><br>Neither estimate is perfect. A gust can push an ant sideways, uneven ground can change its stride, and the celestial pattern shifts as time passes. Near the expected nest position, ants therefore stop following a straight line and begin a widening search. The shape of that search gives researche