rkable not because any single measurement is exact, but because uncertain measurements are compared and revised. The widening search near the nest is part of the same system, not a sign that the system has failed. A flexible navigator must know not only where to go, but when its estimate has become uncertain enough to justify looking around.<br><br>The width of the final search also changed predictably. Ants trained on smooth, short routes searched within a smaller area than ants that crossed irregular terrain or experienced conflicting filters. Researchers could therefore use search spread as an indirect measure of uncertainty, even though the ant offered no explicit report of confidence.<br><br>Experiments under overcast skies produced another contrast. When polarized patterns weakened, ants relied more heavily on a familiar panorama near the nest. Newly displaced ants without that view searched sooner. No cue was universally dominant; reliability depended on what the environment made available and what the ant had previously learned. This weighting lets the navigation system remain useful as visibility and terrain change."},
131: {skill_tested: "Analyze a variable.", question: "The slope-reversal experiment was designed chiefly to test whether ants:", option1: "use a nonvisual directional cue when evaluating the sky signal.", option2: "prefer walking downhill to finding food.", option3: "count only steps taken on flat ground.", option4: "can see through channel walls.", correctAnswer: "use a nonvisual directional cue when evaluating the sky signal.", explanation: "Step 1: Gravity-related slope information was manipulated independently of light.<br><br>Answer: use a nonvisual directional cue when evaluating the sky signal.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how desert ants maintain a homeward direction when the sky and landscape provide imperfect guidance.<br><br>A foraging desert ant may travel hundreds of body lengths across nearly featureless ground and still return to a nest entrance scarcely wider than its body. It cannot follow a scent trail that survives reliably on hot, shifting sand. Instead, the ant continually estimates the direction and distance of its outbound path, then uses that accumulated estimate to calculate a route home.<br><br>Direction is partly derived from patterns of polarized light in the sky. Human eyes do not ordinarily perceive these patterns, but specialized receptors in an ant's eyes respond to them. The pattern remains informative even when the sun itself is hidden by thin cloud. Distance is estimated through signals related to walking, including the number and length of steps.<br><br>Neither estimate is perfect. A gust can push an ant sideways, uneven ground can change its stride, and the celestial pattern shifts as time passes. Near the expected nest position, ants therefore stop following a straight line and begin a widening search. The shape of that search gives researchers a visible record of where the ant believes home should be.<br><br>In one experiment, scientists trained ants to walk through a channel from their nest to a feeder. After an ant reached the food, they moved it to a flat testing field. Most ants ran the distance and direction predicted by the training route, then began searching. This showed that the insects carried an internal estimate rather than relying only on landmarks encountered during return.<br><br>The researchers next placed a filter above part of the training channel. The filter rotated the apparent direction of polarized light. Ants leaving the nest initially walked according to the altered sky cue, but their paths gradually became less deflected on repeated trips. When later tested without the filter, experienced ants briefly erred in the opposite direction. The reversal suggested that they had adjusted, or calibrated, the sky signal against other information during training.<br><br>What supplied that other information? The channel walls limited the view of distant landmarks, but the ants could still sense the direction of gravity while walking on a slightly sloped floor. When researchers reversed the slope while keeping the light filter unchanged, adaptation slowed. The ants apparently treated the stable relationship among multiple cues as evidence about which signal required correction.<br><br>Calibration was not unlimited. A filter rotation of moderate size produced gradual adjustment, whereas an extreme rotation caused erratic paths and broad searches. The ants did not simply replace one compass reading with another. Large disagreement reduced confidence in the entire estimate.<br><br>Researchers also varied the time between training and testing. Recent calibration influenced the ants strongly, but its effect weakened after several days without exposure to the altered condition. Such fading may prevent a temporary local distortion from becoming a permanent error.<br><br>The ant's navigation is remarkable not because any single measurement is exact, but because uncertain measurements are compared and revised. The widening search near the nest is part of the same system, not a sign that the system has failed. A flexible navigator must know not only where to go, but when its estimate has become uncertain enough to justify looking around.<br><br>The width of the final search also changed predictably. Ants trained on smooth, short routes searched within a smaller area than ants that crossed irregular terrain or experienced conflicting filters. Researchers could therefore use search spread as an indirect measure of uncertainty, even though the ant offered no explicit report of confidence.<br><br>Experiments under overcast skies produced another contrast. When polarized patterns weakened, ants relied more heavily on a familiar panorama near the nest. Newly displaced ants without that view searched sooner. No cue was universally dominant; reliability depended on what the environment made available and what the ant had previously learned. This weighting lets the navigation system remain useful as visibility and terrain change."},
132: {skill_tested: "Interpret extreme conflict.", question: "Broad searches after extreme filter rotation suggest that ants:", option1: "always accept the newest cue as correct.", option2: "lower confidence when available cues disagree too sharply.", option3: "become unable to walk after seeing polarized light.", option4: "estimate distance only from clouds.", correctAnswer: "lower confidence when available cues disagree too sharply.", explanation: "Step 1: Extreme disagreement disrupts the combined estimate rather than forcing a simple switch.<br><br>Answer: lower confidence when available cues disagree too sharply.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how desert ants maintain a homeward direction when the sky and landscape provide imperfect guidance.<br><br>A foraging desert ant may travel hundreds of body lengths across nearly featureless ground and still return to a nest entrance scarcely wider than its body. It cannot follow a scent trail that survives reliably on hot, shifting sand. Instead, the ant continually estimates the direction and distance of its outbound path, then uses that accumulated estimate to calculate a route home.<br><br>Direction is partly derived from patterns of polarized light in the sky. Human eyes do not ordinarily perceive these patterns, but specialized receptors in an ant's eyes respond to them. The pattern remains informative even when the sun itself is hidden by thin cloud. Distance is estimated through signals related to walking, including the number and length of steps.<br><br>Neither estimate is perfect. A gust can push an ant sideways, uneven ground can change its stride, and the celestial pattern shifts as time passes. Near the expected nest position, ants therefore stop following a straight line and begin a widening search. The shape of that search gives researchers a visible record of where the ant believes home should be.<br><br>In one experiment, scientists trained ants to walk through a channel from their nest to a feeder. After an ant reached the food, they moved it to a flat testing field. Most ants ran the distance and direction predicted by the training route, then began searching. This showed that the insects carried an internal estimate rather than relying only on landmarks encountered during return.<br><br>The researchers next placed a filter above part of the training channel. The filter rotated the apparent direction of polarized light. Ants leaving the nest initially walked according to the altered sky cue, but their paths gradually became less deflected on repeated trips. When later tested without the filter, experienced ants briefly erred in the opposite direction. The reversal suggested that they had adjusted, or calibrated, the sky signal against other information during training.<br><br>What supplied that other information? The channel walls limited the view of distant landmarks, but the ants could still sense the direction of gravity while walking on a slightly sloped floor. When researchers reversed the slope while keeping the light filter unchanged, adaptation slowed. The ants apparently treated the stable relationship among multiple cues as evidence about which signal required correction.<br><br>Calibration was not unlimited. A filter rotation of moderate size produced gradual adjustment, whereas an extreme rotation caused erratic paths and broad searches. The ants did not simply replace one compass reading with another. Large disagreement reduced confidence in the entire estimate.<br><br>Researchers also varied the time between training and testing. Recent calibration influenced the ants strongly, but its effect weakened after several days without exposure to the altered condition. Such fading may prevent a temporary local distortion from becoming a permanent error.<br><br>The ant's navigation is remarkable not because any single measurement is exact, but because uncertain measurements are compared and revised. The widening search near the nest is part of the same system, not a sign that the system has failed. A flexible navigator must know not only where to go, but when its estimate has become uncertain enough to justify looking around.<br><br>The width of the final search also changed predictably. Ants trained on smooth, short routes searched within a smaller area than ants that crossed irregular terrain or experienced conflicting filters. Researchers could therefore use search spread as an indirect measure of uncertainty, even though the ant offered no explicit report of confidence.<br><br>Experiments under overcast skies produced another contrast. When polarized patterns weakened, ants relied more heavily on a familiar panorama near the nest. Newly displaced ants without that view searched sooner. No cue was universally dominant; reliability depended on what the environment made available and what the ant had previously learned. This weighting lets the navigation system remain useful as visibility and terrain change."},
133: {skill_tested: "Explain fading calibration.", question: "What possible benefit does fading calibration provide?", option1: "It makes every past route equally strong.", option2: "It removes the need to compare cues.", option3: "It prevents a temporary distortion from controlling navigation indefinitely.", option4: "It causes ants to retain extreme errors.", correctAnswer: "It prevents a temporary distortion from controlling navigation indefinitely.", explanation: "Step 1: Decay allows adaptation without making local conditions permanent.<br><br>Answer: It prevents a temporary distortion from controlling navigation indefinitely.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how desert ants maintain a homeward direction when the sky and landscape provide imperfect guidance.<br><br>A foraging desert ant may travel hundreds of body lengths across nearly featureless ground and still return to a nest entrance scarcely wider than its body. It cannot follow a scent trail that survives reliably on hot, shifting sand. Instead, the ant continually estimates the direction and distance of its outbound path, then uses that accumulated estimate to calculate a route home.<br><br>Direction is partly derived from patterns of polarized light in the sky. Human eyes do not ordinarily perceive these patterns, but specialized receptors in an ant's eyes respond to them. The pattern remains informative even when the sun itself is hidden by thin cloud. Distance is estimated through signals related to walking, including the number and length of steps.<br><br>Neither estimate is perfect. A gust can push an ant sideways, uneven ground can change its stride, and the celestial pattern shifts as time passes. Near the expected nest position, ants therefore stop following a straight line and begin a widening search. The shape of that search gives researchers a visible record of where the ant 