ment is exact, but because uncertain measurements are compared and revised. The widening search near the nest is part of the same system, not a sign that the system has failed. A flexible navigator must know not only where to go, but when its estimate has become uncertain enough to justify looking around.<br><br>The width of the final search also changed predictably. Ants trained on smooth, short routes searched within a smaller area than ants that crossed irregular terrain or experienced conflicting filters. Researchers could therefore use search spread as an indirect measure of uncertainty, even though the ant offered no explicit report of confidence.<br><br>Experiments under overcast skies produced another contrast. When polarized patterns weakened, ants relied more heavily on a familiar panorama near the nest. Newly displaced ants without that view searched sooner. No cue was universally dominant; reliability depended on what the environment made available and what the ant had previously learned. This weighting lets the navigation system remain useful as visibility and terrain change."},
134: {skill_tested: "Synthesize search behavior.", question: "Why does the author describe the widening search as part of navigation rather than failure?", option1: "It always leads directly to food.", option2: "It replaces all directional calculations.", option3: "It proves the nest has moved.", option4: "It is a response matched to uncertainty in the ant's estimated endpoint.", correctAnswer: "It is a response matched to uncertainty in the ant's estimated endpoint.", explanation: "Step 1: Search is a controlled strategy used when precision declines.<br><br>Answer: It is a response matched to uncertainty in the ant's estimated endpoint.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how desert ants maintain a homeward direction when the sky and landscape provide imperfect guidance.<br><br>A foraging desert ant may travel hundreds of body lengths across nearly featureless ground and still return to a nest entrance scarcely wider than its body. It cannot follow a scent trail that survives reliably on hot, shifting sand. Instead, the ant continually estimates the direction and distance of its outbound path, then uses that accumulated estimate to calculate a route home.<br><br>Direction is partly derived from patterns of polarized light in the sky. Human eyes do not ordinarily perceive these patterns, but specialized receptors in an ant's eyes respond to them. The pattern remains informative even when the sun itself is hidden by thin cloud. Distance is estimated through signals related to walking, including the number and length of steps.<br><br>Neither estimate is perfect. A gust can push an ant sideways, uneven ground can change its stride, and the celestial pattern shifts as time passes. Near the expected nest position, ants therefore stop following a straight line and begin a widening search. The shape of that search gives researchers a visible record of where the ant believes home should be.<br><br>In one experiment, scientists trained ants to walk through a channel from their nest to a feeder. After an ant reached the food, they moved it to a flat testing field. Most ants ran the distance and direction predicted by the training route, then began searching. This showed that the insects carried an internal estimate rather than relying only on landmarks encountered during return.<br><br>The researchers next placed a filter above part of the training channel. The filter rotated the apparent direction of polarized light. Ants leaving the nest initially walked according to the altered sky cue, but their paths gradually became less deflected on repeated trips. When later tested without the filter, experienced ants briefly erred in the opposite direction. The reversal suggested that they had adjusted, or calibrated, the sky signal against other information during training.<br><br>What supplied that other information? The channel walls limited the view of distant landmarks, but the ants could still sense the direction of gravity while walking on a slightly sloped floor. When researchers reversed the slope while keeping the light filter unchanged, adaptation slowed. The ants apparently treated the stable relationship among multiple cues as evidence about which signal required correction.<br><br>Calibration was not unlimited. A filter rotation of moderate size produced gradual adjustment, whereas an extreme rotation caused erratic paths and broad searches. The ants did not simply replace one compass reading with another. Large disagreement reduced confidence in the entire estimate.<br><br>Researchers also varied the time between training and testing. Recent calibration influenced the ants strongly, but its effect weakened after several days without exposure to the altered condition. Such fading may prevent a temporary local distortion from becoming a permanent error.<br><br>The ant's navigation is remarkable not because any single measurement is exact, but because uncertain measurements are compared and revised. The widening search near the nest is part of the same system, not a sign that the system has failed. A flexible navigator must know not only where to go, but when its estimate has become uncertain enough to justify looking around.<br><br>The width of the final search also changed predictably. Ants trained on smooth, short routes searched within a smaller area than ants that crossed irregular terrain or experienced conflicting filters. Researchers could therefore use search spread as an indirect measure of uncertainty, even though the ant offered no explicit report of confidence.<br><br>Experiments under overcast skies produced another contrast. When polarized patterns weakened, ants relied more heavily on a familiar panorama near the nest. Newly displaced ants without that view searched sooner. No cue was universally dominant; reliability depended on what the environment made available and what the ant had previously learned. This weighting lets the navigation system remain useful as visibility and terrain change."},
135: {skill_tested: "Derive a broader principle.", question: "The experiments most strongly support which principle?", option1: "Reliable behavior can emerge from revising several uncertain signals rather than trusting one perfectly.", option2: "A useful compass must never change.", option3: "Conflicting evidence should always be ignored.", option4: "Memory is accurate only when landmarks are visible.", correctAnswer: "Reliable behavior can emerge from revising several uncertain signals rather than trusting one perfectly.", explanation: "Step 1: Navigation succeeds through comparison and calibration, not a flawless sensor.<br><br>Answer: Reliable behavior can emerge from revising several uncertain signals rather than trusting one perfectly.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how desert ants maintain a homeward direction when the sky and landscape provide imperfect guidance.<br><br>A foraging desert ant may travel hundreds of body lengths across nearly featureless ground and still return to a nest entrance scarcely wider than its body. It cannot follow a scent trail that survives reliably on hot, shifting sand. Instead, the ant continually estimates the direction and distance of its outbound path, then uses that accumulated estimate to calculate a route home.<br><br>Direction is partly derived from patterns of polarized light in the sky. Human eyes do not ordinarily perceive these patterns, but specialized receptors in an ant's eyes respond to them. The pattern remains informative even when the sun itself is hidden by thin cloud. Distance is estimated through signals related to walking, including the number and length of steps.<br><br>Neither estimate is perfect. A gust can push an ant sideways, uneven ground can change its stride, and the celestial pattern shifts as time passes. Near the expected nest position, ants therefore stop following a straight line and begin a widening search. The shape of that search gives researchers a visible record of where the ant believes home should be.<br><br>In one experiment, scientists trained ants to walk through a channel from their nest to a feeder. After an ant reached the food, they moved it to a flat testing field. Most ants ran the distance and direction predicted by the training route, then began searching. This showed that the insects carried an internal estimate rather than relying only on landmarks encountered during return.<br><br>The researchers next placed a filter above part of the training channel. The filter rotated the apparent direction of polarized light. Ants leaving the nest initially walked according to the altered sky cue, but their paths gradually became less deflected on repeated trips. When later tested without the filter, experienced ants briefly erred in the opposite direction. The reversal suggested that they had adjusted, or calibrated, the sky signal against other information during training.<br><br>What supplied that other information? The channel walls limited the view of distant landmarks, but the ants could still sense the direction of gravity while walking on a slightly sloped floor. When researchers reversed the slope while keeping the light filter unchanged, adaptation slowed. The ants apparently treated the stable relationship among multiple cues as evidence about which signal required correction.<br><br>Calibration was not unlimited. A filter rotation of moderate size produced gradual adjustment, whereas an extreme rotation caused erratic paths and broad searches. The ants did not simply replace one compass reading with another. Large disagreement reduced confidence in the entire estimate.<br><br>Researchers also varied the time between training and testing. Recent calibration influenced the ants strongly, but its effect weakened after several days without exposure to the altered condition. Such fading may prevent a temporary local distortion from becoming a permanent error.<br><br>The ant's navigation is remarkable not because any single measurement is exact, but because uncertain measurements are compared and revised. The widening search near the nest is part of the same system, not a sign that the system has failed. A flexible navigator must know not only where to go, but when its estimate has become uncertain enough to justify looking around.<br><br>The width of the final search also changed predictably. Ants trained on smooth, short routes searched within a smaller area than ants that crossed irregular terrain or experienced conflicting filters. Researchers could therefore use search spread as an indirect measure of uncertainty, even though the ant offered no explicit report of confidence.<br><br>Experiments under overcast skies produced another contrast. When polarized patterns weakened, ants relied more heavily on a familiar panorama near the nest. Newly displaced ants without that view searched sooner. No cue was universally dominant; reliability depended on what the environment made available and what the ant had previously learned. This weighting lets the navigation system remain useful as visibility and terrain change."},
136: {skill_tested: "Identify an explicitly stated benefit of linked storage sites.", question: "According to the second paragraph, why are orders from several farms combined at a hub?", option1: "To eliminate labels before delivery", option2: "To create loads large enough for economical transport", option3: "To require every farm to grow the same crop", option4: "To keep all produce at one temperature", correctAnswer: "To create loads large enough for economical transport", explanation: "Step 1: Farms bring produce to nearby cooling sites.<br><br>Step 2: The hub combines smaller orders into loads that can be transported economically.<br><br>Answer: To create loads large enough for economical transport", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines a cooperative system for sharing agricultural cold storage and transport.<br><br>A small farm may harvest enough produce to supply several markets yet too little to justify owning a refrigerated warehouse and truck. Without cooling, leafy crops lose quality quickly, and a delayed pickup can erase the value of a week's work. In some regions, farms address this mismatch through cooperatives that pool storage rooms, vehicles, and schedules while leaving production decisions with individual members.<br><br>The simplest version is a shared building. Farmers reserve space, deliver labeled crates, and pay according to use. More developed systems link several small cooling sites with one larger hub. A farm can bring produce to the nearest site, where orders from multiple farms are combined into loads large enough for economical transport. The hub may also sort orders by destination, temperature requirement, or delivery day.<br><br>The network works because members need different amounts of capacity at different times. One farm's berry harvest peaks before another farm's late vegetables. Empty space can move, in an accounting sense, from one member to another without any wall changing position. Coordinators track reservations and actual use so that a farm expecting a large harvest does not block space it ultimately leaves empty.<br><br>Flexibility becomes especially valuable when demand changes suddenly. If a school cafeteria postpones an order, its crates can remain cool while a restaurant order leaves as scheduled. If one vehicle requires repair, loads can be reassigned among other routes. Such adjustments depend on accurate labels and prompt updates. A crate listed under the wrong destination occupies not only physical space but also a false position in the schedule.<br><br>The central hub produces efficiencies, but it can also concentrate risk. A power failure there may affect produce from many farms at once. Cooperatives reduce that vulnerability with backup generators, temperature alarms, agreements with nearby warehouses, and rules for deciding which goods should move first. Some maintain limited cross-connections among smaller sites so that not every emergency must pass through the hub.<br><br>Access to the network is not automatically equal. Farms close to a cooling site spend less time and fuel delivering produce. Larger members may have staff available to update forecasts, while a grower working alone may confirm a harvest only after picking begins. If reservation penalties are too strict, the system can punish the members with the least predictable labor and crops. If rules are too loose, unused reservations can deprive others of space.<br><br>Several cooperatives address this tension by dividing capacity into layers. Members reserve a dependable base amount in advance, while a smaller flexible pool is allocated closer to harvest. Coordinators may also hold emergency space for unexpected heat or vehicle failure. The layers do not guarantee that every request will be met. They prevent the entire system from being governed either by rigid forecasts or by last-minute competition.<br><br>Shared infrastructure can change what farms choose to grow, but that effect should not be confused with direct control. Reliable cooling may make a delicate crop less risky or allow harvest to occur at a better stage of ripeness. Each farm still weighs soil, labor, market, and experience. The cooperative expands feasible choices; it does not make those choices identical.<br><br>Evaluating the network therefore requires more than counting crates at the hub. Useful measures include spoilage avoided, travel distance, unused reservations, emergency transfers, delivery reliability, and which members gain access during peak weeks. A highly efficient route may still serve only the largest farms. A lightly used rural site may appear inefficient while providing the only practical entry point for distant members.<br><br>The cooperative's strength lies in coordinated difference. Farms do not need identical crops or schedules for the network to work; their differences can help smooth demand. But coordination makes dependencies visible. Cooling, transport, information, and fair rules must reinforce one another. The system succeeds not when every crate passes through the largest building, but when produce can move through several linked options without one member's uncertainty becoming everyone else's loss."},
137: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of an abstract term.", question: "As used in the eighth paragraph, the word 'feasible' most nearly means:", option1: "identical for every member.", option2: "required by cooperative rules.", option3: "practical or possible.", option4: "measured only by profit.", correctAnswer: "practical or possible.", explanation: "Step 1: Reliable cooling can make delicate crops less risky.<br><br>Step 2: It expands what farms can reasonably choose to grow, so 'feasible' means practical or possible.<br><br>Answer: practical or possible.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines a cooperative system for sharing agricultural cold storage and transport.<br><br>A small farm may harvest enough produce to supply several markets yet too little to justify owning a refrigerated warehouse and truck. Without cooling, leafy crops lose quality quickly, and a delayed pickup can erase the value of a week's work. In some regions, farms address this mismatch through cooperatives that pool storage rooms, vehicles, and schedules while leaving production decisions with individual members.<br><br>The simplest version is a shared building. Farmers reserve space, deliver labeled crates, and pay according to use. More developed systems link several small cooling sites with one larger hub. A farm can bring produce to the nearest site, where orders from multiple farms are combined into loads large enough for economical transport. The hub may also sort orders by destination, temperature requirement, or delivery day.<br><br>The network works because members need different amounts of capacity at different times. One farm's berry harvest peaks before another farm's late vegetables. Empty space can move, in an accounting sense, from one member to another without any wall changing position. Coordinators track reservations and actual use so that a farm expecting a large harvest does not block space it ultimately leaves empty.<br><br>Flexibility becomes especially valuable when demand changes suddenly. If a school cafeteria postpones an order, its crates can remain cool while a restaurant order leaves as scheduled. If one vehicle requires repair, loads can be reassigned among other routes. Such adjustments depend on accurate labels and prompt updates. A crate listed under the wrong destination occupies not only physical space but also a false position in the schedule.<br><br>The central hub produces efficiencies, but it can also concentrate risk. A power failure there may affect produce from many farms at once. Cooperatives reduce that vulnerability with backup generators, temperature alarms, agreements with nearby warehouses, and rules for deciding which goods should move first. Some maintain limited cross-connections among smaller sites so that not every emergency must pass through the hub.<br><br>Access to the network is not automatically equal. Farms close to a cooling site spend less time and fuel delivering produce. Larger members may have staff available to update forecasts, while a grower working alone may confirm a harvest only after picking begins. If reservation penalties are too strict, the system can punish the members with the least predictable labor and crops. If rules are too loose, unused reservations can deprive others of space.<br><br>Several cooperatives address this tension by dividing capacity into layers. Members reserve a dependable base amount in advance, while a smaller flexible pool is allocated closer to harvest. Coordinators may also hold emergency space for unexpected heat or vehicle failure. The layers do not guarantee that every request will be met. They prevent the entire system from being governed either by rigid forecasts or by last-minute competition.<br><br>Shared infrastructure can change what farms choose to grow, but that effect should not be confused with direct control. Reliable cooling may make a delicate crop less risky or allow harvest to occur at a better stage of ripeness. Each farm still weighs soil, labor, market, and experience. The cooperative expands feasible choices; it does not make those choices identical.<br><br>Evaluating the network therefore requires more than counting crates at the hub. Useful measures include spoilage avoided, travel distance, unused reservations, emergency transfers, delivery reliability, and which members gain access during peak weeks. A highly efficient route may still serve only the largest farms. A lightly used rural site may appear inefficient while providing the only practical entry point for distant members.<br><br>The cooperative's strength lies in coordinated difference. Farms do not need identical crops or schedules for the network to work; their differences can help smooth demand. But coordination makes dependencies visible. Cooling, transport, information, and fair rules must reinforce one another. The system succeeds not when every crate passes through the largest building, but when produce can move through several linked options without one member's uncertainty becoming everyone else's loss."},
138: {skill_tested: "Identify a central idea about cooperative networks.", question: "Which statement best expresses the passage's central idea?", option1: "A cooperative works best when all members follow identical harvest schedules.", option2: "The largest storage hub should control which crops every member produces.", option3: "Route efficiency alone provides a complete measure of cooperative success.", option4: "Shared infrastructure can turn varied needs into collective capacity, but it requires resilient systems and fair coordination.", correctAnswer: "Shared infrastructure can turn varied needs into collective capacity, but it requires resilient systems and fair coordination.", explanation: "Step 1: Different harvest peaks help members share limited storage and transport.<br><br>Step 2: Hub failures, unequal access, reservations, information, and fair rules determine whether that sharing remains useful.<br><br>Answer: Shared infrastructure can turn varied needs into collective capacity, but it requires resilient systems and fair coordination.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines a cooperative system for sharing agricultural cold storage and transport.<br><br>A small farm may harvest enough produce to supply several markets yet too little to justify owning a refrigerated warehouse and truck. Without cooling, leafy crops lose quality quickly, and a delayed pickup can erase the value of a week's work. In some regions, farms address this mismatch through cooperatives that pool storage rooms, vehicles, and schedules while leaving production decisions with individual members.<br><br>The simplest version is a shared building. Farmers reserve space, deliver labeled crates, and pay according to use. More developed systems link several small cooling sites with one larger hub. A farm can bring produce to the nearest site, where orders from multiple farms are combined into loads large enough for economical transport. The hub may also sort orders by destination, temperature requirement, or delivery day.<br><br>The network works because members need different amounts of capacity at different times. One farm's berry harvest peaks before another farm's late vegetables. Empty space can move, in an accounting sense, from one member to another without any wall changing position. Coordinators track reservations and actual use so that a farm expecting a large harvest does not block space it ultimately leaves empty.<br><br>Flexibility becomes especially valuable when demand changes suddenly. If a school cafeteria postpones an order, its crates can remain cool while a restaurant order leaves as scheduled. If one vehicle requires repair, loads can be reassigned among other routes. Such adjustments depend on accurate labels and prompt updates. A crate listed under the wrong destination occupies not only physical space but also a false position in the schedule.<br><br>The central hub produces efficiencies, but it can also concentrate risk. A power failure there may affect produce from many farms at once. Cooperatives reduce that vulnerability with backup generators, temperature alarms, agreements with nearby warehouses, and rules for deciding which goods should move first. Some maintain limited cross-connections among smaller sites so that not every emergency must pass through the hub.<br><br>Access to the network is not automatically equal. Farms close to a cooling site spend less time and fuel delivering produce. Larger members may have staff available to update forecasts, while a grower working alone may confirm a harvest only after picking begins. If reservation penalties are too strict, the system can punish the members with the least predictable labor and crops. If rules are too loose, unused reservations can deprive others of space.<br><br>Several cooperatives address this tension by dividing capacity into layers. Members reserve a dependable base amount in advance, while a smaller flexible pool is allocated closer to harvest. Coordinators may also hold emergency space for unexpected heat or vehicle failure. The layers do not guarantee that every request will be met. They prevent the entire system from being governed either by rigid forecasts or by last-minute competition.<br><br>Shared infrastructure can change what farms choose to grow, but that effect should not be confused with direct control. Reliable cooling may make a delicate crop less risky or allow harvest to occur at a better stage of ripeness. Each farm still weighs soil, labor, market, and experience. The cooperative expands feasible choices; it does not make those choices identical.<br><br>Evaluating the network therefore requires more than counting crates at the hub. Useful measures include spoilage avoided, travel distance, unused reservations, emergency transfers, delivery reliability, and which members gain access during peak weeks. A highly efficient route may still serve only the largest farms. A lightly used rural site may appear inefficient while providing the only practical entry point for distant members.<br><br>The cooperative's strength lies in coordinated difference. Farms do not need identical crops or schedules for the network to work; their differences can help smooth demand. But coordination makes dependencies visible. Cooling, transport, information, and fair rules must reinforce one another. The system succeeds not when every crate passes through the largest building, but when produce can move through several linked options without one member's uncertainty becoming everyone else's loss."},
139: {skill_tested: "Explain an abstract description of pooled capacity.", question: "The statement that empty space can move in an accounting sense primarily means that:", option1: "unused capacity can be reassigned among members whose peak needs occur at different times.", option2: "warehouse walls are physically shifted between harvests.", option3: "empty crates are transported to the largest farm.", option4: "members may reserve unlimited space without updating forecasts.", correctAnswer: "unused capacity can be reassigned among members whose peak needs occur at different times.", explanation: "Step 1: Farms reach peak harvest at different times.<br><br>Step 2: Coordinators compare reservations with actual use and release unused capacity.<br><br>Step 3: The physical room remains fixed, but the right to use portions of it shifts among members.<br><br>Answer: unused capacity can be reassigned among members whose peak needs occur at different times.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines a cooperative system for sharing agricultural cold storage and transport.<br><br>A small farm may harvest enough produce to supply several markets yet too little to justify owning a refrigerated warehouse and truck. Without cooling, leafy crops lose quality quickly, and a delayed pickup can erase the value of a week's work. In some regions, farms address this mismatch through cooperatives that pool storage rooms, vehicles, and schedules while leaving production decisions with individual members.<br><br>The simplest version is a shared building. Farmers reserve space, deliver labeled crates, and pay according to use. More developed systems link several small cooling sites with one larger hub. A farm can bring produce to the nearest site, where orders from multiple farms are combined into loads large enough for economical transport. The hub may also sort orders by destination, temperature requirement, or delivery day.<br><br>The network works because members need different amounts of capacity at different times. One farm's berry harvest peaks before another farm's late vegetables. Empty space can move, in an accounting sense, from one member to another without any wall changing position. Coordinators track reservations and actual use so that a farm expecting a large harvest does not block space it ultimately leaves empty.<br><br>Flexibility becomes especially valuable when demand changes suddenly. If a school cafeteria postpones an order, its crates can remain cool while a restaurant order leaves as scheduled. If one vehicle requires repair, loads can be reassigned among other routes. Such adjustments depend on accurate labels and prompt updates. A crate listed under the wrong destination occupies not only physical space but also a false position in the schedule.<br><br>The central hub produces efficiencies, but it can also concentrate risk. A power failure there may affect produce from many farms at once. Cooperatives reduce that vulnerability with backup generators, temperature alarms, agreements with nearby warehouses, and rules for deciding which goods should move first. Some maintain limited cross-connections among smaller sites so that not every emergency must pass through the hub.<br><br>Access to the network is not automatically equal. Farms close to a cooling site spend less time and fuel delivering produce. Larger members may have staff available to update forecasts, while a grower working alone may confirm a harvest only after picking begins. If reservation penalties are too strict, the system can punish the members with the least predictable labor and crops. If rules are too loose, unused reservations can deprive others of space.<br><br>Several cooperatives address this tension by dividing capacity into layers. Members reserve a dependable base amount in advance, while a smaller flexible pool is allocated closer to harvest. Coordinators may also hold emergency space for unexpected heat or vehicle failure. The layers do not guarantee that every request will be met. They prevent the entire system from being governed either by rigid forecasts or by last-minute competition.<br><br>Shared infrastructure can change what farms choose to grow, but that effect should not be confused with direct control. Reliable cooling may make a delicate crop less risky or allow harvest to occur at a better stage of ripeness. Each farm still weighs soil, labor, market, and experience. The cooperative expands feasible choices; it does not make those choices identical.<br><br>Evaluating the network therefore requires more than counting crates at the hub. Useful measures include spoilage avoided, travel distance, unused reservations, emergency transfers, delivery reliability, and which members gain access during peak weeks. A highly efficient route may still serve only the largest farms. A lightly used rural site may appear inefficient while providing the only practical entry point for distant members.<br><br>The cooperative's strength lies in coordinated difference. Farms do not need identical crops or schedules for the network to work; their differences can help smooth demand. But coordination makes dependencies visible. Cooling, transport, information, and fair rules must reinforce one another. The system succeeds not when every crate passes through the largest building, but when produce can move through several linked options without one member's uncertainty becoming everyone else's loss."},
140: {skill_tested: "Analyze why information functions as part of physical logistics.", question: "Why does the passage describe a mislabeled crate as occupying a false position in the schedule?", option1: "Its label physically prevents other crates from fitting in the room.", option2: "Its incorrect information can cause storage and transport decisions to be made for the wrong destination or time.", option3: "It changes the temperature requirement of every nearby product.", option4: "It proves that the cooperative no longer needs actual storage space.", correctAnswer: "Its incorrect information can cause storage and transport decisions to be made for the wrong destination or time.", explanation: "Step 1: Coordinators reassign loads and preserve orders through accurate, prompt updates.<br><br>Step 2: A wrong destination makes planners allocate route and timing capacity inaccurately.<br><br>Step 3: The crate therefore disrupts the informational arrangement even while occupying an ordinary physical location.<br><br>Answer: Its incorrect information can cause storage and transport decisions to be made for the wrong destination or time.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines a cooperative system for sharing agricultural cold storage and transport.<br><br>A small farm may harvest enough produce to supply several markets yet too little to justify owning a refrigerated warehouse and truck. Without cooling, leafy crops lose quality quickly, and a delayed pickup can erase the value of a week's work. In some regions, farms address this mismatch through cooperatives that pool storage rooms, vehicles, and schedules while leaving production decisions with individual members.<br><br>The simplest version is a shared building. Farmers reserve space, deliver labeled crates, and pay according to use. More developed systems link several small cooling sites with one larger hub. A farm can bring produce to the nearest site, where orders from multiple farms are combined into loads large enough for economical transport. The hub may also sort orders by destination, temperature requirement, or delivery day.<br><br>The network works because members need different amounts of capacity at different times. One farm's berry harvest peaks before another farm's late vegetables. Empty space can move, in an accounting sense, from one member to another without any wall changing position. Coordinators track reservations and actual use so that a farm expecting a large harvest does not block space it ultimately leaves empty.<br><br>Flexibility becomes especially valuable when demand changes suddenly. If a school cafeteria postpones an order, its crates can remain cool while a restaurant order leaves as scheduled. If one vehicle requires repair, loads can be reassigned among other routes. Such adjustments depend on accurate labels and prompt updates. A crate listed under the wrong destination occupies not only physical space but also a false position in the schedule.<br><br>The central hub produces efficiencies, but it can also concentrate risk. A power failure there may affect produce from many farms at once. Cooperatives reduce that vulnerability with backup generators, temperature alarms, agreements with nearby warehouses, and rules for deciding which goods should move first. Some maintain limited cross-connections among smaller sites so that not every emergency must pass through the hub.<br><br>Access to the network is not automatically equal. Farms close to a cooling site spend less time and fuel delivering produce. Larger members may have staff available to update forecasts, while a grower working alone may confirm a harvest only after picking begins. If reservation penalties are too strict, the system can punish the members with the least predictable labor and crops. If rules are too loose, unused reservations can deprive others of space.<br><br>Several cooperatives address this tension by dividing capacity into layers. Members reserve a dependable base amount in advance, while a smaller flexible pool is allocated closer to harvest. Coordinators may also hold emergency space for unexpected heat or vehicle failure. The layers do not guarantee that every request will be met. They prevent the entire system from being governed either by rigid forecasts or by last-minute competition.<br><br>Shared infrastructure can change what farms choose to grow, but that effect should not be confused with direct control. Reliable cooling may make a delicate crop less risky or allow harvest to occur at a better stage of ripeness. Each farm still weighs soil, labor, market, and experience. The cooperative expands feasible choices; it does not make those choices identical.<br><br>Evaluating the network therefore requires more than counting crates at the hub. Useful measures include spoilage avoided, travel distance, unused reservations, emergency transfers, delivery reliability, and which members gain access during peak weeks. A highly efficient route may still serve only the largest farms. A lightly used rural site may appear inefficient while providing the only practical entry point for distant members.<br><br>The cooperative's strength lies in coordinated difference. Farms do not need identical crops or schedules for the network to work; their differences can help smooth demand. But coordination makes dependencies visible. Cooling, transport, information, and fair rules must reinforce one another. The system succeeds not when every crate passes through the largest building, but when produce can move through several linked options without one member's uncertainty becoming everyone else's loss."},
141: {skill_tested: "Infer why hub efficiency can reduce resilience.", question: "The central hub can become a vulnerability primarily because:", option1: "smaller sites are prohibited from cooling produce.", option2: "all hub equipment must be operated without temperature alarms.", option3: "concentrating many farms' goods and transfers makes one failure affect a large portion of the network.", option4: "produce cannot be moved after reaching the hub.", correctAnswer: "concentrating many farms' goods and transfers makes one failure affect a large portion of the network.", explanation: "Step 1: The hub combines orders and coordinates major storage and transport functions.<br><br>Step 2: A power failure there can affect produce belonging to many farms simultaneously.<br><br>Step 3: The same concentration that creates efficiency increases the reach of a single disruption.<br><br>Answer: concentrating many farms' goods and transfers makes one failure affect a large portion of the network.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines a cooperative system for sharing agricultural cold storage and transport.<br><br>A small farm may harvest enough produce to supply several markets yet too little to justify owning a refrigerated warehouse and truck. Without cooling, leafy crops lose quality quickly, and a delayed pickup can erase the value of a week's work. In some regions, farms address this mismatch through cooperatives that pool storage rooms, vehicles, and schedules while leaving production decisions with individual members.<br><br>The simplest version is a shared building. Farmers reserve space, deliver labeled crates, and pay according to use. More developed systems link several small cooling sites with one larger hub. A farm can bring produce to the nearest site, where orders from multiple farms are combined into loads large enough for economical transport. The hub may also sort orders by destination, temperature requirement, or delivery day.<br><br>The network works because members need different amounts of capacity at different times. One farm's berry harvest peaks before another farm's late vegetables. Empty space can move, in an accounting sense, from one member to another without any wall changing position. Coordinators track reservations and actual use so that a farm expecting a large harvest does not block space it ultimately leaves empty.<br><br>Flexibility becomes especially valuable when demand changes suddenly. If a school cafeteria postpones an order, its crates can remain cool while a restaurant order leaves as scheduled. If one vehicle requires repair, loads can be reassigned among other routes. Such adjustments depend on accurate labels and prompt updates. A crate listed under the wrong destination occupies not only physical space but also a false position in the schedule.<br><br>The central hub produces efficiencies, but it can also concentrate risk. A power failure there may affect produce from many farms at once. Cooperatives reduce that vulnerability with backup generators, temperature alarms, agreements with nearby warehouses, and rules for deciding which goods should move first. Some maintain limited cross-connections among smaller sites so that not every emergency must pass through the hub.<br><br>Access to the network is not automatically equal. Farms close to a cooling site spend less time and fuel delivering produce. Larger members may have staff available to update forecasts, while a grower working alone may confirm a harvest only after picking begins. If reservation penalties are too strict, the system can punish the members with the least predictable labor and crops. If rules are too loose, unused reservations can deprive others of space.<br><br>Several cooperatives address this tension by dividing capacity into layers. Members reserve a dependable base amount in advance, while a smaller flexible pool is allocated closer to harvest. Coordinators may also hold emergency space for unexpected heat or vehicle failure. The layers do not guarantee that every request will be met. They prevent the entire system from being governed either by rigid forecasts or by last-minute competition.<br><br>Shared infrastructure can change what farms choose to grow, but that effect should not be confused with direct control. Reliable cooling may make a delicate crop less risky or allow harvest to occur at a better stage of ripeness. Each farm still weighs soil, labor, market, and experience. The cooperative expands feasible choices; it does not make those choices identical.<br><br>Evaluating the network therefore requires more than counting crates at the hub. Useful measures include spoilage avoided, travel distance, unused reservations, emergency transfers, delivery reliability, and which members gain access during peak weeks. A highly efficient route may still serve only the largest farms. A lightly used rural site may appear inefficient while providing the only practical entry point for distant members.<br><br>The cooperative's strength lies in coordinated difference. Farms do not need identical crops or schedules for the network to work; their differences can help smooth demand. But coordination makes dependencies visible. Cooling, transport, information, and fair rules must reinforce one another. The system succeeds not when every crate passes through the largest building, but when produce can move through several linked options without one member's uncertainty becoming everyone else's loss."},
142: {skill_tested: "Analyze how layered capacity balances competing access risks.", question: "The base, flexible, and emergency capacity layers primarily help the cooperative avoid:", option1: "tracking actual storage use after reservations are made.", option2: "serving farms with different harvest periods.", option3: "maintaining any unused room for unexpected events.", option4: "both inflexible advance forecasts and unrestricted last-minute competition.", correctAnswer: "both inflexible advance forecasts and unrestricted last-minute competition.", explanation: "Step 1: Base reservations provide dependable access before harvest.<br><br>Step 2: Flexible and emergency pools accommodate uncertainty closer to actual need.<br><br>Step 3: Combining layers prevents either strict forecasts or immediate competition from controlling all capacity.<br><br>Answer: both inflexible advance forecasts and unrestricted last-minute competition.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines a cooperative system for sharing agricultural cold storage and transport.<br><br>A small farm may harvest enough produce to supply several markets yet too little to justify owning a refrigerated warehouse and truck. Without cooling, leafy crops lose quality quickly, and a delayed pickup can erase the value of a week's work. In some regions, farms address this mismatch through cooperatives that pool storage rooms, vehicles, and schedules while leaving production decisions with individual members.<br><br>The simplest version is a shared building. Farmers reserve space, deliver labeled crates, and pay according to use. More developed systems link several small cooling sites with one larger hub. A farm can bring produce to the nearest site, where orders from multiple farms are combined into loads large enough for economical transport. The hub may also sort orders by destination, temperature requirement, or delivery day.<br><br>The network works because members need different amounts of capacity at different times. One farm's berry harvest peaks before another farm's late vegetables. Empty space can move, in an accounting sense, from one member to another without any wall changing position. Coordinators track reservations and actual use so that a farm expecting a large harvest does not block space it ultimately leaves empty.<br><br>Flexibility becomes especially valuable when demand changes suddenly. If a school cafeteria postpones an order, its crates can remain cool while a restaurant order leaves as scheduled. If one vehicle requires repair, loads can be reassigned among other routes. Such adjustments depend on accurate labels and prompt updates. A crate listed under the wrong destination occupies not only physical space but also a false position in the schedule.<br><br>The central hub produces efficiencies, but it can also concentrate risk. A power failure there may affect produce from many farms at once. Cooperatives reduce that vulnerability with backup generators, temperature alarms, agreements with nearby warehouses, and rules for deciding which goods should move first. Some maintain limited cross-connections among smaller sites so that not every emergency must pass through the hub.<br><br>Access to the network is not automatically equal. Farms close to a cooling site spend less time and fuel delivering produce. Larger members may have staff available to update forecasts, while a grower working alone may confirm a harvest only after picking begins. If reservation penalties are too strict, the system can punish the members with the least predictable labor and crops. If rules are too loose, unused reservations can deprive others of space.<br><br>Several cooperatives address this tension by dividing capacity into layers. Members reserve a dependable base amount in advance, while a smaller flexible pool is allocated closer to harvest. Coordinators may also hold emergency space for unexpected heat or vehicle failure. The layers do not guarantee that every request will be met. They prevent the entire system from being governed either by rigid forecasts or by last-minute competition.<br><br>Shared infrastructure can change what farms choose to grow, but that effect should not be confused with direct control. Reliable cooling may make a delicate crop less risky or allow harvest to occur at a better stage of ripeness. Each farm still weighs soil, labor, market, and experience. The cooperative expands feasible choices; it does not make those choices identical.<br><br>Evaluating the network therefore requires more than counting crates at the hub. Useful measures include spoilage avoided, travel distance, unused reservations, emergency transfers, delivery reliability, and which members gain access during peak weeks. A highly efficient route may still serve only the largest farms. A lightly used rural site may appear inefficient while providing the only practical entry point for distant members.<br><br>The cooperative's strength lies in coordinated difference. Farms do not need identical crops or schedules for the network to work; their differences can help smooth demand. But coordination makes dependencies visible. Cooling, transport, information, and fair rules must reinforce one another. The system succeeds not when every crate passes through the largest building, but when produce can move through several linked options without one member's uncertainty becoming everyone else's loss."},
143: {skill_tested: "Synthesize efficiency and equity in evaluating a network.", question: "Why might a lightly used rural cooling site still be valuable to the cooperative?", option1: "It may provide distant farms their only practical access, a benefit not captured by utilization alone.", option2: "It necessarily stores more crates than the central hub during peak weeks.", option3: "Its low use guarantees that it cannot experience a power failure.", option4: "It allows members to avoid all reservation rules.", correctAnswer: "It may provide distant farms their only practical access, a benefit not captured by utilization alone.", explanation: "Step 1: Distance and fuel costs make access unequal, and a route can be efficient while serving only large farms.<br><br>Step 2: A rural site may have fewer crates but connect otherwise excluded members.<br><br>Step 3: Its network value therefore includes geographic access, not merely the percentage of storage filled.<br><br>Answer: It may provide distant farms their only practical access, a benefit not captured by utilization alone.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines a cooperative system for sharing agricultural cold storage and transport.<br><br>A small farm may harvest enough produce to supply several markets yet too little to justify owning a refrigerated warehouse and truck. Without cooling, leafy crops lose quality quickly, and a delayed pickup can erase the value of a week's work. In some regions, farms address this mismatch through cooperatives that pool storage rooms, vehicles, and schedules while leaving production decisions with individual members.<br><br>The simplest version is a shared building. Farmers reserve space, deliver labeled crates, and pay according to use. More developed systems link several small cooling sites with one larger hub. A farm can bring produce to the nearest site, where orders from multiple farms are combined into loads large enough for economical transport. The hub may also sort orders by destination, temperature requirement, or delivery day.<br><br>The network works because members need different amounts of capacity at different times. One farm's berry harvest peaks before another farm's late vegetables. Empty space can move, in an accounting sense, from one member to another without any wall changing position. Coordinators track reservations and actual use so that a farm expecting a large harvest does not block space it ultimately leaves empty.<br><br>Flexibility becomes especially valuable when demand changes suddenly. If a school cafeteria postpones an order, its crates can remain cool while a restaurant order leaves as scheduled. If one vehicle requires repair, loads can be reassigned among other routes. Such adjustments depend on accurate labels and prompt updates. A crate listed under the wrong destination occupies not only physical space but also a false position in the schedule.<br><br>The central hub produces efficiencies, but it can also concentrate risk. A power failure there may affect produce from many farms at once. Cooperatives reduce that vulnerability with backup generators, temperature alarms, agreements with nearby warehouses, and rules for deciding which goods should move first. Some maintain limited cross-connections among smaller sites so that not every emergency must pass through the hub.<br><br>Access to the network is not automatically equal. Farms close to a cooling site spend less time and fuel delivering produce. Larger members may have staff available to update forecasts, while a grower working alone may confirm a harvest only after picking begins. If reservation penalties are too strict, the system can punish the members with the least predictable labor and crops. If rules are too loose, unused reservations can deprive others of space.<br><br>Several cooperatives address this tension by dividing capacity into layers. Members reserve a dependable base amount in advance, while a smaller flexible pool is allocated closer to harvest. Coordinators may also hold emergency space for unexpected heat or vehicle failure. The layers do not guarantee that every request will be met. They prevent the entire system from being governed either by rigid forecasts or by last-minute competition.<br><br>Shared infrastructure can change what farms choose to grow, but that effect should not be confused with direct control. Reliable cooling may make a delicate crop less risky or allow harvest to occur at a better stage of ripeness. Each farm still weighs soil, labor, market, and experience. The cooperative expands feasible choices; it does not make those choices identical.<br><br>Evaluating the network therefore requires more than counting crates at the hub. Useful measures include spoilage avoided, travel distance, unused reservations, emergency transfers, delivery reliability, and which members gain access during peak weeks. A highly efficient route may still serve only the largest farms. A lightly used rural site may appear inefficient while providing the only practical entry point for distant members.<br><br>The cooperative's strength lies in coordinated difference. Farms do not need identical crops or schedules for the network to work; their differences can help smooth demand. But coordination makes dependencies visible. Cooling, transport, information, and fair rules must reinforce one another. The system succeeds not when every crate passes through the largest building, but when produce can move through several linked options without one member's uncertainty becoming everyone else's loss."},
144: {skill_tested: "Derive how difference can strengthen a coordinated network.", question: "The phrase 'coordinated difference' in the final paragraph most strongly emphasizes that:", option1: "members must surrender all independent production decisions to the hub.", option2: "varied crops and schedules can smooth shared demand when linked by accurate information and fair rules.", option3: "every uncertainty should be transferred from one farm to all others.", option4: "the network succeeds only when every crate passes through one building.", correctAnswer: "varied crops and schedules can smooth shared demand when linked by accurate information and fair rules.", explanation: "Step 1: Farms require storage at different times, allowing unused capacity to be reassigned.<br><br>Step 2: Those differences become useful only through reservations, updates, transport, and equitable allocation.<br><br>Step 3: Coordination turns variation into complementary demand without erasing each farm's choices.<br><br>Answer: varied crops and schedules can smooth shared demand when linked by accurate information and fair rules.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines a cooperative system for sharing agricultural cold storage and transport.<br><br>A small farm may harvest enough produce to supply several markets yet too little to justify owning a refrigerated warehouse and truck. Without cooling, leafy crops lose quality quickly, and a delayed pickup can erase the value of a week's work. In some regions, farms address this mismatch through cooperatives that pool storage rooms, vehicles, and schedules while leaving production decisions with individual members.<br><br>The simplest version is a shared building. Farmers reserve space, deliver labeled crates, and pay according to use. More developed systems link several small cooling sites with one larger hub. A farm can bring produce to the nearest site, where orders from multiple farms are combined into loads large enough for economical transport. The hub may also sort orders by destination, temperature requirement, or delivery day.<br><br>The network works because members need different amounts of capacity at different times. One farm's berry harvest peaks before another farm's late vegetables. Empty space can move, in an accounting sense, from one member to another without any wall changing position. Coordinators track reservations and actual use so that a farm expecting a large harvest does not block space it ultimately leaves empty.<br><br>Flexibility becomes especially valuable when demand changes suddenly. If a school cafeteria postpones an order, its crates can remain cool while a restaurant order leaves as scheduled. If one vehicle requires repair, loads can be reassigned among other routes. Such adjustments depend on accurate labels and prompt updates. A crate listed under the wrong destination occupies not only physical space but also a false position in the schedule.<br><br>The central hub produces efficiencies, but it can also concentrate risk. A power failure there may affect produce from many farms at once. Cooperatives reduce that vulnerability with backup generators, temperature alarms, agreements with nearby warehouses, and rules for deciding which goods should move first. Some maintain limited cross-connections among smaller sites so that not every emergency must pass through the hub.<br><br>Access to the network is not automatically equal. Farms close to a cooling site spend less time and fuel delivering produce. Larger members may have staff available to update forecasts, while a grower working alone may confirm a harvest only after picking begins. If reservation penalties are too strict, the system can punish the members with the least predictable labor and crops. If rules are too loose, unused reservations can deprive others of space.<br><br>Several cooperatives address this tension by dividing capacity into layers. Members reserve a dependable base amount in advance, while a smaller flexible pool is allocated closer to harvest. Coordinators may also hold emergency space for unexpected heat or vehicle failure. The layers do not guarantee that every request will be met. They prevent the entire system from being governed either by rigid forecasts or by last-minute competition.<br><br>Shared infrastructure can change what farms choose to grow, but that effect should not be confused with direct control. Reliable cooling may make a delicate crop less risky or allow harvest to occur at a better stage of ripeness. Each farm still weighs soil, labor, market, and experience. The cooperative expands feasible choices; it does not make those choices identical.<br><br>Evaluating the network therefore requires more than counting crates at the hub. Useful measures include spoilage avoided, travel distance, unused reservations, emergency transfers, delivery reliability, and which members gain access during peak weeks. A highly efficient route may still serve only the largest farms. A lightly used rural site may appear inefficient while providing the only practical entry point for distant members.<br><br>The cooperative's strength lies in coordinated difference. Farms do not need identical crops or schedules for the network to work; their differences can help smooth demand. But coordination makes dependencies visible. Cooling, transport, information, and fair rules must reinforce one another. The system succeeds not when every crate passes through the largest building, but when produce can move through several linked options without one member's uncertainty becoming everyone else's loss."},
145: {skill_tested: "Identify an initial belief.", question: "Why does Nia first suspect Mr. Vale?", option1: "He admits closing the main valve.", option2: "He refuses to enter the utility room.", option3: "His prior complaints and calm expression seem to suggest he expected the outage.", option4: "He owns the courtyard hose.", correctAnswer: "His prior complaints and calm expression seem to suggest he expected the outage.", explanation: "Step 1: Nia misreads his complaints and calm as satisfaction.<br><br>Answer: His prior complaints and calm expression seem to suggest he expected the outage.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> In the selection below, a teenager helps during an apartment-building water outage.<br><br>When every faucet in Building C coughed air at once, Nia blamed Mr. Vale. He lived in the ground-floor apartment beside the utility room and had complained for weeks about people leaving the courtyard hose running. At the emergency meeting, he stood near the back holding a ring of keys, his expression so unsurprised that Nia mistook calm for satisfaction.<br><br>The property manager was across town. A plumber could not arrive until evening. Mr. Vale said the main valve was open, so the blockage must be elsewhere. Several residents answered at once, offering theories about the roof tank and the road repairs. Mr. Vale waited, then asked Nia whether she still made maps for the school newspaper. She said she did, though she did not see what that had to do with water.<br><br>He led her into the basement corridor. Pipes crossed the ceiling, disappearing behind walls and reappearing under stairwells. Their old labels had peeled away. Mr. Vale handed Nia a roll of blue tape and asked her to mark each branch while he tested valves. She drew the building as a set of boxes, adding lines wherever a pipe vanished. The work felt less like plumbing than tracing an argument whose middle sentences had been erased.<br><br>At the first valve, nothing changed. At the second, a pipe shuddered but remained silent. Nia heard residents upstairs opening taps after each test, then calling down the same answer. No water. Mr. Vale never rushed. He touched the pipes, listened, and wrote the result beside Nia's lines.<br><br>They found an unexpected branch behind a storage cabinet. Moving the cabinet revealed a narrow access panel painted the color of the wall. Inside, a valve handle sat halfway between open and closed. Nia reached for it, but Mr. Vale stopped her. If it fed the damaged laundry line, opening it could flood the basement. They needed evidence before motion.<br><br>Nia returned to the courtyard and compared apartment numbers with her map. The dry taps formed no simple vertical stack. Then a mother from the third floor mentioned that her kitchen tap had failed before her bathroom tap. That detail placed the interruption beyond the roof tank but before two branches separated. Nia circled the hidden valve.<br><br>Back downstairs, Mr. Vale turned it slowly while Nia watched the laundry pipe. It stayed dry. Above them came a distant thump, then a cheer traveling floor by floor as water reached the apartments. Someone must have brushed the handle while moving the cabinet days earlier, gradually narrowing the flow until sediment completed the blockage.<br><br>At the meeting that evening, residents praised Mr. Vale. He corrected them, pointing to Nia's map. She wanted to apologize for suspecting him, but the words seemed designed to make him comfort her. Instead, she asked why he had looked unsurprised that morning.<br><br>Mr. Vale lifted the key ring. He had managed a theater building years ago, he explained, and old buildings were always hiding one more route than their plans admitted. Surprise was expensive during an outage. Curiosity was more useful.<br><br>Nia taped a clean copy of the map inside the utility-room door. Beside the newly labeled valve, she added a note: Check before turning. Mr. Vale read it and nodded. The sentence applied to valves, she thought, but also to conclusions reached too quickly.<br><br>Before leaving, they walked the route once more. Nia added arrows showing normal flow and photographed each valve with its label visible. Mr. Vale suggested keeping the paper map too. A phone could lose power during the next emergency, and a diagram on the wall could be read by whoever arrived first. Their two records served the same facts under different conditions.<br><br>The following morning, Nia passed Mr. Vale in the courtyard. He was tightening the hose connection he had complained about. This time his concentrated expression did not seem accusing. She asked whether the map needed a line for the outdoor tap. He handed her the blue tape."},
146: {skill_tested: "Interpret a comparison.", question: "Tracing the pipes feels like tracing an argument because:", option1: "every pipe contains written sentences.", option2: "Nia wants to debate the residents.", option3: "the plumbing follows the newspaper layout.", option4: "important connections are hidden between visible sections.", correctAnswer: "important connections are hidden between visible sections.", explanation: "Step 1: Both systems require reconstructing missing links.<br><br>Answer: important connections are hidden between visible sections.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> In the selection below, a teenager helps during an apartment-building water outage.<br><br>When every faucet in Building C coughed air at once, Nia blamed Mr. Vale. He lived in the ground-floor apartment beside the utility room and had complained for weeks about people leaving the courtyard hose running. At the emergency meeting, he stood near the back holding a ring of keys, his expression so unsurprised that Nia mistook calm for satisfaction.<br><br>The property manager was across town. A plumber could not arrive until evening. Mr. Vale said the main valve was open, so the blockage must be elsewhere. Several residents answered at once, offering theories about the roof tank and the road repairs. Mr. Vale waited, then asked Nia whether she still made maps for the school newspaper. She said she did, though she did not see what that had to do with water.<br><br>He led her into the basement corridor. Pipes crossed the ceiling, disappearing behind walls and reappearing under stairwells. Their old labels had peeled away. Mr. Vale handed Nia a roll of blue tape and asked her to mark each branch while he tested valves. She drew the building as a set of boxes, adding lines wherever a pipe vanished. The work felt less like plumbing than tracing an argument whose middle sentences had been erased.<br><br>At the first valve, nothing changed. At the second, a pipe shuddered but remained silent. Nia heard residents upstairs opening taps after each test, then calling down the same answer. No water. Mr. Vale never rushed. He touched the pipes, listened, and wrote the result beside Nia's lines.<br><br>They found an unexpected branch behind a storage cabinet. Moving the cabinet revealed a narrow access panel painted the color of the wall. Inside, a valve handle sat halfway between open and closed. Nia reached for it, but Mr. Vale stopped her. If it fed the damaged laundry line, opening it could flood the basement. They needed evidence before motion.<br><br>Nia returned to the courtyard and compared apartment numbers with her map. The dry taps formed no simple vertical stack. Then a mother from the third floor mentioned that her kitchen tap had failed before her bathroom tap. That detail placed the interruption beyond the roof tank but before two branches separated. Nia circled the hidden valve.<br><br>Back downstairs, Mr. Vale turned it slowly while Nia watched the laundry pipe. It stayed dry. Above them came a distant thump, then a cheer traveling floor by floor as water reached the apartments. Someone must have brushed the handle while moving the cabinet days earlier, gradually narrowing the flow until sediment completed the blockage.<br><br>At the meeting that evening, residents praised Mr. Vale. He corrected them, pointing to Nia's map. She wanted to apologize for suspecting him, but the words seemed designed to make him comfort her. Instead, she asked why he had looked unsurprised that morning.<br><br>Mr. Vale lifted the key ring. He had managed a theater building years ago, he explained, and old buildings were always hiding one more route than their plans admitted. Surprise was expensive during an outage. Curiosity was more useful.<br><br>Nia taped a clean copy of the map inside the utility-room door. Beside the newly labeled valve, she added a note: Check before turning. Mr. Vale read it and nodded. The sentence applied to valves, she thought, but also to conclusions reached too quickly.<br><br>Before leaving, they walked the route once more. Nia added arrows showing normal flow and photographed each valve with its label visible. Mr. Vale suggested keeping the paper map too. A phone could lose power during the next emergency, and a diagram on the wall could be read by whoever arrived first. Their two records served the same facts under different conditions.<br><br>The following morning, Nia passed Mr. Vale in the courtyard. He was tightening the hose connection he had complained about. This time his concentrated expression did not seem accusing. She asked whether the map needed a line for the outdoor tap. He handed her the blue tape."},
147: {skill_tested: "Identify central theme.", question: "Which theme is most central to the passage?", option1: "Careful evidence can correct both practical problems and premature judgments.", option2: "Old buildings should always be replaced.", option3: "Public praise is the best reward for skill.", option4: "Speed matters more than caution during emergencies.", correctAnswer: "Careful evidence can correct both practical problems and premature judgments.", explanation: "Step 1: Nia solves the outage and revises her opinion through evidence.<br><br>Answer: Careful evidence can correct both practical problems and premature judgments.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> In the selection below, a teenager helps during an apartment-building water outage.<br><br>When every faucet in Building C coughed air at once, Nia blamed Mr. Vale. He lived in the ground-floor apartment beside the utility room and had complained for weeks about people leaving the courtyard hose running. At the emergency meeting, he stood near the back holding a ring of keys, his expression so unsurprised that Nia mistook calm for satisfaction.<br><br>The property manager was across town. A plumber could not arrive until evening. Mr. Vale said the main valve was open, so the blockage must be elsewhere. Several residents answered at once, offering theories about the roof tank and the road repairs. Mr. Vale waited, then asked Nia whether she still made maps for the school newspaper. She said she did, though she did not see what that had to do with water.<br><br>He led her into the basement corridor. Pipes crossed the ceiling, disappearing behind walls and reappearing under stairwells. Their old labels had peeled away. Mr. Vale handed Nia a roll of blue tape and asked her to mark each branch while he tested valves. She drew the building as a set of boxes, adding lines wherever a pipe vanished. The work felt less like plumbing than tracing an argument whose middle sentences had been erased.<br><br>At the first valve, nothing changed. At the second, a pipe shuddered but remained silent. Nia heard residents upstairs opening taps after each test, then calling down the same answer. No water. Mr. Vale never rushed. He touched the pipes, listened, and wrote the result beside Nia's lines.<br><br>They found an unexpected branch behind a storage cabinet. Moving the cabinet revealed a narrow access panel painted the color of the wall. Inside, a valve handle sat halfway between open and closed. Nia reached for it, but Mr. Vale stopped her. If it fed the damaged laundry line, opening it could flood the basement. They needed evidence before motion.<br><br>Nia returned to the courtyard and compared apartment numbers with her map. The dry taps formed no simple vertical stack. Then a mother from the third floor mentioned that her kitchen tap had failed before her bathroom tap. That detail placed the interruption beyond the roof tank but before two branches separated. Nia circled the hidden valve.<br><br>Back downstairs, Mr. Vale turned it slowly while Nia watched the laundry pipe. It stayed dry. Above them came a distant thump, then a cheer traveling floor by floor as water reached the apartments. Someone must have brushed the handle while moving the cabinet days earlier, gradually narrowing the flow until sediment completed the blockage.<br><br>At the meeting that evening, residents praised Mr. Vale. He corrected them, pointing to Nia's map. She wanted to apologize for suspecting him, but the words seemed designed to make him comfort her. Instead, she asked why he had looked unsurprised that morning.<br><br>Mr. Vale lifted the key ring. He had managed a theater building years ago, he explained, and old buildings were always hiding one more route than their plans admitted. Surprise was expensive during an outage. Curiosity was more useful.<br><br>Nia taped a clean copy of the map inside the utility-room door. Beside the newly labeled valve, she added a note: Check before turning. Mr. Vale read it and nodded. The sentence applied to valves, she thought, but also to conclusions reached too quickly.<br><br>Before leaving, they walked the route once more. Nia added arrows showing normal flow and photographed each valve with its label visible. Mr. Vale suggested keeping the paper map too. A phone could lose power during the next emergency, and a diagram on the wall could be read by whoever arrived first. Their two records served the same facts under different conditions.<br><br>The following morning, Nia passed Mr. Vale in the courtyard. He was tightening the hose connection he had complained about. This time his concentrated expression did not seem accusing. She asked whether the map needed a line for the outdoor tap. He handed her the blue tape."},
148: {skill_tested: "Analyze characterization.", question: "Mr. Vale stops Nia from turning the hidden valve chiefly because he is:", option1: "unwilling to let younger people help.", option2: "methodical about consequences that have not yet been ruled out.", option3: "certain the valve is unrelated.", option4: "afraid residents will hear the pipes.", correctAnswer: "methodical about consequences that have not yet been ruled out.", explanation: "Step 1: He requires evidence before an action that might cause flooding.<br><br>Answer: methodical about consequences that have not yet been ruled out.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Character and Relationship Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> In the selection below, a teenager helps during an apartment-building water outage.<br><br>When every faucet in Building C coughed air at once, Nia blamed Mr. Vale. He lived in the ground-floor apartment beside the utility room and had complained for weeks about people leaving the courtyard hose running. At the emergency meeting, he stood near the back holding a ring of keys, his expression so unsurprised that Nia mistook calm for satisfaction.<br><br>The property manager was across town. A plumber could not arrive until evening. Mr. Vale said the main valve was open, so the blockage must be elsewhere. Several residents answered at once, offering theories about the roof tank and the road repairs. Mr. Vale waited, then asked Nia whether she still made maps for the school newspaper. She said she did, though she did not see what that had to do with water.<br><br>He led her into the basement corridor. Pipes crossed the ceiling, disappearing behind walls and reappearing under stairwells. Their old labels had peeled away. Mr. Vale handed Nia a roll of blue tape and asked her to mark each branch while he tested valves. She drew the building as a set of boxes, adding lines wherever a pipe vanished. The work felt less like plumbing than tracing an argument whose middle sentences had been erased.<br><br>At the first valve, nothing changed. At the second, a pipe shuddered but remained silent. Nia heard residents upstairs opening taps after each test, then calling down the same answer. No water. Mr. Vale never rushed. He touched the pipes, listened, and wrote the result beside Nia's lines.<br><br>They found an unexpected branch behind a storage cabinet. Moving the cabinet revealed a narrow access panel painted the color of the wall. Inside, a valve handle sat halfway between open and closed. Nia reached for it, but Mr. Vale stopped her. If it fed the damaged laundry line, opening it could flood the basement. They needed evidence before motion.<br><br>Nia returned to the courtyard and compared apartment numbers with her map. The dry taps formed no simple vertical stack. Then a mother from the third floor mentioned that her kitchen tap had failed before her bathroom tap. That detail placed the interruption beyond the roof tank but before two branches separated. Nia circled the hidden valve.<br><br>Back downstairs, Mr. Vale turned it slowly while Nia watched the laundry pipe. It stayed dry. Above them came a distant thump, then a cheer traveling floor by floor as water reached the apartments. Someone must have brushed the handle while moving the cabinet days earlier, gradually narrowing the flow until sediment completed the blockage.<br><br>At the meeting that evening, residents praised Mr. Vale. He corrected them, pointing to Nia's map. She wanted to apologize for suspecting him, but the words seemed designed to make him comfort her. Instead, she asked why he had looked unsurprised that morning.<br><br>Mr. Vale lifted the key ring. He had managed a theater building years ago, he explained, and old buildings were always hiding one more route than their plans admitted. Surprise was expensive during an outage. Curiosity was more useful.<br><br>Nia taped a clean copy of the map inside the utility-room door. Beside the newly labeled valve, she added a note: Check before turning. Mr. Vale read it and nodded. The sentence applied to valves, she thought, but also to conclusions reached too quickly.<br><br>Before leaving, they walked the route once more. Nia added arrows showing normal flow and photographed each valve with its label visible. Mr. Vale suggested keeping the paper map too. A phone could lose power during the next emergency, and a diagram on the wall could be read by whoever arrived first. Their two records served the same facts under different conditions.<br><br>The following morning, Nia passed Mr. Vale in the courtyard. He was tightening the hose connection he had complained about. This time his concentrated expression did not seem accusing. She asked whether the map needed a line for the outdoor tap. He handed her the blue tape."},
149: {skill_tested: "Trace reasoning.", question: "The third-floor resident's observation helps Nia by:", option1: "proving the roof tank is empty.", option2: "identifying who moved the cabinet.", option3: "locating the interruption relative to where pipe branches divide.", option4: "showing every apartment failed simultaneously.", correctAnswer: "locating the interruption relative to where pipe branches divide.", explanation: "Step 1: Different failure timing narrows the blockage location.<br><br>Answer: locating the interruption relative to where pipe branches divide.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> In the selection below, a teenager helps during an apartment-building water outage.<br><br>When every faucet in Building C coughed air at once, Nia blamed Mr. Vale. He lived in the ground-floor apartment beside the utility room and had complained for weeks about people leaving the courtyard hose running. At the emergency meeting, he stood near the back holding a ring of keys, his expression so unsurprised that Nia mistook calm for satisfaction.<br><br>The property manager was across town. A plumber could not arrive until evening. Mr. Vale said the main valve was open, so the blockage must be elsewhere. Several residents answered at once, offering theories about the roof tank and the road repairs. Mr. Vale waited, then asked Nia whether she still made maps for the school newspaper. She said she did, though she did not see what that had to do with water.<br><br>He led her into the basement corridor. Pipes crossed the ceiling, disappearing behind walls and reappearing under stairwells. Their old labels had peeled away. Mr. Vale handed Nia a roll of blue tape and asked her to mark each branch while he tested valves. She drew the building as a set of boxes, adding lines wherever a pipe vanished. The work felt less like plumbing than tracing an argument whose middle sentences had been erased.<br><br>At the first valve, nothing changed. At the second, a pipe shuddered but remained silent. Nia heard residents upstairs opening taps after each test, then calling down the same answer. No water. Mr. Vale never rushed. He touched the pipes, listened, and wrote the result beside Nia's lines.<br><br>They found an unexpected branch behind a storage cabinet. Moving the cabinet revealed a narrow access panel painted the color of the wall. Inside, a valve handle sat halfway between open and closed. Nia reached for it, but Mr. Vale stopped her. If it fed the damaged laundry line, opening it could flood the basement. They needed evidence before motion.<br><br>Nia returned to the courtyard and compared apartment numbers with her map. The dry taps formed no simple vertical stack. Then a mother from the third floor mentioned that her kitchen tap had failed before her bathroom tap. That detail placed the interruption beyond the roof tank but before two branches separated. Nia circled the hidden valve.<br><br>Back downstairs, Mr. Vale turned it slowly while Nia watched the laundry pipe. It stayed dry. Above them came a distant thump, then a cheer traveling floor by floor as water reached the apartments. Someone must have brushed the handle while moving the cabinet days earlier, gradually narrowing the flow until sediment completed the blockage.<br><br>At the meeting that evening, residents praised Mr. Vale. He corrected them, pointing to Nia's map. She wanted to apologize for suspecting him, but the words seemed designed to make him comfort her. Instead, she asked why he had looked unsurprised that morning.<br><br>Mr. Vale lifted the key ring. He had managed a theater building years ago, he explained, and old buildings were always hiding one more route than their plans admitted. Surprise was expensive during an outage. Curiosity was more useful.<br><br>Nia taped a clean copy of the map inside the utility-room door. Beside the newly labeled valve, she added a note: Check before turning. Mr. Vale read it and nodded. The sentence applied to valves, she thought, but also to conclusions reached too quickly.<br><br>Before leaving, they walked the route once more. Nia added arrows showing normal flow and photographed each valve with its label visible. Mr. Vale suggested keeping the paper map too. A phone could lose power during the next emergency, and a diagram on the wall could be read by whoever arrived first. Their two records served the same facts under different conditions.<br><br>The following morning, Nia passed Mr. Vale in the courtyard. He was tightening the hose connection he had complained about. This time his concentrated expression did not seem accusing. She asked whether the map needed a line for the outdoor tap. He handed her the blue tape."},
150: {skill_tested: "Analyze structure.", question: "The traveling cheer after the valve turns primarily:", option1: "introduces a second plumbing failure.", option2: "shows residents did not notice the outage.", option3: "causes Nia to discard the map.", option4: "confirms the solution through a sound that moves across the building.", correctAnswer: "confirms the solution through a sound that moves across the building.", explanation: "Step 1: The sequence makes restored flow audible and spatial.<br><br>Answer: confirms the solution through a sound that moves across the building.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> In the selection below, a teenager helps during an apartment-building water outage.<br><br>When every faucet in Building C coughed air at once, Nia blamed Mr. Vale. He lived in the ground-floor apartment beside the utility room and had complained for weeks about people leaving the courtyard hose running. At the emergency meeting, he stood near the back holding a ring of keys, his expression so unsurprised that Nia mistook calm for satisfaction.<br><br>The property manager was across town. A plumber could not arrive until evening. Mr. Vale said the main valve was open, so the blockage must be elsewhere. Several residents answered at once, offering theories about the roof tank and the road repairs. Mr. Vale waited, then asked Nia whether she still made maps for the school newspaper. She said she did, though she did not see what that had to do with water.<br><br>He led her into the basement corridor. Pipes crossed the ceiling, disappearing behind walls and reappearing under stairwells. Their old labels had peeled away. Mr. Vale handed Nia a roll of blue tape and asked her to mark each branch while he tested valves. She drew the building as a set of boxes, adding lines wherever a pipe vanished. The work felt less like plumbing than tracing an argument whose middle sent