ide the newly labeled valve, she added a note: Check before turning. Mr. Vale read it and nodded. The sentence applied to valves, she thought, but also to conclusions reached too quickly.<br><br>Before leaving, they walked the route once more. Nia added arrows showing normal flow and photographed each valve with its label visible. Mr. Vale suggested keeping the paper map too. A phone could lose power during the next emergency, and a diagram on the wall could be read by whoever arrived first. Their two records served the same facts under different conditions.<br><br>The following morning, Nia passed Mr. Vale in the courtyard. He was tightening the hose connection he had complained about. This time his concentrated expression did not seem accusing. She asked whether the map needed a line for the outdoor tap. He handed her the blue tape."},
153: {skill_tested: "Synthesize character development.", question: "Nia's development is best described as a movement from:", option1: "avoiding responsibility to accepting public praise.", option2: "trusting maps to rejecting all plans.", option3: "assigning blame from appearances to testing connections before concluding.", option4: "working independently to refusing Mr. Vale's help.", correctAnswer: "assigning blame from appearances to testing connections before concluding.", explanation: "Step 1: Her investigative method becomes an ethical lesson about judgment.<br><br>Answer: assigning blame from appearances to testing connections before concluding.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Character and Relationship Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> In the selection below, a teenager helps during an apartment-building water outage.<br><br>When every faucet in Building C coughed air at once, Nia blamed Mr. Vale. He lived in the ground-floor apartment beside the utility room and had complained for weeks about people leaving the courtyard hose running. At the emergency meeting, he stood near the back holding a ring of keys, his expression so unsurprised that Nia mistook calm for satisfaction.<br><br>The property manager was across town. A plumber could not arrive until evening. Mr. Vale said the main valve was open, so the blockage must be elsewhere. Several residents answered at once, offering theories about the roof tank and the road repairs. Mr. Vale waited, then asked Nia whether she still made maps for the school newspaper. She said she did, though she did not see what that had to do with water.<br><br>He led her into the basement corridor. Pipes crossed the ceiling, disappearing behind walls and reappearing under stairwells. Their old labels had peeled away. Mr. Vale handed Nia a roll of blue tape and asked her to mark each branch while he tested valves. She drew the building as a set of boxes, adding lines wherever a pipe vanished. The work felt less like plumbing than tracing an argument whose middle sentences had been erased.<br><br>At the first valve, nothing changed. At the second, a pipe shuddered but remained silent. Nia heard residents upstairs opening taps after each test, then calling down the same answer. No water. Mr. Vale never rushed. He touched the pipes, listened, and wrote the result beside Nia's lines.<br><br>They found an unexpected branch behind a storage cabinet. Moving the cabinet revealed a narrow access panel painted the color of the wall. Inside, a valve handle sat halfway between open and closed. Nia reached for it, but Mr. Vale stopped her. If it fed the damaged laundry line, opening it could flood the basement. They needed evidence before motion.<br><br>Nia returned to the courtyard and compared apartment numbers with her map. The dry taps formed no simple vertical stack. Then a mother from the third floor mentioned that her kitchen tap had failed before her bathroom tap. That detail placed the interruption beyond the roof tank but before two branches separated. Nia circled the hidden valve.<br><br>Back downstairs, Mr. Vale turned it slowly while Nia watched the laundry pipe. It stayed dry. Above them came a distant thump, then a cheer traveling floor by floor as water reached the apartments. Someone must have brushed the handle while moving the cabinet days earlier, gradually narrowing the flow until sediment completed the blockage.<br><br>At the meeting that evening, residents praised Mr. Vale. He corrected them, pointing to Nia's map. She wanted to apologize for suspecting him, but the words seemed designed to make him comfort her. Instead, she asked why he had looked unsurprised that morning.<br><br>Mr. Vale lifted the key ring. He had managed a theater building years ago, he explained, and old buildings were always hiding one more route than their plans admitted. Surprise was expensive during an outage. Curiosity was more useful.<br><br>Nia taped a clean copy of the map inside the utility-room door. Beside the newly labeled valve, she added a note: Check before turning. Mr. Vale read it and nodded. The sentence applied to valves, she thought, but also to conclusions reached too quickly.<br><br>Before leaving, they walked the route once more. Nia added arrows showing normal flow and photographed each valve with its label visible. Mr. Vale suggested keeping the paper map too. A phone could lose power during the next emergency, and a diagram on the wall could be read by whoever arrived first. Their two records served the same facts under different conditions.<br><br>The following morning, Nia passed Mr. Vale in the courtyard. He was tightening the hose connection he had complained about. This time his concentrated expression did not seem accusing. She asked whether the map needed a line for the outdoor tap. He handed her the blue tape."},
154: {skill_tested: "Identify a stated contrast in characters' work methods.", question: "According to the third paragraph, how do Julian's and Mara's approaches differ?", option1: "Julian reads every paper, while Mara tests projector equipment.", option2: "Julian wants to keep everything, while Mara wants to discard everything.", option3: "Julian works only upstairs, while Mara refuses to enter the lobby.", option4: "Julian documents equipment for sale, while Mara examines objects and papers for personal meaning.", correctAnswer: "Julian documents equipment for sale, while Mara examines objects and papers for personal meaning.", explanation: "Step 1: Julian tests, records, and photographs equipment.<br><br>Step 2: Mara opens drawers and attends to found objects and unsent notes.<br><br>Answer: Julian documents equipment for sale, while Mara examines objects and papers for personal meaning.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Character and Relationship Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> This passage describes two siblings clearing their late father's neighborhood cinema.<br><br>Mara arrived at the Rialto with boxes folded flat beneath her arm. Julian was already inside, standing on the concession counter to remove the hand-painted price board. He had labeled three piles—KEEP, SELL, DISCARD—in chalk on the lobby floor. Mara set her boxes directly across the lines. Julian looked down at them, then at her, and climbed from the counter without saying hello.<br><br>They had not worked together since they were teenagers, when their father assigned Mara the ticket window and Julian the projectors. Mara remembered Julian emerging from the booth only between reels, smelling of warm dust. Julian remembered Mara letting neighborhood children enter with half-price tickets when their father was not watching. Each had privately considered the other's job the easier one.<br><br>Now the cinema had been closed for seven months. A buyer wanted the building empty by Friday. Julian moved quickly, testing equipment, checking serial numbers, and photographing anything that might be sold. Mara opened drawers. She found birthday candles, umbrellas, a single silver shoe, and envelopes containing notes their father had never mailed. Julian called these discoveries delays. Mara called his photographs another way of refusing to look.<br><br>The argument began over a dented metal film can. Julian placed it in DISCARD. Mara retrieved it, recognizing the faded title of the first movie their father had shown after buying the Rialto. The can was empty, but she insisted it belonged in KEEP. Julian said that if memory were enough, they would have to preserve every broken chair. Mara accused him of treating the cinema like a failed machine.<br><br>Julian carried the can upstairs to the projection booth. Mara followed, prepared to continue. Instead, he opened a cabinet containing rows of notebooks. For every screening, their father had recorded attendance, bulb hours, sound problems, and complaints. In the margins Julian had added instructions in his own narrow handwriting. He explained that during their father's last year, he had returned each Sunday to inspect the projectors, though the two of them had barely spoken. The photographs downstairs were for an equipment archive he intended to give the local film club.<br><br>Mara sat in the operator's chair. She had interpreted Julian's speed as indifference because it resembled his silence, and she had always treated silence as empty. The notebooks made that interpretation harder. Julian, meanwhile, turned the film can over and discovered a date scratched into its base. It was not the opening-night can Mara remembered; it came from a repair made years later. Her certainty had supplied a history the object did not possess.<br><br>They returned to the lobby and erased the three chalk labels. In their place they made four: SELL, RECYCLE, CLUB ARCHIVE, FAMILY. The empty can went into FAMILY—not because Mara's story was accurate, Julian said, but because the argument now belonged to them. Mara placed the price board in CLUB ARCHIVE after Julian showed her the pencil calculations on its back.<br><br>By evening, their methods had not merged. Julian still photographed each projector part before packing it, and Mara still read every scrap of paper. But he began bringing her objects whose histories he could not identify, while she stopped assuming that obscurity made an object precious. They worked at different speeds, crossing the lobby with fewer collisions.<br><br>Neither apologized directly, but each had begun asking questions where accusation had previously supplied an answer.<br><br>Before leaving, Julian switched on the auditorium aisle lights. Most bulbs had failed, producing a broken path toward the screen. Mara resisted the urge to turn the pattern into a symbol. She simply stood beside him while the remaining lights warmed. On Friday, the building would be empty. Nothing they sorted could prevent that. Still, the four labeled areas behind them held a record neither would have made alone: not the cinema preserved, but two incomplete ways of remembering made answerable to each other."},
155: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of a descriptive term.", question: "As used in the final paragraph, the word 'restrained' most nearly describes an action that is:", option1: "deliberately kept from becoming overly symbolic or emotional.", option2: "physically prevented by broken lights.", option3: "performed with great speed.", option4: "intended to preserve the building unchanged.", correctAnswer: "deliberately kept from becoming overly symbolic or emotional.", explanation: "Step 1: Mara feels an urge to turn the broken lights into a symbol but resists it.<br><br>Step 2: Her response remains controlled and limited to standing beside Julian, so 'restrained' describes deliberate emotional moderation.<br><br>Answer: deliberately kept from becoming overly symbolic or emotional.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> This passage describes two siblings clearing their late father's neighborhood cinema.<br><br>Mara arrived at the Rialto with boxes folded flat beneath her arm. Julian was already inside, standing on the concession counter to remove the hand-painted price board. He had labeled three piles—KEEP, SELL, DISCARD—in chalk on the lobby floor. Mara set her boxes directly across the lines. Julian looked down at them, then at her, and climbed from the counter without saying hello.<br><br>They had not worked together since they were teenagers, when their father assigned Mara the ticket window and Julian the projectors. Mara remembered Julian emerging from the booth only between reels, smelling of warm dust. Julian remembered Mara letting neighborhood children enter with half-price tickets when their father was not watching. Each had privately considered the other's job the easier one.<br><br>Now the cinema had been closed for seven months. A buyer wanted the building empty by Friday. Julian moved quickly, testing equipment, checking serial numbers, and photographing anything that might be sold. Mara opened drawers. She found birthday candles, umbrellas, a single silver shoe, and envelopes containing notes their father had never mailed. Julian called these discoveries delays. Mara called his photographs another way of refusing to look.<br><br>The argument began over a dented metal film can. Julian placed it in DISCARD. Mara retrieved it, recognizing the faded title of the first movie their father had shown after buying the Rialto. The can was empty, but she insisted it belonged in KEEP. Julian said that if memory were enough, they would have to preserve every broken chair. Mara accused him of treating the cinema like a failed machine.<br><br>Julian carried the can upstairs to the projection booth. Mara followed, prepared to continue. Instead, he opened a cabinet containing rows of notebooks. For every screening, their father had recorded attendance, bulb hours, sound problems, and complaints. In the margins Julian had added instructions in his own narrow handwriting. He explained that during their father's last year, he had returned each Sunday to inspect the projectors, though the two of them had barely spoken. The photographs downstairs were for an equipment archive he intended to give the local film club.<br><br>Mara sat in the operator's chair. She had interpreted Julian's speed as indifference because it resembled his silence, and she had always treated silence as empty. The notebooks made that interpretation harder. Julian, meanwhile, turned the film can over and discovered a date scratched into its base. It was not the opening-night can Mara remembered; it came from a repair made years later. Her certainty had supplied a history the object did not possess.<br><br>They returned to the lobby and erased the three chalk labels. In their place they made four: SELL, RECYCLE, CLUB ARCHIVE, FAMILY. The empty can went into FAMILY—not because Mara's story was accurate, Julian said, but because the argument now belonged to them. Mara placed the price board in CLUB ARCHIVE after Julian showed her the pencil calculations on its back.<br><br>By evening, their methods had not merged. Julian still photographed each projector part before packing it, and Mara still read every scrap of paper. But he began bringing her objects whose histories he could not identify, while she stopped assuming that obscurity made an object precious. They worked at different speeds, crossing the lobby with fewer collisions.<br><br>Neither apologized directly, but each had begun asking questions where accusation had previously supplied an answer.<br><br>Before leaving, Julian switched on the auditorium aisle lights. Most bulbs had failed, producing a broken path toward the screen. Mara resisted the urge to turn the pattern into a symbol. She simply stood beside him while the remaining lights warmed. On Friday, the building would be empty. Nothing they sorted could prevent that. Still, the four labeled areas behind them held a record neither would have made alone: not the cinema preserved, but two incomplete ways of remembering made answerable to each other."},
156: {skill_tested: "Identify a central theme about memory and relationships.", question: "Which statement best expresses a central theme of the passage?", option1: "Accurate records make personal memories unnecessary.", option2: "Different, incomplete ways of remembering can correct and deepen one another.", option3: "Family conflict ends only when inherited property is preserved.", option4: "Objects possess meaning only when their histories can be proven exactly.", correctAnswer: "Different, incomplete ways of remembering can correct and deepen one another.", explanation: "Step 1: Mara values stories and objects but invents an inaccurate history for the film can.<br><br>Step 2: Julian values documentation but initially dismisses items whose significance is not operational.<br><br>Answer: Different, incomplete ways of remembering can correct and deepen one another.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> This passage describes two siblings clearing their late father's neighborhood cinema.<br><br>Mara arrived at the Rialto with boxes folded flat beneath her arm. Julian was already inside, standing on the concession counter to remove the hand-painted price board. He had labeled three piles—KEEP, SELL, DISCARD—in chalk on the lobby floor. Mara set her boxes directly across the lines. Julian looked down at them, then at her, and climbed from the counter without saying hello.<br><br>They had not worked together since they were teenagers, when their father assigned Mara the ticket window and Julian the projectors. Mara remembered Julian emerging from the booth only between reels, smelling of warm dust. Julian remembered Mara letting neighborhood children enter with half-price tickets when their father was not watching. Each had privately considered the other's job the easier one.<br><br>Now the cinema had been closed for seven months. A buyer wanted the building empty by Friday. Julian moved quickly, testing equipment, checking serial numbers, and photographing anything that might be sold. Mara opened drawers. She found birthday candles, umbrellas, a single silver shoe, and envelopes containing notes their father had never mailed. Julian called these discoveries delays. Mara called his photographs another way of refusing to look.<br><br>The argument began over a dented metal film can. Julian placed it in DISCARD. Mara retrieved it, recognizing the faded title of the first movie their father had shown after buying the Rialto. The can was empty, but she insisted it belonged in KEEP. Julian said that if memory were enough, they would have to preserve every broken chair. Mara accused him of treating the cinema like a failed machine.<br><br>Julian carried the can upstairs to the projection booth. Mara followed, prepared to continue. Instead, he opened a cabinet containing rows of notebooks. For every screening, their father had recorded attendance, bulb hours, sound problems, and complaints. In the margins Julian had added instructions in his own narrow handwriting. He explained that during their father's last year, he had returned each Sunday to inspect the projectors, though the two of them had barely spoken. The photographs downstairs were for an equipment archive he intended to give the local film club.<br><br>Mara sat in the operator's chair. She had interpreted Julian's speed as indifference because it resembled his silence, and she had always treated silence as empty. The notebooks made that interpretation harder. Julian, meanwhile, turned the film can over and discovered a date scratched into its base. It was not the opening-night can Mara remembered; it came from a repair made years later. Her certainty had supplied a history the object did not possess.<br><br>They returned to the lobby and erased the three chalk labels. In their place they made four: SELL, RECYCLE, CLUB ARCHIVE, FAMILY. The empty can went into FAMILY—not because Mara's story was accurate, Julian said, but because the argument now belonged to them. Mara placed the price board in CLUB ARCHIVE after Julian showed her the pencil calculations on its back.<br><br>By evening, their methods had not merged. Julian still photographed each projector part before packing it, and Mara still read every scrap of paper. But he began bringing her objects whose histories he could not identify, while she stopped assuming that obscurity made an object precious. They worked at different speeds, crossing the lobby with fewer collisions.<br><br>Neither apologized directly, but each had begun asking questions where accusation had previously supplied an answer.<br><br>Before leaving, Julian switched on the auditorium aisle lights. Most bulbs had failed, producing a broken path toward the screen. Mara resisted the urge to turn the pattern into a symbol. She simply stood beside him while the remaining lights warmed. On Friday, the building would be empty. Nothing they sorted could prevent that. Still, the four labeled areas behind them held a record neither would have made alone: not the cinema preserved, but two incomplete ways of remembering made answerable to each other."},
157: {skill_tested: "Infer how childhood roles shaped a later misunderstanding.", question: "The second paragraph suggests that Mara and Julian's adult conflict is partly rooted in:", option1: "their father having forbidden them to work inside the cinema.", option2: "both wanting to operate the projectors as teenagers.", option3: "each having underestimated the complexity of the other's earlier responsibilities.", option4: "Mara having kept formal records of every discounted ticket.", correctAnswer: "each having underestimated the complexity of the other's earlier responsibilities.", explanation: "Step 1: Mara worked at the ticket window while Julian stayed near the projectors.<br><br>Step 2: Each privately judged the other's assignment easier.<br><br>Step 3: Their adult tendency to treat the other's method as deficient continues this long-standing failure to see the work behind it.<br><br>Answer: each having underestimated the complexity of the other's earlier responsibilities.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Character and Relationship Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> This passage describes two siblings clearing their late father's neighborhood cinema.<br><br>Mara arrived at the Rialto with boxes folded flat beneath her arm. Julian was already inside, standing on the concession counter to remove the hand-painted price board. He had labeled three piles—KEEP, SELL, DISCARD—in chalk on the lobby floor. Mara set her boxes directly across the lines. Julian looked down at them, then at her, and climbed from the counter without saying hello.<br><br>They had not worked together since they were teenagers, when their father assigned Mara the ticket window and Julian the projectors. Mara remembered Julian emerging from the booth only between reels, smelling of warm dust. Julian remembered Mara letting neighborhood children enter with half-price tickets when their father was not watching. Each had privately considered the other's job the easier one.<br><br>Now the cinema had been closed for seven months. A buyer wanted the building empty by Friday. Julian moved quickly, testing equipment, checking serial numbers, and photographing anything that might be sold. Mara opened drawers. She found birthday candles, umbrellas, a single silver shoe, and envelopes containing notes their father had never mailed. Julian called these discoveries delays. Mara called his photographs another way of refusing to look.<br><br>The argument began over a dented metal film can. Julian placed it in DISCARD. Mara retrieved it, recognizing the faded title of the first movie their father had shown after buying the Rialto. The can was empty, but she insisted it belonged in KEEP. Julian said that if memory were enough, they would have to preserve every broken chair. Mara accused him of treating the cinema like a failed machine.<br><br>Julian carried the can upstairs to the projection booth. Mara followed, prepared to continue. Instead, he opened a cabinet containing rows of notebooks. For every screening, their father had recorded attendance, bulb hours, sound problems, and complaints. In the margins Julian had added instructions in his own narrow handwriting. He explained that during their father's last year, he had returned each Sunday to inspect the projectors, though the two of them had barely spoken. The photographs downstairs were for an equipment archive he intended to give the local film club.<br><br>Mara sat in the operator's chair. She had interpreted Julian's speed as indifference because it resembled his silence, and she had always treated silence as empty. The notebooks made that interpretation harder. Julian, meanwhile, turned the film can over and discovered a date scratched into its base. It was not the opening-night can Mara remembered; it came from a repair made years later. Her certainty had supplied a history the object did not possess.<br><br>They returned to the lobby and erased the three chalk labels. In their place they made four: SELL, RECYCLE, CLUB ARCHIVE, FAMILY. The empty can went into FAMILY—not because Mara's story was accurate, Julian said, but because the argument now belonged to them. Mara placed the price board in CLUB ARCHIVE after Julian showed her the pencil calculations on its back.<br><br>By evening, their methods had not merged. Julian still photographed each projector part before packing it, and Mara still read every scrap of paper. But he began bringing her objects whose histories he could not identify, while she stopped assuming that obscurity made an object precious. They worked at different speeds, crossing the lobby with fewer collisions.<br><br>Neither apologized directly, but each had begun asking questions where accusation had previously supplied an answer.<br><br>Before leaving, Julian switched on the auditorium aisle lights. Most bulbs had failed, producing a broken path toward the screen. Mara resisted the urge to turn the pattern into a symbol. She simply stood beside him while the remaining lights warmed. On Friday, the building would be empty. Nothing they sorted could prevent that. Still, the four labeled areas behind them held a record neither would have made alone: not the cinema preserved, but two incomplete ways of remembering made answerable to each other."},
158: {skill_tested: "Determine how documentary evidence revises a character judgment.", question: "The notebooks most directly challenge Mara's belief that Julian:", option1: "cannot operate or maintain the projectors.", option2: "remembers the cinema's opening night accurately.", option3: "has already donated every record to the film club.", option4: "is indifferent to the cinema and interested only in disposing of equipment.", correctAnswer: "is indifferent to the cinema and interested only in disposing of equipment.", explanation: "Step 1: Mara interprets Julian's speed and photographs as refusal to look or care.<br><br>Step 2: The notebooks reveal years of maintenance, and his photographs are intended for a community archive.<br><br>Step 3: His care has taken documentary and technical forms that Mara did not recognize.<br><br>Answer: is indifferent to the cinema and interested only in disposing of equipment.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> This passage describes two siblings clearing their late father's neighborhood cinema.<br><br>Mara arrived at the Rialto with boxes folded flat beneath her arm. Julian was already inside, standing on the concession counter to remove the hand-painted price board. He had labeled three piles—KEEP, SELL, DISCARD—in chalk on the lobby floor. Mara set her boxes directly across the lines. Julian looked down at them, then at her, and climbed from the counter without saying hello.<br><br>They had not worked together since they were teenagers, when their father assigned Mara the ticket window and Julian the projectors. Mara remembered Julian emerging from the booth only between reels, smelling of warm dust. Julian remembered Mara letting neighborhood children enter with half-price tickets when their father was not watching. Each had privately considered the other's job the easier one.<br><br>Now the cinema had been closed for seven months. A buyer wanted the building empty by Friday. Julian moved quickly, testing equipment, checking serial numbers, and photographing anything that might be sold. Mara opened drawers. She found birthday candles, umbrellas, a single silver shoe, and envelopes containing notes their father had never mailed. Julian called these discoveries delays. Mara called his photographs another way of refusing to look.<br><br>The argument began over a dented metal film can. Julian placed it in DISCARD. Mara retrieved it, recognizing the faded title of the first movie their father had shown after buying the Rialto. The can was empty, but she insisted it belonged in KEEP. Julian said that if memory were enough, they would have to preserve every broken chair. Mara accused him of treating the cinema like a failed machine.<br><br>Julian carried the can upstairs to the projection booth. Mara followed, prepared to continue. Instead, he opened a cabinet containing rows of notebooks. For every screening, their father had recorded attendance, bulb hours, sound problems, and complaints. In the margins Julian had added instructions in his own narrow handwriting. He explained that during their father's last year, he had returned each Sunday to inspect the projectors, though the two of them had barely spoken. The photographs downstairs were for an equipment archive he intended to give the local film club.<br><br>Mara sat in the operator's chair. She had interpreted Julian's speed as indifference because it resembled his silence, and she had always treated silence as empty. The notebooks made that interpretation harder. Julian, meanwhile, turned the film can over and discovered a date scratched into its base. It was not the opening-night can Mara remembered; it came from a repair made years later. Her certainty had supplied a history the object did not possess.<br><br>They returned to the lobby and erased the three chalk labels. In their place they made four: SELL, RECYCLE, CLUB ARCHIVE, FAMILY. The empty can went into FAMILY—not because Mara's story was accurate, Julian said, but because the argument now belonged to them. Mara placed the price board in CLUB ARCHIVE after Julian showed her the pencil calculations on its back.<br><br>By evening, their methods had not merged. Julian still photographed each projector part before packing it, and Mara still read every scrap of paper. But he began bringing her objects whose histories he could not identify, while she stopped assuming that obscurity made an object precious. They worked at different speeds, crossing the lobby with fewer collisions.<br><br>Neither apologized directly, but each had begun asking questions where accusation had previously supplied an answer.<br><br>Before leaving, Julian switched on the auditorium aisle lights. Most bulbs had failed, producing a broken path toward the screen. Mara resisted the urge to turn the pattern into a symbol. She simply stood beside him while the remaining lights warmed. On Friday, the building would be empty. Nothing they sorted could prevent that. Still, the four labeled areas behind them held a record neither would have made alone: not the cinema preserved, but two incomplete ways of remembering made answerable to each other."},
159: {skill_tested: "Analyze how one object exposes weaknesses in both perspectives.", question: "The empty film can primarily serves to show that:", option1: "Mara's memory can invent significance while Julian's initial sorting can dismiss an object before examining it.", option2: "every discarded cinema object contains an accurate family history.", option3: "Julian remembers opening night more clearly than Mara does.", option4: "technical records are less dependable than faded labels.", correctAnswer: "Mara's memory can invent significance while Julian's initial sorting can dismiss an object before examining it.", explanation: "Step 1: Mara confidently assigns the can an opening-night history that its scratched date disproves.<br><br>Step 2: Julian initially discards it without noticing that date or the argument it will come to represent.<br><br>Step 3: The object exposes both sentimental overinterpretation and overly rapid classification.<br><br>Answer: Mara's memory can invent significance while Julian's initial sorting can dismiss an object before examining it.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> This passage describes two siblings clearing their late father's neighborhood cinema.<br><br>Mara arrived at the Rialto with boxes folded flat beneath her arm. Julian was already inside, standing on the concession counter to remove the hand-painted price board. He had labeled three piles—KEEP, SELL, DISCARD—in chalk on the lobby floor. Mara set her boxes directly across the lines. Julian looked down at them, then at her, and climbed from the counter without saying hello.<br><br>They had not worked together since they were teenagers, when their father assigned Mara the ticket window and Julian the projectors. Mara remembered Julian emerging from the booth only between reels, smelling of warm dust. Julian remembered Mara letting neighborhood children enter with half-price tickets when their father was not watching. Each had privately considered the other's job the easier one.<br><br>Now the cinema had been closed for seven months. A buyer wanted the building empty by Friday. Julian moved quickly, testing equipment, checking serial numbers, and photographing anything that might be sold. Mara opened drawers. She found birthday candles, umbrellas, a single silver shoe, and envelopes containing notes their father had never mailed. Julian called these discoveries delays. Mara called his photographs another way of refusing to look.<br><br>The argument began over a dented metal film can. Julian placed it in DISCARD. Mara retrieved it, recognizing the faded title of the first movie their father had shown after buying the Rialto. The can was empty, but she insisted it belonged in KEEP. Julian said that if memory were enough, they would have to preserve every broken chair. Mara accused him of treating the cinema like a failed machine.<br><br>Julian carried the can upstairs to the projection booth. Mara followed, prepared to continue. Instead, he opened a cabinet containing rows of notebooks. For every screening, their father had recorded attendance, bulb hours, sound problems, and complaints. In the margins Julian had added instructions in his own narrow handwriting. He explained that during their father's last year, he had returned each Sunday to inspect the projectors, though the two of them had barely spoken. The photographs downstairs were for an equipment archive he intended to give the local film club.<br><br>Mara sat in the operator's chair. She had interpreted Julian's speed as indifference because it resembled his silence, and she had always treated silence as empty. The notebooks made that interpretation harder. Julian, meanwhile, turned the film can over and discovered a date scratched into its base. It was not the opening-night can Mara remembered; it came from a repair made years later. Her certainty had supplied a history the object did not possess.<br><br>They returned to the lobby and erased the three chalk labels. In their place they made four: SELL, RECYCLE, CLUB ARCHIVE, FAMILY. The empty can went into FAMILY—not because Mara's story was accurate, Julian said, but because the argument now belonged to them. Mara placed the price board in CLUB ARCHIVE after Julian showed her the pencil calculations on its back.<br><br>By evening, their methods had not merged. Julian still photographed each projector part before packing it, and Mara still read every scrap of paper. But he began bringing her objects whose histories he could not identify, while she stopped assuming that obscurity made an object precious. They worked at different speeds, crossing the lobby with fewer collisions.<br><br>Neither apologized directly, but each had begun asking questions where accusation had previously supplied an answer.<br><br>Before leaving, Julian switched on the auditorium aisle lights. Most bulbs had failed, producing a broken path toward the screen. Mara resisted the urge to turn the pattern into a symbol. She simply stood beside him while the remaining lights warmed. On Friday, the building would be empty. Nothing they sorted could prevent that. Still, the four labeled areas behind them held a record neither would have made alone: not the cinema preserved, but two incomplete ways of remembering made answerable to each other."},
160: {skill_tested: "Trace how revised categories reflect relationship change.", question: "Replacing three sorting labels with four most strongly indicates that the siblings:", option1: "have agreed that every item belongs in the family collection.", option2: "are creating categories capable of representing both documentary and personal forms of value.", option3: "no longer plan to sell or recycle any cinema property.", option4: "have decided the film club should determine all classifications.", correctAnswer: "are creating categories capable of representing both documentary and personal forms of value.", explanation: "Step 1: The original labels reduce decisions to possession, sale, or disposal.<br><br>Step 2: CLUB ARCHIVE recognizes public documentary value, while FAMILY recognizes relational value that may not depend on an accurate origin story.<br><br>Step 3: The new system is a practical compromise shaped by both siblings' revised understanding.<br><br>Answer: are creating categories capable of representing both documentary and personal forms of value.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> This passage describes two siblings clearing their late father's neighborhood cinema.<br><br>Mara arrived at the Rialto with boxes folded flat beneath her arm. Julian was already inside, standing on the concession counter to remove the hand-painted price board. He had labeled three piles—KEEP, SELL, DISCARD—in chalk on the lobby floor. Mara set her boxes directly across the lines. Julian looked down at them, then at her, and climbed from the counter without saying hello.<br><br>They had not worked together since they were teenagers, when their father assigned Mara the ticket window and Julian the projectors. Mara remembered Julian emerging from the booth only between reels, smelling of warm dust. Julian remembered Mara letting neighborhood children enter with half-price tickets when their father was not watching. Each had privately considered the other's job the easier one.<br><br>Now the cinema had been closed for seven months. A buyer wanted the building empty by Friday. Julian moved quickly, testing equipment, checking serial numbers, and photographing anything that might be sold. Mara opened drawers. She found birthday candles, umbrellas, a single silver shoe, and envelopes containing notes their father had never mailed. Julian called these discoveries delays. Mara called his photographs another way of refusing to look.<br><br>The argument began over a dented metal film can. Julian placed it in DISCARD. Mara retrieved it, recognizing the faded title of the first movie their father had shown after buying the Rialto. The can was empty, but she insisted it belonged in KEEP. Julian said that if memory were enough, they would have to preserve every broken chair. Mara accused him of treating the cinema like a failed machine.<br><br>Julian carried the can upstairs to the projection booth. Mara followed, prepared to continue. Instead, he opened a cabinet containing rows of notebooks. For every screening, their father had recorded attendance, bulb hours, sound problems, and complaints. In the margins Julian had added instructions in his own narrow handwriting. He explained that during their father's last year, he had returned each Sunday to inspect the projectors, though the two of them had barely spoken. The photographs downstairs were for an equipment archive he intended to give the local film club.<br><br>Mara sat in the operator's chair. She had interpreted Julian's speed as indifference because it resembled his silence, and she had always treated silence as empty. The notebooks made that interpretation harder. Julian, meanwhile, turned the film can over and discovered a date scratched into its base. It was not the opening-night can Mara remembered; it came from a repair made years later. Her certainty had supplied a history the object did not possess.<br><br>They returned to the lobby and erased the three chalk labels. In their place they made four: SELL, RECYCLE, CLUB ARCHIVE, FAMILY. The empty can went into FAMILY—not because Mara's story was accurate, Julian said, but because the argument now belonged to them. Mara placed the price board in CLUB ARCHIVE after Julian showed her the pencil calculations on its back.<br><br>By evening, their methods had not merged. Julian still photographed each projector part before packing it, and Mara still read every scrap of paper. But he began bringing her objects whose histories he could not identify, while she stopped assuming that obscurity made an object precious. They worked at different speeds, crossing the lobby with fewer collisions.<br><br>Neither apologized directly, but each had begun asking questions where accusation had previously supplied an answer.<br><br>Before leaving, Julian switched on the auditorium aisle lights. Most bulbs had failed, producing a broken path toward the screen. Mara resisted the urge to turn the pattern into a symbol. She simply stood beside him while the remaining lights warmed. On Friday, the building would be empty. Nothing they sorted could prevent that. Still, the four labeled areas behind them held a record neither would have made alone: not the cinema preserved, but two incomplete ways of remembering made answerable to each other."},
161: {skill_tested: "Synthesize the distinction between cooperation and complete agreement.", question: "Why does the passage emphasize that Mara's and Julian's methods have not merged by evening?", option1: "To show that their cooperation depends on abandoning the sorting task", option2: "To prove that the four new categories have failed", option3: "To clarify that their relationship improves through mutual correction without requiring identical habits", option4: "To suggest that neither sibling has learned anything about the other", correctAnswer: "To clarify that their relationship improves through mutual correction without requiring identical habits", explanation: "Step 1: Julian continues photographing, and Mara continues reading each scrap.<br><br>Step 2: He now asks about unknown histories, while she no longer treats obscurity as sufficient value.<br><br>Step 3: Their distinct methods remain, but each becomes answerable to evidence supplied by the other.<br><br>Answer: To clarify that their relationship improves through mutual correction without requiring identical habits", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Character and Relationship Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> This passage describes two siblings clearing their late father's neighborhood cinema.<br><br>Mara arrived at the Rialto with boxes folded flat beneath her arm. Julian was already inside, standing on the concession counter to remove the hand-painted price board. He had labeled three piles—KEEP, SELL, DISCARD—in chalk on the lobby floor. Mara set her boxes directly across the lines. Julian looked down at them, then at her, and climbed from the counter without saying hello.<br><br>They had not worked together since they were teenagers, when their father assigned Mara the ticket window and Julian the projectors. Mara remembered Julian emerging from the booth only between reels, smelling of warm dust. Julian remembered Mara letting neighborhood children