plied a history the object did not possess.<br><br>They returned to the lobby and erased the three chalk labels. In their place they made four: SELL, RECYCLE, CLUB ARCHIVE, FAMILY. The empty can went into FAMILY—not because Mara's story was accurate, Julian said, but because the argument now belonged to them. Mara placed the price board in CLUB ARCHIVE after Julian showed her the pencil calculations on its back.<br><br>By evening, their methods had not merged. Julian still photographed each projector part before packing it, and Mara still read every scrap of paper. But he began bringing her objects whose histories he could not identify, while she stopped assuming that obscurity made an object precious. They worked at different speeds, crossing the lobby with fewer collisions.<br><br>Neither apologized directly, but each had begun asking questions where accusation had previously supplied an answer.<br><br>Before leaving, Julian switched on the auditorium aisle lights. Most bulbs had failed, producing a broken path toward the screen. Mara resisted the urge to turn the pattern into a symbol. She simply stood beside him while the remaining lights warmed. On Friday, the building would be empty. Nothing they sorted could prevent that. Still, the four labeled areas behind them held a record neither would have made alone: not the cinema preserved, but two incomplete ways of remembering made answerable to each other."},
162: {skill_tested: "Interpret a qualified ending through earlier character development.", question: "The final phrase 'made answerable to each other' most nearly suggests that the siblings' memories now:", option1: "must be identical before either can preserve an object.", option2: "have been replaced entirely by their father's notebooks.", option3: "prevent them from accepting the building's sale.", option4: "are tested and supplemented by the other's different evidence and attention.", correctAnswer: "are tested and supplemented by the other's different evidence and attention.", explanation: "Step 1: Mara's story about the can is corrected by a date, while Julian's apparent indifference is corrected by notebooks and archival purpose.<br><br>Step 2: Their revised sorting system incorporates personal and documentary value.<br><br>Step 3: Being answerable means neither way of remembering operates as unquestioned truth; each must respond to what the other reveals.<br><br>Answer: are tested and supplemented by the other's different evidence and attention.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> This passage describes two siblings clearing their late father's neighborhood cinema.<br><br>Mara arrived at the Rialto with boxes folded flat beneath her arm. Julian was already inside, standing on the concession counter to remove the hand-painted price board. He had labeled three piles—KEEP, SELL, DISCARD—in chalk on the lobby floor. Mara set her boxes directly across the lines. Julian looked down at them, then at her, and climbed from the counter without saying hello.<br><br>They had not worked together since they were teenagers, when their father assigned Mara the ticket window and Julian the projectors. Mara remembered Julian emerging from the booth only between reels, smelling of warm dust. Julian remembered Mara letting neighborhood children enter with half-price tickets when their father was not watching. Each had privately considered the other's job the easier one.<br><br>Now the cinema had been closed for seven months. A buyer wanted the building empty by Friday. Julian moved quickly, testing equipment, checking serial numbers, and photographing anything that might be sold. Mara opened drawers. She found birthday candles, umbrellas, a single silver shoe, and envelopes containing notes their father had never mailed. Julian called these discoveries delays. Mara called his photographs another way of refusing to look.<br><br>The argument began over a dented metal film can. Julian placed it in DISCARD. Mara retrieved it, recognizing the faded title of the first movie their father had shown after buying the Rialto. The can was empty, but she insisted it belonged in KEEP. Julian said that if memory were enough, they would have to preserve every broken chair. Mara accused him of treating the cinema like a failed machine.<br><br>Julian carried the can upstairs to the projection booth. Mara followed, prepared to continue. Instead, he opened a cabinet containing rows of notebooks. For every screening, their father had recorded attendance, bulb hours, sound problems, and complaints. In the margins Julian had added instructions in his own narrow handwriting. He explained that during their father's last year, he had returned each Sunday to inspect the projectors, though the two of them had barely spoken. The photographs downstairs were for an equipment archive he intended to give the local film club.<br><br>Mara sat in the operator's chair. She had interpreted Julian's speed as indifference because it resembled his silence, and she had always treated silence as empty. The notebooks made that interpretation harder. Julian, meanwhile, turned the film can over and discovered a date scratched into its base. It was not the opening-night can Mara remembered; it came from a repair made years later. Her certainty had supplied a history the object did not possess.<br><br>They returned to the lobby and erased the three chalk labels. In their place they made four: SELL, RECYCLE, CLUB ARCHIVE, FAMILY. The empty can went into FAMILY—not because Mara's story was accurate, Julian said, but because the argument now belonged to them. Mara placed the price board in CLUB ARCHIVE after Julian showed her the pencil calculations on its back.<br><br>By evening, their methods had not merged. Julian still photographed each projector part before packing it, and Mara still read every scrap of paper. But he began bringing her objects whose histories he could not identify, while she stopped assuming that obscurity made an object precious. They worked at different speeds, crossing the lobby with fewer collisions.<br><br>Neither apologized directly, but each had begun asking questions where accusation had previously supplied an answer.<br><br>Before leaving, Julian switched on the auditorium aisle lights. Most bulbs had failed, producing a broken path toward the screen. Mara resisted the urge to turn the pattern into a symbol. She simply stood beside him while the remaining lights warmed. On Friday, the building would be empty. Nothing they sorted could prevent that. Still, the four labeled areas behind them held a record neither would have made alone: not the cinema preserved, but two incomplete ways of remembering made answerable to each other."},
163: {skill_tested: "Identify the purpose of calibrated and repeated routes.", question: "According to Passage A, why do researchers calibrate instruments and repeat survey routes at comparable times?", option1: "To separate persistent heat patterns from unusual individual readings", option2: "To ensure that every neighborhood records the same temperature", option3: "To replace fixed weather stations with resident workshops", option4: "To determine how long each pedestrian remains outdoors", correctAnswer: "To separate persistent heat patterns from unusual individual readings", explanation: "Step 1: A single reading can be affected by a stopped vehicle or reflected sunlight.<br><br>Step 2: Calibration and repeated routes help researchers determine whether a corridor is consistently hot.<br><br>Answer: To separate persistent heat patterns from unusual individual readings", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider two approaches to mapping heat in cities.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>On a July afternoon, a sensor mounted on a city bus may record a temperature several degrees higher beside an asphalt lot than beneath a row of mature trees. Multiply that reading by thousands of locations, and planners obtain a thermal map: a picture of how heat is distributed across streets, roofs, parks, and blocks.<br><br>Such maps are useful because ordinary weather stations are sparse. A station at an airport can accurately report regional conditions while missing sharp variations within a neighborhood. Mobile surveys, satellite images, and fixed sensors fill that gap. Each method observes a different surface and interval. A satellite may cover the whole city on one cloudless afternoon, while a curbside sensor can sample the same block repeatedly. Researchers also record wind, recent rain, and sensor height because those conditions affect comparisons. The resulting map is therefore not a simple photograph; it is a set of measurements organized by an explicit protocol. When instruments are calibrated and routes are repeated at comparable times, researchers can distinguish a consistently hot corridor from an unusually warm reading caused by a stopped vehicle or reflected sunlight.<br><br>Yet a thermal map is not automatically a map of human exposure. A playground may be extremely hot at noon but empty then. A moderately warm bus stop may expose hundreds of riders who wait there each afternoon. Buildings also matter: an apartment can remain hot overnight even after its surrounding street cools. The measurement describes a physical condition at a particular place and time; it does not by itself reveal who encounters that condition, for how long, or with what alternatives.<br><br>For this reason, some planning teams combine temperature data with transit schedules, housing information, and pedestrian counts. The added layers do not make the original measurements less objective. They make the question more precise. Instead of asking only where the city is hottest, planners can ask where heat and unavoidable daily activity overlap.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When residents of the East Ward were asked to mark the hottest parts of their neighborhood, they did not begin with the broad avenue that appeared darkest on the city's thermal map. They circled the laundromat where customers waited outside, the unsheltered crossing used by children after school, and a narrow path older residents took to a pharmacy.<br><br>Their map recorded movement as much as temperature. Workshop participants drew morning and evening routes in different colors. They noted benches that were technically shaded but difficult to reach with a wheelchair, stores that allowed people to refill water bottles, and a fence that forced pedestrians to take a longer path across an exposed lot. Several marked a cool library as important even though it was outside the neighborhood boundary used in the official study.<br><br>This knowledge is sometimes called anecdotal, a word that can make it sound casual or untestable. But repeated accounts can reveal stable patterns. If many residents independently identify the same crossing as difficult, planners have evidence about a recurring experience that a passing sensor cannot measure. Residents also explain why people remain in a hot place: the bus comes infrequently, the safer route is longer, or a public building closes early.<br><br>Participatory maps have limitations of their own. Workshops may attract people who have time to attend, while night workers or residents who speak less common languages may be underrepresented. Memory can emphasize an exceptional day. A map made in August may overlook risks faced during the first sudden heat of spring.<br><br>The strongest projects therefore treat neither map as a decorative supplement to the other. Sensor data can test whether a reported hot route remains warm across many days. Resident accounts can guide sensors toward overlooked places and help planners decide which measured differences matter. Agreement between the maps increases confidence; disagreement identifies a question to investigate rather than a reason to discard one source."},
164: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of a methodological label.", question: "As used in Passage B, the word 'anecdotal' most nearly describes information regarded as:", option1: "limited to measurements taken from satellites.", option2: "based on personal reports rather than systematic evidence.", option3: "certain to represent every member of a population.", option4: "too technical for residents to interpret.", correctAnswer: "based on personal reports rather than systematic evidence.", explanation: "Step 1: The term is applied to residents' accounts.<br><br>Step 2: The passage notes that the label can make those accounts seem casual or untestable.<br><br>Answer: based on personal reports rather than systematic evidence.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider two approaches to mapping heat in cities.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>On a July afternoon, a sensor mounted on a city bus may record a temperature several degrees higher beside an asphalt lot than beneath a row of mature trees. Multiply that reading by thousands of locations, and planners obtain a thermal map: a picture of how heat is distributed across streets, roofs, parks, and blocks.<br><br>Such maps are useful because ordinary weather stations are sparse. A station at an airport can accurately report regional conditions while missing sharp variations within a neighborhood. Mobile surveys, satellite images, and fixed sensors fill that gap. Each method observes a different surface and interval. A satellite may cover the whole city on one cloudless afternoon, while a curbside sensor can sample the same block repeatedly. Researchers also record wind, recent rain, and sensor height because those conditions affect comparisons. The resulting map is therefore not a simple photograph; it is a set of measurements organized by an explicit protocol. When instruments are calibrated and routes are repeated at comparable times, researchers can distinguish a consistently hot corridor from an unusually warm reading caused by a stopped vehicle or reflected sunlight.<br><br>Yet a thermal map is not automatically a map of human exposure. A playground may be extremely hot at noon but empty then. A moderately warm bus stop may expose hundreds of riders who wait there each afternoon. Buildings also matter: an apartment can remain hot overnight even after its surrounding street cools. The measurement describes a physical condition at a particular place and time; it does not by itself reveal who encounters that condition, for how long, or with what alternatives.<br><br>For this reason, some planning teams combine temperature data with transit schedules, housing information, and pedestrian counts. The added layers do not make the original measurements less objective. They make the question more precise. Instead of asking only where the city is hottest, planners can ask where heat and unavoidable daily activity overlap.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When residents of the East Ward were asked to mark the hottest parts of their neighborhood, they did not begin with the broad avenue that appeared darkest on the city's thermal map. They circled the laundromat where customers waited outside, the unsheltered crossing used by children after school, and a narrow path older residents took to a pharmacy.<br><br>Their map recorded movement as much as temperature. Workshop participants drew morning and evening routes in different colors. They noted benches that were technically shaded but difficult to reach with a wheelchair, stores that allowed people to refill water bottles, and a fence that forced pedestrians to take a longer path across an exposed lot. Several marked a cool library as important even though it was outside the neighborhood boundary used in the official study.<br><br>This knowledge is sometimes called anecdotal, a word that can make it sound casual or untestable. But repeated accounts can reveal stable patterns. If many residents independently identify the same crossing as difficult, planners have evidence about a recurring experience that a passing sensor cannot measure. Residents also explain why people remain in a hot place: the bus comes infrequently, the safer route is longer, or a public building closes early.<br><br>Participatory maps have limitations of their own. Workshops may attract people who have time to attend, while night workers or residents who speak less common languages may be underrepresented. Memory can emphasize an exceptional day. A map made in August may overlook risks faced during the first sudden heat of spring.<br><br>The strongest projects therefore treat neither map as a decorative supplement to the other. Sensor data can test whether a reported hot route remains warm across many days. Resident accounts can guide sensors toward overlooked places and help planners decide which measured differences matter. Agreement between the maps increases confidence; disagreement identifies a question to investigate rather than a reason to discard one source."},
165: {skill_tested: "Identify a shared central claim across paired passages.", question: "Which statement best describes an idea shared by both passages?", option1: "The hottest measured location always deserves the first public investment.", option2: "Resident memory is more accurate than calibrated equipment in every season.", option3: "A useful heat plan must connect physical conditions with how people encounter them.", option4: "Regional airport stations provide sufficient detail for neighborhood planning.", correctAnswer: "A useful heat plan must connect physical conditions with how people encounter them.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A distinguishes temperature from human exposure and adds activity data.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B maps routes, access, and reasons people remain in hot places.<br><br>Answer: A useful heat plan must connect physical conditions with how people encounter them.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider two approaches to mapping heat in cities.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>On a July afternoon, a sensor mounted on a city bus may record a temperature several degrees higher beside an asphalt lot than beneath a row of mature trees. Multiply that reading by thousands of locations, and planners obtain a thermal map: a picture of how heat is distributed across streets, roofs, parks, and blocks.<br><br>Such maps are useful because ordinary weather stations are sparse. A station at an airport can accurately report regional conditions while missing sharp variations within a neighborhood. Mobile surveys, satellite images, and fixed sensors fill that gap. Each method observes a different surface and interval. A satellite may cover the whole city on one cloudless afternoon, while a curbside sensor can sample the same block repeatedly. Researchers also record wind, recent rain, and sensor height because those conditions affect comparisons. The resulting map is therefore not a simple photograph; it is a set of measurements organized by an explicit protocol. When instruments are calibrated and routes are repeated at comparable times, researchers can distinguish a consistently hot corridor from an unusually warm reading caused by a stopped vehicle or reflected sunlight.<br><br>Yet a thermal map is not automatically a map of human exposure. A playground may be extremely hot at noon but empty then. A moderately warm bus stop may expose hundreds of riders who wait there each afternoon. Buildings also matter: an apartment can remain hot overnight even after its surrounding street cools. The measurement describes a physical condition at a particular place and time; it does not by itself reveal who encounters that condition, for how long, or with what alternatives.<br><br>For this reason, some planning teams combine temperature data with transit schedules, housing information, and pedestrian counts. The added layers do not make the original measurements less objective. They make the question more precise. Instead of asking only where the city is hottest, planners can ask where heat and unavoidable daily activity overlap.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When residents of the East Ward were asked to mark the hottest parts of their neighborhood, they did not begin with the broad avenue that appeared darkest on the city's thermal map. They circled the laundromat where customers waited outside, the unsheltered crossing used by children after school, and a narrow path older residents took to a pharmacy.<br><br>Their map recorded movement as much as temperature. Workshop participants drew morning and evening routes in different colors. They noted benches that were technically shaded but difficult to reach with a wheelchair, stores that allowed people to refill water bottles, and a fence that forced pedestrians to take a longer path across an exposed lot. Several marked a cool library as important even though it was outside the neighborhood boundary used in the official study.<br><br>This knowledge is sometimes called anecdotal, a word that can make it sound casual or untestable. But repeated accounts can reveal stable patterns. If many residents independently identify the same crossing as difficult, planners have evidence about a recurring experience that a passing sensor cannot measure. Residents also explain why people remain in a hot place: the bus comes infrequently, the safer route is longer, or a public building closes early.<br><br>Participatory maps have limitations of their own. Workshops may attract people who have time to attend, while night workers or residents who speak less common languages may be underrepresented. Memory can emphasize an exceptional day. A map made in August may overlook risks faced during the first sudden heat of spring.<br><br>The strongest projects therefore treat neither map as a decorative supplement to the other. Sensor data can test whether a reported hot route remains warm across many days. Resident accounts can guide sensors toward overlooked places and help planners decide which measured differences matter. Agreement between the maps increases confidence; disagreement identifies a question to investigate rather than a reason to discard one source."},
166: {skill_tested: "Analyze the function of a contrasting example.", question: "In Passage A, the contrast between the empty playground and the busy bus stop primarily serves to:", option1: "prove that playground measurements are always inaccurate.", option2: "argue that buses create all significant urban heat.", option3: "explain why fixed sensors should be placed only indoors.", option4: "show that temperature alone does not determine the amount of human exposure.", correctAnswer: "show that temperature alone does not determine the amount of human exposure.", explanation: "Step 1: The playground is hotter but may be empty, while the bus stop is less hot but heavily used.<br><br>Step 2: The comparison separates physical intensity from the number and duration of encounters.<br><br>Step 3: It supports the passage's call for contextual layers beyond temperature.<br><br>Answer: show that temperature alone does not determine the amount of human exposure.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider two approaches to mapping heat in cities.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>On a July afternoon, a sensor mounted on a city bus may record a temperature several degrees higher beside an asphalt lot than beneath a row of mature trees. Multiply that reading by thousands of locations, and planners obtain a thermal map: a picture of how heat is distributed across streets, roofs, parks, and blocks.<br><br>Such maps are useful because ordinary weather stations are sparse. A station at an airport can accurately report regional conditions while missing sharp variations within a neighborhood. Mobile surveys, satellite images, and fixed sensors fill that gap. Each method observes a different surface and interval. A satellite may cover the whole city on one cloudless afternoon, while a curbside sensor can sample the same block repeatedly. Researchers also record wind, recent rain, and sensor height because those conditions affect comparisons. The resulting map is therefore not a simple photograph; it is a set of measurements organized by an explicit protocol. When instruments are calibrated and routes are repeated at comparable times, researchers can distinguish a consistently hot corridor from an unusually warm reading caused by a stopped vehicle or reflected sunlight.<br><br>Yet a thermal map is not automatically a map of human exposure. A playground may be extremely hot at noon but empty then. A moderately warm bus stop may expose hundreds of riders who wait there each afternoon. Buildings also matter: an apartment can remain hot overnight even after its surrounding street cools. The measurement describes a physical condition at a particular place and time; it does not by itself reveal who encounters that condition, for how long, or with what alternatives.<br><br>For this reason, some planning teams combine temperature data with transit schedules, housing information, and pedestrian counts. The added layers do not make the original measurements less objective. They make the question more precise. Instead of asking only where the city is hottest, planners can ask where heat and unavoidable daily activity overlap.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When residents of the East Ward were asked to mark the hottest parts of their neighborhood, they did not begin with the broad avenue that appeared darkest on the city's thermal map. They circled the laundromat where customers waited outside, the unsheltered crossing used by children after school, and a narrow path older residents took to a pharmacy.<br><br>Their map recorded movement as much as temperature. Workshop participants drew morning and evening routes in different colors. They noted benches that were technically shaded but difficult to reach with a wheelchair, stores that allowed people to refill water bottles, and a fence that forced pedestrians to take a longer path across an exposed lot. Several marked a cool library as important even though it was outside the neighborhood boundary used in the official study.<br><br>This knowledge is sometimes called anecdotal, a word that can make it sound casual or untestable. But repeated accounts can reveal stable patterns. If many residents independently identify the same crossing as difficult, planners have evidence about a recurring experience that a passing sensor cannot measure. Residents also explain why people remain in a hot place: the bus comes infrequently, the safer route is longer, or a public building closes early.<br><br>Participatory maps have limitations of their own. Workshops may attract people who have time to attend, while night workers or residents who speak less common languages may be underrepresented. Memory can emphasize an exceptional day. A map made in August may overlook risks faced during the first sudden heat of spring.<br><br>The strongest projects therefore treat neither map as a decorative supplement to the other. Sensor data can test whether a reported hot route remains warm across many days. Resident accounts can guide sensors toward overlooked places and help planners decide which measured differences matter. Agreement between the maps increases confidence; disagreement identifies a question to investigate rather than a reason to discard one source."},
167: {skill_tested: "Infer how one passage would evaluate evidence in the other.", question: "The author of Passage A would most likely view the East Ward residents' route map as:", option1: "a useful source for identifying where additional measurements and activity data are needed.", option2: "proof that calibrated instruments have no role in public planning.", option3: "a complete measurement of indoor nighttime temperature.", option4: "unreliable solely because it extends beyond an official boundary.", correctAnswer: "a useful source for identifying where additional measurements and activity data are needed.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A supports adding transit, housing, and pedestrian information to thermal measurements.<br><br>Step 2: The residents' map identifies routes, use patterns, and overlooked locations.<br><br>Step 3: Those observations would help refine where and what planners measure.<br><br>Answer: a useful source for identifying where additional measurements and activity data are needed.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider two approaches to mapping heat in cities.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>On a July afternoon, a sensor mounted on a city bus may record a temperature several degrees higher beside an asphalt lot than beneath a row of mature trees. Multiply that reading by thousands of locations, and planners obtain a thermal map: a picture of how heat is distributed across streets, roofs, parks, and blocks.<br><br>Such maps are useful because ordinary weather stations are sparse. A station at an airport can accurately report regional conditions while missing sharp variations within a neighborhood. Mobile surveys, satellite images, and fixed sensors fill that gap. Each method observes a different surface and interval. A satellite may cover the whole city on one cloudless afternoon, while a curbside sensor can sample the same block repeatedly. Researchers also record wind, recent rain, and sensor height because those conditions affect comparisons. The resulting map is therefore not a simple photograph; it is a set of measurements organized by an explicit protocol. When instruments are calibrated and routes are repeated at comparable times, researchers can distinguish a consistently hot corridor from an unusually warm reading caused by a stopped vehicle or reflected sunlight.<br><br>Yet a thermal map is not automatically a map of human exposure. A playground may be extremely hot at noon but empty then. A moderately warm bus stop may expose hundreds of riders who wait there each afternoon. Buildings also matter: an apartment can remain hot overnight even after its surrounding street cools. The measurement describes a physical condition at a particular place and time; it does not by itself reveal who encounters that condition, for how long, or with what alternatives.<br><br>For this reason, some planning teams combine temperature data with transit schedules, housing information, and pedestrian counts. The added layers do not make the original measurements less objective. They make the question more precise. Instead of asking only where the city is hottest, planners can ask where heat and unavoidable daily activity overlap.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When residents of the East Ward were asked to mark the hottest parts of their neighborhood, they did not begin with the broad avenue that appeared darkest on the city's thermal map. They circled the laundromat where customers waited outside, the unsheltered crossing used by children after school, and a narrow path older residents took to a pharmacy.<br><br>Their map recorded movement as much as temperature. Workshop participants drew morning and evening routes in different colors. They noted benches that were technically shaded but difficult to reach with a wheelchair, stores that allowed people to refill water bottles, and a fence that forced pedestrians to take a longer path across an exposed lot. Several marked a cool library as important even though it was outside the neighborhood boundary used in the official study.<br><br>This knowledge is sometimes called anecdotal, a word that can make it sound casual or untestable. But repeated accounts can reveal stable patterns. If many residents independently identify the same crossing as difficult, planners have evidence about a recurring experience that a passing sensor cannot measure. Residents also explain why people remain in a hot place: the bus comes infrequently, the safer route is longer, or a public building closes early.<br><br>Participatory maps have limitations of their own. Workshops may attract people who have time to attend, while night workers or residents who speak less common languages may be underrepresented. Memory can emphasize an exceptional day. A map made in August may overlook risks faced during the first sudden heat of spring.<br><br>The strongest projects therefore treat neither map as a decorative supplement to the other. Sensor data can test whether a reported hot route remains warm across many days. Resident accounts can guide sensors toward overlooked places and help planners decide which measured differences matter. Agreement between the maps increases confidence; disagreement identifies a question to investigate rather than a reason to discard one source."},
168: {skill_tested: "Analyze parallel qualifications in paired passages.", question: "The discussion of an apartment remaining hot overnight in Passage A and the discussion of underrepresented night workers in Passage B both emphasize that:", option1: "night workers always experience higher temperatures than other residents.", option2: "daytime neighborhood studies can miss important dimensions of heat exposure.", option3: "indoor temperatures can be measured only through personal memory.", option4: "spring heat presents no risk to apartment residents.", correctAnswer: "daytime neighborhood studies can miss important dimensions of heat exposure.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A notes that street cooling may conceal indoor nighttime heat.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B warns that workshops may omit night workers.<br><br>Step 3: Both examples reveal experiences outside the most visible daytime sample.<br><br>Answer: daytime neighborhood studies can miss important dimensions of heat exposure.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider two approaches to mapping heat in cities.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>On a July afternoon, a sensor mounted on a city bus may record a temperature several degrees higher beside an asphalt lot than beneath a row of mature trees. Multiply that reading by thousands of locations, and planners obtain a thermal map: a picture of how heat is distributed across streets, roofs, parks, and blocks.<br><br>Such maps are useful because ordinary weather stations are sparse. A station at an airport can accurately report regional conditions while missing sharp variations within a neighborhood. Mobile surveys, satellite images, and fixed sensors fill that gap. Each method observes a different surface and interval. A satellite may cover the whole city on one cloudless afternoon, while a curbside sensor can sample the same block repeatedly. 