take a longer path across an exposed lot. Several marked a cool library as important even though it was outside the neighborhood boundary used in the official study.<br><br>This knowledge is sometimes called anecdotal, a word that can make it sound casual or untestable. But repeated accounts can reveal stable patterns. If many residents independently identify the same crossing as difficult, planners have evidence about a recurring experience that a passing sensor cannot measure. Residents also explain why people remain in a hot place: the bus comes infrequently, the safer route is longer, or a public building closes early.<br><br>Participatory maps have limitations of their own. Workshops may attract people who have time to attend, while night workers or residents who speak less common languages may be underrepresented. Memory can emphasize an exceptional day. A map made in August may overlook risks faced during the first sudden heat of spring.<br><br>The strongest projects therefore treat neither map as a decorative supplement to the other. Sensor data can test whether a reported hot route remains warm across many days. Resident accounts can guide sensors toward overlooked places and help planners decide which measured differences matter. Agreement between the maps increases confidence; disagreement identifies a question to investigate rather than a reason to discard one source."},
169: {skill_tested: "Infer the significance of disagreement between evidence sources.", question: "Based on the final paragraph, when a thermal map and resident map disagree, planners should first:", option1: "average the two maps without examining either method.", option2: "remove the resident reports from the project.", option3: "treat the discrepancy as a clue that scale, timing, use, or sampling requires investigation.", option4: "assume the warmer location is automatically the more important one.", correctAnswer: "treat the discrepancy as a clue that scale, timing, use, or sampling requires investigation.", explanation: "Step 1: Each method has a different strength and a stated limitation.<br><br>Step 2: The passage explicitly treats disagreement as a question to investigate.<br><br>Step 3: Examining timing, use, and sampling can explain why the maps capture different conditions.<br><br>Answer: treat the discrepancy as a clue that scale, timing, use, or sampling requires investigation.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider two approaches to mapping heat in cities.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>On a July afternoon, a sensor mounted on a city bus may record a temperature several degrees higher beside an asphalt lot than beneath a row of mature trees. Multiply that reading by thousands of locations, and planners obtain a thermal map: a picture of how heat is distributed across streets, roofs, parks, and blocks.<br><br>Such maps are useful because ordinary weather stations are sparse. A station at an airport can accurately report regional conditions while missing sharp variations within a neighborhood. Mobile surveys, satellite images, and fixed sensors fill that gap. Each method observes a different surface and interval. A satellite may cover the whole city on one cloudless afternoon, while a curbside sensor can sample the same block repeatedly. Researchers also record wind, recent rain, and sensor height because those conditions affect comparisons. The resulting map is therefore not a simple photograph; it is a set of measurements organized by an explicit protocol. When instruments are calibrated and routes are repeated at comparable times, researchers can distinguish a consistently hot corridor from an unusually warm reading caused by a stopped vehicle or reflected sunlight.<br><br>Yet a thermal map is not automatically a map of human exposure. A playground may be extremely hot at noon but empty then. A moderately warm bus stop may expose hundreds of riders who wait there each afternoon. Buildings also matter: an apartment can remain hot overnight even after its surrounding street cools. The measurement describes a physical condition at a particular place and time; it does not by itself reveal who encounters that condition, for how long, or with what alternatives.<br><br>For this reason, some planning teams combine temperature data with transit schedules, housing information, and pedestrian counts. The added layers do not make the original measurements less objective. They make the question more precise. Instead of asking only where the city is hottest, planners can ask where heat and unavoidable daily activity overlap.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When residents of the East Ward were asked to mark the hottest parts of their neighborhood, they did not begin with the broad avenue that appeared darkest on the city's thermal map. They circled the laundromat where customers waited outside, the unsheltered crossing used by children after school, and a narrow path older residents took to a pharmacy.<br><br>Their map recorded movement as much as temperature. Workshop participants drew morning and evening routes in different colors. They noted benches that were technically shaded but difficult to reach with a wheelchair, stores that allowed people to refill water bottles, and a fence that forced pedestrians to take a longer path across an exposed lot. Several marked a cool library as important even though it was outside the neighborhood boundary used in the official study.<br><br>This knowledge is sometimes called anecdotal, a word that can make it sound casual or untestable. But repeated accounts can reveal stable patterns. If many residents independently identify the same crossing as difficult, planners have evidence about a recurring experience that a passing sensor cannot measure. Residents also explain why people remain in a hot place: the bus comes infrequently, the safer route is longer, or a public building closes early.<br><br>Participatory maps have limitations of their own. Workshops may attract people who have time to attend, while night workers or residents who speak less common languages may be underrepresented. Memory can emphasize an exceptional day. A map made in August may overlook risks faced during the first sudden heat of spring.<br><br>The strongest projects therefore treat neither map as a decorative supplement to the other. Sensor data can test whether a reported hot route remains warm across many days. Resident accounts can guide sensors toward overlooked places and help planners decide which measured differences matter. Agreement between the maps increases confidence; disagreement identifies a question to investigate rather than a reason to discard one source."},
170: {skill_tested: "Synthesize distinct definitions of evidentiary precision.", question: "Taken together, the passages suggest that making a heat study more precise requires:", option1: "collecting more temperature readings while excluding all accounts of daily movement.", option2: "allowing neighborhood boundaries to determine which experiences count as evidence.", option3: "selecting whichever method produces the most urgent-looking map.", option4: "matching reliable physical measurements to clearly defined questions about people, places, and times.", correctAnswer: "matching reliable physical measurements to clearly defined questions about people, places, and times.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A says contextual layers make the question more precise, not the instruments less objective.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B shows how route knowledge identifies use, access, timing, and overlooked sites.<br><br>Step 3: Precision therefore comes from relating sound measurements to the actual exposure question being asked.<br><br>Answer: matching reliable physical measurements to clearly defined questions about people, places, and times.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider two approaches to mapping heat in cities.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>On a July afternoon, a sensor mounted on a city bus may record a temperature several degrees higher beside an asphalt lot than beneath a row of mature trees. Multiply that reading by thousands of locations, and planners obtain a thermal map: a picture of how heat is distributed across streets, roofs, parks, and blocks.<br><br>Such maps are useful because ordinary weather stations are sparse. A station at an airport can accurately report regional conditions while missing sharp variations within a neighborhood. Mobile surveys, satellite images, and fixed sensors fill that gap. Each method observes a different surface and interval. A satellite may cover the whole city on one cloudless afternoon, while a curbside sensor can sample the same block repeatedly. Researchers also record wind, recent rain, and sensor height because those conditions affect comparisons. The resulting map is therefore not a simple photograph; it is a set of measurements organized by an explicit protocol. When instruments are calibrated and routes are repeated at comparable times, researchers can distinguish a consistently hot corridor from an unusually warm reading caused by a stopped vehicle or reflected sunlight.<br><br>Yet a thermal map is not automatically a map of human exposure. A playground may be extremely hot at noon but empty then. A moderately warm bus stop may expose hundreds of riders who wait there each afternoon. Buildings also matter: an apartment can remain hot overnight even after its surrounding street cools. The measurement describes a physical condition at a particular place and time; it does not by itself reveal who encounters that condition, for how long, or with what alternatives.<br><br>For this reason, some planning teams combine temperature data with transit schedules, housing information, and pedestrian counts. The added layers do not make the original measurements less objective. They make the question more precise. Instead of asking only where the city is hottest, planners can ask where heat and unavoidable daily activity overlap.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When residents of the East Ward were asked to mark the hottest parts of their neighborhood, they did not begin with the broad avenue that appeared darkest on the city's thermal map. They circled the laundromat where customers waited outside, the unsheltered crossing used by children after school, and a narrow path older residents took to a pharmacy.<br><br>Their map recorded movement as much as temperature. Workshop participants drew morning and evening routes in different colors. They noted benches that were technically shaded but difficult to reach with a wheelchair, stores that allowed people to refill water bottles, and a fence that forced pedestrians to take a longer path across an exposed lot. Several marked a cool library as important even though it was outside the neighborhood boundary used in the official study.<br><br>This knowledge is sometimes called anecdotal, a word that can make it sound casual or untestable. But repeated accounts can reveal stable patterns. If many residents independently identify the same crossing as difficult, planners have evidence about a recurring experience that a passing sensor cannot measure. Residents also explain why people remain in a hot place: the bus comes infrequently, the safer route is longer, or a public building closes early.<br><br>Participatory maps have limitations of their own. Workshops may attract people who have time to attend, while night workers or residents who speak less common languages may be underrepresented. Memory can emphasize an exceptional day. A map made in August may overlook risks faced during the first sudden heat of spring.<br><br>The strongest projects therefore treat neither map as a decorative supplement to the other. Sensor data can test whether a reported hot route remains warm across many days. Resident accounts can guide sensors toward overlooked places and help planners decide which measured differences matter. Agreement between the maps increases confidence; disagreement identifies a question to investigate rather than a reason to discard one source."},
171: {skill_tested: "Evaluate how limitations make paired methods complementary.", question: "Which choice best explains why the limitations described in the two passages make the methods complementary rather than mutually exclusive?", option1: "Instrument surveys can test patterns beyond individual memory, while resident accounts can reveal use and access that instruments do not record.", option2: "Both methods fail for exactly the same reasons and therefore always produce identical maps.", option3: "Resident workshops calibrate sensors, while sensors ensure every language group attends a workshop.", option4: "Each method becomes reliable only after the other method's evidence has been discarded.", correctAnswer: "Instrument surveys can test patterns beyond individual memory, while resident accounts can reveal use and access that instruments do not record.", explanation: "Step 1: Repeated calibrated surveys address unusual readings and memory of exceptional days.<br><br>Step 2: Residents identify routes, barriers, schedules, and reasons people remain in hot places.<br><br>Step 3: Each method supplies information missing from the other while also offering a way to investigate its claims.<br><br>Answer: Instrument surveys can test patterns beyond individual memory, while resident accounts can reveal use and access that instruments do not record.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider two approaches to mapping heat in cities.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>On a July afternoon, a sensor mounted on a city bus may record a temperature several degrees higher beside an asphalt lot than beneath a row of mature trees. Multiply that reading by thousands of locations, and planners obtain a thermal map: a picture of how heat is distributed across streets, roofs, parks, and blocks.<br><br>Such maps are useful because ordinary weather stations are sparse. A station at an airport can accurately report regional conditions while missing sharp variations within a neighborhood. Mobile surveys, satellite images, and fixed sensors fill that gap. Each method observes a different surface and interval. A satellite may cover the whole city on one cloudless afternoon, while a curbside sensor can sample the same block repeatedly. Researchers also record wind, recent rain, and sensor height because those conditions affect comparisons. The resulting map is therefore not a simple photograph; it is a set of measurements organized by an explicit protocol. When instruments are calibrated and routes are repeated at comparable times, researchers can distinguish a consistently hot corridor from an unusually warm reading caused by a stopped vehicle or reflected sunlight.<br><br>Yet a thermal map is not automatically a map of human exposure. A playground may be extremely hot at noon but empty then. A moderately warm bus stop may expose hundreds of riders who wait there each afternoon. Buildings also matter: an apartment can remain hot overnight even after its surrounding street cools. The measurement describes a physical condition at a particular place and time; it does not by itself reveal who encounters that condition, for how long, or with what alternatives.<br><br>For this reason, some planning teams combine temperature data with transit schedules, housing information, and pedestrian counts. The added layers do not make the original measurements less objective. They make the question more precise. Instead of asking only where the city is hottest, planners can ask where heat and unavoidable daily activity overlap.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When residents of the East Ward were asked to mark the hottest parts of their neighborhood, they did not begin with the broad avenue that appeared darkest on the city's thermal map. They circled the laundromat where customers waited outside, the unsheltered crossing used by children after school, and a narrow path older residents took to a pharmacy.<br><br>Their map recorded movement as much as temperature. Workshop participants drew morning and evening routes in different colors. They noted benches that were technically shaded but difficult to reach with a wheelchair, stores that allowed people to refill water bottles, and a fence that forced pedestrians to take a longer path across an exposed lot. Several marked a cool library as important even though it was outside the neighborhood boundary used in the official study.<br><br>This knowledge is sometimes called anecdotal, a word that can make it sound casual or untestable. But repeated accounts can reveal stable patterns. If many residents independently identify the same crossing as difficult, planners have evidence about a recurring experience that a passing sensor cannot measure. Residents also explain why people remain in a hot place: the bus comes infrequently, the safer route is longer, or a public building closes early.<br><br>Participatory maps have limitations of their own. Workshops may attract people who have time to attend, while night workers or residents who speak less common languages may be underrepresented. Memory can emphasize an exceptional day. A map made in August may overlook risks faced during the first sudden heat of spring.<br><br>The strongest projects therefore treat neither map as a decorative supplement to the other. Sensor data can test whether a reported hot route remains warm across many days. Resident accounts can guide sensors toward overlooked places and help planners decide which measured differences matter. Agreement between the maps increases confidence; disagreement identifies a question to investigate rather than a reason to discard one source."},
172: {skill_tested: "Identify the earliest indicators described in a restoration sequence.", question: "According to Passage A, which changes are generally monitored first after concrete channel walls are removed?", option1: "The return of every fish species formerly present", option2: "Changes in flow, floodplain contact, and sediment deposition", option3: "The number of visitors using new overlooks", option4: "The maturity of newly established riverside trees", correctAnswer: "Changes in flow, floodplain contact, and sediment deposition", explanation: "Step 1: Removing the channel allows water to slow, curve, spread, and deposit sediment.<br><br>Step 2: Passage A identifies these physical changes as the first indicators often monitored.<br><br>Answer: Changes in flow, floodplain contact, and sediment deposition", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how to judge the restoration of an urban river.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>Removing a concrete channel from a river does not instantly restore the ecosystem that existed before construction. It changes the conditions under which recovery may occur. Water can slow along newly curved banks, spread into a floodplain, and deposit sediment in places that once received only a fast, narrow current. These physical changes are often the first indicators monitored by restoration scientists. Teams may install gauges before excavation, survey cross sections after storms, and place marked stones where sediment is expected to collect. Repeating those observations matters because a dry summer can make a redesigned channel appear stable even if it will erode during high water. Measurements taken before and after comparable flows help separate the effect of construction from ordinary seasonal variation.<br><br>Physical response, however, is not the same as biological recovery. Insects may return within a season if gravel and leaf litter accumulate. Fish that require cool pools may take longer, especially if upstream water remains warm. Riverside trees need years to produce shade and woody debris. A survey conducted shortly after construction can therefore find that the channel is behaving more naturally while the food web remains simplified.<br><br>Scientists address this difference in timing by establishing several reference points. They measure the restored reach before work begins, compare it with an unrestored reach nearby, and return during different seasons. No single indicator is decisive. Clear water may look healthy while containing few nutrients needed by small organisms; abundant algae may indicate either a productive season or excessive runoff. Evidence gains meaning through patterns among flow, temperature, sediment, plants, insects, and fish.<br><br>This approach makes patience measurable rather than vague. A project need not be declared either a triumph or a failure after one year. Managers can identify which processes have begun, which are delayed, and which remain blocked by conditions beyond the project boundary.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>Before restoration, the Millstone River ran behind warehouses and chain-link fences. Many residents knew it mainly as a blue line on municipal maps. When the concrete walls were removed, the project also added footpaths, low overlooks, and two entrances connecting the river to bus routes. Those features were not biological interventions, yet they changed what happened along the water.<br><br>A neighborhood stewardship group began weekly walks. Members recorded clogged drains and newly eroded banks, but they also counted where visitors stopped, which paths became muddy, and whether signs could be read from a wheelchair. Their observations sometimes reached maintenance crews faster than the project's annual ecological report. After a broken drain discharged cloudy water, walkers photographed the plume and traced it to a street inlet before the next scheduled sampling day.<br><br>Public access can create pressure as well as protection. A shortcut through young plants damaged one bank, and food containers accumulated near the busiest overlook. The group responded by moving a path rather than simply adding warnings. It also asked schools to stagger large visits during nesting season. In this case, use was not the opposite of restoration; it was a condition that had to be designed and revised.<br><br>Some critics describe benches, paths, and programs as amenities separate from ecological success. That distinction is useful when it prevents visitor counts from being mistaken for evidence about fish or water quality. It becomes less useful when it implies that a river can be sustained without a public able to notice and defend it. Residents do not replace laboratory analysis, but their repeated presence expands the times and places at which change can be observed.<br><br>A restored river is thus both a set of processes and a relationship. Its curves, insects, and temperatures matter whether anyone visits. Yet access can create witnesses, and witnesses can become stewards. The strongest assessment asks not whether social and ecological measures are interchangeable, but whether each helps the recovery endure."},
173: {skill_tested: "Identify a stated contribution of resident monitoring.", question: "According to Passage B, how did the stewardship group respond to the cloudy-water event?", option1: "It waited for the annual report before observing the river.", option2: "It measured the population of every fish species.", option3: "It photographed the plume and traced it to a street inlet.", option4: "It permanently closed both river entrances.", correctAnswer: "It photographed the plume and traced it to a street inlet.", explanation: "Step 1: Walkers saw cloudy water between scheduled sampling dates.<br><br>Step 2: They documented the plume and followed it to a street inlet.<br><br>Answer: It photographed the plume and traced it to a street inlet.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how to judge the restoration of an urban river.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>Removing a concrete channel from a river does not instantly restore the ecosystem that existed before construction. It changes the conditions under which recovery may occur. Water can slow along newly curved banks, spread into a floodplain, and deposit sediment in places that once received only a fast, narrow current. These physical changes are often the first indicators monitored by restoration scientists. Teams may install gauges before excavation, survey cross sections after storms, and place marked stones where sediment is expected to collect. Repeating those observations matters because a dry summer can make a redesigned channel appear stable even if it will erode during high water. Measurements taken before and after comparable flows help separate the effect of construction from ordinary seasonal variation.<br><br>Physical response, however, is not the same as biological recovery. Insects may return within a season if gravel and leaf litter accumulate. Fish that require cool pools may take longer, especially if upstream water remains warm. Riverside trees need years to produce shade and woody debris. A survey conducted shortly after construction can therefore find that the channel is behaving more naturally while the food web remains simplified.<br><br>Scientists address this difference in timing by establishing several reference points. They measure the restored reach before work begins, compare it with an unrestored reach nearby, and return during different seasons. No single indicator is decisive. Clear water may look healthy while containing few nutrients needed by small organisms; abundant algae may indicate either a productive season or excessive runoff. Evidence gains meaning through patterns among flow, temperature, sediment, plants, insects, and fish.<br><br>This approach makes patience measurable rather than vague. A project need not be declared either a triumph or a failure after one year. Managers can identify which processes have begun, which are delayed, and which remain blocked by conditions beyond the project boundary.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>Before restoration, the Millstone River ran behind warehouses and chain-link fences. Many residents knew it mainly as a blue line on municipal maps. When the concrete walls were removed, the project also added footpaths, low overlooks, and two entrances connecting the river to bus routes. Those features were not biological interventions, yet they changed what happened along the water.<br><br>A neighborhood stewardship group began weekly walks. Members recorded clogged drains and newly eroded banks, but they also counted where visitors stopped, which paths became muddy, and whether signs could be read from a wheelchair. Their observations sometimes reached maintenance crews faster than the project's annual ecological report. After a broken drain discharged cloudy water, walkers photographed the plume and traced it to a street inlet before the next scheduled sampling day.<br><br>Public access can create pressure as well as protection. A shortcut through young plants damaged one bank, and food containers accumulated near the busiest overlook. The group responded by moving a path rather than simply adding warnings. It also asked schools to stagger large visits during nesting season. In this case, use was not the opposite of restoration; it was a condition that had to be designed and revised.<br><br>Some critics describe benches, paths, and programs as amenities separate from ecological success. That distinction is useful when it prevents visitor counts from being mistaken for evidence about fish or water quality. It becomes less useful when it implies that a river can be sustained without a public able to notice and defend it. Residents do not replace laboratory analysis, but their repeated presence expands the times and places at which change can be observed.<br><br>A restored river is thus both a set of processes and a relationship. Its curves, insects, and temperatures matter whether anyone visits. Yet access can create witnesses, and witnesses can become stewards. The strongest assessment asks not whether social and ecological measures are interchangeable, but whether each helps the recovery endure."},
174: {skill_tested: "Identify a shared view of restoration assessment.", question: "Both passages characterize restoration assessment as:", option1: "a judgment that should depend on a single visible sign.", option2: "a task that can be completed before construction begins.", option3: "a choice between scientific measurements and public observation.", option4: "an ongoing process requiring different observations over time.", correctAnswer: "an ongoing process requiring different observations over time.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A compares multiple indicators, seasons, and reference reaches.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B describes repeated public observation and revisions to access.<br><br>Answer: an ongoing process requiring different observations over time.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how to judge the restoration of an urban river.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>Removing a concrete channel from a river does not instantly restore the ecosystem that existed before construction. It changes the conditions under which recovery may occur. Water can slow along newly curved banks, spread into a floodplain, and deposit sediment in places that once received only a fast, narrow current. These physical changes are often the first indicators monitored by restoration scientists. Teams may install gauges before excavation, survey cross sections after storms, and place marked stones where sediment is expected to collect. Repeating those observations matters because a dry summer can make a redesigned channel appear stable even if it will erode during high water. Measurements taken before and after comparable flows help separate the effect of construction from ordinary seasonal variation.<br><br>Physical response, however, is not the same as biological recovery. Insects may return within a season if gravel and leaf litter accumulate. Fish that require cool pools may take longer, especially if upstream water remains warm. Riverside trees need years to produce shade and woody debris. A survey conducted shortly after construction can therefore find that the channel is behaving more naturally while the food web remains simplified.<br><br>Scientists address this difference in timing by establishing several reference points. They measure the restored reach before work begins, compare it with an unrestored reach nearby, and return during different seasons. No single indicator is decisive. Clear water may look healthy while containing few nutrients needed by small organisms; abundant algae may indicate either a productive season or excessive runoff. Evidence gains meaning through patterns among flow, temperature, sediment, plants, insects, and fish.<br><br>This approach makes patience measurable rather than vague. A project need not be declared either a triumph or a failure after one year. Managers can identify which processes have begun, which are delayed, and which remain blocked by conditions beyond the project boundary.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>Before restoration, the Millstone River ran behind warehouses and chain-link fences. Many residents knew it mainly as a blue line on municipal maps. When the concrete walls were removed, the project also added footpaths, low overlooks, and two entrances connecting the river to bus routes. Those features were not biological interventions, yet they changed what happened along the water.<br><br>A neighborhood stewardship group began weekly walks. Members recorded clogged drains and newly eroded banks, but they also counted where visitors stopped, which paths became muddy, and whether signs could be read from a wheelchair. Their observations sometimes reached maintenance crews faster than the project's annual ecological report. After a broken drain discharged cloudy water, walkers photographed the plume and traced it to a street inlet before the next scheduled sampling day.<br><br>Public access can create pressure as well as protection. A shortcut through young plants damaged one bank, and food containers accumulated near the busiest overlook. The group responded by moving a path rather than simply adding warnings. It also asked schools to stagger large visits during nesting season. In this case, use was not the opposite of restoration; it was a condition that had to be designed and revised.<br><br>Some critics describe benches, paths, and programs as amenities separate from ecological success. That distinction is useful when it prevents visitor counts from being mistaken for evidence about fish or water quality. It becomes less useful when it implies that a river can be sustained without a public able to notice and defend it. Residents do not replace laboratory analysis, but their repeated presence expands the times and places at which change can be observed.<br><br>A restored river is thus both a set of processes and a relationship. Its curves, insects, and temperatures matter whether anyone visits. Yet access can create witnesses, and witnesses can become stewards. The strongest assessment asks not whether social and ecological measures are interchangeable, but whether each helps the recovery endure."},
175: {skill_tested: "Analyze the function of contrasting interpretations of one indicator.", question: "Passage A's discussion of clear water and abundant algae primarily demonstrates that:", option1: "visible conditions require context from other indicators before they establish ecological health.", option2: "clear water always contains too few nutrients for any organism.", option3: "algae are reliable proof that runoff has damaged a river.", option4: "scientists should replace field observations with visitor counts.", correctAnswer: "visible conditions require context from other indicators before they establish ecological health.", explanation: "Step 1: Clear water and algae can each correspond to more than one ecological condition.<br><br>Step 2: The passage says evidence gains meaning through patterns among many indicators.<br><br>Step 3: The examples warn against treating appearance as a decisive measure.<br><br>Answer: visible conditions require context from other indicators before they establish ecological health.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how to judge the restoration of an urban river.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>Removing a concrete channel from a river does not instantly restore the ecosystem that existed before construction. It changes the conditions under which recovery may occur. Water can slow along newly curved banks, spread into a floodplain, and deposit sediment in places that once received only a fast, narrow current. These physical changes are often the first indicators monitored by restoration scientists. Teams may install gauges before excavation, survey cross sections after storms, and place marked stones where sediment is expected to collect. Repeating those observations matters because a dry summer can make a redesigned channel appear stable even if it will erode during high water. Measurements taken before and after comparable flows help separate the effect of construction from ordinary seasonal variation.<br><br>Physical response, however, is not the same as biological recovery. Insects may return within a season if gravel and leaf litter accumulate. Fish that require cool pools may take longer, especially if upstream water remains warm. Riverside trees need years to produce shade and woody debris. A survey conducted shortly after construction can therefore find that the channel is behaving more naturally while the food web remains simplified.<br><br>Scientists address this difference in timing by establishing several reference points. They measure the restored reach before work begins, compare it with an unrestored reach nearby, and return during different seasons. No single indicator is decisive. Clear water may look healthy while containing few nutrients needed by small organisms; abundant algae may indicate either a productive season or excessive runoff. Evidence gains meaning through patterns among flow, temperature, sediment, plants, insects, and fish.<br><br>This approach makes patience measurable rather than vague. A project need not be declared either a triumph or a failure after one year. Managers can identify which processes have begun, which are delayed, and which remain blocked by conditions beyond the project boundary.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>Before restoration, the Millstone River ran behind warehouses and chain-link fences. Many residents knew it mainly as a blue line on municipal maps. When the concrete walls were removed, the project also added footpaths, low overlooks, and two entrances connecting the river to bus routes. Those features were not biological interventions, yet they changed what happened along the water.<br><br>A neighborhood stewardship group began weekly walks. Members recorded clogged drains and newly eroded banks, but they also counted where visitors stopped, which paths became muddy, and whether signs could be read from a wheelchair. Their observations sometimes reached maintenance crews faster than the project's annual ecological report. After a broken drain discharged cloudy water, walkers photographed the plume and traced it to a street inlet before the next scheduled sampling day.<br><br>Public access can create pressure as well as protection. A shortcut through young plants damaged one bank, and food containers accumulated near the busiest overlook. The group responded by moving a path rather than simply adding warnings. It also asked schools to stagger large visits during nesting season. In this case, use was not the opposite of restoration; it was a condition that had to be designed and revised.<br><br>Some critics describe benches, paths, and programs as amenities separate from ecological success. That distinction is useful when it prevents visitor counts from being mistaken for evidence about fish or water quality. It becomes less useful when it implies that a river can be sustained without a public able to notice and defend it. Residents do not replace laboratory analysis, but their repeated presence expands the times and places at which change can be observed.<br><br>A restored river is thus both a set of processes and a relationship. Its curves, insects, and temperatures matter whether anyone visits. Yet access can create witnesses, and witnesses can become stewards. The strongest assessment asks not whether social and ecological measures are interchangeable, but whether each helps the recovery endure."},
176: {skill_tested: "Infer how adaptive access supports restoration.", question: "The stewardship group's decision to move a path rather than merely post warnings suggests that the group viewed visitor behavior as:", option1: "fixed and unrelated to the design of the site.", option2: "something that could be redirected through changes to the physical setting.", option3: "a reason to abandon public access entirely.", option4: "more important than every biological measure.", correctAnswer: "something that could be redirected through changes to the physical setting.", explanation: "Step 1: A shortcut damaged young plants despite the existing route.<br><br>Step 2: Moving the path changed the convenient behavior available to visitors.<br><br>Step 3: The response treats use as adjustable through design, not simply disobedient.<br><br>Answer: something that could be redirected through changes to the physical setting.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how to judge the restoration of an urban river.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>Removing a concrete channel from a river does not instantly restore the ecosystem that existed before construction. It changes the conditions under which recovery may occur. Water can slow along newly curved banks, spread into a floodplain, and deposit sediment in places that once received only a fast, narrow current. These physical changes are often the first indicators monitored by restoration scientists. Teams may install gauges before excavation, survey cross sections after storms, and place marked stones where sediment is expected to collect. Repeating those observations matters because a dry summer can make a redesigned channel appear stable even if it will erode during high water. Measurements taken before and after comparable flows help separate the effect of construction from ordinary seasonal variation.<br><br>Physical response, however, is not the same as biological recovery. Insects may return within a season if gravel and leaf litter accumulate. Fish that require cool pools may take longer, especially if upstream water remains warm. Riverside trees need years to produce shade and woody debris. A survey conducted shortly after construction can therefore find that the channel is behaving more naturally while the food web remains simplified.<br><br>Scientists address this difference in timing by establishing several reference points. They measure the restored reach before work begins, compare it with an unrestored reach nearby, and return during different seasons. No single indicator is decisive. Clear water may look healthy while containing few nutrients needed by small organisms; abundant algae may indicate either a productive season or excessive runoff. Evidence gains meaning through patterns among flow, temperature, sediment, plants, insects, and fish.<br><br>This approach makes patience measurable rather than vague. A project need not be declared either a triumph or a failure after one year. Managers can identify which processes have begun, which are delayed, and which remain blocked by conditions beyond the project boundary.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>Before restoration, the Millstone River ran behind warehouses and chain-link fences. Many residents knew it mainly as a blue line on municipal maps. When the concrete walls were removed, the project also added footpaths, low overlooks, and two entrances connecting the river to bus routes. Those features were not biological interventions, yet they changed what happened along the water.<br><br>A neighborhood stewardship group began weekly walks. Members recorded clogged drains and newly eroded banks, but they also counted where visitors stopped, which paths became muddy, and whether signs could be read from a wheelchair. Their observations sometimes reached maintenance crews faster than the project's annual ecological report. After a broken drain discharged cloudy water, walkers photographed the plume and traced it to a street inlet before the next scheduled sampling day.<br><br>Public access can create pressure as well as protection. A shortcut through young plants damaged one bank, and food containers accumulated near the busiest overlook. The group responded by moving a path rather than simply adding warnings. It also asked schools to stagger large visits during nesting season. In this case, use was not the opposite of restoration; it was a condition that had to be designed and revised.<br><br>Some critics describe benches, paths, and programs as amenities separate from ecological success. That distinction is useful when it prevents visitor counts from being mistaken for evidence about fish or water quality. It becomes less useful when it implies that a river can be sustained without a public able to notice and defend it. Residents do not replace laboratory analysis, but their repeated presence expands the times and places at which change can be observed.<br><br>A restored river is thus both a set of processes and a relationship. Its curves, insects, and temperatures matter whether anyone visits. Yet access can create witnesses, and witnesses can become stewards. The strongest assessment asks not whether social and ecological measures are interchangeable, but whether each helps the recovery endure."},
177: {skill_tested: "Compare how passages handle distinct forms of evidence.", question: "Which statement accurately compares the roles assigned to evidence in the two passages?", option1: "Passage A rejects seasonal comparison, while Passage B rejects all laboratory analysis.", option2: "Passage A measures only visitor use, while Passage B measures only water temperature.", option3: "Passage A combines ecological indicators to interpret recovery, while Passage B uses recurring public presence to widen observation and support stewardship.", option4: "Both passages argue that one successful observation proves permanent recovery.", correctAnswer: "Passage A combines ecological indicators to interpret recovery, while Passage B uses recurring public presence to widen observation and support stewardship.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A relates flow, temperature, sediment, plants, insects, and fish across time.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B emphasizes walkers' repeated observations and their role in maintenance and advocacy.<br><br>Step 3: The evidence serves different but compatible assessment functions.<br><br>Answer: Passage A combines ecological indicators to interpret recovery, while Passage B uses recurring public presence to widen observation and support stewardship.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how to judge the restoration of an urban river.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>Removing a concrete channel from a river does not instantly restore the ecosystem that existed before construction. It changes the conditions under which recovery may occur. Water can slow along newly curved banks, spread into a floodplain, and deposit sediment in places that once received only a fast, narrow current. These physical changes are often the first indicators monitored by restoration scientists. Teams may install gauges before excavation, survey cross sections after storms, and place marked stones where sediment is expected to collect. Repeating those observations matters because a dry summer can make a redesigned channel appear stable even if it will erode during high water. Measurements taken before and after comparable flows help separate the effect of construction from ordinary seasonal variation.<br><br>Physical response, however, is not the same as biological recovery. Insects may return within a season if gravel and leaf litter accumulate. Fish that require cool pools may take longer, especially if upstream water remains warm. Riverside trees need years to produce shade and woody debris. A survey conducted shortly after construction can therefore find that the channel is behaving more naturally while the food web remains simplified.<br><br>Scientists address this difference in timing by establishing several reference points. They measure the restored reach before work begins, compare it with an unrestored reach nearby, and return during different seasons. No single indicator is decisive. Clear water may look healthy while containing few nutrients needed by small organisms; abundant algae may indicate either a productive season or excessive runoff. Evidence gains meaning through patterns among flow, temperature, sediment, plants, insects, and fish.<br><br>This approach makes patience measurable rather than vague. A project need not be declared either a triumph or a failure after one year. Managers can identify which processes have begun, which are delayed, and which remain blocked by conditions beyond the project boundary.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>Before restoration, the Millstone River ran behind warehouses and chain-link fences. Many residents knew it mainly as a blue line on municipal maps. When the concrete walls were removed, the project also added footpaths, low overlooks, and two entrances connecting the river to bus routes. Those features were not biological interventions, yet they changed what happened along the water.<br><br>A neighborhood stewardship group began weekly walks. Members recorded clogged drains and newly eroded banks, but they also counted where visitors stopped, which paths became muddy, and whether signs could be read from a wheelchair. Their observations sometimes reached maintenance crews faster than the project's annual ecological report. After a broken drain discharged cloudy water, walkers photographed the plume and traced it to a street inlet before the next scheduled sampling day.<br><br>Public access can create pressure as well as protection. A shortcut through young plants damaged one bank, and food containers accumulated near the busiest overlook. The group responded by moving a path rather than simply adding warnings. It also asked schools to stagger large visits during nesting season. In this case, use was not the opposite of restoration; it was a condition that had to be designed and revised.<br><br>Some critics describe benches, paths, and programs as amenities separate from ecological success. That distinction is useful when it prevents visitor counts from being mistaken for evidence about fish or water quality. It becomes less useful when it implies that a river can be sustained without a public able to notice and defend it. Residents do not replace laboratory analysis, but their repeated presence expands the times and places at which change can be observed.<br><br>A restored river is thus both a set of processes and a relationship. Its curves, insects, and temperatures matter whether anyone visits. Yet access can create witnesses, and witnesses can become stewards. The strongest assessment asks not whether social and ecological measures are interchangeable, but whether each helps the recovery endure."},
178: {skill_tested: "Analyze a qualification about public-use measures.", question: "Why does Passage B call the distinction between amenities and ecological success useful in some circumstances?", option1: "It establishes that paths can never contribute to long-term stewardship.", option2: "It permits residents to replace every scheduled scientific survey.", option3: "It shows that ecological processes matter only when people observe them.", option4: "It prevents visitor counts from being treated as direct proof of fish populations or water quality.", correctAnswer: "It prevents visitor counts from being treated as direct proof of fish populations or water quality.", explanation: "Step 1: The passage says social and ecological measures are not interchangeable.<br><br>Step 2: It specifically warns against mistaking visitor counts for evidence about fish or water quality.<br><br>Step 3: The distinction protects the meaning of each measure while allowing interaction between them.<br><br>Answer: It prevents visitor counts from being treated as direct proof of fish populations or water quality.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how to judge the restoration of an urban river.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>Removing a concrete channel from a river does not instantly restore the ecosystem that existed before construction. It changes the conditions under which recovery may occur. Water can slow along newly curved banks, spread into a floodplain, and deposit sediment in places that once received only a fast, narrow current. These physical changes are often the first indicators monitored by restoration scientists. Teams may install gauges before excavation, survey cross sections after storms, and place marked stones where sediment is expected to collect. Repeating those observations matters because a dry summer can make a redesigned channel appear stable even if it will erode during high water. Measurements taken before and after comparable flows help separate the effect of construction from ordinary seasonal variation.<br><br>Physical response, however, is not the same as biological recovery. Insects may return within a season if gravel and leaf litter accumulate. Fish that require cool pools may take longer, especially if upstream water remains warm. Riverside trees need years to produce shade and woody debris. A survey conducted shortly after construction can therefore find that the channel is behaving more naturally while the food web remains simplified.<br><br>Scientists address this difference in timing by establishing several reference points. They measure the restored reach before work begins, compare it with an unrestored reach nearby, and return during different seasons. No single indicator is decisive. Clear water may look healthy while containing few nutrients needed by small organisms; abundant algae may indicate either a productive season or excessive runoff. Evidence gains meaning through patterns among flow, temperature, sediment, plants, insects, and fish.<br><br>This approach makes patience measurable rather than vague. A project need not be declared either a triumph or a failure after one year. Managers can identify which processes have begun, which are delayed, and which remain blocked by conditions beyond the project boundary.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>Before restoration, the Millstone River ran behind warehouses and chain-link fences. Many residents knew it mainly as a blue line on municipal maps. When the concrete walls were removed, the project also added footpaths, low overlooks, and two entrances connecting the river to bus routes. Those features were not biological interventions, yet they changed what happened along the water.<br><br>A neighborhood stewardship group began weekly walks. Members recorded clogged drains and newly eroded banks, but they also counted where visitors stopped, which paths became muddy, and whether signs could be read from a wheelchair. Their observations sometimes reached maintenance crews faster than the project's annual ecological report. After a broken drain discharged cloudy water, walkers photographed the plume and traced it to a street inlet before the next scheduled sampling day.<br><br>Public access can create pressure as well as protection. A shortcut through young plants damaged one bank, and food containers accumulated near the busiest overlook. The group responded by moving a path rather than simply adding warnings. It also asked schools to stagger large visits during nesting season. In this case, use was not the opposite of restoration; it was a condition that had to be designed and revised.<br><br>Some critics describe benches, paths, and programs as amenities separate from ecological success. That distinction is useful when it prevents visitor counts from being mistaken for evidence about fish or water quality. It becomes less useful when it implies that a river can be sustained without a public able to notice and defend it. Residents do not replace laboratory analysis, but their repeated presence expands the times and places at which change can be observed.<br><br>A restored river is thus both a set of processes and a relationship. Its curves, insects, and temperatures matter whether anyone visits. Yet access can create witnesses, and witnesses can become stewards. The strongest assessment asks not whether social and ecological measures are interchangeable, but whether each helps the recovery endure."},
179: {skill_tested: "Synthesize temporal and social dimensions of durable recovery.", question: "Taken together, the passages most strongly support which conclusion about a restoration project's durability?", option1: "Ecological processes need time and connected conditions, while informed public involvement can help detect and address threats during that recovery.", option2: "Durability is guaranteed once the river channel follows a curved path.", option3: "Durability depends only on attracting the highest possible number of visitors.", option4: "Public access and ecological monitoring should occur in separate locations so they cannot influence one another.", correctAnswer: "Ecological processes need time and connected conditions, while informed public involvement can help detect and address threats during that recovery.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A describes biological lags and barriers beyond the project boundary.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B shows residents detecting problems and adapting use.<br><br>Step 3: Durable recovery therefore combines long-term ecological conditions with continuing capacity to notice and respond.<br><br>Answer: Ecological processes need time and connected conditions, while informed public involvement can help detect and address threats during that recovery.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how to judge the restoration of an urban river.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>Removing a concrete channel from a river does not instantly restore the ecosystem that existed before construction. It changes the conditions under which recovery may occur. Water can slow along newly curved banks, spread into a floodplain, and deposit sediment in places that once received only a fast, narrow current. These physical changes are often the first indicators monitored by restoration scientists. Teams may install gauges before excavation, survey cross sections after storms, and place marked stones where sediment is expected to collect. Repeating those observations matters because a dry summer can make a redesigned channel appear stable even if it will erode during high water. Measurements taken before and after comparable flows help separate the effect of construction from ordinary seasonal variation.<br><br>Physical response, however, is not the same as biological recovery. Insects may return within a season if gravel and leaf litter accumulate. Fish that require cool pools may take longer, especially if upstream water remains warm. Riverside trees need years to produce shade and woody debris. A survey conducted shortly after construction can therefore find that the channel is behaving more naturally while the food web remains simplified.<br><br>Scientists address this difference in timing by establishing several reference points. They measure the restored reach before work begins, compare it with an unrestored reach nearby, and return during different seasons. No single indicator is decisive. Clear water may look healthy while containing few nutrients needed by small organisms; abundant algae may indicate either a productive season or excessive runoff. Evidence gains meaning through patterns among flow, temperature, sediment, plants, insects, and fish.<br><br>This approach makes patience measurable rather than vague. A project need not be declared either a triumph or a failure after one year. Managers can identify which processes have begun, which are delayed, and which remain blocked by conditions beyond the project boundary.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>Before restoration, the Millstone River ran behind warehouses and chain-link fences. Many residents knew it mainly as a blue line on municipal maps. When the concrete walls were removed, the project also added footpaths, low overlooks, and two entrances connecting the river to bus routes. Those features were not biological interventions, yet they changed what happened along the water.<br><br>A neighborhood stewardship group began weekly walks. Members recorded clogged drains and newly eroded banks, but they also counted where visitors stopped, which paths became muddy, and whether signs could be read from a wheelchair. Their observations sometimes reached maintenance crews faster than the project's annual ecological report. After a broken drain discharged cloudy water, walkers photographed the plume and traced it to a street inlet before the next scheduled sampling day.<br><br>Public access can create pressure as well as protection. A shortcut through young plants damaged one bank, and food containers accumulated near the busiest overlook. The group responded by moving a path rather than simply adding warnings. It also asked schools to stagger large visits during nesting season. In this case, use was not the opposite of restoration; it was a condition that had to be designed and revised.<br><br>Some critics describe benches, paths, and programs as amenities separate from ecological success. That distinction is useful when it prevents visitor counts from being mistaken for evidence about fish or water quality. It becomes less useful when it implies that a river can be sustained without a public able to notice and defend it. Residents do not replace laboratory analysis, but their repeated presence expands the times and places at which change can be observed.<br><br>A restored river is thus both a set of processes and a relationship. Its curves, insects, and temperatures matter whether anyone visits. Yet access can create witnesses, and witnesses can become stewards. The strongest assessment asks not whether social and ecological measures are interchangeable, but whether each helps the recovery endure."},
180: {skill_tested: "Distinguish complementary endpoints from interchangeable measures.", question: "Which choice best captures the relationship between the final claims of Passage A and Passage B?", option1: "Passage A rejects any judgment of failure, whereas Passage B declares every accessible project successful.", option2: "Passage A calls for diagnosing stages and barriers in ecological recovery, while Passage B argues that civic relationships can help that recovery persist without substituting for ecological evidence.", option3: "Passage A treats patience as impossible to measure, while Passage B treats public use as impossible to redesign.", option4: "Both passages define success exclusively as returning the river to its exact preconstruction state.", correctAnswer: "Passage A calls for diagnosing stages and barriers in ecological recovery, while Passage B argues that civic relationships can help that recovery persist without substituting for ecological evidence.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A replaces a quick success-or-failure verdict with measures of begun, delayed, and blocked processes.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B distinguishes social measures from ecological ones while connecting witnesses to stewardship.<br><br>Step 3: The claims are complementary because one defines recovery and the other addresses a relationship that may sustain it.<br><br>Answer: Passage A calls for diagnosing stages and barriers in ecological recovery, while Passage B argues that civic relationships can help that recovery persist without substituting for ecological evidence.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how to judge the restoration of an urban river.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>Removing a concrete channel from a river does not instantly restore the ecosystem that existed before construction. It changes the conditions under which recovery may occur. Water can slow along newly curved banks, spread into a floodplain, and deposit sediment in places that once received only a fast, narrow current. These physical changes are often the first indicators monitored by restoration scientists. Teams may install gauges before excavation, survey cross sections after storms, and place marked stones where sediment is expected to collect. Repeating those observations matters because a dry summer can make a redesigned channel appear stable even if it will erode during high water. Measurements taken before and after comparable flows help separate the effect of construction from ordinary seasonal variation.<br><br>Physical response, however, is not the same as biological recovery. Insects may return within a season if gravel and leaf litter accumulate. Fish that require cool pools may take longer, especially if upstream water remains warm. Riverside trees need years to produce shade and woody debris. A survey conducted shortly after construction can therefore find that the channel is behaving more naturally while the food web remains simplified.<br><br>Scientists address this difference in timing by establishing several reference points. They measure the restored reach before work begins, compare it with an unrestored reach nearby, and return during different seasons. No single indicator is decisive. Clear water may look healthy while containing few nutrients needed by small organisms; abundant algae may indicate either a productive season or excessive runoff. Evidence gains meaning through patterns among flow, temperature, sediment, plants, insects, and fish.<br><br>This approach makes patience measurable rather than vague. A project need not be declared either a triumph or a failure after one year. Managers can identify which processes have begun, which are delayed, and which remain blocked by conditions beyond the project boundary.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>Before restoration, the Millstone River ran behind warehouses and chain-link fences. Many residents knew it mainly as a blue line on municipal maps. When the concrete walls were removed, the project also added footpaths, low overlooks, and two entrances connecting the river to bus routes. Those features were not biological interventions, yet they changed what happened along the water.<br><br>A neighborhood stewardship group began weekly walks. Members recorded clogged drains and newly eroded banks, but they also counted where visitors stopped, which paths became muddy, and whether signs could be read from a wheelchair. Their observations sometimes reached maintenance crews faster than the project's annual ecological report. After a broken drain discharged cloudy water, walkers photographed the plume and traced it to a street inlet before the next scheduled sampling day.<br><br>Public access can create pressure as well as protection. A shortcut through young plants damaged one bank, and food containers accumulated near the busiest overlook. The group responded by moving a path rather than simply adding warnings. It also asked schools to stagger large visits during nesting season. In this case, use was not the opposite of restoration; it was a condition that had to be designed and revised.<br><br>Some critics describe benches, paths, and programs as amenities separate from ecological success. That distinction is useful when it prevents visitor counts from being mistaken for evidence about fish or water quality. It becomes less useful when it implies that a river can be sustained without a public able to notice and defend it. Residents do not replace laboratory analysis, but their repeated presence expands the times and places at which change can be observed.<br><br>A restored river is thus both a set of processes and a relationship. Its curves, insects, and temperatures matter whether anyone visits. Yet access can create witnesses, and witnesses can become stewards. The strongest assessment asks not whether social and ecological measures are interchangeable, but whether each helps the recovery endure."},
181: {skill_tested: "Identify the narrator's initial assumption.", question: "At the beginning of the passage, the narrator assumes that the pale patch should be:", option1: "removed along with the chair arm.", option2: "dated through laboratory testing.", option3: "stained until it is no longer visible.", option4: "displayed separately from the chair.", correctAnswer: "stained until it is no longer visible.", explanation: "Step 1: The narrator sees the patch interrupting the dark grain.<br><br>Step 2: The narrator initially believes the obvious task is to stain it until it disappears.<br><br>Answer: stained until it is no longer visible.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>HUMANITIES:</b> This passage examines how furniture repairs can serve as historical evidence.<br><br>During my first month in a conservation workshop, I was assigned a walnut chair with a pale wooden patch beneath one arm. The patch interrupted the dark grain and sat slightly above the surrounding surface. I assumed my task was obvious: stain the repair until it disappeared. My supervisor asked me to wait.<br><br>Under angled light, the patch revealed shallow knife marks. Its maker had shaped it by hand, probably decades after the chair was built. A small hole nearby showed where an earlier brace had been attached. The repair was not part of the original design, my supervisor explained, but it was part of the object's history. Concealing it might restore visual unity while erasing evidence of how someone had kept the chair useful.<br><br>Furniture conservation often appears to concern stable objects, yet every object arrives through a chain of decisions. A drawer runner is replaced because repeated motion wears it thin. A table leg is shortened after a floor changes. Upholstery is covered to follow fashion or to protect scarce fabric beneath it. These alterations can be clumsy, elegant, temporary, or ingenious. They record what owners valued enough to mend and what materials or skills were available to them. Reading those records requires more than noticing that two pieces of wood differ. Tool marks can suggest whether a patch was sawn quickly or fitted patiently. Fasteners may place a change within a broad manufacturing period, though rarely an exact year. Dirt trapped beneath a brace can show that the brace remained through years of use. Each clue is partial, and several clues may support more than one account. A responsible interpretation states that uncertainty openly. The aim is to narrow possibilities through material evidence, not to turn a plausible repair history into a convenient certainty.<br><br>This does not mean every repair must remain untouched. A corroded metal bracket may split nearby wood. A modern adhesive may continue to discolor a surface. An added screw can prevent a joint from moving as its maker intended. Conservators sometimes remove later work to protect the object, but responsible removal begins with documentation. Photographs, measurements, and written observations preserve information that the treatment itself may eliminate.<br><br>The difficult cases are repairs that are structurally harmless but visually awkward. Museums once commonly presented furniture as though it had moved directly from its maker's bench into a display case. Filling scratches and matching patches supported that illusion. More recent practice often asks a different question: which moment in an object's life is the display meant to represent? A pristine appearance may clarify a designer's original geometry. Visible wear may better explain ordinary ownership, repeated handling, or adaptation.<br><br>The answer also depends on context. A replacement handle made by a notable craftsperson may deserve attention in its own right. The same handle on another object may be undocumented and misleading. A repaired school desk displayed in a history classroom may invite discussion of maintenance and limited budgets; in an exhibition about manufacturing, the repair could distract from standardized construction. Evidence does not announce its own importance. Curators frame it through selection and explanation.<br><br>After examining the walnut chair, we cleaned the pale patch but did not darken it completely. We reduced the sharpest contrast so that it no longer dominated the chair from across the room. At close range, however, the knife marks remained visible. A label described the patch as an undated repair rather than assigning it a story we could not prove.<br><br>I had entered the workshop imagining conservation as a contest against change. The chair suggested a less tidy responsibility. Damage, repair, and use can occupy the same surface, and preserving an object may require deciding which changes should remain legible. The goal is not to make age vanish. It is to keep the material record readable without pretending that every mark says more than the evidence allows."},
182: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of an interpretive term.", question: "As used in the final paragraph, the word 'legible' most nearly means:", option1: "protected from all physical contact.", option2: "identical to its original condition.", option3: "marked with a precise date.", option4: "capable of being interpreted or understood.", correctAnswer: "capable of being interpreted or understood.", explanation: "Step 1: The final paragraph describes a material record containing damage, repair, and use.<br><br>Step 2: Keeping that record legible means preserving the ability to interpret its evidence.<br><br>Answer: capable of being interpreted or understood.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>HUMANITIES:</b> This passage examines how furniture repairs can serve as historical evidence.<br><br>During my first month in a conservation workshop, I was assigned a walnut chair with a pale wooden patch beneath one arm. The patch interrupted the dark grain and sat slightly above the surrounding surface. I assumed my task was obvious: stain the repair until it disappeared. My supervisor asked me to wait.<br><br>Under angled light, the patch revealed shallow knife marks. Its maker had shaped it by hand, probably decades after the chair was built. A small hole nearby showed where an earlier brace had been attached. The repair was not part of the original design, my supervisor explained, but it was part of the object's history. Concealing it might restore visual unity while erasing evidence of how someone had kept the chair useful.<br><br>Furniture conservation often appears to concern stable objects, yet every object arrives through a chain of decisions. A drawer runner is replaced because repeated motion wears it thin. A table leg is shortened after a floor changes. Upholstery is covered to follow fashion or to protect scarce fabric beneath it. These alterations can be clumsy, elegant, temporary, or ingenious. They record what owners valued enough to mend and what materials or skills were available to them. Reading those records requires more than noticing that two pieces of wood differ. Tool marks can suggest whether a patch was sawn quickly or fitted patiently. Fasteners may place a change within a broad manufacturing period, though rarely an exact year. Dirt trapped beneath a brace can show that the brace remained through years of use. Each clue is partial, and several clues may support more than one account. A responsible interpretation states that uncertainty openly. The aim is to narrow possibilities through material evidence, not to turn a plausible repair history into a convenient certainty.<br><br>This does not mean every repair must remain untouched. A corroded metal bracket may split nearby wood. A modern adhesive may continue to discolor a surface. An added screw can prevent a joint from moving as its maker intended. Conservators sometimes remove later work to protect the object, but responsible removal begins with documentation. Photographs, measurements, and written observations preserve information that the treatment itself may eliminate.<br><br>The difficult cases are repairs that are structurally harmless but visually awkward. Museums once commonly presented furniture as though it had moved directly from its maker's bench into a display case. Filling scratches and matching patches supported that illusion. More recent practice often asks a different question: which moment in an object's life is the display meant to represent? A pristine appearance may clarify a designer's original geometry. Visible wear may better explain ordinary ownership, repeated handling, or adaptation.<br><br>The answer also depends on context. A replacement handle made by a notable craftsperson may deserve attention in its own right. The same handle on another object may be undocumented and misleading. A repaired school desk displayed in a history classroom may invite discussion of maintenance and limited budgets; in an exhibition about manufacturing, the repair could distract from standardized construction. Evidence does not announce its own importance. Curators frame it through selection and explanation.<br><br>After examining the walnut chair, we cleaned the pale patch but did not darken it completely. We reduced the sharpest contrast so that it no longer dominated the chair from across the room. At close range, however, the knife marks remained visible. A label described the patch as an undated repair rather than assigning it a story we could not prove.<br><br>I had entered the workshop imagining conservation as a contest against change. The chair suggested a less tidy responsibility. Damage, repair, and use can occupy the same surface, and preserving an object may require deciding which changes should remain legible. The goal is not to make age vanish. It is to keep the material record readable without pretending that every mark says more than the evidence allows."},
183: {skill_tested: "Identify the passage's central argument.", question: "Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?", option1: "Conservation requires judging which changes carry useful evidence and how honestly that evidence can be presented.", option2: "Conservation should make every object appear newly manufactured.", option3: "Later repairs are always more important than an object's original design.", option4: "Museums should display only furniture with documented repair dates.", correctAnswer: "Conservation requires judging which changes carry useful evidence and how honestly that evidence can be presented.", explanation: "Step 1: Repairs may reveal use and decisions but may also damage or mislead.<br><br>Step 2: Context, documentation, and evidentiary limits guide treatment and display.<br><br>Answer: Conservation requires judging which changes carry useful evidence and how honestly that evidence can be presented.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>HUMANITIES:</b> This passage examines how furniture repairs can serve as historical evidence.<br><br>During my first month in a conservation workshop, I was assigned a walnut chair with a pale wooden patch beneath one arm. The patch interrupted the dark grain and sat slightly above the surrounding surface. I assumed my task was obvious: stain the repair until it disappeared. My supervisor asked me to wait.<br><br>Under angled light, the patch revealed shallow knife marks. Its maker had shaped it by hand, probably decades after the chair was built. A small hole nearby showed where an earlier brace had been attached. The repair was not part of the original design, my supervisor explained, but it was part of the object's history. Concealing it might restore visual unity while erasing evidence of how someone had kept the chair useful.<br><br>Furniture conservation often appears to concern stable objects, yet every object arrives through a chain of decisions. A drawer runner is replaced because repeated motion wears it thin. A table leg is shortened after a floor changes. Upholstery is covered to follow fashion or to protect scarce fabric beneath it. These alterations can be clumsy, elegant, temporary, or ingenious. They record what owners valued enough to mend and what materials or skills were available to them. Reading those records requires more than noticing that two pieces of wood differ. Tool marks can suggest whether a patch was sawn quickly or fitted patiently. Fasteners may place a change within a broad manufacturing period, though rarely an exact year. Dirt trapped beneath a brace can show that the brace remained through years of use. Each clue is partial, and several clues may support more than one account. A responsible interpretation states that uncertainty openly. The aim is to narrow possibilities through material evidence, not to turn a plausible repair history into a convenient certainty.<br><br>This does not mean every repair must remain untouched. A corroded metal bracket may split nearby wood. A modern adhesive may continue to discolor a surface. An added screw can prevent a joint from moving as its maker intended. Conservators sometimes remove later work to protect the object, but responsible removal begins with documentation. Photographs, measurements, and written observations preserve information that the treatment itself may eliminate.<br><br>The difficult cases are repairs that are structurally harmless but visually awkward. Museums once commonly presented furniture as though it had moved directly from its maker's bench into a display case. Filling scratches and matching patches supported that illusion. More recent practice often asks a different question: which moment in an object's life is the display meant to represent? A pristine appearance may clarify a designer's original geometry. Visible wear may better explain ordinary ownership, repeated handling, or adaptation.<br><br>The answer also depends on context. A replacement handle made by a notable craftsperson may deserve attention in its own right. The same handle on another object may be undocumented and misleading. A repaired school desk displayed in a history classroom may invite discussion of maintenance and limited budgets; in an exhibition about manufacturing, the repair could distract from standardized construction. Evidence does not announce its own importance. Curators frame it through selection and explanation.<br><br>After examining the walnut chair, we cleaned the pale patch but did not darken it completely. We reduced the sharpest contrast so that it no longer dominated the chair from across the room. At close range, however, the knife marks remained visible. A label described the patch as an undated repair rather than assigning it a story we could not prove.<br><br>I had entered the workshop imagining conservation as a contest against change. The chair suggested a less tidy responsibility. Damage, repair, and use can occupy the same surface, and preserving an object may require deciding which changes should remain legible. The goal is not to make age vanish. It is to keep the material record readable without pretending that every mark says more than the evidence allows."},
184: {skill_tested: "Analyze the function of a series of examples.", question: "The drawer runner, shortened table leg, and covered upholstery in the third paragraph primarily serve to:", option1: "prove that all old furniture was poorly designed.", option2: "show several ways furniture records practical choices made after manufacture.", option3: "list repairs that conservators are required to remove.", option4: "explain why walnut is unsuitable for chairs.", correctAnswer: "show several ways furniture records practical choices made after manufacture.", explanation: "Step 1: Each example is an alteration prompted by wear, environment, fashion, or protection.<br><br>Step 2: Together they support the claim that objects pass through a chain of decisions.<br><br>Step 3: The list broadens the argument beyond the single walnut chair.<br><br>Answer: show several ways furniture records practical choices made after manufacture.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>HUMANITIES:</b> This passage examines how furniture repairs can serve as historical evidence.<br><br>During my first month in a conservation workshop, I was assigned a walnut chair with a pale wooden patch beneath one arm. The patch interrupted the dark grain and sat slightly above the surrounding surface. I assumed my task was obvious: stain the repair until it disappeared. My supervisor asked me to wait.<br><br>Under angled light, the patch revealed shallow knife marks. Its maker had shaped it by hand, probably decades after the chair was built. A small hole nearby showed where an earlier brace had been attached. The repair was not part of the original design, my supervisor explained, but it was part of the object's history. Concealing it might restore visual unity while erasing evidence of how someone had kept the chair useful.<br><br>Furniture conservation often appears to concern stable objects, yet every object arrives through a chain of decisions. A drawer runner is replaced because repeated motion wears it thin. A table leg is shortened after a floor changes. Upholstery is covered to follow fashion or to protect scarce fabric beneath it. These alterations can be clumsy, elegant, temporary, or ingenious. They record what owners valued enough to mend and what materials or skills were available to them. Reading those records requires more than noticing that two pieces of wood differ. Tool marks can suggest whether a patch was sawn quickly or fitted patiently. Fasteners may place a change within a broad manufacturing period, though rarely an exact year. Dirt trapped beneath a brace can show that the brace remained through years of use. Each clue is partial, and several clues may support more than one account. A responsible interpretation states that uncertainty openly. The aim is to narrow possibilities through material evidence, not to turn a plausible repair history into a convenient certainty.<br><br>This does not mean every repair must remain untouched. A corroded metal bracket may split nearby wood. A modern adhesive may continue to discolor a surface. An added screw can prevent a joint from moving as its maker intended. Conservators sometimes remove later work to protect the object, but responsible removal begins with documentation. Photographs, measurements, and written observations preserve information that the treatment itself may eliminate.<br><br>The difficult cases are repairs that are structurally harmless but visually awkward. Museums once commonly presented furniture as though it had moved directly from its maker's bench into a display case. Filling scratches and matching patches supported that illusion. More recent practice often asks a different question: which moment in an object's life is the display meant to represent? A pristine appearance may clarify a designer's original geometry. Visible wear may better explain ordinary ownership, repeated handling, or adaptation.<br><br>The answer also depends on context. A replacement handle made by a notable craftsperson may deserve attention in its own right. The same handle on another object may be undocumented and misleading. A repaired school desk displayed in a history classroom may invite discussion of maintenance and limited budgets; in an exhibition about manufacturing, the repair could distract from standardized construction. Evidence does not announce its own importance. Curators frame it through selection and explanation.<br><br>After examining the walnut chair, we cleaned the pale patch but did not darken it completely. We reduced the sharpest contrast so that it no longer dominated the chair from across the room. At close range, however, the knife marks remained visible. A label described the patch as an undated repair rather than assigning it a story we could not prove.<br><br>I had entered the workshop imagining conservation as a contest against change. The chair suggested a less tidy responsibility. Damage, repair, and use can occupy the same surface, and preserving an object may require deciding which changes should remain legible. The goal is not to make age vanish. It is to keep the material record readable without pretending that every mark says more than the evidence allows."},
185: {skill_tested: "Explain why documentation precedes irreversible treatment.", question: "Why does the author emphasize documenting a harmful repair before removing it?", option1: "Documentation guarantees that the repair will become structurally harmless.", option2: "Written observations can replace the original object in every exhibition.", option3: "Documentation can preserve evidence that the physical treatment will destroy.", option4: "Measurements prove who made every undocumented repair.", correctAnswer: "Documentation can preserve evidence that the physical treatment will destroy.", explanation: "Step 1: Some later work must be removed to protect an object.<br><br>Step 2: Removal can eliminate information contained in that work.<br><br>Step 3: Recording it preserves access to evidence even when the material cannot remain.<br><br>Answer: Documentation can preserve evidence that the physical treatment will destroy.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>HUMANITIES:</b> This passage examines how furniture repairs can serve as historical evidence.<br><br>During my first month in a conservation workshop, I was assigned a walnut chair with a pale wooden patch beneath one arm. The patch interrupted the dark grain and sat slightly above the surrounding surface. I assumed my task was obvious: stain the repair until it disappeared. My supervisor asked me to wait.<br><br>Under angled light, the patch revealed shallow knife marks. Its maker had shaped it by hand, probably decades after the chair was built. A small hole nearby showed where an earlier brace had been attached. The repair was not part of the original design, my supervisor explained, but it was part of the object's history. Concealing it might restore visual unity while erasing evidence of how someone had kept the chair useful.<br><br>Furniture conservation often appears to concern stable objects, yet every object arrives through a chain