e answer also depends on context. A replacement handle made by a notable craftsperson may deserve attention in its own right. The same handle on another object may be undocumented and misleading. A repaired school desk displayed in a history classroom may invite discussion of maintenance and limited budgets; in an exhibition about manufacturing, the repair could distract from standardized construction. Evidence does not announce its own importance. Curators frame it through selection and explanation.<br><br>After examining the walnut chair, we cleaned the pale patch but did not darken it completely. We reduced the sharpest contrast so that it no longer dominated the chair from across the room. At close range, however, the knife marks remained visible. A label described the patch as an undated repair rather than assigning it a story we could not prove.<br><br>I had entered the workshop imagining conservation as a contest against change. The chair suggested a less tidy responsibility. Damage, repair, and use can occupy the same surface, and preserving an object may require deciding which changes should remain legible. The goal is not to make age vanish. It is to keep the material record readable without pretending that every mark says more than the evidence allows."},
186: {skill_tested: "Infer the effect of exhibition purpose on interpretation.", question: "The discussion of the repaired school desk implies that the same physical feature may be:", option1: "visible only when a classroom has limited funds.", option2: "evidence of standardized construction in every context.", option3: "removed whenever its maker is unknown.", option4: "historically useful in one exhibition and distracting in another.", correctAnswer: "historically useful in one exhibition and distracting in another.", explanation: "Step 1: In a history classroom, the repair can prompt discussion of maintenance and budgets.<br><br>Step 2: In a manufacturing exhibition, it may distract from standardized construction.<br><br>Step 3: Interpretive relevance changes with the question an exhibition asks.<br><br>Answer: historically useful in one exhibition and distracting in another.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>HUMANITIES:</b> This passage examines how furniture repairs can serve as historical evidence.<br><br>During my first month in a conservation workshop, I was assigned a walnut chair with a pale wooden patch beneath one arm. The patch interrupted the dark grain and sat slightly above the surrounding surface. I assumed my task was obvious: stain the repair until it disappeared. My supervisor asked me to wait.<br><br>Under angled light, the patch revealed shallow knife marks. Its maker had shaped it by hand, probably decades after the chair was built. A small hole nearby showed where an earlier brace had been attached. The repair was not part of the original design, my supervisor explained, but it was part of the object's history. Concealing it might restore visual unity while erasing evidence of how someone had kept the chair useful.<br><br>Furniture conservation often appears to concern stable objects, yet every object arrives through a chain of decisions. A drawer runner is replaced beca