mate how strongly each measured factor changes departure probability, but unmeasured conditions remain. Age, prior route experience, health, and conditions beyond the bay may all contribute. A model that predicts many departures correctly is still a description of tendencies, not a command followed by every bird.<br><br>The decision to depart is best understood as a moving threshold. Fuel establishes what is physically possible, while wind, season, habitat, and danger alter what is worthwhile now. The arrow on a migration map is therefore the accumulated result of many moments in which waiting and leaving exchange advantages.<br><br>Researchers improve their models by testing predictions at another bay rather than merely refining the first dataset. A relationship that appears strong at a sheltered site may weaken where storms develop faster or feeding flats vanish earlier with the tide. Successful prediction across locations suggests a general process; failure can reveal a condition the original study overlooked.<br><br>Departure is not the same as successful arrival. Some transmitters detect a bird leaving but not where it lands. Linking receiver arrays along the route helps distinguish a long migratory flight from a short relocation to another feeding area. That distinction prevents researchers from treating every nighttime movement as equivalent."},
278: {skill_tested: "Evaluate evidence limits.", question: "Why is departure time alone insufficient after a falcon appears?", option1: "Radio tags stop working near predators.", option2: "Researchers cannot observe feeding behavior.", option3: "Flight direction never provides useful evidence.", option4: "The falcon may reduce feeding or directly encourage departure, producing different mechanisms.", correctAnswer: "The falcon may reduce feeding or directly encourage departure, producing different mechanisms.", explanation: "Step 1: The same timing outcome can arise through distinct causal paths.<br><br>Answer: The falcon may reduce feeding or directly encourage departure, producing different mechanisms.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how migratory shorebirds decide when to leave a coastal stopover.<br><br>Migration maps often show clean arrows between breeding and wintering grounds. For an individual bird, however, the journey is a sequence of uncertain departures. At a stopover, the bird must rebuild energy reserves while time, weather, predators, and competition continue to change. Leaving too soon may mean running out of fuel. Waiting too long may forfeit favorable winds or delay arrival at a destination where nesting space is limited.<br><br>Researchers studying sandpipers along a tidal bay weigh and tag birds during brief captures. Small radio transmitters record when each bird departs. Because mass alone does not reveal fat reserves precisely, researchers also measure wing length and use repeated samples to estimate a fuel index. Weather stations record wind direction, speed, pressure, and cloud cover.<br><br>A simple hypothesis predicts that birds leave upon reaching a fixed fuel threshold. Fuel matters strongly: very lean birds rarely depart on long flights. Yet tagged birds above the apparent threshold sometimes remain for several nights