280: {skill_tested: "Identify how phase change stores energy.", question: "According to the first paragraph, how does a phase-change material store heat differently from an ordinary wall?", option1: "It generates cold whenever its temperature rises.", option2: "It absorbs substantial energy while melting near a stable temperature.", option3: "It stores energy only by increasing its mass.", option4: "It prevents all heat from entering a room.", correctAnswer: "It absorbs substantial energy while melting near a stable temperature.", explanation: "Step 1: An ordinary wall stores heat as its temperature rises.<br><br>Step 2: The phase-change material also absorbs energy during melting while staying near its transition temperature.<br><br>Answer: It absorbs substantial energy while melting near a stable temperature.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage explains how phase-change materials can store heat inside building components.<br><br>A wall ordinarily stores heat by becoming warmer. The amount depends on its mass and heat capacity, so a lightweight wall changes temperature relatively quickly. A phase-change material adds another form of storage. As it melts, it absorbs energy while remaining near its melting temperature. When it later freezes, it releases much of that energy. The storage is tied to a change of state rather than only to a rising temperature. Because melting and freezing are reversible, the same material can repeat the exchange many times. Useful storage therefore depends not only on one cycle's capacity but on retaining that capacity through years of transitions.<br><br>For a building, the selected melting range matters. A substance that melts far below indoor temperature will already be liquid when cooling is needed. One that melts too high may never activate. Ideally, the material absorbs daytime heat as a room approaches the upper edge of comfort and solidifies after temperatures fall, ready to repeat the cycle the next day.<br><br>Many useful phase-change substances are waxes or salt mixtures that cannot simply be poured into wallboard. Liquid could leak during melting or react with surrounding material. Manufacturers enclose tiny droplets in polymer or mineral shells. Millions of microcapsules can then be blended into plaster, ceiling panels, or insulation while the shell keeps the active core separated.<br><br>Encapsulation solves one problem while creating others. The shell occupies space but stores little heat, so a composite holds less active material than a pure block of the substance. Capsules can also break during mixing, cutting, or repeated expansion. Their surfaces may interfere with the binder that gives a panel strength. Designers balance capsule concentration against mechanical durability and manufacturability.<br><br>Heat must reach the capsule before storage can occur. Some phase-change materials conduct heat poorly. A thick panel may contain substantial capacity at its center but charge too slowly during the useful part of the day. Adding conductive particles or thin metal paths can increase heat transfer, although those additions raise cost, add weight, or alter fire performance. Capacity and delivery rate are separate design questions.<br><br>Laboratory evaluation therefore extends beyond measuring one melting point. Researchers cycle samples through heating and cooling hundreds or thousands of times. They check whether the transition temperature drifts, whether shells leak, and how much storage remains. Full panels are tested for bending strength, flame spread, moisture response, and heat flow. A capsule that performs well in a small sealed vial may fail during ordinary construction.<br><br>Building tests require realistic schedules. A panel may reduce afternoon peaks in a climate with cool nights because the material freezes before morning. During a sequence of hot nights, it may remain liquid and begin each day with little empty capacity. Mechanical ventilation or nighttime cooling can reset it, but the energy used for that reset must be included when judging savings.<br><br>Placement matters too. A panel hidden behind heavy furniture exchanges heat with the room slowly. Material near a sunlit surface may charge early, while ceiling placement may better intercept rising warm air. Control systems can coordinate shades, ventilation, and cooling equipment so that stored heat is released when outdoor conditions are favorable instead of returning to the occupied room.<br><br>Phase-change panels are sometimes described as thermal batteries. The comparison is useful because both devices shift energy in time, but it can mislead. The panel does not generate cooling, and its storage range is constrained by temperature. It is more like a reusable buffer that delays part of a heat load. Whether delay reduces energy use depends on what happens when the stored heat is released.<br><br>The technology's success is therefore not captured by the largest laboratory storage number. A useful product must survive manufacturing and cycling, exchange heat at the needed rate, melt and freeze within the building's actual schedule, and cooperate with other systems. Material capacity creates an opportunity; design and operation determine whether the building can use it."},
281: {skill_tested: "Identify the purpose of microcapsules.", question: "Why are phase-change substances enclosed in tiny shells before being blended into panels?", option1: "To raise every substance's melting point above indoor temperature", option2: "To ensure the core conducts heat instantly", option3: "To contain the liquid phase and separate it from surrounding material", option4: "To eliminate the need for a panel binder", correctAnswer: "To contain the liquid phase and separate it from surrounding material", explanation: "Step 1: Waxes or salts may leak when they melt or react with other materials.<br><br>Step 2: Capsule shells contain each droplet while allowing it to be mixed into a composite.<br><br>Answer: To contain the liquid phase and separate it from surrounding material", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage explains how phase-change materials can store heat inside building components.<br><br>A wall ordinarily stores heat by becoming warmer. The amount depends on its mass and heat capacity, so a lightweight wall changes temperature relatively quickly. A phase-change material adds another form of storage. As it melts, it absorbs energy while remaining near its melting temperature. When it later freezes, it releases much of that energy. The storage is tied to a change of state rather than only to a rising temperature. Because melting and freezing are reversible, the same material can repeat the exchange many times. Useful storage therefore depends not only on one cycle's capacity but on retaining that capacity through years of transitions.<br><br>For a building, the selected melting range matters. A substance that melts far below indoor temperature will already be liquid when cooling is needed. One that melts too high may never activate. Ideally, the material absorbs daytime heat as a room approaches the upper edge of comfort and solidifies after temperatures fall, ready to repeat the cycle the next day.<br><br>Many useful phase-change substances are waxes or salt mixtures that cannot simply be poured into wallboard. Liquid could leak during melting or react with surrounding material. Manufacturers enclose tiny droplets in polymer or mineral shells. Millions of microcapsules can then be blended into plaster, ceiling panels, or insulation while the shell keeps the active core separated.<br><br>Encapsulation solves one problem while creating others. The shell occupies space but stores little heat, so a composite holds less active material than a pure block of the substance. Capsules can also break during mixing, cutting, or repeated expansion. Their surfaces may interfere with the binder that gives a panel strength. Designers balance capsule concentration against mechanical durability and manufacturability.<br><br>Heat must reach the capsule before storage can occur. Some phase-change materials conduct heat poorly. A thick panel may contain substantial capacity at its center but charge too slowly during the useful part of the day. Adding conductive particles or thin metal paths can increase heat transfer, although those additions raise cost, add weight, or alter fire performance. Capacity and delivery rate are separate design questions.<br><br>Laboratory evaluation therefore extends beyond measuring one melting point. Researchers cycle samples through heating and cooling hundreds or thousands of times. They check whether the transition temperature drifts, whether shells leak, and how much storage remains. Full panels are tested for bending strength, flame spread, moisture response, and heat flow. A capsule that performs well in a small sealed vial may fail during ordinary construction.<br><br>Building tests require realistic schedules. A panel may reduce afternoon peaks in a climate with cool nights because the material freezes before morning. During a sequence of hot nights, it may remain liquid and begin each day with little empty capacity. Mechanical ventilation or nighttime cooling can reset it, but the energy used for that reset must be included when judging savings.<br><br>Placement matters too. A panel hidden behind heavy furniture exchanges heat with the room slowly. Material near a sunlit surface may charge early, while ceiling placement may better intercept rising warm air. Control systems can coordinate shades, ventilation, and cooling equipment so that stored heat is released when outdoor conditions are favorable instead of returning to the occupied room.<br><br>Phase-change panels are sometimes described as thermal batteries. The comparison is useful because both devices shift energy in time, but it can mislead. The panel does not generate cooling, and its storage range is constrained by temperature. It is more like a reusable buffer that delays part of a heat load. Whether delay reduces energy use depends on what happens when the stored heat is released.<br><br>The technology's success is therefore not captured by the largest laboratory storage number. A useful product must survive manufacturing and cycling, exchange heat at the needed rate, melt and freeze within the building's actual schedule, and cooperate with other systems. Material capacity creates an opportunity; design and operation determine whether the building can use it."},
282: {skill_tested: "Identify the passage's central claim.", question: "Which statement best expresses the passage's central idea?", option1: "The material with the largest measured heat capacity will succeed in every building.", option2: "Microcapsules generate cooling without releasing stored heat.", option3: "A phase-change panel works best when it remains liquid every night.", option4: "Phase-change materials can shift heat in time, but useful performance depends on containment, transfer rate, durability, operating temperatures, and system integration.", correctAnswer: "Phase-change materials can shift heat in time, but useful performance depends on containment, transfer rate, durability, operating temperatures, and system integration.", explanation: "Step 1: Melting and freezing provide reusable thermal storage.<br><br>Step 2: The passage identifies shell, strength, conductivity, cycling, schedule, placement, and control constraints.<br><br>Answer: Phase-change materials can shift heat in time, but useful performance depends on containment, transfer rate, durability, operating temperatures, and system integration.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage explains how phase-change materials can store heat inside building components.<br><br>A wall ordinarily stores heat by becoming warmer. The amount depends on its mass and heat capacity, so a lightweight wall changes temperature relatively quickly. A phase-change material adds another form of storage. As it melts, it absorbs energy while remaining near its melting temperature. When it later freezes, it releases much of that energy. The storage is tied to a change of state rather than only to a rising temperature. Because melting and freezing are reversible, the same material can repeat the exchange many times. Useful storage therefore depends not only on one cycle's capacity but on retaining that capacity through years of transitions.<br><br>For a building, the selected melting range matters. A substance that melts far below indoor temperature will already be liquid when cooling is needed. One that melts too high may never activate. Ideally, the material absorbs daytime heat as a room approaches the upper edge of comfort and solidifies after temperatures fall, ready to repeat the cycle the next day.<br><br>Many useful phase-change substances are waxes or salt mixtures that cannot simply be poured into wallboard. Liquid could leak during melting or react with surrounding material. Manufacturers enclose tiny droplets in polymer or mineral shells. Millions of microcapsules can then be blended into plaster, ceiling panels, or insulation while the shell keeps the active core separated.<br><br>Encapsulation solves one problem while creating others. The shell occupies space but stores little heat, so a composite holds less active material than a pure block of the substance. Capsules can also break during mixing, cutting, or repeated expansion. Their surfaces may interfere with the binder that gives a panel strength. Designers balance capsule concentration against mechanical durability and manufacturability.<br><br>Heat must reach the capsule before storage can occur. Some phase-change materials conduct heat poorly. A thick panel may contain substantial capacity at its center but charge too slowly during the useful part of the day. Adding conductive particles or thin metal paths can increase heat transfer, although those additions raise cost, add weight, or alter fire performance. Capacity and delivery rate are separate design questions.<br><br>Laboratory evaluation therefore extends beyond measuring one melting point. Researchers cycle samples through heating and cooling hundreds or thousands of times. They check whether the transition temperature drifts, whether shells leak, and how much storage remains. Full panels are tested for bending strength, flame spread, moisture response, and heat flow. A capsule that performs well in a small sealed vial may fail during ordinary construction.<br><br>Building tests require realistic schedules. A panel may reduce afternoon peaks in a climate with cool nights because the material freezes before morning. During a sequence of hot nights, it may remain liquid and begin each day with little empty capacity. Mechanical ventilation or nighttime cooling can reset it, but the energy used for that reset must be included when judging savings.<br><br>Placement matters too. A panel hidden behind heavy furniture exchanges heat with the room slowly. Material near a sunlit surface may charge early, while ceiling placement may better intercept rising warm air. Control systems can coordinate shades, ventilation, and cooling equipment so that stored heat is released when outdoor conditions are favorable instead of returning to the occupied room.<br><br>Phase-change panels are sometimes described as thermal batteries. The comparison is useful because both devices shift energy in time, but it can mislead. The panel does not generate cooling, and its storage range is constrained by temperature. It is more like a reusable buffer that delays part of a heat load. Whether delay reduces energy use depends on what happens when the stored heat is released.<br><br>The technology's success is therefore not captured by the largest laboratory storage number. A useful product must survive manufacturing and cycling, exchange heat at the needed rate, melt and freeze within the building's actual schedule, and cooperate with other systems. Material capacity creates an opportunity; design and operation determine whether the building can use it."},
283: {skill_tested: "Infer why melting range must match use conditions.", question: "A material that melts well below normal room temperature would be poorly suited to absorbing an afternoon indoor heat peak because it would likely:", option1: "already be liquid and have little unused phase-change capacity.", option2: "remain permanently frozen during the afternoon.", option3: "increase the wall's mechanical strength too much.", option4: "prevent heat from reaching its capsules.", correctAnswer: "already be liquid and have little unused phase-change capacity.", explanation: "Step 1: Storage occurs as solid material melts.<br><br>Step 2: A very low melting material will have completed that transition before the room reaches its afternoon peak.<br><br>Step 3: With little solid phase remaining, it cannot absorb much more latent heat.<br><br>Answer: already be liquid and have little unused phase-change capacity.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage explains how phase-change materials can store heat inside building components.<br><br>A wall ordinarily stores heat by becoming warmer. The amount depends on its mass and heat capacity, so a lightweight wall changes temperature relatively quickly. A phase-change material adds another form of storage. As it melts, it absorbs energy while remaining near its melting temperature. When it later freezes, it releases much of that energy. The storage is tied to a change of state rather than only to a rising temperature. Because melting and freezing are reversible, the same material can repeat the exchange many times. Useful storage therefore depends not only on one cycle's capacity but on retaining that capacity through years of transitions.<br><br>For a building, the selected melting range matters. A substance that melts far below indoor temperature will already be liquid when cooling is needed. One that melts too high may never activate. Ideally, the material absorbs daytime heat as a room approaches the upper edge of comfort and solidifies after temperatures fall, ready to repeat the cycle the next day.<br><br>Many useful phase-change substances are waxes or salt mixtures that cannot simply be poured into wallboard. Liquid could leak during melting or react with surrounding material. Manufacturers enclose tiny droplets in polymer or mineral shells. Millions of microcapsules can then be blended into plaster, ceiling panels, or insulation while the shell keeps the active core separated.<br><br>Encapsulation solves one problem while creating others. The shell occupies space but stores little heat, so a composite holds less active material than a pure block of the substance. Capsules can also break during mixing, cutting, or repeated expansion. Their surfaces may interfere with the binder that gives a panel strength. Designers balance capsule concentration against mechanical durability and manufacturability.<br><br>Heat must reach the capsule before storage can occur. Some phase-change materials conduct heat poorly. A thick panel may contain substantial capacity at its center but charge too slowly during the useful part of the day. Adding conductive particles or thin metal paths can increase heat transfer, although those additions raise cost, add weight, or alter fire performance. Capacity and delivery rate are separate design questions.<br><br>Laboratory evaluation therefore extends beyond measuring one melting point. Researchers cycle samples through heating and cooling hundreds or thousands of times. They check whether the transition temperature drifts, whether shells leak, and how much storage remains. Full panels are tested for bending strength, flame spread, moisture response, and heat flow. A capsule that performs well in a small sealed vial may fail during ordinary construction.<br><br>Building tests require realistic schedules. A panel may reduce afternoon peaks in a climate with cool nights because the material freezes before morning. During a sequence of hot nights, it may remain liquid and begin each day with little empty capacity. Mechanical ventilation or nighttime cooling can reset it, but the energy used for that reset must be included when judging savings.<br><br>Placement matters too. A panel hidden behind heavy furniture exchanges heat with the room slowly. Material near a sunlit surface may charge early, while ceiling placement may better intercept rising warm air. Control systems can coordinate shades, ventilation, and cooling equipment so that stored heat is released when outdoor conditions are favorable instead of returning to the occupied room.<br><br>Phase-change panels are sometimes described as thermal batteries. The comparison is useful because both devices shift energy in time, but it can mislead. The panel does not generate cooling, and its storage range is constrained by temperature. It is more like a reusable buffer that delays part of a heat load. Whether delay reduces energy use depends on what happens when the stored heat is released.<br><br>The technology's success is therefore not captured by the largest laboratory storage number. A useful product must survive manufacturing and cycling, exchange heat at the needed rate, melt and freeze within the building's actual schedule, and cooperate with other systems. Material capacity creates an opportunity; design and operation determine whether the building can use it."},
284: {skill_tested: "Analyze a composite-design tradeoff.", question: "Why can increasing the proportion of microcapsules eventually weaken a wall panel?", option1: "The active cores become heavier each time they melt.", option2: "Capsules interrupt the binder and their shells contribute little structural strength.", option3: "More capsules always reduce stored thermal energy.", option4: "Capsules prevent the panel from having any melting range.", correctAnswer: "Capsules interrupt the binder and their shells contribute little structural strength.", explanation: "Step 1: More capsules increase active storage material.<br><br>Step 2: They also occupy space in the composite and may interfere with the strength-giving binder.<br><br>Step 3: Storage concentration and mechanical durability must be balanced rather than maximized independently.<br><br>Answer: Capsules interrupt the binder and their shells contribute little structural strength.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage explains how phase-change materials can store heat inside building components.<br><br>A wall ordinarily stores heat by becoming warmer. The amount depends on its mass and heat capacity, so a lightweight wall changes temperature relatively quickly. A phase-change material adds another form of storage. As it melts, it absorbs energy while remaining near its melting temperature. When it later freezes, it releases much of that energy. The storage is tied to a change of state rather than only to a rising temperature. Because melting and freezing are reversible, the same material can repeat the exchange many times. Useful storage therefore depends not only on one cycle's capacity but on retaining that capacity through years of transitions.<br><br>For a building, the selected melting range matters. A substance that melts far below indoor temperature will already be liquid when cooling is needed. One that melts too high may never activate. Ideally, the material absorbs daytime heat as a room approaches the upper edge of comfort and solidifies after temperatures fall, ready to repeat the cycle the next day.<br><br>Many useful phase-change substances are waxes or salt mixtures that cannot simply be poured into wallboard. Liquid could leak during melting or react with surrounding material. Manufacturers enclose tiny droplets in polymer or mineral shells. Millions of microcapsules can then be blended into plaster, ceiling panels, or insulation while the shell keeps the active core separated.<br><br>Encapsulation solves one problem while creating others. The shell occupies space but stores little heat, so a composite holds less active material than a pure block of the substance. Capsules can also break during mixing, cutting, or repeated expansion. Their surfaces may interfere with the binder that gives a panel strength. Designers balance capsule concentration against mechanical durability and manufacturability.<br><br>Heat must reach the capsule before storage can occur. Some phase-change materials conduct heat poorly. A thick panel may contain substantial capacity at its center but charge too slowly during the useful part of the day. Adding conductive particles or thin metal paths can increase heat transfer, although those additions raise cost, add weight, or alter fire performance. Capacity and delivery rate are separate design questions.<br><br>Laboratory evaluation therefore extends beyond measuring one melting point. Researchers cycle samples through heating and cooling hundreds or thousands of times. They check whether the transition temperature drifts, whether shells leak, and how much storage remains. Full panels are tested for bending strength, flame spread, moisture response, and heat flow. A capsule that performs well in a small sealed vial may fail during ordinary construction.<br><br>Building tests require realistic schedules. A panel may reduce afternoon peaks in a climate with cool nights because the material freezes before morning. During a sequence of hot nights, it may remain liquid and begin each day with little empty capacity. Mechanical ventilation or nighttime cooling can reset it, but the energy used for that reset must be included when judging savings.<br><br>Placement matters too. A panel hidden behind heavy furniture exchanges heat with the room slowly. Material near a sunlit surface may charge early, while ceiling placement may better intercept rising warm air. Control systems can coordinate shades, ventilation, and cooling equipment so that stored heat is released when outdoor conditions are favorable instead of returning to the occupied room.<br><br>Phase-change panels are sometimes described as thermal batteries. The comparison is useful because both devices shift energy in time, but it can mislead. The panel does not generate cooling, and its storage range is constrained by temperature. It is more like a reusable buffer that delays part of a heat load. Whether delay reduces energy use depends on what happens when the stored heat is released.<br><br>The technology's success is therefore not captured by the largest laboratory storage number. A useful product must survive manufacturing and cycling, exchange heat at the needed rate, melt and freeze within the building's actual schedule, and cooperate with other systems. Material capacity creates an opportunity; design and operation determine whether the building can use it."},
285: {skill_tested: "Distinguish storage capacity from delivery rate.", question: "A thick panel can possess high theoretical capacity yet perform poorly during a short hot afternoon if:", option1: "all capsules melt and freeze instantaneously.", option2: "the panel is tested for flame spread.", option3: "heat moves too slowly to reach much of the material before the peak passes.", option4: "conductive additions reduce the time needed for charging.", correctAnswer: "heat moves too slowly to reach much of the material before the peak passes.", explanation: "Step 1: Capacity measures how much heat the material can store.<br><br>Step 2: Poor conductivity limits how quickly inner material can receive that heat.<br><br>Step 3: Capacity unavailable within the relevant time window cannot buffer the peak effectively.<br><br>Answer: heat moves too slowly to reach much of the material before the peak passes.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage explains how phase-change materials can store heat inside building components.<br><br>A wall ordinarily stores heat by becoming warmer. The amount depends on its mass and heat capacity, so a lightweight wall changes temperature relatively quickly. A phase-change material adds another form of storage. As it melts, it absorbs energy while remaining near its melting temperature. When it later freezes, it releases much of that energy. The storage is tied to a change of state rather than only to a rising temperature. Because melting and freezing are reversible, the same material can repeat the exchange many times. Useful storage therefore depends not only on one cycle's capacity but on retaining that capacity through years of transitions.<br><br>For a building, the selected melting range matters. A substance that melts far below indoor temperature will already be liquid when cooling is needed. One that melts too high may never activate. Ideally, the material absorbs daytime heat as a room approaches the upper edge of comfort and solidifies after temperatures fall, ready to repeat the cycle the next day.<br><br>Many useful phase-change substances are waxes or salt mixtures that cannot simply be poured into wallboard. Liquid could leak during melting or react with surrounding material. Manufacturers enclose tiny droplets in polymer or mineral shells. Millions of microcapsules can then be blended into plaster, ceiling panels, or insulation while the shell keeps the active core separated.<br><br>Encapsulation solves one problem while creating others. The shell occupies space but stores little heat, so a composite holds less active material than a pure block of the substance. Capsules can also break during mixing, cutting, or repeated expansion. Their surfaces may interfere with the binder that gives a panel strength. Designers balance capsule concentration against mechanical durability and manufacturability.<br><br>Heat must reach the capsule before storage can occur. Some phase-change materials conduct heat poorly. A thick panel may contain substantial capacity at its center but charge too slowly during the useful part of the day. Adding conductive particles or thin metal paths can increase heat transfer, although those additions raise cost, add weight, or alter fire performance. Capacity and delivery rate are separate design questions.<br><br>Laboratory evaluation therefore extends beyond measuring one melting point. Researchers cycle samples through heating and cooling hundreds or thousands of times. They check whether the transition temperature drifts, whether shells leak, and how much storage remains. Full panels are tested for bending strength, flame spread, moisture response, and heat flow. A capsule that performs well in a small sealed vial may fail during ordinary construction.<br><br>Building tests require realistic schedules. A panel may reduce afternoon peaks in a climate with cool nights because the material freezes before morning. During a sequence of hot nights, it may remain liquid and begin each day with little empty capacity. Mechanical ventilation or nighttime cooling can reset it, but the energy used for that reset must be included when judging savings.<br><br>Placement matters too. A panel hidden behind heavy furniture exchanges heat with the room slowly. Material near a sunlit surface may charge early, while ceiling placement may better intercept rising warm air. Control systems can coordinate shades, ventilation, and cooling equipment so that stored heat is released when outdoor conditions are favorable instead of returning to the occupied room.<br><br>Phase-change panels are sometimes described as thermal batteries. The comparison is useful because both devices shift energy in time, but it can mislead. The panel does not generate cooling, and its storage range is constrained by temperature. It is more like a reusable buffer that delays part of a heat load. Whether delay reduces energy use depends on what happens when the stored heat is released.<br><br>The technology's success is therefore not captured by the largest laboratory storage number. A useful product must survive manufacturing and cycling, exchange heat at the needed rate, melt and freeze within the building's actual schedule, and cooperate with other systems. Material capacity creates an opportunity; design and operation determine whether the building can use it."},
286: {skill_tested: "Explain why component tests do not establish product readiness.", question: "Why might a capsule that performs well in a sealed vial still fail in a building panel?", option1: "A sealed vial always has a lower temperature than a building.", option2: "Panel tests measure only the color of the capsule shell.", option3: "The phase-change core stops absorbing energy when placed near a binder.", option4: "Manufacturing, cutting, binder interactions, moisture, fire requirements, and mechanical loads introduce additional conditions.", correctAnswer: "Manufacturing, cutting, binder interactions, moisture, fire requirements, and mechanical loads introduce additional conditions.", explanation: "Step 1: Vial tests isolate capsule thermal behavior.<br><br>Step 2: Panels must survive mixing and construction while meeting structural, moisture, flame, and heat-flow requirements.<br><br>Step 3: Product performance depends on integration conditions absent from the isolated test.<br><br>Answer: Manufacturing, cutting, binder interactions, moisture, fire requirements, and mechanical loads introduce additional conditions.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage explains how phase-change materials can store heat inside building components.<br><br>A wall ordinarily stores heat by becoming warmer. The amount depends on its mass and heat capacity, so a lightweight wall changes temperature relatively quickly. A phase-change material adds another form of storage. As it melts, it absorbs energy while remaining near its melting temperature. When it later freezes, it releases much of that energy. The storage is tied to a change of state rather than only to a rising temperature. Because melting and freezing are reversible, the same material can repeat the exchange many times. Useful storage therefore depends not only on one cycle's capacity but on retaining that capacity through years of transitions.<br><br>For a building, the selected melting range matters. A substance that melts far below indoor temperature will already be liquid when cooling is needed. One that melts too high may never activate. Ideally, the material absorbs daytime heat as a room approaches the upper edge of comfort and solidifies after temperatures fall, ready to repeat the cycle the next day.<br><br>Many useful phase-change substances are waxes or salt mixtures that cannot simply be poured into wallboard. Liquid could leak during melting or react with surrounding material. Manufacturers enclose tiny droplets in polymer or mineral shells. Millions of microcapsules can then be blended into plaster, ceiling panels, or insulation while the shell keeps the active core separated.<br><br>Encapsulation solves one problem while creating others. The shell occupies space but stores little heat, so a composite holds less active material than a pure block of the substance. Capsules can also break during mixing, cutting, or repeated expansion. Their surfaces may interfere with the binder that gives a panel strength. Designers balance capsule concentration against mechanical durability and manufacturability.<br><br>Heat must reach the capsule before storage can occur. Some phase-change materials conduct heat poorly. A thick panel may contain substantial capacity at its center but charge too slowly during the useful part of the day. Adding conductive particles or thin metal paths can increase heat transfer, although those additions raise cost, add weight, or alter fire performance. Capacity and delivery rate are separate design questions.<br><br>Laboratory evaluation therefore extends beyond measuring one melting point. Researchers cycle samples through heating and cooling hundreds or thousands of times. They check whether the transition temperature drifts, whether shells leak, and how much storage remains. Full panels are tested for bending strength, flame spread, moisture response, and heat flow. A capsule that performs well in a small sealed vial may fail during ordinary construction.<br><br>Building tests require realistic schedules. A panel may reduce afternoon peaks in a climate with cool nights because the material freezes before morning. During a sequence of hot nights, it may remain liquid and begin each day with little empty capacity. Mechanical ventilation or nighttime cooling can reset it, but the energy used for that reset must be included when judging savings.<br><br>Placement matters too. A panel hidden behind heavy furniture exchanges heat with the room slowly. Material near a sunlit surface may charge early, while ceiling placement may better intercept rising warm air. Control systems can coordinate shades, ventilation, and cooling equipment so that stored heat is released when outdoor conditions are favorable instead of returning to the occupied room.<br><br>Phase-change panels are sometimes described as thermal batteries. The comparison is useful because both devices shift energy in time, but it can mislead. The panel does not generate cooling, and its storage range is constrained by temperature. It is more like a reusable buffer that delays part of a heat load. Whether delay reduces energy use depends on what happens when the stored heat is released.<br><br>The technology's success is therefore not captured by the largest laboratory storage number. A useful product must survive manufacturing and cycling, exchange heat at the needed rate, melt and freeze within the building's actual schedule, and cooperate with other systems. Material capacity creates an opportunity; design and operation determine whether the building can use it."},
287: {skill_tested: "Evaluate the thermal-battery analogy.", question: "Why does the author call the thermal-battery comparison both useful and potentially misleading?", option1: "The panel shifts energy in time like a battery, but it does not create cooling and works only within a limited temperature cycle.", option2: "The panel stores electricity but cannot release heat.", option3: "Batteries and panels use identical chemical reactions.", option4: "The analogy proves that release timing cannot affect energy use.", correctAnswer: "The panel shifts energy in time like a battery, but it does not create cooling and works only within a limited temperature cycle.", explanation: "Step 1: Both systems store energy for release at another time.<br><br>Step 2: The panel's storage depends on melting and freezing and only delays part of a heat load.<br><br>Step 3: Whether that delay helps depends on when and where the heat is later released.<br><br>Answer: The panel shifts energy in time like a battery, but it does not create cooling and works only within a limited temperature cycle.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage explains how phase-change materials can store heat inside building components.<br><br>A wall ordinarily stores heat by becoming warmer. The amount depends on its mass and heat capacity, so a lightweight wall changes temperature relatively quickly. A phase-change material adds another form of storage. As it melts, it absorbs energy while remaining near its melting temperature. When it later freezes, it releases much of that energy. The storage is tied to a change of state rather than only to a rising temperature. Because melting and freezing are reversible, the same material can repeat the exchange many times. Useful storage therefore depends not only on one cycle's capacity but on retaining that capacity through years of transitions.<br><br>For a building, the selected melting range matters. A substance that melts far below indoor temperature will already be liquid when cooling is needed. One that melts too high may never activate. Ideally, the material absorbs daytime heat as a room approaches the upper edge of comfort and solidifies after temperatures fall, ready to repeat the cycle the next day.<br><br>Many useful phase-change substances are waxes or salt mixtures that cannot simply be poured into wallboard. Liquid could leak during melting or react with surrounding material. Manufacturers enclose tiny droplets in polymer or mineral shells. Millions of microcapsules can then be blended into plaster, ceiling panels, or insulation while the shell keeps the active core separated.<br><br>Encapsulation solves one problem while creating others. The shell occupies space but stores little heat, so a composite holds less active material than a pure block of the substance. Capsules can also break during mixing, cutting, or repeated expansion. Their surfaces may interfere with the binder that gives a panel strength. Designers balance capsule concentration against mechanical durability and manufacturability.<br><br>Heat must reach the capsule before storage can occur. Some phase-change materials conduct heat poorly. A thick panel may contain substantial capacity at its center but charge too slowly during the useful part of the day. Adding conductive particles or thin metal paths can increase heat transfer, although those additions raise cost, add weight, or alter fire performance. Capacity and delivery rate are separate design questions.<br><br>Laboratory evaluation therefore extends beyond measuring one melting point. Researchers cycle samples through heating and cooling hundreds or thousands of times. They check whether the transition temperature drifts, whether shells leak, and how much storage remains. Full panels are tested for bending strength, flame spread, moisture response, and heat flow. A capsule that performs well in a small sealed vial may fail during ordinary construction.<br><br>Building tests require realistic schedules. A panel may reduce afternoon peaks in a climate with cool nights because the material freezes before morning. During a sequence of hot nights, it may remain liquid and begin each day with little empty capacity. Mechanical ventilation or nighttime cooling can reset it, but the energy used for that reset must be included when judging savings.<br><br>Placement matters too. A panel hidden behind heavy furniture exchanges heat with the room slowly. Material near a sunlit surface may charge early, while ceiling placement may better intercept rising warm air. Control systems can coordinate shades, ventilation, and cooling equipment so that stored heat is released when outdoor conditions are favorable instead of returning to the occupied room.<br><br>Phase-change panels are sometimes described as thermal batteries. The comparison is useful because both devices shift energy in time, but it can mislead. The panel does not generate cooling, and its storage range is constrained by temperature. It is more like a reusable buffer that delays part of a heat load. Whether delay reduces energy use depends on what happens when the stored heat is released.<br><br>The technology's success is therefore not captured by the largest laboratory storage number. A useful product must survive manufacturing and cycling, exchange heat at the needed rate, melt and freeze within the building's actual schedule, and cooperate with other systems. Material capacity creates an opportunity; design and operation determine whether the building can use it."},
288: {skill_tested: "Synthesize material properties with operating context.", question: "Which proposed building test would best evaluate the technology according to the passage's full standard of success?", option1: "Measure one capsule's heat capacity once at its ideal temperature.", option2: "Compare occupied rooms across repeated weather cycles while tracking panel condition, indoor peaks, reset energy, and release timing.", option3: "Count capsules before they are mixed into a binder.", option4: "Select the panel with the highest advertised storage value without measuring heat flow.", correctAnswer: "Compare occupied rooms across repeated weather cycles while tracking panel condition, indoor peaks, reset energy, and release timing.", explanation: "Step 1: Useful performance combines durability, capacity, rate, melting range, schedule, and system interaction.<br><br>Step 2: Repeated occupied-room tests expose actual charging, nighttime reset, release, and physical aging.<br><br>Step 3: Tracking both comfort peaks and reset energy determines whether shifting heat produces a real building benefit.<br><br>Answer: Compare occupied rooms across repeated weather cycles while tracking panel condition, indoor peaks, reset energy, and release timing.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage explains how phase-change materials can store heat inside building components.<br><br>A wall ordinarily stores heat by becoming warmer. The amount depends on its mass and heat capacity, so a lightweight wall changes temperature relatively quickly. A phase-change material adds another form of storage. As it melts, it absorbs energy while remaining near its melting temperature. When it later freezes, it releases much of that energy. The storage is tied to a change of state rather than only to a rising temperature. Because melting and freezing are reversible, the same material can repeat the exchange many times. Useful storage therefore depends not only on one cycle's capacity but on retaining that capacity through years of transitions.<br><br>For a building, the selected melting range matters. A substance that melts far below indoor temperature will already be liquid when cooling is needed. One that melts too high may never activate. Ideally, the material absorbs daytime heat as a room approaches the upper edge of comfort and solidifies after temperatures fall, ready to repeat the cycle the next day.<br><br>Many useful phase-change substances are waxes or salt mixtures that cannot simply be poured into wallboard. Liquid could leak during melting or react with surrounding material. Manufacturers enclose tiny droplets in polymer or mineral shells. Millions of microcapsules can then be blended into plaster, ceiling panels, or insulation while the shell keeps the active core separated.<br><br>Encapsulation solves one problem while creating others. The shell occupies space but stores little heat, so a composite holds less active material than a pure block of the substance. Capsules can also break during mixing, cutting, or repeated expansion. Their surfaces may interfere with the binder that gives a panel strength. Designers balance capsule concentration against mechanical durability and manufacturability.<br><br>Heat must reach the capsule before storage can occur. Some phase-change materials conduct heat poorly. A thick panel may contain substantial capacity at its center but charge too slowly during the useful part of the day. Adding conductive particles or thin metal paths can increase heat transfer, although those additions raise cost, add weight, or alter fire performance. Capacity and delivery rate are separate design questions.<br><br>Laboratory evaluation therefore extends beyond measuring one melting point. Researchers cycle samples through heating and cooling hundreds or thousands of times. They check whether the transition temperature drifts, whether shells leak, and how much storage remains. Full panels are tested for bending strength, flame spread, moisture response, and heat flow. A capsule that performs well in a small sealed vial may fail during ordinary construction.<br><br>Building tests require realistic schedules. A panel may reduce afternoon peaks in a climate with cool nights because the material freezes before morning. During a sequence of hot nights, it may remain liquid and begin each day with little empty capacity. Mechanical ventilation or nighttime cooling can reset it, but the energy used for that reset must be included when judging savings.<br><br>Placement matters too. A panel hidden behind heavy furniture exchanges heat with the room slowly. Material near a sunlit surface may charge early, while ceiling placement may better intercept rising warm air. Control systems can coordinate shades, ventilation, and cooling equipment so that stored heat is released when outdoor conditions are favorable instead of returning to the occupied room.<br><br>Phase-change panels are sometimes described as thermal batteries. The comparison is useful because both devices shift energy in time, but it can mislead. The panel does not generate cooling, and its storage range is constrained by temperature. It is more like a reusable buffer that delays part of a heat load. Whether delay reduces energy use depends on what happens when the stored heat is released.<br><br>The technology's success is therefore not captured by the largest laboratory storage number. A useful product must survive manufacturing and cycling, exchange heat at the needed rate, melt and freeze within the building's actual schedule, and cooperate with other systems. Material capacity creates an opportunity; design and operation determine whether the building can use it."}
